var GRAPE_TABLE = {
"albarino": {"slug":"albarino","name":"Albariño","colour":"white","kind":"grape","status":"full","mode":"home","court":true,"aliases":[],"notIn":[],"say":"ahl-bah-REE-nyo, in Portugal ahl-vah-REE-nyoo","lines":{"s10":"Albariño is the crisp, dry Atlantic white of north west Spain: white peach, lime and a breath of sea salt.","s20":"Albariño comes from Rías Baixas, on the wet, green Atlantic coast of Galicia in north west Spain. It tastes of white peach, lime and grapefruit with a salty finish, bone dry and bright, and it rarely sees oak.","s45":"Albariño grows within sight of the Atlantic, in Rías Baixas, on the rainy green coast of north west Spain. It tastes of white peach, apricot and lime, with a salty edge like sea spray. It is dry and light, with high, mouthwatering acidity. Most ferments in steel tanks and is bottled young, with no oak. Across the river Miño, in Portugal, it is called Alvarinho and is often a touch riper and rounder. It is a wine for shellfish: oysters, grilled prawns or octopus. The vines are trained overhead on granite posts, high enough to walk beneath, so the sea wind can dry the grapes after rain.","sale":"Albariño gives you ripe peach on the nose and the bite of lime on the tongue, with a salty finish. Cold, it suits shrimp grilled in garlic and lemon, or the octopus."},"glass":{"lead":"White peach, apricot and lime with honeysuckle and a salty, wet stone finish, light, dry and sharply fresh.","notes":[{"family":"stone fruit","notes":["white peach","apricot"]},{"family":"citrus","notes":["lemon zest","lime","pink grapefruit"]},{"family":"orchard fruit","notes":["green apple","ripe pear"]},{"family":"floral","notes":["honeysuckle","acacia blossom"]},{"family":"mineral","notes":["sea salt","wet stone"]}],"ladder":[{"climate":"cool","where":"Val do Salnés, on the coast of Rías Baixas","notes":["lime","green apple","white peach","sea salt"]},{"climate":"moderate","where":"O Rosal and Condado do Tea; Monção e Melgaço in Portugal","notes":["ripe peach","apricot","honeysuckle"]}],"structure":"Dry. Acidity high, alcohol moderate, body light to medium, with a faint, pithy bitterness on the finish and rarely any oak."},"worlds":{"home":{"home":"Rías Baixas, on the cool, wet Atlantic coast of Galicia, where the vines stand on granite and are trained overhead on pergolas to keep the bunches dry. A wine labelled Rías Baixas Albariño is the grape alone. It is usually fermented cold in steel and bottled young; some rests on its lees, and a little sees oak.","abroad":"Across the Miño in Portugal it is Alvarinho, the fuller white of Vinho Verde, above all from Monção e Melgaço. Uruguay's Atlantic coast, California and New Zealand grow it too, in much the same crisp style, mostly without oak."}},"service":{"temp":"45 to 50 degrees Fahrenheit, 7 to 10 degrees Celsius: cold, but not so cold that the peach goes quiet.","glass":"A white wine glass, tapered to hold the perfume.","decant":"Usually unnecessary; open it cold and pour.","age":"Drunk young, within one to three years; old vine and lees aged bottles keep a few years longer."},"food":{"with":[{"dish":"Gulf oysters on the half shell","why":"salt meets salt, and the acidity does the work of lemon"},{"dish":"Grilled prawns with garlic","why":"peach fruit and bright acidity lift garlic and oil"},{"dish":"Octopus with paprika and olive oil","why":"the Galician match: acidity cuts through the oil"},{"dish":"Ceviche or raw fish","why":"lime in the wine echoes lime on the plate"},{"dish":"Steamed mussels or clams","why":"a light, salty wine for briny shellfish"}]},"labels":[{"term":"Rías Baixas","means":"The denomination on Galicia's Atlantic coast; labelled Rías Baixas Albariño, the wine is the grape alone."},{"term":"Alvarinho","means":"The grape's Portuguese name, on Vinho Verde from Monção e Melgaço and beyond."},{"term":"Val do Salnés","means":"The coolest, most coastal subzone of Rías Baixas, around Cambados: the leanest, saltiest wines."},{"term":"O Rosal, Condado do Tea","means":"Warmer subzones on the Miño, where Albariño may be blended with Loureira or Treixadura."},{"term":"Sobre lías","means":"On the lees: the wine was kept on its spent yeast for a rounder texture."}],"guest":[{"q":"Is Albariño sweet?","a":"No. It smells of ripe peach and apricot, which can suggest sweetness, but it is bone dry, with high acidity and a salty finish."},{"q":"I usually drink Sauvignon Blanc. Will I like this?","a":"Very likely. It is as crisp and as dry, but the fruit is peach and lime where Sauvignon Blanc has cut grass, and the finish is salty."},{"q":"The Portuguese list says Alvarinho. Is that the same grape?","a":"Yes. Alvarinho is its name across the river in Portugal, where it makes the fuller whites of Vinho Verde, above all in Monção e Melgaço."}],"compare":[{"slug":"riesling","tell":"Dry Riesling shows lime, green apple and petrol with even sharper acidity; Albariño is peachier, with a salty finish and no petrol."},{"slug":"pinot-grigio","tell":"Pinot Grigio is more neutral and less salty, with pear and a soft finish; Albariño has more perfume, more acidity and a briny edge."},{"slug":"godello","tell":"Godello is broader, stonier and quieter on the nose, often with a creamy feel from its lees; Albariño is more scented, lighter and saltier."}],"cards":[{"id":"gt-xzwa8s8s","front":"Albariño: which region is home, on what soil and what training?","back":"Rías Baixas, on Galicia's cool, wet Atlantic coast. Granite soils, and vines trained overhead on pergolas, the parra, so that air can dry the bunches after rain."},{"id":"gt-x7c0rude","front":"What is Albariño called in Portugal, and where is its home there?","back":"Alvarinho. It makes the fuller, still whites of Vinho Verde, above all in the sub-region of Monção e Melgaço, across the Miño from Rías Baixas."},{"id":"gt-4qt3iir0","front":"Albariño blind: what parts it from a dry Riesling?","back":"White peach and apricot with a salty, pithy finish and no petrol. Riesling shows lime and green apple, still sharper acidity and, with age, petrol."}],"questions":[{"id":"gt-njyzkrp5","q":"A blind white is pale lemon with green tints, dry and unoaked: white peach, apricot and lime, high acidity, moderate alcohol and a salty, faintly bitter finish. Which grape and origin fit it?","opts":["Viognier, Condrieu","Chardonnay, Meursault","Albariño, Rías Baixas","Gewürztraminer, Alsace"],"a":2,"exp":"Peach and apricot with high acidity, a salty finish and no oak point to Albariño from the Atlantic coast. Viognier shares the apricot but is fuller, with low acidity and more alcohol."},{"id":"gt-yqv2f01u","q":"A guest orders a bottle labelled Rías Baixas Albariño and asks whether it is a blend. What do you tell her?","opts":["It is Albariño alone","Albariño with Loureira and Treixadura","At least 85 per cent Albariño","At least 70 per cent Albariño"],"a":0,"exp":"A wine labelled Rías Baixas Albariño is made wholly of the grape. The 70 per cent rule belongs to the subzone labels, such as Rosal, which may blend in Loureira."},{"id":"gt-le8qua93","q":"A guest loved the Albariño she drank in Spain and now sees Alvarinho on a Portuguese list. Which region is that bottle most likely to come from?","opts":["Alentejo","Vinho Verde","Bairrada","Douro Valley"],"a":1,"exp":"Alvarinho is the Portuguese name of Albariño, and its home there is Vinho Verde, above all Monção e Melgaço. The Douro's whites rest on other grapes, such as Gouveio and Rabigato."}],"sources":[{"title":"Consejo Regulador Rías Baixas: the wines of the denomination","url":"https://doriasbaixas.com/en/the-wines-of-d-o-rias-baixas/"},{"title":"Rías Baixas Wines: geography and terroir","url":"https://www.riasbaixaswines.com/about/geography-terroir/"},{"title":"Wines of Portugal: Alvarinho","url":"https://winesofportugal.com/en/portuguese-wines/grape-varieties/alvarinho/"},{"title":"Uruguay Wine: the regions","url":"https://uruguay.wine/en/regions/"}]},
"chardonnay": {"slug":"chardonnay","name":"Chardonnay","colour":"white","kind":"grape","status":"full","mode":"contrast","court":true,"aliases":[],"notIn":[],"say":"shar-doh-NAY","lines":{"s10":"Chardonnay is the white grape of Burgundy, lemon and green apple when lean, ripe peach and toasted hazelnut when rich.","s20":"Chardonnay is Burgundy's white grape, and it takes the shape of the place and the cellar. From a cool site it is lemon, green apple and chalk, and from a warm one or from new oak barrels it is peach, cream and toasted hazelnut.","s45":"Chardonnay is the white grape of Burgundy, and it now grows almost everywhere wine is made. On its own it is quiet: lemon, green apple and white peach, dry, with a rounded, medium to full body. The cellar finishes the picture. New oak barrels add toasted hazelnut and vanilla, and a second, softening fermentation adds a note of butter. Burgundy tends to be leaner and more chalky, while California and Australia are often riper and creamier. Pour it with roast chicken, lobster or anything in a butter sauce. Its parents, found by DNA in 1999, are Pinot and a lowly, almost forgotten grape called Gouais Blanc.","sale":"Chardonnay is the white that can be whatever the table needs, from chalk and lemon to cream and toasted hazelnut. Tell me the dish and I will pour the lean one for oysters or the rich one for lobster."},"glass":{"lead":"Lemon, green apple and white peach on a rounded frame, with toasted hazelnut and cream where the cellar adds new oak.","notes":[{"family":"citrus","notes":["lemon zest","lemon curd"]},{"family":"orchard fruit","notes":["green apple","yellow apple","ripe pear"]},{"family":"stone fruit","notes":["white peach","nectarine"]},{"family":"tropical fruit","notes":["pineapple","mango","honeydew melon"]},{"family":"lees and cellar","notes":["butter","fresh cream","struck match"]},{"family":"oak","notes":["toasted hazelnut","vanilla pod","toast"]}],"ladder":[{"climate":"cool","where":"Chablis and the Côte de Beaune, Champagne, Tasmania","notes":["lemon zest","green apple","white peach","crushed chalk"]},{"climate":"moderate","where":"Napa and Sonoma, the Central Coast, Margaret River","notes":["yellow apple","nectarine","honeydew melon"]},{"climate":"warm","where":"California's Central Valley and inland south eastern Australia","notes":["pineapple","mango","baked apple"]}],"structure":"Dry. Acidity medium plus in a cool site and medium in a warm one, alcohol moderate to high, body medium to full."},"worlds":{"contrast":{"old":{"where":"Burgundy, from Chablis to the Mâconnais, and Champagne","glass":"Tends to be leaner and more restrained: lemon, green apple and white peach over crushed chalk, with bright acidity and moderate alcohol. The place leads and the fruit follows.","cellar":"Chablis is mostly raised in steel or old casks. The Côte de Beaune ferments in barrel, a share of it new, and rests on its lees."},"new":{"where":"California, Oregon, Australia, New Zealand, Chile and South Africa","glass":"Typically riper and broader: yellow apple, ripe peach and pineapple, with softer acidity, fuller body and higher alcohol. Butter and vanilla often sit in front of the fruit.","cellar":"Often full malolactic conversion and a larger share of new French oak, with the lees stirred for a creamy texture."},"why":"Climate first: more sun and warmth mean riper fruit, lower acidity and higher alcohol. Then the cellar's choices of oak and malolactic conversion. Then the label: Burgundy names a place and its rules, the New World names the grape.","blur":"Cool New World sites such as the Sonoma Coast, Tasmania and the Yarra Valley now make lean, bright wines, and a warm year in Burgundy gives ripe, creamy ones.","call":"Chalk, high acidity and oak held in the background lean Old World; pineapple, butter and vanilla up front lean New World."}},"service":{"temp":"50 to 55 degrees Fahrenheit, 10 to 13 degrees Celsius; a lean, unoaked wine a little cooler.","glass":"A white wine glass for lean styles; a wide Burgundy bowl for rich, oaked wines.","decant":"Rarely. A young, tightly wound white Burgundy can open with a short decant; taste a sample first.","age":"Most within two to five years; village and premier cru Burgundy five to ten, the grands crus longer."},"food":{"with":[{"dish":"Oysters and raw shellfish","why":"lean, chalky Chablis mirrors the brine"},{"dish":"Roast chicken","why":"the wine's weight and cream match the roast"},{"dish":"Lobster or crab with drawn butter","why":"a rich, oaked Chardonnay echoes the butter"},{"dish":"Redfish in brown butter","why":"toasted hazelnut in the wine meets the browned butter"}]},"labels":[{"term":"Chablis","means":"Chardonnay from northern Burgundy, usually made without new oak: lean, chalky and bright."},{"term":"Blanc de Blancs","means":"Sparkling wine from white grapes alone; in Champagne that is nearly always Chardonnay."},{"term":"Unoaked","means":"Fermented and aged in steel, so the fruit and the acidity show with no vanilla or toast."},{"term":"Pouilly-Fuissé","means":"Chardonnay from the Mâconnais in southern Burgundy; not Pouilly-Fumé, which is Loire Sauvignon Blanc."}],"guest":[{"q":"Is Chablis a Chardonnay?","a":"Yes. Chablis is Chardonnay from the cool north of Burgundy, usually made without new oak, so it tastes of lemon, green apple and chalk and not of butter."},{"q":"I do not like oaky, buttery Chardonnay. What should I try?","a":"Ask for an unoaked one: Chablis, a Mâcon, or a cool coast wine from Sonoma or Tasmania. The butter and vanilla come from the cellar, not the grape."},{"q":"Why does one Chardonnay taste so different from the next?","a":"The grape itself is quiet, so climate and cellar speak loudly. A cool site gives lemon and green apple, a warm one pineapple, and new oak barrels add toast and vanilla."}],"compare":[{"slug":"chenin-blanc","tell":"Chenin Blanc shows higher acidity with quince, honey and a wet wool note; Chardonnay is rounder and takes its accent from oak and lees."},{"slug":"pinot-grigio","tell":"Pinot Gris is oilier, with spiced pear and a faint bitterness; Chardonnay is cleaner in line and shows cream or toast from the cellar."},{"slug":"viognier","tell":"Viognier is loudly perfumed, all apricot and honeysuckle, with lower acidity; Chardonnay has no such perfume of its own."}],"cards":[{"id":"gt-bhm5jlyh","front":"Chardonnay: where is it at home, and who are its parents?","back":"Burgundy, from Chablis to the Mâconnais. DNA work published in 1999 found it to be a cross of Pinot and Gouais Blanc."},{"id":"gt-9g6nc7tu","front":"Old World or New World Chardonnay: what does a blind taster lean on?","back":"Chalk, high acidity and oak in the background lean Old World. Pineapple, butter and vanilla up front, with softer acidity and higher alcohol, lean New World."},{"id":"gt-rp845xu5","front":"What does the cellar add to Chardonnay, and from what?","back":"Butter from malolactic conversion; toasted hazelnut, vanilla and toast from new oak; cream and bread dough from time on the lees."}],"questions":[{"id":"gt-vcmpl7ay","q":"A blind white is pale gold and dry, with pineapple, ripe peach, butter and vanilla, soft acidity and high alcohol. Which origin fits it?","opts":["Chablis, Burgundy","Mosel, Germany","Sancerre, Loire","Napa Valley, California"],"a":3,"exp":"Tropical ripeness, butter from malolactic conversion and vanilla from new oak on a full body point to a sunny New World site. Chablis would be lean, chalky and unoaked.","tag":"worlds"},{"id":"gt-10y4swe0","q":"A guest who dislikes buttery, oaky whites still wants a Chardonnay with her oysters. Which bottle do you bring?","opts":["Pouilly-Fumé","Chablis","Meursault","Napa Valley Chardonnay"],"a":1,"exp":"Chablis is Chardonnay from northern Burgundy, usually made without new oak: lemon, green apple and chalk. Meursault and Napa are typically barrel aged, and Pouilly-Fumé is Sauvignon Blanc."},{"id":"gt-753e385n","q":"A guest asks what Blanc de Blancs means on a bottle of Champagne. What do you tell him?","opts":["White wine from white grapes, nearly always Chardonnay","A Champagne from a single harvest year","A Champagne with no sugar added at the end","White wine from black grapes, Pinot Noir or Meunier"],"a":0,"exp":"Blanc de Blancs is white wine from white grapes, which in Champagne nearly always means Chardonnay. White from black grapes is Blanc de Noirs."}],"sources":[{"title":"Bourgogne Wines: Chardonnay, a Bourgogne original that has travelled the world","url":"https://www.bourgogne-wines.com/wine-and-terroir/our-grape-varietals-our-colors/chardonnay/chardonnay-a-bourgogne-original-that-has-travelled-the-world,2797,10602.html"},{"title":"University of California, Davis: DNA fingerprinting reveals surprise in wine grape family tree","url":"https://www.ucdavis.edu/news/dna-fingerprinting-reveals-surprise-wine-grape-family-tree"},{"title":"Comité Champagne: Champagne Blanc de Blancs","url":"https://www.champagne.fr/en/champagne-tasting/type-of-champagne/blanc-de-blancs"},{"title":"Wine Australia: Discover Australian wine, regions and varieties","url":"https://www.wineaustralia.com/getmedia/598f7973-7adc-4706-be7e-54e5e35dac13/DIGI_Web_Discover-booklet-E.pdf"}]},
"chenin-blanc": {"slug":"chenin-blanc","name":"Chenin Blanc","colour":"white","kind":"grape","status":"full","mode":"contrast","court":true,"aliases":[],"notIn":[],"say":"shuh-NAN BLAHN","lines":{"s10":"Chenin Blanc is the Loire's shape shifter: dry, sweet or sparkling, with quince, baked apple and bright acidity in every style.","s20":"Chenin Blanc is the white grape of Vouvray and Savennières in the Loire, and of South Africa's old vines. It tastes of quince, yellow apple and honey, and its high acidity carries it from bone dry to richly sweet.","s45":"Chenin Blanc does everything a white grape can do, from bone dry to sparkling to honey sweet. Its home is the middle Loire, in Vouvray, Savennières and Anjou. It tastes of quince, yellow apple and honey, with a note like beeswax, and its high acidity keeps even the sweet ones fresh. The Loire mostly keeps new oak away, while South Africa makes riper wines, often from old vines and in barrel. Pour a dry one with roast pork, and an off dry one with spiced food. At the Cape it was long called Steen, and only in the 1960s was it shown to be the Loire's grape.","sale":"Chenin Blanc gives you richness and freshness in the same glass: quince and honey on a spine of bright acidity. I would pour a dry Vouvray with the roast pork, or an off dry one with anything spiced."},"glass":{"lead":"Quince, yellow apple and honey with a waxy, wet wool note, held taut by high acidity at any sweetness.","notes":[{"family":"orchard fruit","notes":["quince","yellow apple","baked apple","ripe pear"]},{"family":"citrus","notes":["lemon","candied lemon peel"]},{"family":"tropical fruit","notes":["pineapple","guava"]},{"family":"herbal","notes":["chamomile","straw"]},{"family":"honeyed","notes":["acacia honey","beeswax","lanolin"]},{"family":"oak","notes":["vanilla pod","toasted almond"]}],"ladder":[{"climate":"cool","where":"Vouvray, Montlouis and Savennières in the middle Loire","notes":["quince","yellow apple","lemon","chamomile"]},{"climate":"warm","where":"Swartland, Stellenbosch and Paarl in South Africa","notes":["ripe pear","pineapple","guava","honeyed melon"]}],"structure":"Dry to lusciously sweet. Acidity high at every sweetness, alcohol moderate, body medium; fuller in ripe, barrel raised South African wines."},"worlds":{"contrast":{"old":{"where":"The middle Loire: Vouvray, Montlouis, Savennières, Anjou and the Layon","glass":"Tends to be taut and chalky: quince, yellow apple and lemon with chamomile, beeswax and a wet wool note, at moderate alcohol. It may be dry, off dry or sweet, and the acidity is high throughout.","cellar":"Usually fermented in steel or old casks, with little new oak; the year decides whether a wine is dry, off dry, sweet or sparkling."},"new":{"where":"South Africa: Swartland, Stellenbosch, Paarl and the wider Western Cape","glass":"Typically riper and broader: yellow apple, ripe pear, pineapple and guava, with a fuller body, more alcohol and acidity a notch lower. Old vine wines are richer, with vanilla and toasted almond from barrels.","cellar":"Two broad styles: unwooded wines made cold in steel for early drinking, and richer old bush vine wines, often fermented and aged in barrel."},"why":"The Cape is warmer and sunnier than the Loire, so the fruit ripens further and alcohol rises; old, low yielding bush vines add concentration. The Loire names a place and a sweetness, South Africa the grape.","blur":"Savennières and warm Loire years can be rich and powerful, some Loire growers now use barrels, and cooler Cape sites make lean, taut, unwooded wines.","call":"Chalk, wet wool and piercing acidity at moderate alcohol lean Loire; riper pineapple and guava, more body and often vanilla from barrels lean South Africa."}},"service":{"temp":"45 to 50 degrees Fahrenheit, 7 to 10 degrees Celsius; rich barrel aged wines a little warmer, sweet ones colder.","glass":"A white wine glass; a small glass for the sweet wines and a tulip for the sparkling.","decant":"Usually unnecessary; a young, tight Savennières can open with a short decant.","age":"Most dry wines within five years, Savennières ten or more; sweet Vouvray and Layon wines keep for decades."},"food":{"with":[{"dish":"Roast pork with apples","why":"quince and apple in the wine meet the pork"},{"dish":"Roast chicken or veal in cream","why":"acidity cuts the cream, body matches the dish"},{"dish":"Crawfish étouffée","why":"an off dry wine cools the cayenne"},{"dish":"Tarte Tatin or blue cheese","why":"for the sweet wines: honey against caramel or salt"}]},"labels":[{"term":"Vouvray","means":"Chenin Blanc from Touraine in the Loire: dry, off dry, sweet or sparkling, so read the label."},{"term":"Sec, demi-sec, moelleux","means":"Dry, off dry and sweet: the sweetness words on a Loire Chenin Blanc."},{"term":"Savennières","means":"A dry, firm, long lived Chenin Blanc from Anjou, on the Loire near Angers."},{"term":"Coteaux du Layon","means":"Sweet Chenin Blanc from Anjou, often touched by noble rot; Bonnezeaux and Quarts de Chaume lie within it."},{"term":"Steen","means":"The old South African name for Chenin Blanc, still seen on some labels."}],"guest":[{"q":"Is Vouvray sweet or dry?","a":"Either. Vouvray is Chenin Blanc, and it comes dry, off dry, sweet and sparkling. Look for sec for dry, demi-sec for a touch of sweetness and moelleux for sweet."},{"q":"I usually drink Chardonnay. Would I like this?","a":"A dry, barrel raised South African Chenin Blanc is the nearest thing: full and round, with ripe pear and a little vanilla. It has brighter acidity than most Chardonnay."},{"q":"What is that waxy, woolly smell?","a":"It is the grape's own mark, often described as beeswax, lanolin or wet wool. Tasters look for it beside quince and honey to recognise Chenin Blanc."}],"compare":[{"slug":"riesling","tell":"Riesling shows lime, white peach and, with age, petrol; Chenin Blanc shows quince, baked apple and wet wool on a broader body."},{"slug":"chardonnay","tell":"Chardonnay is rounder, with lower acidity and no waxy, woolly note; Chenin Blanc keeps a sharper edge and tastes of quince and honey."},{"slug":"semillon","tell":"Sémillon is waxy too, but softer in acidity, with lemon and toast as it ages; Chenin Blanc is sharper, with quince and apple."}],"cards":[{"id":"gt-tsytn0g7","front":"Chenin Blanc: name its Loire homes and its old South African name.","back":"Vouvray, Montlouis, Savennières and Anjou in the middle Loire. In South Africa it was long called Steen, and was matched to the Loire's grape in the 1960s."},{"id":"gt-k959dx3n","front":"Chenin Blanc blind: Loire or South Africa, what tips it?","back":"Chalk, wet wool and piercing acidity at moderate alcohol lean Loire. Riper pineapple and guava, more body and often vanilla from barrels lean South Africa."},{"id":"gt-cupfiktb","front":"On a Vouvray, which is Chenin Blanc, what do sec, demi-sec and moelleux mean?","back":"The sweetness. Sec is dry, demi-sec is off dry and moelleux is sweet. Vouvray is also made sparkling, as pétillant or mousseux."}],"questions":[{"id":"gt-lqty1qyy","q":"Two dry Chenin Blancs are poured blind. One is taut, with quince, chalk and a wet wool note at moderate alcohol; the other is fuller, with pineapple, guava and vanilla. Which pair of origins fits, in that order?","opts":["Swartland, then Vouvray","Stellenbosch, then Savennières","Paarl, then Montlouis","Vouvray, then Swartland"],"a":3,"exp":"Chalk, wet wool and piercing acidity at moderate alcohol tend to mark the Loire. Riper pineapple and guava, a fuller body and vanilla from barrels tend to mark South Africa's old vine wines.","tag":"worlds"},{"id":"gt-xvfr5njv","q":"A guest wants a Vouvray with only a touch of sweetness for a spiced pork dish. Which word on the label do you look for?","opts":["Moelleux","Brut","Demi-sec","Sec"],"a":2,"exp":"Demi-sec is off dry: a touch of sweetness balanced by high acidity. Sec is dry, moelleux is fully sweet, and brut is the word for a dry sparkling wine."},{"id":"gt-4hjqn0vc","q":"A blind white is medium gold and off dry, with quince, baked apple, honey and a wet wool note over searing acidity. Which grape is it most likely to be?","opts":["Chenin Blanc","Sémillon","Riesling","Chardonnay"],"a":0,"exp":"Honeyed quince and baked apple with wet wool over searing acidity is Chenin Blanc. Riesling would show lime and often petrol, and Chardonnay and Sémillon have softer acidity."}],"sources":[{"title":"Vins du Val de Loire: Vouvray","url":"https://www.vinsvaldeloire.fr/en/wine/vouvray"},{"title":"Vins du Val de Loire: Savennières","url":"https://vinsvaldeloire.fr/en/wine/savennieres"},{"title":"Chenin Blanc Association of South Africa: History of Chenin","url":"https://www.chenin.co.za/history-of-chenin"},{"title":"Wines of South Africa: White wine varieties","url":"https://www.wosa.co.za/The-Industry/Varieties-and-Styles/White-Wine-Varieties/"}]},
"gewurztraminer": {"slug":"gewurztraminer","name":"Gewürztraminer","colour":"white","kind":"grape","status":"full","mode":"contrast","court":true,"aliases":[],"notIn":[],"say":"guh-VURTS-trah-mee-ner","lines":{"s10":"Gewürztraminer is the perfumed white of Alsace: lychee, rose petal and ginger, rich and soft, often with a touch of sweetness.","s20":"Gewürztraminer is a pink skinned grape that makes a deep gold, heady white, at home in Alsace. It smells of lychee, rose petal and ginger, feels rich and soft in the mouth, and is often just off dry.","s45":"Gewürztraminer announces itself before the glass reaches you. It is a pink skinned grape, and its home is Alsace, in the east of France. It smells of lychee, rose petal and ginger, and it is full and soft, low in acidity and often just off dry. The cellar keeps out of the way: no new oak, only ripe fruit. Alsace tends to be rich and heady, while California's cool coast is usually lighter and fresher. Set it beside washed rind cheese, foie gras or a fragrant Thai curry. Gewürz is the German word for spice, and Traminer is named for Tramin, a village in the Italian Alps.","sale":"Gewürztraminer meets fragrant, spiced food perfume for perfume: lychee and rose petal on a soft, rich body. Let me bring it with the curry, or with a ripe washed rind cheese at the end."},"glass":{"lead":"Lychee, rose petal and ginger on a full, oily, low acid frame, deep gold and often just off dry.","notes":[{"family":"tropical fruit","notes":["lychee","mango","passion fruit"]},{"family":"citrus","notes":["pink grapefruit","tangerine"]},{"family":"stone fruit","notes":["canned peach","apricot"]},{"family":"floral","notes":["rose petal","Turkish delight"]},{"family":"spice","notes":["fresh ginger","clove","allspice","cinnamon"]},{"family":"honeyed","notes":["honey","quince paste"]}],"ladder":[{"climate":"cool","where":"Alsace, Alto Adige, the Anderson Valley and Marlborough","notes":["lychee","pink grapefruit","rose petal","fresh ginger"]},{"climate":"moderate","where":"Gisborne and Washington's Columbia Valley","notes":["mango","ripe peach","tangerine"]}],"structure":"Dry to off dry, sometimes sweet. Acidity low, alcohol high, body full, with an oily texture and a faintly bitter finish."},"worlds":{"contrast":{"old":{"where":"Alsace above all; Alto Adige; Germany's Pfalz and Baden","glass":"Alsace tends to be deep gold, full and oily, often off dry: lychee, rose petal, ginger and clove with low acidity, high alcohol and a faintly bitter finish. Alto Adige is usually nearer dry.","cellar":"Picked late and very ripe, fermented in steel or large old casks with no new oak. The sweet late harvests are Vendanges Tardives and Sélection de Grains Nobles."},"new":{"where":"California's Anderson Valley, the Finger Lakes, Washington, New Zealand and Chile","glass":"Typically paler, lighter and fresher: lychee, rose petal and pink grapefruit at lower alcohol, with a little more acidity and often less sweetness; most are dry to off dry.","cellar":"Usually fermented cool in steel to guard the perfume and bottled young; a few late harvest wines are made."},"why":"Alsace lies dry and sunny behind the Vosges, with a long autumn, so the grape hangs late and turns rich, strong and soft. New World growers often choose cool sites, by a cold coast or far south, and seek freshness.","blur":"Alsace also makes taut, dry wines and Alto Adige's stay near dry, while Gisborne's are often oily and just off dry, as Alsace's are.","call":"Deep gold, oily weight and sweetness at high alcohol lean Alsace; a paler, fresher wine at lower alcohol leans New World."}},"service":{"temp":"45 to 50 degrees Fahrenheit, 7 to 10 degrees Celsius; the sweet late harvest wines a little colder.","glass":"A white wine glass; a small glass for the sweet late harvest wines.","decant":"Usually unnecessary: serve it fresh and cold from the bottle.","age":"Most within three to eight years; Vendanges Tardives and Sélection de Grains Nobles keep far longer."},"food":{"with":[{"dish":"Munster and other washed rind cheeses","why":"the wine's perfume and weight match the pungent rind"},{"dish":"Thai green curry","why":"soft, off dry fruit sits easily with chilli and coconut"},{"dish":"Foie gras","why":"a rich, off dry wine meets the richness"},{"dish":"Gumbo with smoked sausage","why":"off dry fruit cools the cayenne and stands up to the smoke"}]},"labels":[{"term":"Vendanges Tardives","means":"Late harvest, in Alsace: very ripe grapes and a rich wine, usually sweet."},{"term":"Sélection de Grains Nobles","means":"Alsace's sweet wine from grapes shrivelled by noble rot, picked over several passes."},{"term":"Alsace Grand Cru","means":"A named, classed vineyard of Alsace; Gewurztraminer is one of the four grapes most of them are kept for."},{"term":"Gewurztraminer","means":"The French spelling, with no umlaut, as Alsace prints it."},{"term":"Traminer Aromatico","means":"The grape's Italian name, seen on bottles from Alto Adige and Trentino."}],"guest":[{"q":"Is it sweet?","a":"Often a little. Many from Alsace are off dry, and the rich texture and low acidity make even a dry one taste sweeter than it is. Late harvest bottles are properly sweet."},{"q":"What does the name mean?","a":"Gewürz is German for spice, and Traminer is an old grape named for Tramin, a village in the Italian Alps. Think of it as the perfumed Traminer."},{"q":"Why is the wine so deep in colour?","a":"The grape's skins are pink, not green, and a little of that colour reaches the wine, which pours deep gold, sometimes with a coppery glint."}],"compare":[{"slug":"muscat-blanc","tell":"Muscat is grapey, with orange blossom, lighter and less oily; Gewürztraminer is lychee, rose petal and ginger on a fuller, softer frame."},{"slug":"torrontes","tell":"Torrontés shares the rose perfume but is lighter and fresher, with a bitter finish; Gewürztraminer is fuller, oilier and lower in acidity."},{"slug":"viognier","tell":"Viognier is apricot and honeysuckle, with no lychee or ginger; Gewürztraminer adds rose petal and lychee and is often off dry."}],"cards":[{"id":"gt-tjaysnlo","front":"Gewürztraminer: what does the name mean, and where is it at home?","back":"Gewürz is German for spice; Traminer is named for Tramin in Alto Adige. Its home on a wine list is Alsace, where it is one of the four noble grapes."},{"id":"gt-075knvyu","front":"Gewürztraminer blind: Alsace or the New World, what tips it?","back":"Deep gold, oily weight, heady lychee and rose with some sweetness at high alcohol lean Alsace. A paler, fresher wine at lower alcohol leans New World, from a cool site such as the Anderson Valley."},{"id":"gt-n51ps14s","front":"Gewürztraminer in the glass: which marks give it away?","back":"Lychee, rose petal, an oily full body and low acidity, often with a little sweetness and a faintly bitter finish."}],"questions":[{"id":"gt-1b115qc2","q":"Two glasses of Gewürztraminer are poured blind. The first is deep gold, oily and off dry, heady with lychee and rose at high alcohol. The second is paler, lighter, fresher and nearly dry. Which pair of origins fits, in that order?","opts":["Anderson Valley, then Marlborough","Finger Lakes, then Alsace","Alsace, then Marlborough","Marlborough, then Alsace"],"a":2,"exp":"Deep colour, oily weight, sweetness and high alcohol mark Alsace, where the grape ripens late and fully. Cool New World sites such as Marlborough tend to give a lighter, fresher, drier wine.","tag":"worlds"},{"id":"gt-d0bq1pat","q":"A guest orders a pungent Munster to finish and asks for a white that can stand up to it. Which grape do you suggest?","opts":["Aligoté","Melon de Bourgogne","Pinot Grigio","Gewürztraminer"],"a":3,"exp":"Alsace pours its own perfumed, full bodied Gewürztraminer with Munster: the wine's weight and scent match the cheese. The other three are light, quiet whites that the rind would bury."},{"id":"gt-o8c5s2ae","q":"A guest reads Vendanges Tardives on a bottle of Alsace Gewurztraminer and asks what it means. What do you answer?","opts":["A dry wine aged in new oak","A late harvest wine, rich and usually sweet","A sparkling wine made in the bottle","A wine from a single classed vineyard"],"a":1,"exp":"Vendanges Tardives means late harvest: very ripe grapes and a rich wine, usually sweet. A single classed vineyard is shown as Alsace Grand Cru, and sparkling Alsace is Crémant."}],"sources":[{"title":"Vins d'Alsace: Gewurztraminer d'Alsace","url":"https://www.vinsalsace.com/en/gouts-et-couleurs/cepages/fra/gewurztraminer-dalsace/"},{"title":"Alto Adige Wines: Gewürztraminer from Alto Adige","url":"https://www.altoadigewines.com/en/wine-varieties/gewuerztraminer"},{"title":"New Zealand Wine: New Zealand Gewurztraminer","url":"https://www.nzwine.com/en/winestyles/gewuerztraminer/"},{"title":"Anderson Valley Winegrowers Association: history of the Anderson Valley white wine festival","url":"https://avwines.com/blog/history-of-anderson-valley-white-wine-festival/"}]},
"gruner-veltliner": {"slug":"gruner-veltliner","name":"Grüner Veltliner","colour":"white","kind":"grape","status":"full","mode":"home","aliases":[],"notIn":[],"say":"GROO-ner FELT-lee-ner","lines":{"s10":"Grüner Veltliner is the white Austria is known for: crisp green apple and lime with a twist of white pepper, dry and mouthwatering.","s20":"Grüner Veltliner is the white grape of Austria, grown along the Danube and north of Vienna. It is dry and brisk, with green apple, lime and a peppery, savoury edge that makes it easy to pour with almost any dish.","s45":"Grüner Veltliner is the peppery one. It is Austria's native white grape, grown along the Danube west of Vienna and across the rolling country to the north. It tastes of green apple, lime and white peach, with a snap of white pepper and a green note like celery. It is dry, with bright acidity, and it runs from light and brisk to rich and full. Most of it is made in steel or big old casks, with no new oak. Try it with a schnitzel, asparagus or anything fried. Austrians have a word for that peppery snap: Pfefferl, the little pepper.","sale":"Grüner Veltliner gets on with the green things that trouble other wines, asparagus and artichoke among them. With the first courses, its lime and white pepper meet every green plate on the table."},"glass":{"lead":"Green apple, lime and white peach with a snap of white pepper and a green note of lentil and celery, dry and brisk.","notes":[{"family":"orchard fruit","notes":["green apple","ripe pear"]},{"family":"citrus","notes":["lime","lemon zest","grapefruit"]},{"family":"stone fruit","notes":["white peach","ripe apricot"]},{"family":"spice","notes":["white pepper","ginger"]},{"family":"herbal","notes":["lentil","green bean","celery","radish"]}],"ladder":[{"climate":"cool","where":"The Weinviertel, the Kamptal and the cooler, higher Wachau","notes":["green apple","lime","white pepper","radish"]},{"climate":"moderate","where":"Sun warmed Danube terraces, picked late as Smaragd","notes":["white peach","ripe pear","ripe apricot"]}],"structure":"Dry. Acidity high, alcohol moderate, body light to medium; fuller and stronger in a Smaragd or a reserve."},"worlds":{"home":{"home":"Austria, above all Niederösterreich: the broad Weinviertel north of Vienna, and the Wachau, Kamptal and Kremstal along the Danube. Most is fermented in steel or large old casks and sold young, light and peppery. Riper grapes from the steep Danube terraces give fuller, longer lived wines, the Wachau's Smaragd among them.","abroad":"Its neighbours grow it too: Slovakia, the Czech Republic and Hungary. A little is grown in Alto Adige's Isarco Valley, New Zealand, the Adelaide Hills and the Finger Lakes, and bottled under the grape's own name."}},"service":{"temp":"45 to 50 degrees Fahrenheit, 7 to 10 degrees Celsius; a rich Smaragd a few degrees warmer.","glass":"A white wine glass; a wider bowl suits a rich, mature Smaragd.","decant":"Usually unnecessary; a young, powerful Smaragd can open with a short decant, so taste a sample first.","age":"Most within two or three years; a Smaragd or a reserve rewards five and can keep far longer."},"food":{"with":[{"dish":"Wiener schnitzel","why":"acidity cuts the fried crumb, as lemon would"},{"dish":"Asparagus, green or white","why":"the wine's own green, peppery side welcomes it"},{"dish":"Artichoke with lemon and oil","why":"fresh and unoaked, with a herbal echo of the dish"},{"dish":"Vietnamese salads and fresh spring rolls","why":"lime and white pepper meet mint and coriander"},{"dish":"Shrimp remoulade","why":"pepper and celery in the wine echo the sauce"}]},"labels":[{"term":"Steinfeder, Federspiel, Smaragd","means":"The Wachau's three weights of dry white, from light to rich; Smaragd is the ripest and fullest."},{"term":"Weinviertel DAC","means":"Austria's first protected regional style, from the 2002 vintage: dry, peppery Grüner Veltliner alone."},{"term":"Kamptal, Kremstal","means":"Districts beside the Wachau whose protected dry whites are Grüner Veltliner or Riesling."},{"term":"Reserve","means":"On an Austrian label, a riper, fuller dry wine released later."}],"guest":[{"q":"I usually drink Sauvignon Blanc. Will I like this?","a":"Very likely. It is just as dry and fresh, with lime and green apple, but where Sauvignon Blanc is grassy this is peppery, and a little rounder."},{"q":"Is it really peppery?","a":"A little: a prickle of white pepper on the finish, not heat. Austrians call it the Pfefferl, and it shows most in the light, young wines of the Weinviertel."},{"q":"What does Smaragd mean on the label?","a":"It is the Wachau's word for its ripest, fullest dry wines, named for the emerald green lizard that suns itself on the terrace walls. Federspiel is the lighter style."}],"compare":[{"slug":"riesling","tell":"Dry Riesling is lime, white peach and, with age, petrol, on sharper acidity; Grüner Veltliner is peppery and green, with lentil and celery."},{"slug":"sauvignon-blanc","tell":"Sauvignon Blanc is grassy, with gooseberry and passion fruit; Grüner Veltliner is quieter, with white pepper, lentil and a rounder middle."},{"slug":"albarino","tell":"Albariño is salty, with peach and apricot; Grüner Veltliner has green apple, lime and a white pepper finish, with no note of sea salt."}],"cards":[{"id":"gt-80qdh981","front":"Grüner Veltliner: where is home, and what gives it away blind?","back":"Austria, above all Niederösterreich. White pepper with lentil, green bean and celery over green apple and lime, dry, with high acidity and no new oak."},{"id":"gt-fhrg617k","front":"Grüner Veltliner in the Wachau: name its three styles, from light to rich.","back":"Steinfeder, Federspiel and Smaragd. Smaragd is the ripest and fullest, named for the emerald lizard of the terrace walls, and the one that rewards keeping."},{"id":"gt-xciuq8id","front":"Grüner Veltliner: who are its parents, and how was one found?","back":"Traminer and a vine known as St. Georgen. A single old plant was found in 2000 at St. Georgen in Burgenland, and DNA work showed it to be the missing parent."}],"questions":[{"id":"gt-4doilgq7","q":"A blind white is pale with green tints, dry and unoaked, with green apple, lime, a prickle of white pepper and a note of lentil. The acidity is high. Which grape is it most likely to be?","opts":["Albariño","Grüner Veltliner","Pinot Grigio","Sauvignon Blanc"],"a":1,"exp":"White pepper with lentil and green bean over lime and green apple is the mark of Grüner Veltliner. Sauvignon Blanc would be grassy, with gooseberry or passion fruit, and Albariño salty and peachy."},{"id":"gt-nd5ln8kl","q":"A guest asks for the ripest, fullest dry white the Wachau makes. Which word on the label do you look for?","opts":["Smaragd","Steinfeder","Federspiel","Kabinett"],"a":0,"exp":"Smaragd is the Wachau's ripest, fullest dry style. Federspiel is the middle weight and Steinfeder the light one, and Kabinett is not one of the Wachau's three."},{"id":"gt-765b8siu","q":"A table has ordered artichokes with lemon and then asparagus, and asks for one dry white for both. Which do you pour?","opts":["Oaked Napa Valley Chardonnay","White Hermitage","Grüner Veltliner Federspiel","Condrieu"],"a":2,"exp":"A dry, fresh Grüner Veltliner with no new oak, and with green, peppery notes of its own, is the model choice with artichoke and asparagus. Heavy new oak, or a rich, low acid white such as Condrieu or white Hermitage, would crowd both dishes."}],"sources":[{"title":"Austrian Wine: Grüner Veltliner","url":"https://www.austrianwine.com/our-wine/grape-varieties/white-wine/gruener-veltliner"},{"title":"Austrian Wine: Weinviertel","url":"https://www.austrianwine.com/our-wine/winegrowing-regions/niederoesterreich-lower-austria/weinviertel"},{"title":"Vinea Wachau: Grüner Veltliner Smaragd","url":"https://www.vinea-wachau.at/fileadmin/user_upload/Gruner-Veltliner-Smaragd.pdf"},{"title":"wein.plus Glossary: Grüner Veltliner","url":"https://glossary.wein-plus.eu/gruener-veltliner"}]},
"muscat-blanc": {"slug":"muscat-blanc","name":"Muscat Blanc","colour":"white","kind":"grape","status":"full","mode":"contrast","aliases":["Moscato Bianco"],"notIn":[],"say":"moos-KAH BLAHN, in Italy mohs-KAH-toh BYAHN-koh","lines":{"s10":"Muscat Blanc is the grape that tastes of grapes, perfumed with orange blossom and usually sweet, from fizzy Moscato to rich dessert wine.","s20":"Muscat Blanc is an old grape of the Mediterranean, and the Italians call it Moscato Bianco. It smells of fresh grapes, white peach and orange blossom, and it is most often made sweet: lightly sparkling in Asti, rich and strong in the south of France.","s45":"Muscat Blanc tastes like biting into a ripe table grape. It is an old Mediterranean grape, at home in Piedmont, the south of France and the Greek islands. Beside the grape itself come white peach, apricot and orange blossom. Most of it is sweet, with gentle acidity. Asti stops the fermentation early for a light sparkle, and the French add grape spirit to keep the sugar. Australia ages a red skinned form for years in cask, until it tastes of raisins and toffee. Pour the light ones with a peach tart, and the dark ones with blue cheese. Moscato d'Asti holds less than half the alcohol of most wines.","sale":"Muscat Blanc tastes of the fresh grape itself, with peach and orange blossom, sweet without weight. A cold Moscato d'Asti goes with the fruit desserts, and a small glass of Rutherglen Muscat with the blue cheese."},"glass":{"lead":"Fresh table grape, white peach and orange blossom, light and soft, most often sweet and sometimes gently sparkling.","notes":[{"family":"orchard fruit","notes":["fresh table grape","ripe pear"]},{"family":"stone fruit","notes":["white peach","apricot"]},{"family":"citrus","notes":["sweet orange","lemon"]},{"family":"floral","notes":["orange blossom","rose petal","elderflower"]},{"family":"dried fruit","notes":["raisin","dried fig","candied orange peel"]},{"family":"age","notes":["toffee","walnut","honey"]}],"ladder":[{"climate":"cool","where":"Alsace, where it is fermented dry","notes":["fresh table grape","elderflower","lemon"]},{"climate":"moderate","where":"The hills of Asti in Piedmont","notes":["fresh table grape","white peach","orange blossom"]},{"climate":"warm","where":"The south of France, Samos and Rutherglen","notes":["apricot","honey","raisin"]}],"structure":"Dry in Alsace, sweet nearly everywhere else. Acidity low to medium, body light to medium, fuller when fortified; alcohol low in Moscato d'Asti and high where spirit is added."},"worlds":{"contrast":{"old":{"where":"Asti in Piedmont; the south of France; Alsace; Samos","glass":"Moscato d'Asti tends to be pale, sweet and gently sparkling at very low alcohol: fresh grape, white peach and orange blossom. The French and Greek sweet wines are usually golden, richer and stronger, with apricot and honey.","cellar":"Asti chills a sealed tank to stop the fermentation, keeping sugar and a light fizz. The south of France adds grape spirit; Alsace ferments its Muscat dry."},"new":{"where":"Rutherglen in Victoria; California; South Africa's Constantia","glass":"Rutherglen Muscat is typically amber to deep brown, luscious and strong: raisin, dried fig, toffee and candied orange peel. California Moscato tends to be pale, light and sweet, often with a little sparkle.","cellar":"Rutherglen shrivels its red skinned Muscat on the vine, adds spirit early and ages it for years in old casks; California Moscato is made in tank."},"why":"Asti's hillsides keep the perfume fresh, and a fermentation stopped early keeps the alcohol low. Rutherglen is hot and dry, so the grapes shrivel and concentrate, and years in warm sheds darken the wine.","blur":"Samos also dries and cask ages some of its Muscat until amber, and Australia and California bottle light, fizzy Moscato in the Asti manner.","call":"Pale, fresh grape and orange blossom with low alcohol lean Asti; amber brown, raisin and toffee with the warmth of spirit lean Rutherglen."}},"service":{"temp":"43 to 46 degrees Fahrenheit, 6 to 8 degrees Celsius for Moscato d'Asti and sweet Muscat; Rutherglen Muscat lightly chilled.","glass":"A white wine glass or a tulip for Moscato d'Asti; a small glass for the sweet and fortified wines.","decant":"No. Pour Moscato d'Asti cold and gently to keep its light sparkle.","age":"Enjoy the fresh wines within a year or two, for the perfume; Rutherglen Muscat is aged before release."},"food":{"with":[{"dish":"Peach or apricot tart","why":"light, sweet Moscato d'Asti echoes the ripe fruit"},{"dish":"Asparagus","why":"dry Alsace Muscat's faint bitterness meets the vegetable's own"},{"dish":"Blue cheese","why":"a sweet, strong Muscat sets off the salt"},{"dish":"Bananas Foster","why":"Rutherglen Muscat's raisin and toffee meet the caramel and rum"}]},"labels":[{"term":"Moscato d'Asti","means":"Piedmont's sweet, gently sparkling Muscat, low in alcohol and bottled young."},{"term":"Asti","means":"The fully sparkling wine of the same grape and hills, usually sweet and a little stronger."},{"term":"Vin Doux Naturel","means":"A French sweet wine kept sweet by adding grape spirit during fermentation, as in Muscat de Beaumes-de-Venise."},{"term":"Muscat Canelli","means":"A California name for this grape, after the town of Canelli near Asti."},{"term":"Muscat à Petits Grains","means":"The grape's full French name: the Muscat with small berries."}],"guest":[{"q":"Is Moscato the same as Muscat?","a":"Yes. Moscato is the Italian word for Muscat, and Moscato d'Asti is made from this very grape, Moscato Bianco. Muscat is a large family, though, and not every Moscato elsewhere is this one."},{"q":"Is it always sweet?","a":"Nearly always, but not in Alsace, where Muscat is fermented dry: the same fresh grape scent with a crisp, slightly bitter finish."},{"q":"Why is Moscato d'Asti so low in alcohol?","a":"The fermentation is stopped early by chilling the tank, so much of the grape's sugar stays as sweetness and only a little becomes alcohol: roughly five per cent."}],"compare":[{"slug":"gewurztraminer","tell":"Gewürztraminer is lychee, rose petal and ginger, oilier and fuller; Muscat is fresh grape and orange blossom, lighter and less oily."},{"slug":"torrontes","tell":"Torrontés mimics the grapey nose but is dry, with more acidity and a bitter finish; Muscat is usually sweet and softer."},{"slug":"viognier","tell":"Viognier is apricot and honeysuckle on a fuller, weightier, dry frame; Muscat smells of the grape itself and is lighter."}],"cards":[{"id":"gt-k2zatrsr","front":"Muscat Blanc: give its Italian name and its two wines from Asti.","back":"Moscato Bianco. Moscato d'Asti is sweet and gently sparkling at about five per cent alcohol; Asti is the fully sparkling wine of the same grape."},{"id":"gt-mu8wwfei","front":"Muscat Blanc blind: Asti or Rutherglen, what tips it?","back":"Pale colour, fresh grape, orange blossom and low alcohol lean Asti. Amber to brown, with raisin, dried fig and toffee and the warmth of spirit, leans Rutherglen."},{"id":"gt-ptnfetya","front":"How does a Muscat vin doux naturel keep its sweetness?","back":"Grape spirit is added part way through fermentation. It stops the yeast, leaves the grape's sugar in the wine and lifts the alcohol to 15 per cent or more."}],"questions":[{"id":"gt-6npb0q4l","q":"A sweet wine poured blind is deep amber brown and luscious, with raisin, dried fig and toffee and the warmth of added spirit. Which origin fits it?","opts":["Alsace, France","Asti, Piedmont","Mosel, Germany","Rutherglen, Victoria"],"a":3,"exp":"Years in old casks in a hot, dry region turn Rutherglen Muscat brown and give it raisin and toffee. Moscato d'Asti is pale, fresh and low in alcohol, with no spirit added.","tag":"worlds"},{"id":"gt-1fdnxhr2","q":"A guest wants something light, sweet and gently fizzy with a peach tart, and as little alcohol as possible. Which wine do you bring?","opts":["Sauternes","Moscato d'Asti","Muscat de Beaumes-de-Venise","Rutherglen Muscat"],"a":1,"exp":"Moscato d'Asti is sweet and gently sparkling at about five per cent alcohol. Beaumes-de-Venise and Rutherglen Muscat are fortified with spirit, and Sauternes is still, rich and far stronger."},{"id":"gt-4tqjoqmh","q":"A guest loves the scent of Muscat but does not want a sweet wine. From which region do you choose a bottle?","opts":["Alsace","Asti","Samos","Beaumes-de-Venise"],"a":0,"exp":"Alsace ferments its Muscat dry: the same fresh grape scent with a crisp finish. Asti, Beaumes-de-Venise and Samos are known for sweet Muscat."}],"sources":[{"title":"Consorzio dell'Asti: Moscato d'Asti","url":"https://www.astidocg.it/en/wines/moscato-dasti-docg"},{"title":"Rhône Valley Vineyards: Muscat de Beaumes-de-Venise, vin doux naturel","url":"https://www.vins-rhone.com/en/aoc-vin-doux-naturel-muscat-de-beaumes-de-venise"},{"title":"Vins d'Alsace: Muscat d'Alsace","url":"https://www.vinsalsace.com/en/gouts-et-couleurs/cepages/frais-et-secs/muscat-dalsace/"},{"title":"University of California, Davis: Muscat blanc","url":"https://devo.ucdavis.edu/sites/g/files/dgvnsk1961/files/media/documents/muscat%20blanc.pdf"}]},
"pinot-grigio": {"slug":"pinot-grigio","name":"Pinot Gris / Grigio","colour":"white","kind":"grape","status":"full","mode":"contrast","court":true,"aliases":[],"notIn":[],"say":"PEE-noh GREE-joh, in France PEE-noh GREE, in Germany GROW-boor-goon-der","lines":{"s10":"Pinot Grigio and Pinot Gris are one grape in two styles: light, crisp pear and lemon from Italy, rich, smoky pear from Alsace.","s20":"Pinot Gris and Pinot Grigio are one grape under its French and Italian names. From Italy it is light, crisp and quiet, all pear and lemon, and from Alsace it is rich and smoky, with honeyed pear.","s45":"Pinot Gris is a pale mutation of Pinot Noir, with a skin that is grey pink, not green. Italy calls it Pinot Grigio, picks it early and makes a light, crisp white of pear and lemon. Alsace lets it ripen and makes something else: full, smoky and honeyed, often with a little sweetness. Oregon and New Zealand tend to sit between the two, dry or off dry, with ripe pear and some weight. Pour the Italian style with light fish and salads, and the Alsace style with roast pork or mushrooms. Leave the juice on those skins for a few hours and the wine turns copper: Friuli calls it ramato.","sale":"Think of Pinot Grigio as the clean slate of white wine: cold, crisp and quiet, with pear, lemon and no oak. It suits the salads and the lighter fish, and I would keep a rich Alsace Pinot Gris for the pork."},"glass":{"lead":"Pear, lemon and green apple, light and crisp as Grigio; ripe pear, honey and smoke on a full, oily body as Alsace Gris.","notes":[{"family":"orchard fruit","notes":["pear","green apple","baked apple"]},{"family":"citrus","notes":["lemon","lime zest"]},{"family":"stone fruit","notes":["white peach","nectarine","dried apricot"]},{"family":"nutty","notes":["bitter almond","hazelnut"]},{"family":"honeyed","notes":["honey","beeswax","gingerbread"]},{"family":"earth","notes":["smoke","mushroom"]}],"ladder":[{"climate":"cool","where":"Friuli and Alto Adige, Alsace, the Willamette Valley, New Zealand's South Island","notes":["pear","green apple","lemon","white peach"]},{"climate":"moderate","where":"The Veneto plain, Baden's Kaiserstuhl, Hawke's Bay and Gisborne","notes":["ripe pear","nectarine","honeydew melon"]}],"structure":"Grigio: dry, acidity medium to medium plus, alcohol medium, body light. Alsace Gris: dry to off dry, acidity low to medium, alcohol medium plus, body medium to full."},"worlds":{"contrast":{"old":{"where":"Alsace; north east Italy; Baden and the Pfalz as Grauburgunder","glass":"Two poles. Italian Pinot Grigio tends to be pale, light and crisp: pear, lemon and a bitter almond finish. Alsace Pinot Gris is typically gold, full and oily, with honeyed pear, smoke and often some sweetness.","cellar":"Italy picks early and ferments cold in steel. Alsace picks ripe, sometimes late, into steel or old casks; German Grauburgunder is usually dry."},"new":{"where":"Oregon's Willamette Valley, New Zealand, Australia, California","glass":"Typically fruit forward and in between: ripe pear, apple and white peach at moderate weight. Oregon tends to be dry and unoaked, New Zealand often richer and just off dry.","cellar":"Mostly fermented cool in steel and bottled young. The label is the cue: Gris for a riper, fuller wine, sometimes with old oak, Grigio for a lighter one."},"why":"Picking date decides most: early for crisp Grigio, late for rich Gris. Alsace's dry, sunny autumns let the grape hang, Italy's plains crop high for volume, and New World makers choose a style and say so on the label.","blur":"Alto Adige and Friuli's hills make Pinot Grigio with real weight, some Alsace houses ferment fully dry, and a New World Gris can be as rich as Alsace.","call":"Smoke, honey and oily weight lean Alsace; a neutral nose and bitter almond lean Italy; bright, clean pear at moderate weight leans New World."}},"service":{"temp":"Pinot Grigio at 45 to 50 degrees Fahrenheit, 7 to 10 degrees Celsius; a rich Alsace Pinot Gris slightly warmer.","glass":"A white wine glass; a wider bowl for a rich Alsace Gris.","decant":"Unnecessary for either style; open it and pour.","age":"Pinot Grigio young, within two years; Alsace Pinot Gris five years or more, late harvest wines far longer."},"food":{"with":[{"dish":"Antipasti, trout or a seafood risotto","why":"a crisp Grigio suits light, delicate plates"},{"dish":"Shrimp remoulade","why":"a cold, crisp Grigio refreshes after the mustard"},{"dish":"Roast pork with apples","why":"Alsace Gris has the weight and the honeyed pear"},{"dish":"Mushroom tart or smoked fish","why":"the smoky Alsace style meets smoke and mushroom"}]},"labels":[{"term":"Pinot Grigio, Pinot Gris","means":"Italian and French for one grape; on a label Grigio signals light and crisp, Gris riper and fuller."},{"term":"Grauburgunder, Ruländer","means":"The German names: Grauburgunder signals dry, Ruländer the older, richer and often sweeter style."},{"term":"Delle Venezie","means":"The Pinot Grigio DOC of 2017 covering the Veneto, Friuli Venezia Giulia and Trentino."},{"term":"Ramato","means":"Copper tinted Pinot Grigio, from a few hours on its grey pink skins."}],"guest":[{"q":"Are Pinot Gris and Pinot Grigio the same grape?","a":"Yes, in French and in Italian. The names now signal style: Grigio is usually light, crisp and dry, Gris riper, fuller and sometimes a little sweet."},{"q":"Why is this Alsace Pinot Gris sweet when my usual Pinot Grigio is dry?","a":"Alsace picks it much riper, and some houses leave a little sugar. Since the 2021 vintage the label must state the sweetness, so look for the word sec."},{"q":"Why does this Pinot Grigio look pink?","a":"The grape's skin is grey pink. A few hours on those skins tint the wine copper: a dry style called ramato, and not a rosé."}],"compare":[{"slug":"pinot-blanc","tell":"Pinot Blanc is lighter, plainer and fresher, with apple and no smoke; Alsace Pinot Gris is richer and oilier, with honeyed pear."},{"slug":"gewurztraminer","tell":"Gewürztraminer is far louder, all lychee and rose petal, with even less acidity; Alsace Pinot Gris is smoky and honeyed, not perfumed."},{"slug":"chardonnay","tell":"Unoaked Chardonnay is firmer and plainer, with no smoke or ginger; Italian Pinot Grigio is lighter still and ends on a faint bitter almond."}],"cards":[{"id":"gt-eanj6sja","front":"Pinot Gris blind: Alsace, Italy or the New World, what tips it?","back":"Smoke, honey and oily weight, often with sweetness, lean Alsace. A neutral nose, crisp acidity and bitter almond lean Italy. Bright, clean pear at moderate weight leans Oregon or New Zealand."},{"id":"gt-g5bgc3ii","front":"Grauburgunder and Ruländer: which grape, and what does each name signal?","back":"Both are Pinot Gris in Germany. Grauburgunder signals a dry wine; Ruländer is the older name, for a richer and often sweeter style."},{"id":"gt-b359kit4","front":"Until 2007, what was Alsace Pinot Gris called, and why did the name go?","back":"Tokay d'Alsace, later Tokay Pinot Gris. The name was retired by 2007 to protect Hungary's Tokaj, which is made from other grapes."}],"questions":[{"id":"gt-rs2ffz0t","q":"Three glasses of the same grape are poured blind: one gold, oily, smoky and slightly sweet; one pale, neutral and crisp with a bitter almond finish; one bright with ripe pear, dry, at moderate weight. Which order of origins fits?","opts":["Oregon, north east Italy, Alsace","North east Italy, Alsace, Oregon","Alsace, Oregon, north east Italy","Alsace, north east Italy, Oregon"],"a":3,"exp":"Smoke, oil and some sweetness tend to mark Alsace Pinot Gris, a neutral, crisp glass with bitter almond Italian Pinot Grigio, and clean ripe pear at moderate weight Oregon, which sits between the two.","tag":"worlds"},{"id":"gt-ysd6p9lh","q":"A guest asks for a dry German Pinot Gris. Which word on the label signals the dry style?","opts":["Spätburgunder","Ruländer","Grauburgunder","Weissburgunder"],"a":2,"exp":"Grauburgunder is the name German growers use for dry Pinot Gris. Ruländer is the older name, for a richer and often sweeter style, and Weissburgunder is Pinot Blanc."},{"id":"gt-lrqsb4a4","q":"A guest holds up a copper pink Pinot Grigio from Friuli and asks whether it is a rosé. What is it?","opts":["Ramato: tinted by brief contact with its own skins","Passito: made from grapes dried before pressing","Rosato: blended with a little red wine","Frizzante: coloured by a second fermentation"],"a":0,"exp":"Ramato is Pinot Grigio left a few hours on its grey pink skins, which tint it copper. It is not blended with red wine, and it is dry."}],"sources":[{"title":"Vins d'Alsace: Pinot Gris","url":"https://www.vinsalsace.com/en/gouts-et-couleurs/cepages/pinot-gris-dalsace/pinot-gris-dalsace/"},{"title":"Wines of Germany: Grauburgunder, Pinot Gris","url":"https://www.winesofgermany.com/our-wine/grape-varieties/grape-variety/100/grauburgunder-pinot-gris"},{"title":"New Zealand Winegrowers: New Zealand Pinot Gris","url":"https://www.nzwine.com/en/winestyles/pinot-gris/"},{"title":"Wine Australia: Pinot Grigio or Pinot Gris, what is the difference","url":"https://www.australianwine.com/experience/articles/pinot-grigio-or-pinot-gris-what-s-the-difference"}]},
"riesling": {"slug":"riesling","name":"Riesling","colour":"white","kind":"grape","status":"full","mode":"contrast","court":true,"aliases":[],"notIn":[],"say":"REES-ling","lines":{"s10":"Riesling is the white of the Mosel and the Rhine: lime, green apple and white peach on piercing acidity, dry or sweet.","s20":"Riesling comes from Germany's river valleys, and it can be bone dry, gently sweet or richly sweet. In every style it tastes of lime, green apple and white peach, with no taste of oak and an acidity that keeps it fresh for decades.","s45":"Riesling is the white that hides nothing: no oak and no cream, only the fruit and the place. Its home is Germany, on the steep slate banks of the Mosel and along the Rhine. It tastes of lime, green apple and white peach, and with years in bottle it gains a note like petrol. It is light, with piercing acidity, and it runs from bone dry to honey sweet. Germany often leaves a touch of sweetness at low alcohol, while Australia ferments it fully dry. It meets crab, smoked trout or anything with chilli. The first record of it is a bill for vines dated 1435.","sale":"Riesling brings lime, green apple and an acidity that makes the next bite taste brighter. An off dry one suits anything that carries heat, and a dry one the crab."},"glass":{"lead":"Lime, green apple and white peach over wet slate, light and unoaked, with piercing acidity and a petrol note with age.","notes":[{"family":"citrus","notes":["lime juice","lemon zest","lime cordial"]},{"family":"orchard fruit","notes":["green apple","yellow apple"]},{"family":"stone fruit","notes":["white peach","apricot","nectarine"]},{"family":"floral","notes":["jasmine","orange blossom"]},{"family":"mineral","notes":["wet slate","wet stone"]},{"family":"age","notes":["petrol","beeswax","honey","toast"]}],"ladder":[{"climate":"cool","where":"The Mosel, the Rheingau and the Nahe; the Finger Lakes","notes":["lime juice","green apple","white peach","wet slate"]},{"climate":"moderate","where":"The Pfalz, the Wachau and Washington's Columbia Valley","notes":["ripe peach","apricot","nectarine"]},{"climate":"warm","where":"The Clare Valley, where hot days meet cold nights","notes":["lime cordial","lemon zest","early petrol"]}],"structure":"Dry to sweet. Acidity high, alcohol low where sugar is left and moderate when dry, body light to medium; no new oak."},"worlds":{"contrast":{"old":{"where":"Germany's Mosel, Rheingau and Pfalz; Alsace; Austria's Wachau","glass":"The Mosel tends to be feather light and often off dry: lime, green apple and white peach over wet slate, at low alcohol. Alsace, the Pfalz and the Wachau are usually dry and fuller.","cellar":"Fermented in steel or in large, old oak casks that add no flavour; in Germany the fermentation is often stopped early to keep a little natural sweetness."},"new":{"where":"South Australia's Clare and Eden Valleys, the Finger Lakes, Washington, New Zealand","glass":"Clare and Eden Valley are typically bone dry and vivid: lime juice, lemon zest and a steely edge at moderate alcohol, with toast and petrol often showing early. The Finger Lakes and Washington range from dry to sweet.","cellar":"Usually fermented cold in steel, kept away from oak and air, and bottled early under screwcap; Australia ferments it fully dry."},"why":"Germany's river valleys sit near the northern limit for ripening, so acidity stays high and a little sweetness is kept to balance it; its law grades grapes by ripeness. Australia's sunnier sites ripen fully, and the wine is fermented dry.","blur":"Germany now bottles much of its Riesling dry, labelled trocken, and the Finger Lakes and New Zealand make light, off dry wines in the Mosel manner.","call":"Sweetness at low alcohol, or peach on a fuller dry frame, leans Old World; taut, bone dry lime with early toast or petrol leans Australia."}},"service":{"temp":"45 to 50 degrees Fahrenheit, 7 to 10 degrees Celsius; the sweet wines a little colder.","glass":"A white wine glass with a narrow bowl; a small glass for the sweet wines.","decant":"Usually unnecessary; pour gently and leave any harmless crystals in the bottle.","age":"It keeps longer than almost any other white: sound dry wines ten years or more, the sweet wines for decades."},"food":{"with":[{"dish":"Thai or Vietnamese dishes with chilli","why":"a touch of sweetness and low alcohol soften the heat"},{"dish":"Crab, dressed or in cakes","why":"bright acidity lifts the sweet meat"},{"dish":"Smoked trout or smoked salmon","why":"acidity cuts the oil and the smoke"},{"dish":"Crawfish étouffée","why":"off dry fruit cools the cayenne, acidity cuts the butter"}]},"labels":[{"term":"Trocken","means":"Dry, on a German label; halbtrocken and feinherb are off dry."},{"term":"Kabinett, Spätlese, Auslese","means":"German grades of ripeness at harvest, in rising order; a Kabinett or a Spätlese may be dry or sweet."},{"term":"Grosses Gewächs","means":"A dry wine from a classed vineyard of the VDP, a German growers' association; often written GG."}],"guest":[{"q":"Is Riesling always sweet?","a":"No. It runs from bone dry to very sweet. On a German label trocken means dry, and wines from Alsace, Austria and Australia are mostly dry."},{"q":"Why does this one smell of petrol?","a":"That is a mark of the grape, not a fault. It builds with years in bottle and shows sooner in wines from hot, sunny sites. Many Riesling drinkers look for it."},{"q":"How can I tell from the label whether it is dry?","a":"Look for trocken on a German bottle, and check the alcohol: near eight or nine per cent means sugar was left, and twelve or more is usually dry."}],"compare":[{"slug":"chenin-blanc","tell":"Chenin Blanc shows quince, honey and a wet wool note on a broader, waxier frame; Riesling is lime and petrol, lighter and more piercing."},{"slug":"gruner-veltliner","tell":"Grüner Veltliner brings white pepper, lentil and celery; dry Riesling is lime, white peach and, with age, petrol, with no peppery note."},{"slug":"albarino","tell":"Albariño is salty, with peach, apricot and a faintly bitter finish; Riesling has higher acidity, lime and wet slate, and may carry sweetness."}],"cards":[{"id":"gt-2y6uwuw3","front":"Riesling: where is it at home, and what is its first record?","back":"Germany, above all the Mosel and the Rhine. A cellar account of 13 March 1435 records Riesling vines bought at Rüsselsheim on the Main, near the Rheingau."},{"id":"gt-6abmjxa8","front":"Riesling blind: the Mosel or the Clare Valley, what tips it?","back":"Sweetness, low alcohol and wet slate lean Mosel. Bone dry lime juice at moderate alcohol, with toast or petrol showing early, leans Clare or Eden Valley."},{"id":"gt-zhlqlocp","front":"On a German Riesling label, what do trocken and Kabinett tell you?","back":"Trocken means dry. Kabinett is the first grade of ripeness at harvest, and it may be dry or carry sweetness; low alcohol hints at sugar left in the wine."}],"questions":[{"id":"gt-6wfn95xd","q":"A blind white is young, pale, bone dry and vivid: lime juice, lemon zest and a touch of toast, with high acidity, moderate alcohol and no oak. Which origin fits it?","opts":["Vouvray, Loire","Clare Valley, Australia","Mosel, Germany","Rías Baixas, Spain"],"a":1,"exp":"Bone dry lime juice with toast showing young, at moderate alcohol, is the Clare Valley's way with Riesling. A typical Mosel wine is lighter, lower in alcohol and often off dry.","tag":"worlds"},{"id":"gt-njvp9kxz","q":"A guest loves German Riesling but wants no sweetness at all. Which word on the label tells you the bottle is dry?","opts":["Feinherb","Auslese","Trocken","Spätlese"],"a":2,"exp":"Trocken means dry. Feinherb is off dry, and Spätlese and Auslese are grades of ripeness that usually carry sweetness unless trocken is printed beside them."},{"id":"gt-vir7bfaq","q":"A guest sniffs a ten year old Riesling and asks, worried, why it smells of petrol. What do you tell her?","opts":["It comes from ageing in new oak","The wine is corked and must be replaced","The bottle was stored beside fuel","It is a normal mark of aged Riesling"],"a":3,"exp":"A petrol note builds in Riesling with bottle age, and sooner from hot, sunny sites; it is no fault. Cork taint smells of damp cardboard, and Riesling sees no new oak."}],"sources":[{"title":"Wines of Germany: Riesling","url":"https://www.winesofgermany.com/our-wine/grape-varieties/grape-variety/105/riesling"},{"title":"Wines of Germany: Riesling, Germany's white star","url":"https://www.winesofgermany.com/fileadmin/DWI/News_Medien/Publikationen/Wein_Intern/Riesling-engl.pdf"},{"title":"Wine Australia: Riesling","url":"https://www.wineaustralia.com/market-insights/regions-and-varieties/riesling"},{"title":"wein.plus Magazine: first mention of the Riesling is celebrated worldwide","url":"https://magazine.wein.plus/news/first-mention-of-the-riesling-is-celebrated-worldwide-german-wine-institute-organizes-international-birthday-events"}]},
"sauvignon-blanc": {"slug":"sauvignon-blanc","name":"Sauvignon Blanc","colour":"white","kind":"grape","status":"full","mode":"contrast","court":true,"aliases":["Fumé Blanc"],"notIn":[],"say":"so-vee-NYOHN BLAHN","lines":{"s10":"Sauvignon Blanc is the bright, bone dry white of Sancerre and Marlborough: grapefruit, gooseberry and cut grass with mouthwatering acidity.","s20":"Sauvignon Blanc is the grape of Sancerre in the Loire and of Marlborough in New Zealand. It is dry and vivid, with grapefruit, gooseberry and fresh cut grass, and it is usually made without oak to keep that snap.","s45":"Sauvignon Blanc is at home in the upper Loire, in Sancerre and Pouilly-Fumé, and it found a second home in Marlborough, New Zealand. It smells of grapefruit, gooseberry and fresh cut grass, and it is dry, light and sharply refreshing. Most of it is fermented cold in steel, with no oak, to keep that freshness. The Loire tends to be quieter and flinty, while Marlborough is louder, with passion fruit on top. It is a natural with goat's cheese, oysters or anything with green herbs. Its name comes from sauvage, the French word for wild.","sale":"Sauvignon Blanc is the wake up call on the list: cold, dry and bright with grapefruit and cut grass. Start with it beside the goat's cheese or the shellfish, before anything richer comes to the table."},"glass":{"lead":"Grapefruit, gooseberry and passion fruit over fresh cut grass, on a light, bone dry frame with piercing acidity.","notes":[{"family":"citrus","notes":["pink grapefruit","lime zest","lemon"]},{"family":"orchard fruit","notes":["gooseberry","green apple"]},{"family":"tropical fruit","notes":["passion fruit","guava","green melon"]},{"family":"herbal","notes":["fresh cut grass","green bell pepper","blackcurrant leaf","boxwood"]},{"family":"mineral","notes":["struck flint","gun smoke","crushed chalk"]},{"family":"oak","notes":["vanilla pod","toast"]}],"ladder":[{"climate":"cool","where":"Sancerre and Pouilly-Fumé, Marlborough, Styria, Chile's Casablanca Valley","notes":["pink grapefruit","gooseberry","fresh cut grass","green bell pepper"]},{"climate":"moderate","where":"Bordeaux and Margaret River, often blended with Sémillon","notes":["lemon","white peach","green melon"]},{"climate":"warm","where":"Napa Valley, inland Sonoma and California's warmer valleys","notes":["ripe melon","nectarine","fresh fig"]}],"structure":"Bone dry as a rule. Acidity high, alcohol moderate, body light to medium; a little rounder where it is blended with Sémillon or raised in barrel."},"worlds":{"contrast":{"old":{"where":"The upper Loire, led by Sancerre and Pouilly-Fumé; Bordeaux; Styria","glass":"Tends to be restrained and taut: grapefruit, lime and green apple with cut grass and a smoky, struck flint edge, bone dry, with high acidity and moderate alcohol. In Bordeaux it is usually blended with Sémillon and rounder.","cellar":"Usually fermented cool in steel and bottled young, with no new oak; some rest on their lees, and Pessac-Léognan often ferments in barrel."},"new":{"where":"Marlborough, Chile's Casablanca Valley, California, South Africa and the Adelaide Hills","glass":"Typically louder and more pungent: passion fruit, guava and pink grapefruit over cut grass and green bell pepper, with high acidity. California is often softer and rounder, with melon and white peach.","cellar":"Mostly fermented cold in steel and sold within the year, under the grape's own name; California's Fumé Blanc has often seen some oak."},"why":"Marlborough's bright sun, long days and cool nights build intense aroma and hold the acidity; the upper Loire, far inland on limestone and flint, gives quieter fruit. The Loire label names the village, the New World the grape.","blur":"Marlborough's cooler, windier Awatere Valley makes leaner, greener wines, and a warm year gives Sancerre riper, rounder fruit; barrels appear on both sides.","call":"Restraint, green apple and struck flint lean Loire; pungent passion fruit and guava over loud cut grass lean Marlborough."}},"service":{"temp":"45 to 50 degrees Fahrenheit, 7 to 10 degrees Celsius; a barrel fermented white Bordeaux a little warmer.","glass":"A white wine glass: the smaller bowl keeps it cold and focuses the scent.","decant":"Usually unnecessary: pour it cold and fresh from the bottle.","age":"Enjoy it young, within two or three years; barrel fermented white Bordeaux and a few Sancerres keep longer."},"food":{"with":[{"dish":"Goat's cheese, fresh or lightly aged","why":"the Loire's own match: acidity meets the tang"},{"dish":"Oysters and raw shellfish","why":"the acidity works like a squeeze of lemon"},{"dish":"Shrimp remoulade","why":"acidity and green notes stand up to the sharp sauce"},{"dish":"Asparagus and green salads","why":"the wine's own grassy side welcomes them"}]},"labels":[{"term":"Sancerre, Pouilly-Fumé","means":"Upper Loire place names whose white wines are Sauvignon Blanc; the label names the place, not the grape."},{"term":"Fumé Blanc","means":"A California name from the 1960s for dry Sauvignon Blanc, often aged in oak; a nod to Pouilly-Fumé."},{"term":"Bordeaux Blanc, Pessac-Léognan","means":"White Bordeaux: Sauvignon Blanc usually blended with Sémillon, and in Pessac-Léognan often fermented in barrel."},{"term":"Marlborough","means":"The New Zealand region that made the pungent, passion fruit style known."}],"guest":[{"q":"Is Sancerre a Sauvignon Blanc?","a":"Yes. White Sancerre is Sauvignon Blanc from the upper Loire, and so is Pouilly-Fumé across the river. They tend to be quieter and flintier than a New Zealand one."},{"q":"What is the difference between Fumé Blanc and Sauvignon Blanc?","a":"It is the same grape. Fumé Blanc is a name California gave its dry Sauvignon Blanc in the 1960s, and a bottle that wears it has often seen some oak."},{"q":"I find New Zealand Sauvignon Blanc too grassy. What else is there?","a":"Try a Sancerre for something quieter and flinty, or a white Bordeaux, where Sémillon rounds it out."}],"compare":[{"slug":"gruner-veltliner","tell":"Grüner Veltliner shows white pepper and lentil where Sauvignon Blanc shows cut grass and gooseberry, and it feels a little broader in the mouth."},{"slug":"verdejo","tell":"Verdejo is grassy too, but it adds fennel and a faintly bitter finish; Sauvignon Blanc is sharper, with more grapefruit and gooseberry."},{"slug":"albarino","tell":"Albariño trades the grass for white peach, apricot and a salty finish; Sauvignon Blanc is greener and more pungent."}],"cards":[{"id":"gt-yu6fnd8o","front":"Sauvignon Blanc: its home in the Loire, and the red grape it parented?","back":"Sancerre and Pouilly-Fumé, in the upper Loire. With Cabernet Franc it is a parent of Cabernet Sauvignon, shown by DNA work published in 1997."},{"id":"gt-wkhpoi9m","front":"Sauvignon Blanc blind: Loire or Marlborough, what tips it?","back":"Restraint, green apple and a smoky, struck flint note lean Loire. Pungent passion fruit and guava over loud cut grass lean Marlborough."},{"id":"gt-9qb3lti8","front":"What does Fumé Blanc mean on a California label?","back":"Sauvignon Blanc. The name was coined in California in the 1960s for a dry style, a nod to Pouilly-Fumé, and the wine has often seen some oak."}],"questions":[{"id":"gt-aedgzoll","q":"A blind white is pale and bone dry, and pungent from the first sniff: passion fruit, guava and pink grapefruit over loud cut grass, with high acidity and no oak. Which origin fits it?","opts":["Napa Valley, California","Marlborough, New Zealand","Pessac-Léognan, Bordeaux","Sancerre, Loire"],"a":1,"exp":"Pungent passion fruit and guava over cut grass at high acidity is the Marlborough signature. Sancerre tends to be quieter and flinty, and Pessac-Léognan and Napa are often rounder, with vanilla from barrels or ripe melon.","tag":"worlds"},{"id":"gt-5txqwqnu","q":"A guest orders the warm goat's cheese and asks for the white that grows beside it in the upper Loire. Which bottle do you bring?","opts":["Sancerre","Vouvray","Muscadet","Chablis"],"a":0,"exp":"Sancerre is Sauvignon Blanc from the upper Loire, where goat's cheese is the local match. Vouvray is Chenin Blanc from the middle Loire, Muscadet comes from the Atlantic end, and Chablis is Burgundy."},{"id":"gt-o4lzr5bq","q":"A dry white Bordeaux is poured: grapefruit and cut grass, but rounder and waxier than a Sancerre. Which grape is usually blended with the Sauvignon Blanc?","opts":["Viognier","Chenin Blanc","Sémillon","Melon de Bourgogne"],"a":2,"exp":"White Bordeaux usually blends Sauvignon Blanc with Sémillon, which adds body and a waxy texture. Chenin Blanc and Melon de Bourgogne belong to the Loire, not to Bordeaux."}],"sources":[{"title":"New Zealand Wine: New Zealand Sauvignon Blanc","url":"https://www.nzwine.com/en/winestyles/sauvignonblanc/"},{"title":"University of California, Davis, Foundation Plant Services: Sauvignon blanc and the Sauternes","url":"https://fps.ucdavis.edu/grapebook/winebook.cfm?chap=SauvignonBlanc"},{"title":"University of California, Davis: DNA fingerprinting reveals parentage of classic wine grape","url":"https://www.ucdavis.edu/news/dna-fingerprinting-reveals-parentage-classic-wine-grape"},{"title":"Bordeaux Wines: Sauvignon Blanc","url":"https://www.bordeaux.com/en/grape-varieties/sauvignon-blanc/"}]},
"torrontes": {"slug":"torrontes","name":"Torrontés","colour":"white","kind":"grape","status":"full","mode":"home","court":true,"aliases":[],"notIn":["treixadura"],"say":"tor-ron-TES","lines":{"s10":"Torrontés is Argentina's own white, scented like a rose garden with peach and orange blossom, yet dry and fresh when you taste it.","s20":"Torrontés grows high in the Andes of north west Argentina, above all around the town of Cafayate in Salta. Its scent of rose petal, jasmine and ripe peach promises sweetness, and then it drinks dry and fresh, with a faint bitter twist at the end.","s45":"Torrontés plays one trick: it smells sweet and drinks dry. It is Argentina's own white grape, at home around Cafayate in the far north west, where vines stand over a mile high in desert sun. The scent is rose petal, jasmine and ripe peach. The taste is dry, light and fresh, with a faint bitter twist. Oak would smother that perfume, so the wine is made in cool steel tanks and bottled young. Serve it with empanadas, ceviche or anything with a little chilli heat. It arose by chance in Argentina, a cross of Muscat of Alexandria and the mission grape the Spanish brought.","sale":"Torrontés smells like a sweet wine, all rose petal and ripe peach, and then finishes clean and dry. I would pour it tonight beside the crawfish étouffée, where the scent meets the cayenne and the freshness cuts the butter."},"glass":{"lead":"Rose petal, jasmine and orange blossom over white peach and lemon, dry and light, with a faint bitter note on the finish.","notes":[{"family":"floral","notes":["rose petal","jasmine","orange blossom","geranium"]},{"family":"stone fruit","notes":["white peach","apricot"]},{"family":"citrus","notes":["lemon zest","lime","orange peel"]},{"family":"tropical fruit","notes":["lychee","muscat grape"]}],"ladder":[{"climate":"moderate","where":"The upper Calchaquí valleys, higher and colder than Cafayate","notes":["lime","lemon zest","jasmine"]},{"climate":"warm","where":"Cafayate in Salta, La Rioja's Famatina Valley, San Juan and Mendoza","notes":["ripe peach","apricot","rose petal","orange peel"]}],"structure":"Dry, though the scent suggests sweetness. Acidity medium to medium plus, alcohol medium to medium plus, body light to medium, with a faint bitterness on the finish."},"worlds":{"home":{"home":"Argentina, where it was born. Cafayate in Salta, about 1,700 metres up in the Calchaquí valleys, is the address a guest meets, with La Rioja, San Juan and Mendoza behind it. Vines are often trained on a high pergola that shades the fruit, and the wine is fermented cool in steel, dry, and bottled young.","abroad":"It hardly travels: Chile and Uruguay grow a little, and much of Chile's goes to the still for pisco. The Torrontés of Galicia in Spain is a different, unrelated grape that shares the name."}},"service":{"temp":"45 to 50 degrees Fahrenheit, 7 to 10 degrees Celsius: well chilled, so the perfume stays fresh.","glass":"A white wine glass, with room above the wine for the perfume to gather.","decant":"No. A light aromatic white wants only a cold bottle and a clean glass.","age":"The freshest bottle you can find, within a year or two of the harvest; the perfume fades."},"food":{"with":[{"dish":"Empanadas, baked or fried","why":"the match of its own home in Salta"},{"dish":"Ceviche","why":"lime and chilli meet its lemon and freshness"},{"dish":"Crawfish étouffée","why":"perfume and freshness against cayenne and butter"},{"dish":"Thai green curry","why":"a rose scented, dry white sits easily beside chilli heat"}]},"labels":[{"term":"Cafayate","means":"The town in Salta's Calchaquí valleys, about 1,700 metres up: the address a list most often names for Torrontés."},{"term":"Torrontés Riojano","means":"The full name of the most perfumed of three Torrontés varieties, after La Rioja province; labels usually say only Torrontés."},{"term":"Cosecha tardía","means":"Late harvest: a sweet Torrontés, where nearly every other still bottle is dry."},{"term":"Valles Calchaquíes","means":"The high valley system shared by Salta, Tucumán and Catamarca; Cafayate lies at the southern end of Salta's stretch."}],"guest":[{"q":"It smells so sweet. Is this a sweet wine?","a":"No. The perfume comes from its Muscat parent, but the wine is fermented dry. A bottle marked cosecha tardía, late harvest, is the sweet one."},{"q":"Is this the same Torrontés I drank in Spain?","a":"No. Galicia grows a grape of the same name that is no relation. Argentina's Torrontés arose in Argentina, a cross of Muscat of Alexandria and the mission grape, Criolla Chica."},{"q":"Why does the label print the altitude?","a":"Because the height is the point. Around Cafayate the vines stand about 1,700 metres up, where strong sun ripens the fruit and cold nights keep the wine fresh."},{"q":"Should I keep a bottle for a few years?","a":"I would not. Its charm is the fresh perfume, and that fades in the bottle. Drink it cold, within a year or two of the vintage."}],"compare":[{"slug":"muscat-blanc","tell":"Muscat Blanc is grapier and softer in acidity, often sweet or lightly sparkling; Torrontés carries the same rose and orange blossom on a dry, fresher frame with a bitter finish."},{"slug":"gewurztraminer","tell":"Gewürztraminer is oilier, fuller and lower in acidity, with lychee and ginger; Torrontés shares the rose petal but is lighter and fresher, with a bitter finish."},{"slug":"viognier","tell":"Viognier is fuller and richer, with apricot and honeysuckle and soft acidity; Torrontés is lighter, with rose petal where Viognier has apricot."}],"cards":[{"id":"gt-ftf7hnof","front":"Torrontés: which country is home, and which town does a list name?","back":"Argentina. Cafayate, in Salta's Calchaquí valleys, about 1,700 metres up, is the address a list most often names; La Rioja, San Juan and Mendoza grow it too."},{"id":"gt-cap7qix8","front":"Which two grapes are the parents of Torrontés Riojano?","back":"Muscat of Alexandria and Criolla Chica, the mission grape also called Listán Prieto: a natural cross that arose in Argentina. The Muscat parent explains the perfume."},{"id":"gt-431y7yxm","front":"Torrontés blind: what parts it from Muscat and from Gewürztraminer?","back":"It smells as sweetly of rose petal and jasmine as either, then drinks dry, with fresher acidity, a lighter body and a faint bitter finish. Gewürztraminer is oilier, with lychee; Muscat is grapier and softer."}],"questions":[{"id":"gt-eo9zxfds","q":"A blind white is pale lemon with green tints. The nose is all rose petal, jasmine and ripe peach and seems to promise sugar, yet the palate is dry, light and fresh, with a faint bitter finish. Which grape and origin fit it?","opts":["Viognier, Condrieu","Torrontés, Salta","Gewürztraminer, Alsace","Muscat Blanc, Asti"],"a":1,"exp":"Rose petal and jasmine that promise sugar, on a dry, light, fresh palate with a bitter twist, mark Torrontés. Gewürztraminer from Alsace would be oilier and fuller, with lychee and lower acidity."},{"id":"gt-sjbys8wl","q":"A guest sniffs her glass of Torrontés, says it smells like dessert, and asks why a dry wine should smell so sweet. What is the reason?","opts":["Unfermented sugar is left in the wine","It is aged in new oak barrels","It is fortified with grape spirit","One of its parents is Muscat of Alexandria"],"a":3,"exp":"Torrontés Riojano is a natural cross of Muscat of Alexandria and Criolla Chica, and the Muscat parent gives the perfume. The wine is fermented dry, so no sugar is left to explain it."},{"id":"gt-yos01g59","q":"A guest enjoys a Torrontés from Cafayate and asks whether to lay a case down for five years. What do you advise?","opts":["Drink it young, within a year or two","Wait three years for the oak to settle","Keep it five years to gain honey","Keep it ten years, as you would Riesling"],"a":0,"exp":"The charm of Torrontés is its fresh perfume, which fades in bottle, so it is drunk within a year or two. It is made in steel without oak, and it does not gain with age as Riesling can."}],"sources":[{"title":"Wines of Argentina: the varieties, Torrontés","url":"https://winesofargentina.org/es/argentina/variedades/malbec-torrontes/torrontes"},{"title":"Instituto Nacional de Vitivinicultura: the annual report on the variety Torrontés Riojano","url":"https://argentina.gob.ar/sites/default/files/2018/10/01-torrontes_2023.pdf"},{"title":"Wine Grapes, entries on Torrontés Riojano, Torrontés Sanjuanino and Torrontés Mendocino","url":""},{"title":"The Oxford Companion to Wine, entries on Torrontés and Salta","url":""}]},
"viognier": {"slug":"viognier","name":"Viognier","colour":"white","kind":"grape","status":"full","mode":"contrast","aliases":[],"notIn":[],"say":"vee-oh-NYAY","lines":{"s10":"Viognier is the perfumed white of Condrieu in the northern Rhône: apricot, ripe peach and honeysuckle on a full, silky body.","s20":"Viognier comes from Condrieu, a short run of granite terraces above the Rhône. It smells of apricot, peach and honeysuckle, and it is dry and full, with a soft, almost oily feel and very little sharpness.","s45":"Viognier belongs to Condrieu, a short run of steep granite terraces above the Rhône, south of Lyon. It smells of apricot, ripe peach and honeysuckle. It is dry, full and silky, with gentle acidity and a warm finish. Most cellars use steel or older barrels, so the perfume stays in front. Condrieu tends to be the more restrained, while California is often riper and broader. Next door in Côte-Rôtie a little is fermented with Syrah to lift the red's scent. Pour it with lobster, roast chicken or a mild curry. By the 1960s it survived on only a few of those terraces.","sale":"Viognier fills the glass with apricot and honeysuckle before you even sip, and the wine is dry, silky and full. Pair it with the lobster, or with a mild curry."},"glass":{"lead":"Apricot, ripe peach and honeysuckle on a full, oily frame, with low acidity and a warm finish.","notes":[{"family":"stone fruit","notes":["apricot","ripe peach","nectarine"]},{"family":"citrus","notes":["tangerine","orange peel"]},{"family":"tropical fruit","notes":["mango","pineapple"]},{"family":"floral","notes":["honeysuckle","violet","jasmine","musk"]}],"ladder":[{"climate":"moderate","where":"Condrieu and Château-Grillet; the Eden Valley","notes":["apricot","white peach","violet","honeysuckle"]},{"climate":"warm","where":"The Languedoc, California's Central Coast, the Swartland and Paarl","notes":["ripe apricot","mango","pineapple"]}],"structure":"Dry. Acidity low to medium, alcohol high, body full, with an oily, glycerol texture."},"worlds":{"contrast":{"old":{"where":"Condrieu and Château-Grillet in the northern Rhône; the Languedoc","glass":"Condrieu tends to be perfumed but poised: apricot, white peach and violet with a stony, faintly bitter finish, full bodied and low in acidity. Languedoc wines are usually lighter and simpler.","cellar":"Usually dry and bottled alone; fermented in steel or in barrel, a modest share of it new, and often rested on its lees for texture."},"new":{"where":"California's Central Coast, Virginia, the Eden Valley, South Africa","glass":"Typically riper and broader: ripe apricot, mango and orange peel, with higher alcohol and rounder, sweeter seeming fruit. Vanilla from new oak sometimes shows. The cooler Eden Valley tends to be fresher.","cellar":"Bottled under the grape's name. Often picked very ripe, fermented in steel or barrel, and sometimes blended with Marsanne and Roussanne or fermented with a red as Shiraz Viognier."},"why":"Viognier must hang late to find its perfume, so warm, sunny sites push sugar and alcohol up fast. Condrieu's granite slopes ripen it more slowly, and the appellation names the place, not the grape.","blur":"The Eden Valley and other cooler New World sites make fresher, more restrained wines, and a hot year or generous new oak can make Condrieu rich and heady.","call":"Violet, a stony, bitter edge and fruit held in check lean Condrieu; mango, vanilla and a hotter finish lean New World."}},"service":{"temp":"50 to 55 degrees Fahrenheit, 10 to 13 degrees Celsius: too cold and the perfume closes.","glass":"A white wine glass with a generous bowl; a Burgundy bowl for a rich Condrieu.","decant":"Usually unnecessary; pour it and let it warm a little in the glass.","age":"Most within two to five years: it tires early, and the perfume is the first thing to fade."},"food":{"with":[{"dish":"Lobster or scallops in butter","why":"a full, silky white for sweet, rich shellfish"},{"dish":"Roast chicken with apricots or tarragon","why":"weight for the bird, perfume to lift it"},{"dish":"Mild, fragrant curry or a tagine","why":"low acidity and perfume suit gentle spice"},{"dish":"Crawfish étouffée","why":"body to meet the butter and the sweet shellfish"}]},"labels":[{"term":"Condrieu","means":"The northern Rhône appellation for Viognier alone; the label names the place, not the grape."},{"term":"Château-Grillet","means":"A tiny appellation of its own inside the Condrieu zone, a single granite amphitheatre of Viognier."},{"term":"Shiraz Viognier","means":"A red wine: Shiraz fermented with a little Viognier, in the manner of Côte-Rôtie."},{"term":"Pays d'Oc Viognier","means":"A varietal wine from the Languedoc: lighter, simpler and made to be enjoyed young."}],"guest":[{"q":"Is Viognier sweet?","a":"Nearly always dry. The apricot and honeysuckle smell sweet, and the low acidity and full body feel rich, but there is very little sugar in the glass."},{"q":"I usually order Chardonnay. Will I like this?","a":"Very likely. It has the same weight and soft texture with far more perfume: apricot and honeysuckle where Chardonnay has apple and cream."},{"q":"Why is there a white grape in my red Côte-Rôtie?","a":"A little Viognier, up to a fifth by law and usually far less, is fermented in the vat with the Syrah. It lifts the perfume and helps to fix the colour."}],"compare":[{"slug":"gewurztraminer","tell":"Gewürztraminer is lychee and rose petal, with a spicier and often off dry finish; Viognier is apricot and honeysuckle, and nearly always dry."},{"slug":"chardonnay","tell":"Oaked Chardonnay has more acidity and no perfume of its own; Viognier is headier, with apricot and honeysuckle, and softer in acidity."},{"slug":"marsanne","tell":"Marsanne is quieter and nuttier, with pear, almond and beeswax; Viognier is the loud one, all apricot and honeysuckle."}],"cards":[{"id":"gt-0wvkbsrp","front":"Viognier: which two northern Rhône appellations are made from it alone?","back":"Condrieu and Château-Grillet, on granite terraces just south of Côte-Rôtie. Château-Grillet is a single estate with its own appellation inside the Condrieu zone."},{"id":"gt-z3ik84a6","front":"Viognier blind: Condrieu or the New World, what tips it?","back":"Violet, a stony, faintly bitter edge and fruit held in check lean Condrieu. Mango, vanilla from new oak and a hotter finish lean California or another warm New World site."},{"id":"gt-7lwx47u5","front":"How much Viognier may Côte-Rôtie hold, and how is it added?","back":"Up to 20 per cent, though most wines carry far less. It is fermented in the vat with the Syrah, never blended in afterwards."}],"questions":[{"id":"gt-41iv6pbm","q":"A blind white is deep lemon, dry and full, with mango, ripe apricot and vanilla, low acidity and a hot finish. Which origin fits it?","opts":["Mosel, Germany","Chablis, Burgundy","Condrieu, northern Rhône","California's Central Coast"],"a":3,"exp":"Mango, vanilla from new oak and high alcohol on low acidity point to a warm New World Viognier. Condrieu tends to show violet and a stony restraint, with any oak held in the background.","tag":"worlds"},{"id":"gt-jlpljfnn","q":"A guest who loves rich Chardonnay asks for something more perfumed, but still dry and full bodied. Which bottle do you bring?","opts":["Condrieu","Muscadet","Moscato d'Asti","Chablis"],"a":0,"exp":"Condrieu is Viognier: dry and full like a rich Chardonnay, with a perfume of apricot and honeysuckle. Moscato d'Asti is perfumed too, but it is sweet, light and gently sparkling."},{"id":"gt-bq2ta6lc","q":"A guest reads that her red Côte-Rôtie holds a little white grape and asks which one it is. What do you tell her?","opts":["Marsanne","Roussanne","Viognier","Chardonnay"],"a":2,"exp":"Côte-Rôtie may ferment up to 20 per cent Viognier with its Syrah. Marsanne and Roussanne are the white grapes allowed in red Hermitage and Saint-Joseph, not in Côte-Rôtie."}],"sources":[{"title":"Inter Rhône: Viognier, the grape variety","url":"https://www.vins-rhone.com/en/viognier-grape-variety"},{"title":"Inter Rhône: Côtes du Rhône Cru Condrieu","url":"https://www.vins-rhone.com/en/cotes-du-rhone-cru-aoc-condrieu"},{"title":"Wine Australia: Australian wine, Viognier","url":"https://www.australianwine.com/our-wines/viognier"},{"title":"The Oxford Companion to Wine: Viognier","url":""}]},
"cabernet-franc": {"slug":"cabernet-franc","name":"Cabernet Franc","colour":"red","kind":"grape","status":"full","mode":"contrast","court":true,"aliases":[],"notIn":[],"say":"kah-bair-NAY FRAHN","lines":{"s10":"Cabernet Franc is the fragrant red of the Loire: raspberry, violet and pencil shavings on a light, fresh frame.","s20":"Cabernet Franc is the red of Chinon and Bourgueil in the Loire, and the partner of Merlot in Saint-Émilion. It is lighter and more perfumed than Cabernet Sauvignon, with raspberry, violet and a leafy edge of green pepper.","s45":"Cabernet Franc came first of the two Cabernets, and its home today is the Loire Valley, in Chinon, Bourgueil and Saumur-Champigny. It tastes of raspberry and redcurrant, with violet and a streak of pencil shavings and green bell pepper. It is medium in body, bright and fresh, with fine tannin. The Loire raises it with little new oak, while Argentina and California often make it darker, riper and rounder. Serve it a little cool, with roast chicken, charcuterie or goat's cheese. Crossed by chance with Sauvignon Blanc, it gave the world Cabernet Sauvignon, and it is a parent of Merlot too.","sale":"Cabernet Franc is a red with the freshness of a white: raspberry, violet and a cool, leafy snap. Serve it a little cool with the roast chicken or the charcuterie."},"glass":{"lead":"Raspberry and redcurrant with violet, pencil shavings and green bell pepper, on a medium body with bright acidity and fine tannin.","notes":[{"family":"red fruit","notes":["raspberry","redcurrant","red cherry","red plum"]},{"family":"black fruit","notes":["black cherry","black plum"]},{"family":"floral","notes":["violet","potpourri"]},{"family":"herbal","notes":["green bell pepper","jalapeño","chilli flake"]},{"family":"mineral","notes":["pencil shavings","graphite"]},{"family":"earth","notes":["tobacco leaf","dried leaves"]}],"ladder":[{"climate":"cool","where":"Chinon, Bourgueil and Saumur-Champigny; the Finger Lakes and Ontario","notes":["raspberry","redcurrant","green bell pepper","pencil shavings"]},{"climate":"moderate","where":"Saint-Émilion, Long Island, Virginia and Hawke's Bay","notes":["red plum","red cherry","violet","tobacco leaf"]},{"climate":"warm","where":"Mendoza, Napa Valley, Tuscany's coast and Stellenbosch","notes":["black cherry","black plum","roasted red pepper"]}],"structure":"Dry. Acidity medium plus to high, tannin medium to medium plus, fine and chalky, alcohol moderate, body medium."},"worlds":{"contrast":{"old":{"where":"The Loire's Chinon, Bourgueil and Saumur-Champigny; Bordeaux's Right Bank","glass":"Tends to be light to medium and brisk: raspberry, redcurrant and violet with pencil shavings and green bell pepper, high acidity and moderate alcohol. On the Right Bank it is usually blended, and adds perfume to Merlot.","cellar":"In the Loire, usually bottled alone and raised in steel, concrete or old casks with little new oak; gravel gives lighter wines, limestone slopes firmer ones."},"new":{"where":"The Finger Lakes, Long Island, Virginia, Ontario, Mendoza, California and Chile","glass":"Typically riper and fuller: black cherry and plum with roasted pepper, softer acidity, more alcohol and vanilla from new oak in Mendoza and California. New York and Ontario stay closer to the Loire, with raspberry and a leafy edge.","cellar":"Often bottled under the grape's own name, or blended with Cabernet Sauvignon and Merlot; warmer regions tend to pick riper and use more new French oak."},"why":"The Loire is cool and northerly, so the grape ripens only just, keeping high acidity and its leafy edge. Warm, sunny New World sites ripen the green note away. The Loire labels the village, the New World the grape.","blur":"The Finger Lakes and Ontario are cool enough to taste like the Loire, while Tuscany's coast and Villány in Hungary make dark, ripe wines, as warm years do in Chinon.","call":"Raspberry, pencil shavings and high acidity at moderate alcohol lean Loire; black cherry, roasted pepper and vanilla on a rounder body lean New World."}},"service":{"temp":"54 to 59 degrees Fahrenheit, 12 to 15 degrees Celsius, lightly cool; a fuller, oaked wine a few degrees warmer.","glass":"A Bordeaux glass; a Burgundy bowl suits a light, perfumed Loire wine.","decant":"Usually unnecessary for a young Loire red; an old Chinon may be decanted gently off its sediment.","age":"Light Loire wines within three or four years; Chinon and Bourgueil from limestone slopes five to fifteen."},"food":{"with":[{"dish":"Roast chicken","why":"a light body and bright acidity suit white meat"},{"dish":"Charcuterie, rillettes and pâté","why":"fresh acidity balances the richness: the Loire's own match"},{"dish":"Goat's cheese","why":"the wine's freshness meets the cheese's tang"},{"dish":"Grilled redfish","why":"a cool, light red with fine tannin can take firm fish"}]},"labels":[{"term":"Chinon, Bourgueil, Saumur-Champigny","means":"Loire place names for reds made almost wholly from Cabernet Franc: the label names the village."},{"term":"Breton","means":"The old local name for Cabernet Franc in the Loire."},{"term":"Bouchet","means":"The grape's traditional name in Saint-Émilion and Pomerol, where it is blended with Merlot."},{"term":"Cabernet d'Anjou","means":"An off dry Loire rosé made from Cabernet Franc and Cabernet Sauvignon."}],"guest":[{"q":"Is this the same grape as Cabernet Sauvignon?","a":"No, it is one of its parents. Cabernet Franc is lighter and more fragrant, with raspberry and violet where Cabernet Sauvignon has cassis, and its tannin is gentler."},{"q":"Why does it taste a little of green pepper?","a":"That leafy note belongs to the grape, and a cool climate keeps it. In a ripe year it sits quietly behind the raspberry, and wines from warmer places show less of it."},{"q":"Should a red really be served cool?","a":"A light Loire red, yes. A slight chill lifts its fruit and freshness, and too much warmth makes it taste flat. It should feel cool in the mouth, not cold."}],"compare":[{"slug":"cabernet-sauvignon","tell":"Cabernet Sauvignon is darker and firmer, with cassis, cedar and heavier tannin; Cabernet Franc is lighter and more perfumed, with raspberry and violet."},{"slug":"merlot","tell":"Merlot is plummier, rounder and softer, with no pencil shavings; Cabernet Franc is leaner and brighter, with a leafy edge."},{"slug":"carmenere","tell":"Carmenère, its offspring, is darker, fuller and softer, with soy and paprika; Cabernet Franc is lighter, with redcurrant, pencil shavings and higher acidity."}],"cards":[{"id":"gt-17xukwtq","front":"Cabernet Franc: its villages in the Loire, and its two local names?","back":"Chinon, Bourgueil, Saint-Nicolas-de-Bourgueil and Saumur-Champigny. It is Breton in the Loire and Bouchet in Saint-Émilion and Pomerol."},{"id":"gt-5isdknmc","front":"Cabernet Franc blind: the Loire or the New World, what tips it?","back":"Raspberry, pencil shavings, green bell pepper and high acidity at moderate alcohol lean Loire. Black cherry, roasted pepper, vanilla and a rounder body lean Mendoza or California."},{"id":"gt-99ot30n0","front":"Which three well known red grapes have Cabernet Franc as a parent?","back":"Cabernet Sauvignon, Merlot and Carmenère. The cross with Sauvignon Blanc that gave Cabernet Sauvignon was shown by DNA work published in 1997."}],"questions":[{"id":"gt-99m1k6u9","q":"Two glasses of Cabernet Franc are poured blind. The first is pale ruby, with raspberry, pencil shavings and green bell pepper, high acidity and moderate alcohol. The second is darker, with black cherry, roasted pepper and vanilla. Which pair of origins fits, in that order?","opts":["Mendoza, then Napa Valley","Mendoza, then Chinon","Napa Valley, then Bourgueil","Chinon, then Mendoza"],"a":3,"exp":"Tart raspberry, a green edge, high acidity and moderate alcohol say the cool Loire. Darker, riper fruit with vanilla from new oak says a warm, sunny New World site such as Mendoza.","tag":"worlds"},{"id":"gt-gp74m39u","q":"A guest orders a young Chinon with the charcuterie on a warm evening and asks how you will serve it. What do you do?","opts":["Stand the bottle near the kitchen to warm","Serve it lightly cool, straight into the glass","Pour it over ice in a white wine glass","Decant it for an hour at room temperature"],"a":1,"exp":"A light Loire red shows well at 54 to 59 degrees Fahrenheit, 12 to 15 degrees Celsius, and seldom needs decanting. Warmth flattens it, and ice would dilute it."},{"id":"gt-jb49eta6","q":"A guest sees the word Breton beside a red on a Loire list and asks which grape it is. What do you tell him?","opts":["Cabernet Sauvignon","Pinot Noir","Cabernet Franc","Petit Verdot"],"a":2,"exp":"Breton is the old Loire name for Cabernet Franc, the grape of Chinon and Bourgueil. Cabernet Sauvignon is allowed in Chinon only as a small share, and Breton is not its name."}],"sources":[{"title":"Loire Valley Wines: Chinon","url":"https://www.vinsvaldeloire.fr/en/wine/chinon"},{"title":"Chinon: the appellation's rule, cahier des charges","url":"https://www.chinon.com/wp-content/uploads/cdc-aoc-chinon-homologue-par-decret-du-15-novembre-2011-jo-du-18-novembre-2011-modifie-le-14-juin-2018-jo-du-22-juin-2018.pdf"},{"title":"New York Wines: New York Cabernet Franc moves forward","url":"https://newyorkwines.org/new-york-cabernet-franc-moves-forward/"},{"title":"The Oxford Companion to Wine, entry on Cabernet Franc","url":""}]},
"cabernet-sauvignon": {"slug":"cabernet-sauvignon","name":"Cabernet Sauvignon","colour":"red","kind":"grape","status":"full","mode":"contrast","court":true,"aliases":[],"notIn":[],"say":"kah-bair-NAY soh-vee-NYOHN","lines":{"s10":"Cabernet Sauvignon is the firm, dark red of Bordeaux's Left Bank and of Napa: cassis, cedar and a long, drying grip.","s20":"Cabernet Sauvignon is the backbone of red Bordeaux and the grape Napa is known for. Expect cassis and blackberry, cedar and a firm, drying tannin that softens with age and with red meat.","s45":"Cabernet Sauvignon comes from the gravel of Bordeaux's Left Bank, where it leads the blend. It tastes of cassis, blackberry and black cherry, with cedar and a touch of mint. It is full bodied, with firm, drying tannin, and it keeps for decades. It is usually raised in small oak barrels, a good share of them new. Bordeaux tends to be firmer and more cedary, while Napa is typically riper, softer and fuller. It is made for red meat: a rib of beef, lamb or a dry aged steak. At a blind tasting in Paris in 1976, French judges placed a Napa Cabernet above the red Bordeaux beside it.","sale":"Cabernet Sauvignon has the cassis fruit and the firm grip that a fatty cut of beef calls for. Tonight I would pour it with a ribeye, and decant it while the kitchen works."},"glass":{"lead":"Cassis and blackberry over cedar and graphite, on a full body with firm, drying tannin.","notes":[{"family":"black fruit","notes":["cassis","blackberry","black cherry"]},{"family":"herbal","notes":["green bell pepper","mint","eucalyptus"]},{"family":"mineral","notes":["graphite","warm gravel"]},{"family":"oak","notes":["cedar","vanilla pod","coffee"]},{"family":"earth","notes":["tobacco leaf","cigar box","leather"]}],"ladder":[{"climate":"moderate","where":"The Médoc and Graves, Coonawarra, Margaret River","notes":["cassis","redcurrant","green bell pepper","mint"]},{"climate":"warm","where":"Napa Valley, Paso Robles, Maipo and the Barossa","notes":["ripe blackberry","black cherry","black plum"]}],"structure":"Dry. Acidity medium plus, tannin high and drying, alcohol medium plus to high, body full."},"worlds":{"contrast":{"old":{"where":"Bordeaux's Left Bank, the Médoc and Graves; Bolgheri in Tuscany","glass":"Tends to be firmer and drier in feel: cassis and redcurrant with cedar, graphite and a leafy edge of green bell pepper, moderate alcohol and tannin that needs time.","cellar":"Nearly every wine is a blend, with Merlot, Cabernet Franc and a little Petit Verdot, aged in small French oak barrels, a share of them new."},"new":{"where":"Napa Valley, Washington, Coonawarra, Margaret River, Maipo and Stellenbosch","glass":"Typically riper and fuller: blackberry, black cherry and cassis, often with mint or eucalyptus, plusher tannin and higher alcohol. Vanilla and coffee from new oak tend to show.","cellar":"Often bottled under the grape's own name, alone or with a little Merlot; picked riper and aged in a larger share of new oak."},"why":"Bordeaux's mild, damp, maritime climate ripens Cabernet only just, so it is blended and stays firm. Sunnier, drier New World sites ripen it fully. The label follows: Bordeaux names the place, the New World the grape.","blur":"Coonawarra and Margaret River keep a leafy edge close to Bordeaux, while Bolgheri on the Tuscan coast and warm recent years in Bordeaux give riper, fuller wines.","call":"Cedar, graphite and a dry, firm finish at moderate alcohol lean Bordeaux; ripe blackberry, mint and vanilla at higher alcohol lean New World."}},"service":{"temp":"61 to 64 degrees Fahrenheit, 16 to 18 degrees Celsius: cool room temperature, never warm.","glass":"A tall Bordeaux glass, with room to swirl.","decant":"Consider it for a young, tannic wine after tasting a sample; an old one may be decanted off its sediment.","age":"Most open up at five to ten years; ten to thirty from a serious address."},"food":{"with":[{"dish":"Ribeye or a rib of beef","why":"fat and protein soften the tannin"},{"dish":"Roast or grilled lamb","why":"the classic Médoc match, rosemary meeting the cedar"},{"dish":"Braised short rib","why":"richness that needs the wine's grip and acidity"},{"dish":"Aged hard cheese","why":"salt and fat round off the firm tannin"}]},"labels":[{"term":"Médoc, Pauillac, Margaux","means":"Left Bank Bordeaux place names: the label names the village, and Cabernet Sauvignon leads the blend."},{"term":"Bordeaux blend","means":"Cabernet Sauvignon with Merlot, Cabernet Franc and sometimes Petit Verdot or Malbec, wherever it is made."},{"term":"Cru Classé","means":"A classed growth: an estate ranked in the Médoc classification of 1855."},{"term":"Meritage","means":"An American name for blends of the Bordeaux grapes, not a legal category; it rhymes with heritage."}],"guest":[{"q":"Why does this Cabernet dry my mouth out?","a":"That is tannin, from the grape's thick skins and from oak. It grips most when the wine is young, and it softens with air, with age and with a fatty cut of meat."},{"q":"Is Bordeaux a Cabernet?","a":"Red Bordeaux is a blend. On the Left Bank, in the Médoc and Graves, Cabernet Sauvignon usually leads; on the Right Bank, Merlot does."},{"q":"Should we decant it?","a":"For a young, firm bottle it often helps, and I can pour you a taste first. For an old one I would decant gently to leave the sediment behind."}],"compare":[{"slug":"merlot","tell":"Merlot is softer and rounder, with plum where Cabernet has cassis, and less of the drying grip and cedar."},{"slug":"cabernet-franc","tell":"Cabernet Franc, its parent, is lighter and more fragrant: raspberry, violet and pencil shavings, with a leafier edge and gentler tannin."},{"slug":"syrah","tell":"Syrah brings cracked black pepper, smoked meat and black olive; Cabernet has cassis and cedar and a firmer, more drying tannin."}],"cards":[{"id":"gt-q03mnlpi","front":"Cabernet Sauvignon: its two parents, and where it is at home?","back":"Cabernet Franc and Sauvignon Blanc, shown by DNA work published in 1997. Home is the gravel of Bordeaux's Left Bank: the Médoc and Graves."},{"id":"gt-1udzehui","front":"Cabernet Sauvignon blind: Bordeaux or the New World, what tips it?","back":"Cedar, graphite and a dry, firm finish at moderate alcohol lean Bordeaux. Riper blackberry, mint, vanilla and higher alcohol lean Napa, Coonawarra or Maipo."},{"id":"gt-xxu31v1j","front":"Why does Cabernet Sauvignon age, and what does age bring?","back":"Small berries with thick skins give high tannin and deep colour. With years the tannin softens and tobacco leaf, cigar box and leather appear."}],"questions":[{"id":"gt-3rwze8z3","q":"Two glasses of Cabernet Sauvignon are poured blind. One is cedary and firm at moderate alcohol, the other riper, with mint, vanilla and higher alcohol. Which pair of origins fits, in that order?","opts":["Paso Robles, then Margaux","Pauillac, then Napa Valley","Maipo, then Coonawarra","Napa Valley, then Pauillac"],"a":1,"exp":"Cedar, graphite and firm tannin at moderate alcohol tend to mark Left Bank Bordeaux. Riper fruit, mint, vanilla from new oak and higher alcohol tend to mark a warm New World site such as Napa.","tag":"worlds"},{"id":"gt-zyeo6yv0","q":"A guest orders a young Pauillac with a rib of beef and asks why you suggest decanting it. What is the reason?","opts":["Air can open the aroma of a young, firm wine","It takes the tannin out of the wine","It lowers the alcohol","It warms the wine to room temperature"],"a":0,"exp":"Decanting a young, tannic red gives it air, which can open the aroma and ease the first impression of tannin. It does not remove tannin, and a sample tells you whether it helps."},{"id":"gt-4vph9ivi","q":"A guest asks where Cabernet Sauvignon came from. Which two grapes are its parents?","opts":["Pinot and Gouais Blanc","Merlot and Cabernet Franc","Cabernet Franc and Sauvignon Blanc","Carmenère and Petit Verdot"],"a":2,"exp":"DNA work at the University of California, Davis, published in 1997, showed a cross of Cabernet Franc and Sauvignon Blanc, a surprise because one parent is white."}],"sources":[{"title":"Bordeaux Wines: Cabernet Sauvignon","url":"https://www.bordeaux.com/en/grape-varieties/cabernet-sauvignon/"},{"title":"University of California, Davis: DNA fingerprinting reveals parentage of classic wine grape","url":"https://www.ucdavis.edu/news/dna-fingerprinting-reveals-parentage-classic-wine-grape"},{"title":"Wine Australia: Cabernet Sauvignon","url":"https://www.wineaustralia.com/market-insights/regions-and-varieties/cabernet-sauvignon"},{"title":"Smithsonian Magazine: the Paris tasting of May 1976","url":"https://www.smithsonianmag.com/smithsonian-institution/that-revolutionary-may-day-1976-when-california-wines-bested-france-finest-180958971/"}]},
"gamay": {"slug":"gamay","name":"Gamay","colour":"red","kind":"grape","status":"full","mode":"home","court":true,"aliases":[],"notIn":[],"say":"gah-MAY","lines":{"s10":"Gamay is the grape of Beaujolais: a light, juicy red of sour cherry, raspberry and violet that takes a light chill well.","s20":"Gamay is the red grape of Beaujolais, light in colour and body and bright with acidity. It tastes of sour cherry, raspberry and violet, and the ten villages called crus make a deeper wine with a stony edge.","s45":"Gamay was banished from the heart of Burgundy in 1395, and it found its home to the south, on the granite hills of Beaujolais. It is light and bright, with sour cherry, raspberry and violet, high acidity and hardly any tannin. Most of it ferments as whole bunches in a sealed vat, which keeps the fruit fresh and can add a hint of banana. Oregon and Ontario now make it in the same bright style. Pour it lightly chilled with charcuterie, roast chicken or the holiday turkey. Its first wine of the year, Beaujolais Nouveau, goes on sale on the third Thursday of November.","sale":"Gamay can go in the ice bucket: all sour cherry and raspberry, with barely any tannin to turn hard in the cold. Open it with the charcuterie and let it run right through the roast chicken."},"glass":{"lead":"Sour cherry, raspberry and cranberry with violet and peony, light and juicy, on high acidity and very little tannin.","notes":[{"family":"red fruit","notes":["sour cherry","raspberry","cranberry","redcurrant"]},{"family":"black fruit","notes":["black cherry","blackberry"]},{"family":"floral","notes":["violet","peony"]},{"family":"lees and cellar","notes":["banana","bubblegum","kirsch"]},{"family":"mineral","notes":["crushed granite","wet stone"]}],"ladder":[{"climate":"cool","where":"Beaujolais, Touraine, the Niagara Peninsula and the Willamette Valley","notes":["sour cherry","cranberry","raspberry","violet"]},{"climate":"moderate","where":"The sunny Swiss Valais, and the granite crus in a warm year","notes":["black cherry","ripe raspberry","kirsch"]}],"structure":"Dry. Acidity high, tannin low, alcohol moderate, body light, rising to medium in the crus."},"worlds":{"home":{"home":"Beaujolais, on the granite hills south of Burgundy. Most of it ferments as whole bunches in a sealed vat, the semi-carbonic way, for bright fruit to drink young; the ten crus of the north are firmer, and some are made and aged like red Burgundy. Touraine and the Swiss Valais grow it too.","abroad":"Oregon's Willamette Valley and Ontario's Niagara Peninsula bottle it under its own name in the same bright style: red cherry and raspberry, high acidity and soft tannin, and in Oregon often whole bunches in the Beaujolais manner."}},"service":{"temp":"54 to 59 degrees Fahrenheit, 12 to 15 degrees Celsius; Nouveau a little cooler still.","glass":"A Burgundy bowl for a cru; a plain red wine glass serves a simple Beaujolais.","decant":"Usually unnecessary. A young, firm Morgon may open with a little air.","age":"Young, within two or three years; a cru such as Morgon or Moulin-à-Vent keeps ten."},"food":{"with":[{"dish":"Charcuterie, pâté and dry sausage","why":"high acidity cuts the fat, as in the bistros of Lyon"},{"dish":"Roast chicken","why":"a light red that does not crowd the bird"},{"dish":"Roast turkey with cranberry sauce","why":"tart cherry fruit echoes the sauce"},{"dish":"Chicken and andouille gumbo","why":"served cool, with little tannin to clash with smoke and pepper"}]},"labels":[{"term":"Beaujolais-Villages","means":"The middle tier, from the granite hamlets of the north: riper and firmer than plain Beaujolais."},{"term":"Morgon, Fleurie, Moulin-à-Vent","means":"Three of the ten crus: the label names the village and need not say Beaujolais or Gamay."},{"term":"Beaujolais Nouveau","means":"The first wine of the harvest, released on the third Thursday of November and drunk young."},{"term":"Gamay Noir","means":"A name on Oregon and Ontario labels; in full Gamay Noir à Jus Blanc, black skinned with white juice."},{"term":"Gamay Beaujolais","means":"An old California name for a clone of Pinot Noir, not true Gamay; barred from American labels since 2007."}],"guest":[{"q":"Is all Beaujolais like Nouveau?","a":"No. Nouveau is the first and simplest wine of the year. A cru such as Morgon or Moulin-à-Vent is a serious red that can keep ten years and grow to taste like Burgundy."},{"q":"Is Beaujolais a Burgundy?","a":"In law it belongs to greater Burgundy, but it is its own country: Gamay on granite, not Pinot Noir on limestone, and a lighter, juicier wine."},{"q":"Can you really chill a red wine?","a":"This one, yes. Gamay has so little tannin that a light chill only lifts its fruit. A firm, tannic red would turn hard, which is why those are served warmer."}],"compare":[{"slug":"pinot-noir","tell":"Pinot Noir is silkier, with forest floor beneath the fruit and a longer finish; Gamay is lighter, tarter and more candied, with a violet rim."},{"slug":"cabernet-franc","tell":"Loire Cabernet Franc is leafier, with green bell pepper, pencil shavings and firmer tannin; Gamay is softer and fruitier, with no green edge."},{"slug":"zweigelt","tell":"Zweigelt is darker in colour, with a peppery snap and a little more tannin; Gamay is paler and lighter, with a scent of violet."}],"cards":[{"id":"gt-3wct057o","front":"Gamay: where is it at home, and on what soil?","back":"Beaujolais, south of Burgundy, on the pink granite and schist of the northern hills, where the ten crus lie. A ducal edict of 1395 drove it out of the Côte d'Or."},{"id":"gt-qxgh3d2w","front":"Gamay abroad: where is it bottled under its own name, and how?","back":"Oregon's Willamette Valley and Ontario's Niagara Peninsula, often as Gamay Noir: bright, juicy reds of red cherry and raspberry, in Oregon often made with whole bunches in the Beaujolais manner."},{"id":"gt-kc6k902u","front":"Name the ten crus of Beaujolais, Gamay's home, north to south.","back":"Saint-Amour, Juliénas, Chénas, Moulin-à-Vent, Fleurie, Chiroubles, Morgon, Régnié, Côte de Brouilly, Brouilly."}],"questions":[{"id":"gt-9m5v5y1t","q":"A blind red is bright purple with a violet rim, light and tart, with sour cherry, banana and bubblegum and almost no tannin. The table agrees it is Gamay. Which method of making it explains the banana and bubblegum?","opts":["A long soak on the skins after fermentation","Long ageing in new American oak","Grapes dried on mats before pressing","Whole bunches fermented in a sealed vat"],"a":3,"exp":"Whole bunches fermenting in a sealed vat, the carbonic way of Beaujolais, give banana and bubblegum. New American oak would add vanilla and dill instead, and a long soak on the skins would add tannin."},{"id":"gt-uirrudre","q":"A guest wants a bottle from Gamay's home to lay down for several years, not one to drink this month. Which label do you bring?","opts":["Beaujolais Blanc","Moulin-à-Vent","Beaujolais-Villages","Beaujolais Nouveau"],"a":1,"exp":"Moulin-à-Vent is a firm, structured cru that can keep ten years. Nouveau is made to drink within months, Villages is a young bistro red, and Beaujolais Blanc is Chardonnay."},{"id":"gt-ephz56pe","q":"On a hot evening a guest asks for a red he can drink cool with a plate of charcuterie. Which grape do you suggest?","opts":["Gamay","Nebbiolo","Tannat","Cabernet Sauvignon"],"a":0,"exp":"Gamay has high acidity and very little tannin, so a light chill lifts its fruit. Cabernet Sauvignon, Nebbiolo and Tannat are tannic reds that turn hard and bitter when served cold."}],"sources":[{"title":"Inter Beaujolais: the Beaujolais notebook, the twelve appellations and the grape","url":"https://www.beaujolais.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2020/09/Carnet-Beaujolais-EN.pdf"},{"title":"VQA Ontario: Gamay, the terroir one page guide","url":"https://learn.vqaontario.ca/wp-content/uploads/2026/06/TERROIR-ONE-PAGERS-GAMAY-2025-EN5.pdf"},{"title":"Alcohol and Tobacco Tax and Trade Bureau: Treasury Decision ATF-388a, grape variety names","url":"https://www.ttb.gov/rrd/tdatf-388a"},{"title":"Wine Press Northwest, summer 2021: Gamay in the Willamette Valley","url":"https://issuu.com/winepressnorthwest/docs/wine_press_northwest_summer_2021/s/12880768"}]},
"grenache": {"slug":"grenache","name":"Grenache / Garnacha","colour":"red","kind":"grape","status":"full","mode":"contrast","court":true,"aliases":[],"notIn":["garnacha blanca","garnacha tintorera"],"say":"gruh-NASH, in Spain gar-NAH-chah","lines":{"s10":"Grenache is the warm hearted red of the Mediterranean: ripe strawberry, kirsch and dried thyme, soft in tannin and generous in alcohol.","s20":"Grenache is the grape behind Châteauneuf-du-Pape and most reds of the southern Rhône, and Spain, where it most likely began, calls it Garnacha. It gives ripe strawberry, kirsch and dried thyme, with soft tannin and a warm, generous finish.","s45":"Grenache needs heat, and finds it all round the Mediterranean, above all in the southern Rhône and Spain. It tastes of ripe strawberry, raspberry and kirsch, with a little white pepper. The colour is pale, the tannin soft and the alcohol high. It is often raised in concrete or old casks and blended with Syrah and Mourvèdre. The Rhône and Spain tend to be savoury, with dried thyme and rosemary, while Australia is often brighter and fruitier. It wants roast lamb, grilled sausages or a slow braise. It most likely began in Aragon, in northern Spain, as Garnacha, and went with that kingdom to Sardinia and southern France.","sale":"Grenache tastes of the warm south: ripe strawberry, kirsch and the dried thyme of a hot hillside, with tannin soft enough for anyone. Have it with the lamb or a slow braise."},"glass":{"lead":"Ripe strawberry, raspberry and kirsch with dried thyme and white pepper, pale in colour, soft in tannin and warm with alcohol.","notes":[{"family":"red fruit","notes":["wild strawberry","raspberry","red plum","kirsch"]},{"family":"dried fruit","notes":["dried cherry","dried fig"]},{"family":"herbal","notes":["dried thyme","rosemary","lavender"]},{"family":"spice","notes":["white pepper","liquorice"]},{"family":"savoury","notes":["cured meat","leather"]}],"ladder":[{"climate":"moderate","where":"The Sierra de Gredos, Navarra and higher McLaren Vale sites","notes":["wild strawberry","red cherry","white pepper"]},{"climate":"warm","where":"Châteauneuf-du-Pape, Gigondas, Priorat, Campo de Borja and the Barossa","notes":["kirsch","raspberry jam","dried cherry","liquorice"]}],"structure":"Dry. Acidity low to medium, tannin low to medium and soft, alcohol high, body medium plus to full."},"worlds":{"contrast":{"old":{"where":"Southern Rhône, Roussillon, Spain as Garnacha, Sardinia as Cannonau","glass":"Tends to be savoury and warm: kirsch, ripe strawberry and dried cherry with thyme, rosemary, white pepper and leather, pale in colour, soft in tannin and high in alcohol. Old vine Priorat and Aragon are typically darker and denser.","cellar":"In the Rhône usually the lead in a blend with Syrah and Mourvèdre, in Spain often alone; raised in concrete or large old casks, since Grenache oxidises easily."},"new":{"where":"McLaren Vale and the Barossa, California's Central Coast, Washington, the Swartland","glass":"Typically more openly fruity: raspberry, strawberry and red plum, sometimes candied, with less of the dried thyme and leather at much the same alcohol. Many Australian wines, now picked earlier, tend to be paler, perfumed and medium bodied.","cellar":"Bottled under the grape's name or as a GSM blend, in Australia often from old bush vines; newer wines are picked earlier and see little new oak."},"why":"Both worlds grow Grenache in heat, so both make it full and warm. The Rhône blends it with firmer grapes, ages it in old casks and names the place; the New World more often bottles it alone, for fruit.","blur":"Garnacha from the high Sierra de Gredos is pale, fresh and fragrant, and old vine Barossa blends can be as dense and savoury as a Châteauneuf-du-Pape.","call":"Dried thyme, leather and a savoury, warm finish lean Old World; bright raspberry and red plum, often perfumed, with little of either lean New World."}},"service":{"temp":"59 to 63 degrees Fahrenheit, 15 to 17 degrees Celsius: a touch cool, to keep the alcohol in check.","glass":"A wide Burgundy bowl, which gathers the perfume.","decant":"Usually unnecessary; taste a sample before decanting a young Châteauneuf-du-Pape or Priorat.","age":"Most within five years; Châteauneuf-du-Pape, Gigondas and Priorat five to fifteen."},"food":{"with":[{"dish":"Roast lamb with rosemary and garlic","why":"the wine's own thyme and rosemary meet the herbs"},{"dish":"Daube or slow braised beef","why":"soft tannin and warmth suit a long braise"},{"dish":"Grilled sausages or chorizo","why":"ripe fruit takes smoke and paprika in its stride"},{"dish":"Gumbo with smoked sausage","why":"soft tannin suits the smoke, if the chilli stays gentle"}]},"labels":[{"term":"Garnacha, Cannonau","means":"The Spanish and the Sardinian names for the same grape."},{"term":"Châteauneuf-du-Pape","means":"A southern Rhône village whose red blends up to thirteen grapes, Grenache in the lead."},{"term":"GSM","means":"Grenache, Syrah and Mourvèdre: the southern Rhône blend, named by its initials in Australia and California."},{"term":"Tavel","means":"A southern Rhône village that makes only rosé, deep pink, dry and full, led by Grenache."},{"term":"Banyuls","means":"A sweet, fortified red from Roussillon, made mostly of Grenache; a match for chocolate."}],"guest":[{"q":"It looks so pale. Is it a light wine?","a":"No, and that is the surprise of Grenache. Its thin skins give little colour and soft tannin, but the grape ripens very sweet in the heat, so the wine is full and warm."},{"q":"Is Garnacha the same as Grenache?","a":"Yes. Garnacha is the Spanish name, and Spain is most likely where the grape began. Sardinia grows it too, and calls it Cannonau."},{"q":"Is Châteauneuf-du-Pape a grape?","a":"No, it is a village in the southern Rhône. Its red is a blend, nearly always led by Grenache, with Syrah and Mourvèdre."}],"compare":[{"slug":"pinot-noir","tell":"Pinot Noir is as pale but cooler in feel, with higher acidity, lower alcohol and forest floor; Grenache is warmer, with kirsch and dried thyme."},{"slug":"zinfandel","tell":"Zinfandel is darker and jammier, with blackberry, a raisin edge and vanilla from oak; Grenache is paler, with kirsch, strawberry and dried thyme."},{"slug":"nebbiolo","tell":"Nebbiolo is just as pale but far higher in acidity and tannin, with rose and tar; Grenache is soft, low in acidity and warm."}],"cards":[{"id":"gt-bg7asxi1","front":"Grenache: its Spanish and Sardinian names, and its likely first home?","back":"Garnacha in Spain and Cannonau in Sardinia. It most likely began in Aragon, in northern Spain, and spread with the Crown of Aragon to Sardinia and Roussillon."},{"id":"gt-5e9xjzdl","front":"Grenache blind: Old World or New World, what tips it?","back":"Dried thyme, rosemary, leather and a savoury, warm finish lean southern Rhône or Spain. Bright raspberry and red plum, often perfumed, with little of either lean Australia or California."},{"id":"gt-2bl8ery1","front":"Why can Grenache be pale in the glass yet high in alcohol?","back":"Thin skins give little colour and soft tannin, while the grape needs a long, hot season and builds a great deal of sugar, so the wine is full and warm."}],"questions":[{"id":"gt-ahwkgs28","q":"Two reds led by Grenache are poured blind. The first smells of kirsch, dried thyme and leather; the second of bright raspberry and candied strawberry, with neither herb nor leather. Which pair of origins fits, in that order?","opts":["Paso Robles, then Châteauneuf-du-Pape","Barossa Valley, then Priorat","McLaren Vale, then Gigondas","Gigondas, then McLaren Vale"],"a":3,"exp":"Dried thyme, leather and a savoury warmth tend to mark the southern Rhône. Bright, candied raspberry and strawberry with little of either tend to mark Australia, so the reverse order does not fit.","tag":"worlds"},{"id":"gt-jdvsojy4","q":"A blind red is pale ruby with an orange rim, yet full and warm on the palate, with kirsch, strawberry, dried thyme and white pepper, soft tannin and low acidity. Which grape is it most likely to be?","opts":["Grenache","Sangiovese","Nebbiolo","Pinot Noir"],"a":0,"exp":"A pale colour with high alcohol, soft tannin and low acidity is the mark of Grenache. Pinot Noir is as pale but higher in acidity and lower in alcohol, and Nebbiolo is far more tannic."},{"id":"gt-se9eoven","q":"A guest asks for the Garnacha she enjoyed in Spain, and the list has nothing under that name. Which of these Italian bottles holds the same grape?","opts":["Chianti Classico","Barbera d'Asti","Cannonau di Sardegna","Montepulciano d'Abruzzo"],"a":2,"exp":"Cannonau is the Sardinian name for Grenache, the Garnacha of Spain. Barbera and Montepulciano are grapes of their own, and Chianti Classico is led by Sangiovese."}],"sources":[{"title":"Inter Rhône: Grenache noir, the grape variety","url":"https://www.vins-rhone.com/en/grenache-noir-grape-variety"},{"title":"Wine Australia: Grenache, stories of Australian wine","url":"https://www.wineaustralia.com/whats-happening/stories-of-australian-wine/october-2017/grenache"},{"title":"Decanter: the essential guide to Spanish Garnacha","url":"https://www.decanter.com/spanish-wine-academy/the-essential-guide-to-spanish-garnacha-431870"},{"title":"The Oxford Companion to Wine, entry on Grenache Noir","url":""}]},
"malbec": {"slug":"malbec","name":"Malbec","colour":"red","kind":"grape","status":"full","mode":"contrast","court":true,"aliases":[],"notIn":[],"say":"mahl-BEK","lines":{"s10":"Malbec is Argentina's red, deep purple and plush, with black plum, blueberry and violet, and made for grilled steak.","s20":"Malbec came from south west France and found its fame in Mendoza, high in the foothills of the Andes. It is dark and velvety, with black plum, blueberry and violet, and a soft finish that flatters red meat.","s45":"Malbec left France to make its name in Argentina, in the high desert vineyards of Mendoza, below the Andes. It tastes of black plum, blackberry and blueberry, with a scent of violet. It is full and plush, deep purple in the glass, with ripe, soft tannin. Oak barrels add cocoa and vanilla. Mendoza is typically ripe and velvety, while Cahors in France, where it began, is firmer and more savoury. It was made for grilled steak, and is as good with lamb or barbecue. Argentina keeps 17 April as Malbec day: on that date in 1853 Mendoza set out to found the vine nursery that brought the grape in.","sale":"Malbec and a grill belong together: black plum, violet and a soft, velvety grip that wraps round charred beef. Order the steak or the lamb chops and I will open one from high in Mendoza."},"glass":{"lead":"Black plum, blackberry and blueberry with violet and cocoa, deep purple and full, on plush, ripe tannin.","notes":[{"family":"black fruit","notes":["black plum","blackberry","black cherry"]},{"family":"blue fruit","notes":["blueberry","damson"]},{"family":"floral","notes":["violet","lavender"]},{"family":"oak","notes":["cocoa","vanilla pod","mocha"]},{"family":"earth","notes":["sweet tobacco","leather","iron"]}],"ladder":[{"climate":"cool","where":"Patagonia; Touraine in the Loire, as Côt","notes":["raspberry","red cherry","tart black plum"]},{"climate":"moderate","where":"Cahors in south west France","notes":["black plum","black cherry","damson"]},{"climate":"warm","where":"Mendoza, from Luján de Cuyo to the Uco Valley; Salta","notes":["ripe black plum","blueberry","violet","blackberry jam"]}],"structure":"Dry. Acidity medium, tannin medium to medium plus and plush, alcohol medium plus to high, body full."},"worlds":{"contrast":{"old":{"where":"Cahors in south west France; the Loire, as Côt","glass":"Tends to be firmer, drier and more savoury: black plum, damson and blackberry with iron, leather and liquorice, fresher acidity, less alcohol and a grip that wants food or years. The colour is as dark, the fruit less sweet.","cellar":"Cahors must be at least 70 per cent Malbec, with Merlot and Tannat; often a long soak on the skins and time in oak, though lighter styles exist."},"new":{"where":"Mendoza, Salta and Patagonia in Argentina; Chile and Washington State","glass":"Typically plush and ripe: black plum, blueberry and violet over cocoa and vanilla, with soft tannin, medium acidity and generous alcohol. High sites in the Uco Valley tend to be fresher and more violet scented than Luján de Cuyo.","cellar":"Usually bottled alone, under the grape's own name; picked ripe under strong mountain sun and often aged in French oak, some of it new."},"why":"Mendoza is a sunny high desert, watered from the Andes, where Malbec ripens fully and keeps freshness through cold nights. Cahors is damper and cooler, so its tannin stays firm. Cahors labels the place, Argentina the grape.","blur":"Modern Cahors from the river terraces can be ripe, oaked and plush, while high parcels in the Uco Valley and cool, low Patagonia give tauter, fresher Malbec.","call":"Violet, blueberry and plush, ripe tannin at higher alcohol lean Mendoza; iron, leather and a drier, firmer grip lean Cahors."}},"service":{"temp":"61 to 64 degrees Fahrenheit, 16 to 18 degrees Celsius: cool room temperature, so the alcohol does not lead.","glass":"A Bordeaux glass, with room for the perfume to gather.","decant":"A young, firm Cahors can gain from air after a sample; a soft Mendoza wine is ready as poured.","age":"Most within five years; serious Mendoza and Cahors wines keep five to twelve, a few longer."},"food":{"with":[{"dish":"Grilled steak with chimichurri","why":"plush fruit meets the char; soft tannin suits lean beef"},{"dish":"Lamb chops or a leg of lamb","why":"ripe plum fruit beside sweet, fatty meat"},{"dish":"Barbecued brisket or ribs","why":"ripe fruit stands up to smoke and a sweet glaze"},{"dish":"Duck confit or cassoulet, with Cahors","why":"the firmer, drier wine meets the richness"}]},"labels":[{"term":"Cahors","means":"The appellation of south west France: red only, at least 70 per cent Malbec, firmer than Mendoza."},{"term":"Côt, Auxerrois","means":"Names for Malbec in Cahors and the Loire; no relation of the white Auxerrois of Alsace."},{"term":"Luján de Cuyo, Uco Valley","means":"Two zones of Mendoza: Luján rounder and darker, the higher Uco Valley fresher and more scented."},{"term":"Black wine","means":"The old name for the dark wine of Cahors."}],"guest":[{"q":"Is Malbec French or Argentine?","a":"Both. It began in south west France, where Cahors still makes it. Argentina brought it in during the 1850s, and it was Mendoza that made it known round the world."},{"q":"How is it different from Cabernet?","a":"Softer and rounder. Malbec is plum, blueberry and violet with plush tannin, where Cabernet Sauvignon is cassis and cedar with a firmer, drier grip. Both suit a steak."},{"q":"Why does the label talk about altitude?","a":"High vineyards have strong sun and cold nights. The grapes ripen fully yet keep their acidity and their perfume, so the wine tastes fresh as well as rich."}],"compare":[{"slug":"merlot","tell":"Merlot lacks Malbec's violet lift and magenta rim: it is ruby and plummy, where Malbec is purple, with blueberry."},{"slug":"syrah","tell":"Syrah brings cracked black pepper, smoked meat and black olive; Malbec has none of that savour, and is plusher, with softer, rounder tannin."},{"slug":"cabernet-sauvignon","tell":"Cabernet Sauvignon is firmer, with cassis, cedar and drying tannin; Malbec is plusher and rounder, with violet in place of the leafy edge."}],"cards":[{"id":"gt-19cm5xnr","front":"Malbec: its French home, and its names there?","back":"Cahors in south west France, where it is Côt or Auxerrois and must be at least 70 per cent of the wine. The Loire grows it as Côt."},{"id":"gt-64s4rh68","front":"Malbec blind: Mendoza or Cahors, what tips it?","back":"Violet, blueberry and plush, ripe tannin at higher alcohol lean Mendoza. Iron, leather and a drier, firmer grip lean Cahors."},{"id":"gt-51q749fm","front":"What in the look of a glass points to Malbec?","back":"An opaque, deep purple colour with a bright magenta rim. With violet on the nose and plush black plum on the palate, it seals the call."}],"questions":[{"id":"gt-5f4s2yc3","q":"A blind red is opaque purple with a magenta rim. It shows black plum, damson, iron and leather, with fresh acidity and a firm, dry grip and no sweetness of fruit. Which origin fits it?","opts":["Salta, Argentina","Mendoza, Argentina","Colchagua, Chile","Cahors, France"],"a":3,"exp":"The colour says Malbec, and iron, leather and a firm, dry grip tend to mark Cahors. Mendoza and Salta are typically plusher, with violet, sweeter fruit and softer tannin.","tag":"worlds"},{"id":"gt-lsdpe46c","q":"A guest orders the grilled strip steak. He likes a full, soft red and dislikes anything that dries his mouth. Which do you suggest?","opts":["A young Pauillac","A young Barolo","A Malbec from Mendoza","A young Madiran"],"a":2,"exp":"Mendoza Malbec is full and plush, with ripe, soft tannin that suits grilled beef. Madiran, Pauillac and Barolo are all firm and drying when young."},{"id":"gt-9v9jxbk4","q":"A guest asks how a French grape came to be Argentina's red. What do you tell her?","opts":["Spanish missionaries carried it over in the 1500s","Mendoza founded a vine nursery in 1853 that brought it","Italian immigrants brought it from Piedmont in the 1920s","It is native to the Andes, named for France"],"a":1,"exp":"Mendoza moved to found a vine nursery and farm school on 17 April 1853, and the French agronomist Michel Aimé Pouget brought Malbec to it. The missionaries' grape was Criolla, not Malbec."}],"sources":[{"title":"Wines of Argentina: Why is Malbec synonymous with Argentina","url":"https://blog.winesofargentina.com/news/why-is-malbec-synonymous-with-argentina/"},{"title":"Wines of Argentina: Malbec numbers and other facts","url":"https://blog.winesofargentina.com/destacadas/malbec-numbers/"},{"title":"Union Interprofessionnelle du Vin de Cahors: the Cahors vineyard","url":"https://vindecahors.fr/en/cahors-vineyard"},{"title":"Wine Grapes, entry on Cot","url":""}]},
"merlot": {"slug":"merlot","name":"Merlot","colour":"red","kind":"grape","status":"full","mode":"contrast","court":true,"aliases":[],"notIn":[],"say":"mair-LOH","lines":{"s10":"Merlot is the soft red of Bordeaux's Right Bank: ripe plum, black cherry and a little chocolate, with a smooth, round feel.","s20":"Merlot is the grape behind Pomerol and Saint-Émilion, and it ripens earlier and drinks softer than Cabernet Sauvignon. Expect black plum and black cherry with a touch of cocoa, on a round, fleshy body that is easy to like from the first glass.","s45":"Merlot puts the soft heart into red Bordeaux, and on the Right Bank, in Pomerol and Saint-Émilion, it leads the blend. It tastes of ripe plum and black cherry, with a note of chocolate, and it feels round and smooth, with gentle tannin. A year or more in oak barrels adds cedar and vanilla. Bordeaux tends to be firmer and more savoury, while California is typically plusher and riper. Put it beside roast duck, mushrooms or a slow braise. The French blackbird, the merle, is as dark as the grape and said to be fond of it, and may well have given Merlot its name.","sale":"Merlot gives you the depth of a serious red without the hard edges: plum, black cherry and a smooth finish. It is the bottle for a table sharing duck, pork and mushrooms, where a firmer wine would get in the way."},"glass":{"lead":"Black plum and black cherry with cocoa and a touch of bay leaf, on a round, fleshy body with soft tannin.","notes":[{"family":"black fruit","notes":["black plum","black cherry","blackberry"]},{"family":"red fruit","notes":["red plum","red cherry"]},{"family":"dried fruit","notes":["fig","fruitcake"]},{"family":"herbal","notes":["bay leaf","mint"]},{"family":"oak","notes":["cedar","vanilla pod","milk chocolate","coffee"]},{"family":"earth","notes":["truffle","forest floor","tobacco leaf"]}],"ladder":[{"climate":"moderate","where":"Pomerol and Saint-Émilion, Hawke's Bay, the north east of Italy","notes":["red plum","red cherry","black cherry","bay leaf"]},{"climate":"warm","where":"Napa Valley, Sonoma and Chile's Central Valley","notes":["ripe black plum","blackberry jam","fruitcake"]}],"structure":"Dry. Acidity medium, tannin medium and soft, alcohol medium plus to high, body medium plus to full."},"worlds":{"contrast":{"old":{"where":"Bordeaux's Right Bank, Pomerol and Saint-Émilion; north east Italy","glass":"Right Bank wines tend to be savoury and structured: black plum and red cherry with truffle, forest floor and cedar, fresh acidity, fine tannin and alcohol short of high. North east Italy's are usually lighter and softer.","cellar":"Right Bank wines are mostly blends, with Cabernet Franc and often a little Cabernet Sauvignon, aged in French oak barrels, a share of them new."},"new":{"where":"California, Washington State, Chile, Hawke's Bay and Stellenbosch","glass":"Typically plusher and riper: black cherry, black plum and blackberry jam wrapped in mocha and vanilla, with softer acidity, rounder tannin, a fuller body and higher alcohol.","cellar":"Usually bottled under the grape's own name, alone or with a little Cabernet; picked riper and often aged in a larger share of new oak."},"why":"Bordeaux's mild maritime climate and its cold clay ripen Merlot steadily and keep its freshness and savour. Sunnier New World valleys give more sugar and softer acidity. Bordeaux labels the place, the New World the grape.","blur":"Washington State and Hawke's Bay make firm, fresh Merlot that can pass for Bordeaux, while Tuscany's coast and warm recent vintages on the Right Bank give riper, fuller wines.","call":"Truffle, cedar and fresh acidity with alcohol short of high lean Right Bank; jammy black plum, mocha and vanilla at higher alcohol lean New World."}},"service":{"temp":"59 to 63 degrees Fahrenheit, 15 to 17 degrees Celsius: enough warmth to open the aroma.","glass":"A Bordeaux glass, tall enough to gather the fruit.","decant":"Taste first: a firm young Right Bank wine can open with air; an old one comes off its sediment.","age":"Most are ready within five years; Pomerol and Saint-Émilion from good sites keep five to fifteen, a few far longer."},"food":{"with":[{"dish":"Roast duck","why":"plum fruit and soft tannin suit the rich meat"},{"dish":"Mushroom risotto, or anything with truffle","why":"truffle and forest floor in the wine echo the plate"},{"dish":"Roast pork loin","why":"round fruit and gentle tannin, nothing to fight"},{"dish":"Chicken and smoked sausage gumbo","why":"ripe fruit and soft tannin sit easily with the dark roux"}]},"labels":[{"term":"Pomerol, Saint-Émilion","means":"Right Bank Bordeaux place names: the label names the village, and Merlot usually leads the blend."},{"term":"Saint-Émilion Grand Cru","means":"An appellation with stricter rules, not a classed estate; Grand Cru Classé is the classification above it."},{"term":"Merlot, on an American label","means":"Federal law asks for at least 75 per cent of the named grape; the rest may be Cabernet or another."}],"guest":[{"q":"Is Merlot sweeter than Cabernet?","a":"No, both are dry. Merlot has softer tannin and riper, plummier fruit, so it feels rounder and can seem sweeter, but there is no sugar left in it."},{"q":"What is the difference between the Left Bank and the Right Bank?","a":"On the Left Bank, in the Médoc, Cabernet Sauvignon leads and the wines are firmer. On the Right Bank, in Pomerol and Saint-Émilion, Merlot leads and they are rounder and ready sooner."},{"q":"I was told not to order Merlot. Is that fair?","a":"It fell out of fashion, not out of quality. Pomerol and Saint-Émilion are built on Merlot, and a well made one has depth as well as softness."}],"compare":[{"slug":"cabernet-sauvignon","tell":"Cabernet Sauvignon shows cassis and firm, drying tannin; Merlot is softer and fleshier, with plum in place of the cassis."},{"slug":"malbec","tell":"Malbec is darker, with a magenta rim, violet and blueberry; Merlot is ruby and plummy, with none of that violet lift."},{"slug":"cabernet-franc","tell":"Cabernet Franc, a parent of Merlot, is leaner and brighter: raspberry, pencil shavings and green bell pepper where Merlot is rounder, softer and fleshier."}],"cards":[{"id":"gt-t1su8txp","front":"Merlot: where is it at home, and who are its parents?","back":"Bordeaux's Right Bank: Pomerol on clay, Saint-Émilion on limestone and clay. A study published in 2009 named its parents: Cabernet Franc and the almost lost Magdeleine Noire des Charentes."},{"id":"gt-wjz078kv","front":"Merlot blind: Right Bank Bordeaux or the New World, what tips it?","back":"Truffle, cedar, fresh acidity and alcohol short of high lean Right Bank. Jammy black plum, mocha, vanilla and higher alcohol lean California or Chile."},{"id":"gt-fnjlmvj2","front":"How does Merlot differ from Cabernet Sauvignon, in the vineyard and the glass?","back":"It ripens earlier and suits cold clay. In the glass it is plum where Cabernet is cassis, with softer tannin, a fleshier middle and less cedar."}],"questions":[{"id":"gt-864frqpd","q":"A blind red is deep ruby, with jammy black plum and black cherry, mocha and vanilla, soft acidity, round tannin and high alcohol. The table agrees it is Merlot. Which origin fits it?","opts":["Napa Valley, California","Friuli, Italy","Pomerol, Bordeaux","Saint-Émilion, Bordeaux"],"a":0,"exp":"Jammy fruit, mocha and vanilla from new oak, soft acidity and high alcohol point to a warm New World site. Pomerol and Saint-Émilion tend to show fresher acidity, truffle and cedar at lower alcohol.","tag":"worlds"},{"id":"gt-7g434kfj","q":"A guest enjoys the cedar of a Left Bank Bordeaux but finds it too firm, and asks for a red Bordeaux that is rounder and ready sooner. Which do you bring?","opts":["Saint-Julien","Saint-Estèphe","Pomerol","Pauillac"],"a":2,"exp":"Pomerol is on the Right Bank, where Merlot leads the blend and gives rounder, softer wines. Pauillac, Saint-Estèphe and Saint-Julien are Left Bank villages led by Cabernet Sauvignon."},{"id":"gt-45qt3sec","q":"A guest asks how Merlot is related to Cabernet Sauvignon. What do you tell her?","opts":["Merlot is a parent of Cabernet Sauvignon","They share a parent, Cabernet Franc","Cabernet Sauvignon is a parent of Merlot","They are unrelated grapes grown side by side"],"a":1,"exp":"Cabernet Franc is a parent of both. With Sauvignon Blanc it gave Cabernet Sauvignon, and with Magdeleine Noire des Charentes it gave Merlot, so neither is the other's parent."}],"sources":[{"title":"Bordeaux Wines: Merlot","url":"https://www.bordeaux.com/en/grape-varieties/merlot/"},{"title":"Bordeaux Wines: Pomerol, the appellation guide","url":"https://www.bordeaux.com/en/designations/saint-emilion-pomerol-fronsac/pomerol/"},{"title":"Australian Journal of Grape and Wine Research: Parentage of Merlot and related winegrape cultivars of southwestern France","url":"https://hal.inrae.fr/hal-02666593"},{"title":"Wine Grapes, entry on Merlot","url":""}]},
"nebbiolo": {"slug":"nebbiolo","name":"Nebbiolo","colour":"red","kind":"grape","status":"full","mode":"home","court":true,"aliases":[],"notIn":[],"say":"neb-bee-OH-loh","lines":{"s10":"Nebbiolo makes Barolo and Barbaresco, a pale red that smells of tar and roses and grips like a far darker wine.","s20":"Nebbiolo is the red grape of Barolo and Barbaresco, from the Langhe hills of Piedmont. It looks pale, but its sour cherry, rose petal and tar ride on high acidity and a tannin that wants years, or a braised dish.","s45":"Nebbiolo looks gentle and is not. It is the grape of Barolo and Barbaresco, from the Langhe hills around Alba in Piedmont. The glass is pale garnet, with sour cherry, rose petal and tar, but the tannin grips hard and the acidity is high. The law holds Barolo back for more than three years, and many cellars still age it in large old casks that add little taste of wood. Match it with braised beef, a mushroom risotto or anything under white truffle. Nebbia is the fog that fills the valleys at the October harvest, and the grape is thought to be named for it.","sale":"Nebbiolo smells as delicate as a rose and grips like a far darker wine, and with food that grip turns to pleasure. I would decant a Barbaresco now and pour it with the braised beef."},"glass":{"lead":"Sour cherry, rose petal and tar in a pale garnet glass, over high acidity and a firm, drying tannin.","notes":[{"family":"red fruit","notes":["sour cherry","red plum","cranberry"]},{"family":"dried fruit","notes":["dried cherry","dried strawberry"]},{"family":"floral","notes":["rose petal","violet"]},{"family":"earth","notes":["tar","leather"]},{"family":"spice","notes":["liquorice","anise"]},{"family":"age","notes":["truffle","dried mushroom"]}],"ladder":[{"climate":"cool","where":"Valtellina, Carema, and Gattinara and Ghemme in the north","notes":["sour cherry","cranberry","dried rose","iron"]},{"climate":"moderate","where":"Barolo, Barbaresco and Roero, around Alba","notes":["red plum","dried cherry","rose petal","tar"]}],"structure":"Dry. Acidity high, tannin high and drying, alcohol medium plus to high, body medium plus to full, and pale in colour for all that."},"worlds":{"home":{"home":"Piedmont, above all the Langhe hills around Alba, where Barolo and Barbaresco are Nebbiolo alone, aged for years, often in large old casks. Roero across the Tanaro is softer. In the north it is Spanna in Gattinara and Ghemme, and Chiavennasca on the terraces of Valtellina in Lombardy.","abroad":"Abroad it is rare. Australia grows it in the King Valley and the Adelaide Hills, and California and Baja California a little; these tend to be softer and fruitier, and some Baja vines so named are not Nebbiolo."}},"service":{"temp":"61 to 64 degrees Fahrenheit, 16 to 18 degrees Celsius, a cool room temperature.","glass":"A wide Burgundy bowl, which gathers the rose and tar perfume.","decant":"Consider it for a young Barolo after tasting a sample; decant an old one gently off its sediment.","age":"Barolo and Barbaresco ten to thirty years, and they need them; most Langhe Nebbiolo within five."},"food":{"with":[{"dish":"Beef braised in red wine","why":"slow cooked fat and protein meet the firm tannin"},{"dish":"Egg pasta with shaved white truffle","why":"the truffle meets the wine's own truffle and rose"},{"dish":"Mushroom risotto","why":"savoury depth beside dried cherry and tar"},{"dish":"Aged hard cheese","why":"salt and fat round off the grip"}]},"labels":[{"term":"Barolo","means":"Nebbiolo alone from the hills south west of Alba, aged at least 38 months, 18 of them in wood."},{"term":"Barbaresco","means":"Nebbiolo from lower, warmer hills north east of Alba, aged at least 26 months, 9 in wood."},{"term":"Langhe Nebbiolo","means":"Nebbiolo from the wider Langhe, released young: a lighter, earlier way into the grape."},{"term":"Spanna, Chiavennasca","means":"Nebbiolo's names in northern Piedmont and in Valtellina, Lombardy."}],"guest":[{"q":"It looks so light. Why does it feel so strong?","a":"Nebbiolo carries little of the pigment that makes a red look deep, so it is pale and turns orange at the rim early. The power is in its tannin and acidity, not its colour."},{"q":"What is the difference between Barolo and Barbaresco?","a":"The same grape from neighbouring hills. Barbaresco lies lower and a little warmer, north east of Alba, ages for a shorter time by law and is often open sooner. It is not a lesser Barolo."},{"q":"Is this young Barolo ready to drink tonight?","a":"It will still grip. A decanter lets the perfume open, and a rich, slow cooked dish makes the tannin feel gentler. Left ten years or more, it softens and truffle and leather come through."}],"compare":[{"slug":"pinot-noir","tell":"Pinot Noir is as pale but silky, with softer tannin and red cherry; Nebbiolo adds tar, higher acidity and a drying grip Pinot lacks."},{"slug":"sangiovese","tell":"Sangiovese is a little deeper, with tomato leaf and dried oregano and less tannin; Nebbiolo is paler, with tar and roses and a bigger grip."},{"slug":"xinomavro","tell":"Xinomavro shares the pale colour, high acidity and firm tannin, but brings sun dried tomato and black olive where Nebbiolo has tar and a fuller scent of roses."}],"cards":[{"id":"gt-ga8297e4","front":"Nebbiolo: where is it at home, and what are its other names?","back":"Piedmont: Barolo, Barbaresco and Roero around Alba, Gattinara and Ghemme in the north. It is Spanna in northern Piedmont and Chiavennasca in Valtellina, Lombardy."},{"id":"gt-svept7d7","front":"Nebbiolo blind: which pairing of colour and structure gives it away?","back":"Pale garnet, orange at the rim, over high tannin and high acidity, with tar and rose petal on the nose. It looks light and drinks powerful."},{"id":"gt-jwaia109","front":"Nebbiolo as Barolo and as Barbaresco: the minimum ageing of each?","back":"Barolo 38 months with 18 in wood, Riserva 62. Barbaresco 26 months with 9 in wood, Riserva 50. Counted from the November after the harvest."}],"questions":[{"id":"gt-92yhnst7","q":"A red poured blind is pale garnet with an orange rim, yet it grips hard: high tannin, high acidity, sour cherry, rose petal and tar. Which grape is it?","opts":["Pinot Noir","Gamay","Grenache","Nebbiolo"],"a":3,"exp":"A pale colour hiding high tannin and high acidity, with tar and roses, is Nebbiolo. Pinot Noir, the nearest wrong option, is as pale but silky, with softer tannin and no tar."},{"id":"gt-662dy8is","q":"A guest who loves Barolo asks for the same grape in a lighter, more perfumed wine from the Alpine terraces of Lombardy. Which wine do you bring?","opts":["Valtellina Superiore","Barbera d'Asti","Dolcetto d'Alba","Valpolicella Classico"],"a":0,"exp":"Valtellina Superiore is at least 90 per cent Nebbiolo, called Chiavennasca there: lighter and more perfumed than Barolo, and ready sooner. Barbera d'Asti, the nearest miss, is another grape, low in tannin."},{"id":"gt-a8lp0n22","q":"A guest asks why the Barbaresco on the list reached the market a year before the Barolo of the same vintage, though both are Nebbiolo. What is the reason?","opts":["It comes from younger vines by law","Its legal minimum ageing is shorter","It is aged in steel alone","It is softened with Barbera"],"a":1,"exp":"Barbaresco must age 26 months with 9 in wood, Barolo 38 months with 18, so Barbaresco is released sooner. Both are Nebbiolo alone, so softening with Barbera is the nearest wrong reason."}],"sources":[{"title":"Consorzio di Tutela Barolo Barbaresco Alba Langhe e Dogliani: Nebbiolo","url":"https://www.langhevini.it/en/?p=7790"},{"title":"Wine Companion: grape varieties and wine styles, Italian reds","url":"https://winecompanion.com.au/resources/varieties/italian-reds"},{"title":"Wine Selectors: Nebbiolo in Australia","url":"https://www.wineselectors.com.au/selector-magazine/wine/state-of-play-nebbiolo"},{"title":"The Oxford Companion to Wine","url":""}]},
"pinot-noir": {"slug":"pinot-noir","name":"Pinot Noir","colour":"red","kind":"grape","status":"full","mode":"contrast","court":true,"aliases":[],"notIn":[],"say":"PEE-noh NWAHR, in Germany SHPAYT-boor-goon-der","lines":{"s10":"Pinot Noir is the pale, silky red of Burgundy: red cherry, raspberry and a quiet note of forest floor, with barely any grip.","s20":"Pinot Noir is the red grape of Burgundy, thin skinned, so the wine is pale and silky where most reds are dark and firm. Expect red cherry and raspberry over forest floor, with bright acidity and a long, fine finish.","s45":"Pinot Noir has a thin skin, and nearly everything in the glass follows from that. Its home is Burgundy, on the limestone slope of the Côte d'Or. It is pale and silky, with red cherry, raspberry and wild strawberry, bright acidity and very little grip. It is aged in French oak barrels, and with time it turns towards mushroom and forest floor. Burgundy tends to be earthier, while California and Oregon are often riper and more openly fruity. Pour it with roast duck, salmon or mushrooms. Its name is said to come from the French for pine cone, the shape of its small, tight bunches.","sale":"Pinot Noir goes where other reds cannot: light enough for fish, with depth enough for duck. If half the table has ordered fish and the other half meat, this is the bottle that suits you all."},"glass":{"lead":"Red cherry, raspberry and wild strawberry over rose petal and forest floor, pale and silky, with bright acidity and gentle tannin.","notes":[{"family":"red fruit","notes":["red cherry","raspberry","wild strawberry","cranberry"]},{"family":"black fruit","notes":["black cherry","black plum"]},{"family":"floral","notes":["rose petal","violet"]},{"family":"earth","notes":["forest floor","wild mushroom","beetroot"]},{"family":"spice","notes":["clove","cinnamon","cola"]},{"family":"oak","notes":["vanilla pod","toast"]}],"ladder":[{"climate":"cool","where":"The Côte d'Or, the Willamette Valley, Germany and Tasmania","notes":["red cherry","raspberry","cranberry","forest floor"]},{"climate":"moderate","where":"Carneros, Sonoma and California's Central Coast","notes":["black cherry","black plum","ripe raspberry","cola"]}],"structure":"Dry. Acidity medium plus to high, tannin low to medium and silky, alcohol moderate in Burgundy and medium plus from warmer sites, body light to medium."},"worlds":{"contrast":{"old":{"where":"Burgundy's Côte d'Or above all; Germany as Spätburgunder, Alsace and Sancerre","glass":"Tends to be paler, earthier and more savoury: red cherry, raspberry and cranberry over forest floor and rose petal, with bright acidity, moderate alcohol and a firm, fine frame that asks for time.","cellar":"Often a cold soak, then whole bunches or destemmed fruit as each estate prefers; twelve to eighteen months in French oak, more of it new for the higher tiers."},"new":{"where":"Oregon, California's cool coast, New Zealand, Tasmania, Chile and South Africa","glass":"Typically riper and more openly fruity: black cherry, ripe raspberry and plum, often with cola and vanilla, softer acidity, a rounder body and higher alcohol. Oregon tends to sit between the two.","cellar":"Made much as in Burgundy, though often picked riper and given a larger share of new French oak; bottled under the grape's name."},"why":"Burgundy is cool and marginal, so its Pinot ripens only just and keeps high acidity and modest alcohol. Sunnier New World sites give riper, darker fruit. Burgundy labels the vineyard or village; the New World names the grape.","blur":"Oregon, Tasmania and the Sonoma Coast make lean, savoury wines that can pass for Burgundy, and warm recent vintages have made red Burgundy and German Spätburgunder riper and darker.","call":"Forest floor, tart red cherry and moderate alcohol lean Burgundy; black cherry, cola and vanilla with softer acidity and more alcohol lean New World."}},"service":{"temp":"54 to 59 degrees Fahrenheit, 12 to 15 degrees Celsius: lightly cool, never warm.","glass":"A wide Burgundy bowl, which gathers the perfume.","decant":"Usually unnecessary. Pour a fragile old bottle gently, and decant only to leave sediment behind.","age":"Most within five years; village and premier cru Burgundy five to fifteen, the grands crus twenty or more."},"food":{"with":[{"dish":"Roast duck","why":"bright acidity cuts the fat, cherry fruit meets the meat"},{"dish":"Salmon, grilled or roasted","why":"little tannin, so a red can sit with rich fish"},{"dish":"Wild mushrooms, or a mushroom risotto","why":"forest floor in the wine echoes the plate"},{"dish":"Blackened redfish","why":"gentle tannin and ripe fruit stand up to the char"}]},"labels":[{"term":"Bourgogne Rouge","means":"Red Burgundy at the regional level, made from Pinot Noir; Gamay sold as Burgundy is labelled Bourgogne Gamay."},{"term":"Spätburgunder","means":"The German name, late Burgundian; Pinot Nero in Italy, Blauburgunder in Austria and the Alto Adige."},{"term":"Premier Cru, Grand Cru","means":"Burgundy ranks the vineyard, not the estate: grand cru is the top tier, premier cru the next."},{"term":"Oregon Pinot Noir","means":"State law asks for at least 90 per cent of the named grape, where federal law asks 75."}],"guest":[{"q":"Why is this red so pale? Is it a thin wine?","a":"Pinot Noir has thin skins, so it gives less colour and less tannin than most red grapes. Pale does not mean weak: judge it by its perfume and its length."},{"q":"Is red Burgundy a Pinot Noir?","a":"Almost always, yes. Burgundy labels the village or the vineyard and leaves the grape off, but the red wines of the Côte d'Or are Pinot Noir."},{"q":"Why did my red wine arrive cool?","a":"A light red with little tannin is fresher and more fragrant a little below room temperature. Ten minutes in an ice bucket is care, not a mistake."}],"compare":[{"slug":"gamay","tell":"Gamay is lighter and more candied, often with a banana or bubblegum note; Pinot Noir is silkier, with forest floor beneath the fruit."},{"slug":"nebbiolo","tell":"Nebbiolo shares the pale colour but grips hard, with high tannin, tar and dried rose; Pinot Noir stays silky and gentle."},{"slug":"grenache","tell":"Grenache runs warmer and jammier, with strawberry jam, white pepper and higher alcohol; Pinot Noir is cooler in feel, with brighter acidity."}],"cards":[{"id":"gt-pjm1vxji","front":"Pinot Noir: where is it at home, and what gives it away?","back":"Burgundy's Côte d'Or. A pale, translucent ruby, silky tannin and red cherry and raspberry over forest floor: thin skins give little colour and little grip."},{"id":"gt-rmg2w1h8","front":"Pinot Noir blind: Burgundy or the New World, what tips it?","back":"Tart red cherry, forest floor and moderate alcohol lean Burgundy. Black cherry, cola, vanilla, softer acidity and higher alcohol lean California; Oregon often sits between the two."},{"id":"gt-s3i22g5m","front":"What is Pinot Noir called in Germany, in Italy and in Austria?","back":"Spätburgunder in Germany, Pinot Nero in Italy, and Blauburgunder in Austria and in the Alto Adige."}],"questions":[{"id":"gt-w0zdhjok","q":"A blind red is translucent ruby, with black cherry, ripe raspberry, cola and vanilla, medium acidity, silky tannin and medium plus alcohol. The table agrees it is Pinot Noir. Which origin fits it?","opts":["Sancerre, in the Loire Valley","The Ahr Valley, Germany","Gevrey-Chambertin, Burgundy","Russian River Valley, California"],"a":3,"exp":"Black cherry, cola, vanilla from new oak, acidity eased to medium and medium plus alcohol point to moderate California. Gevrey-Chambertin, the Ahr and Sancerre tend to be tarter and earthier, at lower alcohol.","tag":"worlds"},{"id":"gt-n7mc3gfj","q":"A guest who loves red Burgundy asks for the same grape from Germany. Which word on the label tells you the bottle holds it?","opts":["Spätburgunder","Lemberger","Dornfelder","Trollinger"],"a":0,"exp":"Spätburgunder, late Burgundian, is the German name for Pinot Noir. Lemberger is Blaufränkisch, and Dornfelder and Trollinger are other red grapes of Germany."},{"id":"gt-g8c4b9do","q":"A guest finds his glass of Pinot Noir cool to the touch and asks whether the bottle was stored wrongly. What do you tell him?","opts":["Cooling a red takes the tannin out of it","Every red should be poured at warm room temperature","A light red shows more perfume served lightly cool","Only an old, fragile red is ever served cool"],"a":2,"exp":"Light reds with little tannin are served at 54 to 59 degrees Fahrenheit, 12 to 15 degrees Celsius, which lifts fruit and acidity. Cooling removes no tannin, and a warm room flattens the wine."}],"sources":[{"title":"Bourgogne Wines: Pinot Noir, the prodigal child of the Bourgogne wine region","url":"https://www.bourgogne-wines.com/wine-and-terroir/our-grape-varietals-our-colors/pinot-noir/the-bourgogne-wine-region-birthplace-of-the-pinot-noir-varietal,2798,10603.html"},{"title":"Wines of Germany: Spätburgunder, Pinot Noir","url":"https://www.winesofgermany.com/our-wine/grape-varieties/grape-variety/94/pinot-noir"},{"title":"Willamette Valley Wineries Association: name protection and labelling standards","url":"https://www.willamettewines.com/about-the-valley/wine-origins/"},{"title":"The Oxford Companion to Wine, entry on Pinot Noir","url":""}]},
"sangiovese": {"slug":"sangiovese","name":"Sangiovese","colour":"red","kind":"grape","status":"full","mode":"home","court":true,"aliases":[],"notIn":[],"say":"san-joh-VEH-zeh","lines":{"s10":"Sangiovese is the red of Chianti and Brunello: sour cherry and tomato leaf on bright acidity and a dusty, savoury grip.","s20":"Sangiovese is Tuscany's red, the grape behind Chianti Classico, Brunello di Montalcino and Vino Nobile. It tastes of sour cherry, tomato leaf and dried oregano, with high acidity and a dusty tannin made for the table.","s45":"Sangiovese makes Chianti, Brunello and Vino Nobile, though the labels rarely say so. It is the red of Tuscany, grown on the hills from Florence south to Montalcino. In the glass it is sour cherry, tomato leaf and dried oregano, usually medium in body, with high acidity and a dusty, drying tannin. Brunello is Sangiovese alone, aged at least two years in oak, while Chianti Classico must be at least four fifths Sangiovese. It wants food: pasta in a meat ragù, a grilled steak or aged pecorino. Ciliegiolo, still grown in Tuscany, seems to be one parent, and an almost lost vine from near Naples the other.","sale":"Sangiovese makes a tomato sauce taste right: its sour cherry and lively acidity meet the tomato head on. With the pasta or the grilled steak, a Chianti Classico is the bottle I would open."},"glass":{"lead":"Sour cherry, tomato leaf and dried oregano on a medium body, with high acidity and a dusty, drying tannin.","notes":[{"family":"red fruit","notes":["sour cherry","red cherry","red plum","cranberry"]},{"family":"herbal","notes":["tomato leaf","dried oregano","tea leaf"]},{"family":"earth","notes":["leather","tobacco leaf"]},{"family":"dried fruit","notes":["dried cherry","dried fig"]},{"family":"oak","notes":["espresso","vanilla pod"]}],"ladder":[{"climate":"moderate","where":"The hills of Chianti Classico, Rufina and Montalcino","notes":["sour cherry","cranberry","tomato leaf","violet"]},{"climate":"warm","where":"The Maremma hills around Scansano, and McLaren Vale","notes":["black cherry","red plum","dried oregano"]}],"structure":"Dry. Acidity high, tannin medium plus to high and dusty, alcohol medium to medium plus, body medium to medium plus, fullest in Brunello."},"worlds":{"home":{"home":"Tuscany: the hills of Chianti Classico, Montalcino, where it is called Brunello, and Montepulciano, where it is Prugnolo Gentile, with the Maremma as Morellino. Brunello is Sangiovese alone; Chianti Classico needs at least 80 per cent. Romagna, Umbria and the Marche grow it too.","abroad":"Corsica calls it Nielluccio, the grape of Patrimonio. California, Washington and McLaren Vale in South Australia bottle a little; these tend to be riper and rounder, with less of the sour cherry snap."}},"service":{"temp":"59 to 63 degrees Fahrenheit, 15 to 17 degrees Celsius for Chianti; Brunello a degree or two warmer.","glass":"A Bordeaux glass, tall enough for the cherry and herb scent to gather.","decant":"Rarely for a young Chianti; consider it for a young Brunello after a taste, or an old one with sediment.","age":"Most Chianti within five years, Riserva longer; Brunello five to twenty."},"food":{"with":[{"dish":"Pasta in a slow cooked meat ragù","why":"acidity meets the tomato and cuts the fat"},{"dish":"Grilled T-bone steak, Florentine style","why":"salt, char and fat soften the dusty tannin"},{"dish":"Aged pecorino","why":"salty sheep's cheese beside sour cherry and grip"},{"dish":"Pizza with tomato and mozzarella","why":"the wine's acidity keeps pace with the tomato"}]},"labels":[{"term":"Chianti Classico","means":"The historic heart between Florence and Siena, its own DOCG under the black rooster: at least 80 per cent Sangiovese."},{"term":"Brunello di Montalcino","means":"Sangiovese alone from Montalcino, at least two years in oak and released years after the harvest."},{"term":"Vino Nobile di Montepulciano","means":"Sangiovese, there called Prugnolo Gentile, from the Tuscan town; nothing to do with the Montepulciano grape."},{"term":"Super Tuscan","means":"A trade name, not a law: Tuscan reds outside the old rules, often Sangiovese with Cabernet or Merlot."}],"guest":[{"q":"Is Chianti a grape?","a":"No, a place. Chianti and Chianti Classico are Tuscan zones, and the grape in both is mostly Sangiovese; Classico must hold at least 80 per cent."},{"q":"Why does this red taste so tart on its own?","a":"Sangiovese keeps high acidity. Alone it can feel sharp, but beside tomato, olive oil or a fatty cut the tartness reads as freshness, which is why Tuscany drinks it with food."},{"q":"What makes Brunello different from Chianti?","a":"Brunello is Sangiovese alone from the warmer hill of Montalcino, with at least two years in oak and a long wait before release: fuller, firmer and longer lived."}],"compare":[{"slug":"nebbiolo","tell":"Nebbiolo is paler, with tar and rose petal and a far bigger, more drying grip; Sangiovese is a little deeper, with tomato leaf and dried oregano."},{"slug":"tempranillo","tell":"Tempranillo from Rioja swaps tomato leaf for dill and coconut from American oak, with softer acidity; Sangiovese is tarter and more savoury."},{"slug":"montepulciano","tell":"Montepulciano is deeper in colour and darker in fruit, black cherry and black plum, with softer acidity; Sangiovese is paler and tarter, with tomato leaf."}],"cards":[{"id":"gt-00pa9sbu","front":"Sangiovese: its local names in Montalcino, Montepulciano, Scansano and Corsica?","back":"Brunello in Montalcino, Prugnolo Gentile in Montepulciano, Morellino in Scansano and Nielluccio in Corsica."},{"id":"gt-aaxl3iin","front":"Sangiovese blind: which two notes and which structure give it away?","back":"Sour cherry and tomato leaf, on high acidity and a dusty, firm tannin, with a medium body and often an orange rim early."},{"id":"gt-1hdxjnan","front":"Sangiovese: the minimum in Chianti Classico, Vino Nobile and Brunello?","back":"Chianti Classico at least 80 per cent, Vino Nobile di Montepulciano at least 70, Brunello di Montalcino 100 per cent."}],"questions":[{"id":"gt-dt6gxbu4","q":"A red poured blind is medium ruby with an orange rim: sour cherry, tomato leaf and dried oregano, high acidity and a dusty, firm tannin. Which grape is it?","opts":["Nebbiolo","Sangiovese","Tempranillo","Grenache"],"a":1,"exp":"Sour cherry and tomato leaf on high acidity and dusty tannin are Sangiovese. Nebbiolo, the nearest wrong option, would be paler, with tar and roses and a bigger grip."},{"id":"gt-m876c1hq","q":"A guest orders Vino Nobile di Montepulciano and asks whether it is made from the grape of the same name. Which grape leads it?","opts":["Montepulciano","Canaiolo","Sangiovese","Aglianico"],"a":2,"exp":"Vino Nobile is named for the Tuscan town and is at least 70 per cent Sangiovese, there called Prugnolo Gentile. The Montepulciano grape, the nearest wrong option, belongs to Abruzzo and the Adriatic side."},{"id":"gt-1miwxx71","q":"A table orders pasta in a long cooked tomato and meat ragù and asks for a red that will not taste flat beside the tomato. Which do you pour?","opts":["Barossa Shiraz","Amarone della Valpolicella","Napa Valley Merlot","Chianti Classico"],"a":3,"exp":"Tomato is high in acid, and a red with less acidity tastes flat beside it. Chianti Classico keeps Sangiovese's high acidity; the Merlot, the nearest wrong option, is softer and lower in acid."}],"sources":[{"title":"Vitis: the parentage of Sangiovese","url":"https://ojs.openagrar.de/index.php/VITIS/article/view/4221"},{"title":"Wine Australia: Sangiovese, stories of Australian wine","url":"https://www.wineaustralia.com/whats-happening/Stories-of-Australian-Wine/April-2017/Sangiovese"},{"title":"The SAGE Encyclopedia of Alcohol: Sangiovese","url":"https://sk.sagepub.com/ency/edvol/embed/the-sage-encyclopedia-of-alcohol-social-cultural-and-historical-perspectives/chpt/sangiovese"},{"title":"The Oxford Companion to Wine","url":""}]},
"syrah": {"slug":"syrah","name":"Syrah / Shiraz","colour":"red","kind":"grape","status":"full","mode":"contrast","court":true,"aliases":[],"notIn":[],"say":"see-RAH, in Australia shih-RAZ","lines":{"s10":"Syrah is the savoury red of the northern Rhône, blackberry with cracked pepper and smoked meat, and Australia calls it Shiraz.","s20":"Syrah is the red grape of the northern Rhône, and in Australia it goes by the name Shiraz. From the Rhône expect blackberry, black olive and cracked pepper, and from the Barossa a fuller, riper wine with dark chocolate and vanilla.","s45":"Syrah and Shiraz are one grape under two names. Its home is the northern Rhône, on the steep river slopes of Hermitage and Côte-Rôtie. It is deep purple and tastes of blackberry, blueberry and cracked black pepper, often with a savoury note of black olive or smoked meat. It is medium to full bodied, with firm tannin, and usually aged in oak. The Rhône tends to be peppery and savoury, while Australian Shiraz is typically riper and richer, with chocolate and vanilla. Reach for it with grilled lamb, venison or a peppered steak. Australia has grown it since 1832, and the Barossa still picks from vines planted in the 1840s.","sale":"Syrah brings blackberry with a savoury streak that few other reds have: cracked pepper, black olive and smoked meat. With the lamb or anything from the grill, I would open one from the northern Rhône."},"glass":{"lead":"Blackberry, blueberry and black plum cut with cracked black pepper, black olive and smoked meat, on a full, firm frame.","notes":[{"family":"black fruit","notes":["blackberry","black plum","black cherry"]},{"family":"blue fruit","notes":["blueberry","mulberry"]},{"family":"floral","notes":["violet","lavender"]},{"family":"spice","notes":["cracked black pepper","liquorice"]},{"family":"savoury","notes":["black olive","smoked meat","bacon fat"]},{"family":"oak","notes":["vanilla pod","dark chocolate","coffee"]}],"ladder":[{"climate":"moderate","where":"The northern Rhône, Hawke's Bay, Canberra and the Yarra Valley","notes":["blackberry","cracked black pepper","violet","black olive"]},{"climate":"warm","where":"Barossa Valley, McLaren Vale, Paso Robles and Stellenbosch","notes":["blackberry jam","dark chocolate","liquorice","eucalyptus"]}],"structure":"Dry. Acidity medium to medium plus, tannin medium plus to high, alcohol medium to high, body medium plus in the Rhône and full in the Barossa."},"worlds":{"contrast":{"old":{"where":"The northern Rhône: Hermitage, Côte-Rôtie, Cornas, Saint-Joseph and Crozes-Hermitage","glass":"Tends to be savoury and perfumed more than sweet fruited: blackberry and blueberry with cracked black pepper, violet, black olive and smoked meat, at medium to medium plus alcohol, with fresh acidity and firm tannin.","cellar":"Usually Syrah alone, though Côte-Rôtie may ferment up to 20 per cent Viognier with it; aged in French oak, often older or larger casks."},"new":{"where":"Barossa, McLaren Vale, Washington, California's Central Coast and Stellenbosch","glass":"Typically riper, fuller and sweeter fruited, above all Australian Shiraz: blackberry jam, blueberry and black plum with dark chocolate, liquorice and a lift of mint or eucalyptus, softer acidity, rounder tannin and higher alcohol. Vanilla from new oak often shows.","cellar":"Usually bottled under the grape's name, as Shiraz or Syrah; picked riper and often aged in new oak, in Australia long American and now often French."},"why":"The northern Rhône is near the cool limit for Syrah: slow ripening keeps pepper, acidity and moderate alcohol. Hotter, drier sites such as the Barossa ripen it fully, towards jam. The Rhône labels the place, the New World the grape.","blur":"Cool sites such as Hawke's Bay, the Yarra Valley and the Sonoma Coast, and the old vines of the Swartland, make peppery, savoury wines; warm Rhône years give riper ones.","call":"Black olive, smoked meat and pepper at moderate alcohol lean northern Rhône; chocolate, eucalyptus and vanilla on jammy fruit at higher alcohol lean Australia."}},"service":{"temp":"61 to 64 degrees Fahrenheit, 16 to 18 degrees Celsius: cool room temperature, never warm.","glass":"A tall Bordeaux glass, with room to swirl.","decant":"Consider it for a young, firm wine after tasting a sample; decant an old one off its sediment.","age":"Most Shiraz is ready on release; northern Rhône and old vine wines reward five to twenty years."},"food":{"with":[{"dish":"Grilled or roast lamb","why":"the Rhône's own match, pepper against sweet fat"},{"dish":"Venison and other game","why":"the wine's smoked meat note echoes the game"},{"dish":"Peppered steak","why":"cracked pepper on the plate and in the glass"},{"dish":"Barbecued brisket or short rib","why":"ripe Shiraz stands up to smoke and char"}]},"labels":[{"term":"Shiraz","means":"The same grape under its Australian name; on a label it often signals a riper, fuller style."},{"term":"Hermitage, Côte-Rôtie, Cornas","means":"Northern Rhône place names: the label names the hill or the village, and the grape is Syrah."},{"term":"GSM","means":"A blend of Grenache, Syrah or Shiraz and Mourvèdre, the southern Rhône recipe, common in Australia."},{"term":"Petite Sirah","means":"A different grape, also called Durif: an offspring of Syrah, darker and more tannic."}],"guest":[{"q":"Are Syrah and Shiraz the same grape?","a":"Yes, one grape. Shiraz is Australia's name for it, and on a label it usually promises a riper, fuller wine, where Syrah suggests a peppery, savoury one."},{"q":"Does Shiraz come from the city of Shiraz in Persia?","a":"It is a good story, but no. DNA work in the late 1990s found its parents to be two old grapes of south eastern France, Dureza and Mondeuse Blanche, so it began near the Rhône."},{"q":"Why does this wine smell of black pepper?","a":"Syrah makes the same aroma compound that gives peppercorns their scent. It shows most in wines from cooler sites and cooler years."}],"compare":[{"slug":"malbec","tell":"Malbec is plusher, with softer, rounder tannin and none of the savour; Syrah brings cracked pepper, black olive and smoked meat."},{"slug":"cabernet-sauvignon","tell":"Cabernet Sauvignon shows cassis, cedar and a leafy edge on a more drying tannin; Syrah has cracked pepper, black olive and smoked meat."},{"slug":"mourvedre","tell":"Mourvèdre is gamier and more rustic, with heavier tannin and a note of leather; Syrah is more polished and peppery, with violet."}],"cards":[{"id":"gt-p2dpo8cj","front":"Syrah: where is it at home, and who are its parents?","back":"The northern Rhône: Hermitage, Côte-Rôtie, Cornas, Saint-Joseph and Crozes-Hermitage. DNA work in the late 1990s found it a cross of Dureza and Mondeuse Blanche, two old grapes of south eastern France."},{"id":"gt-8ipv2uvf","front":"Syrah blind: northern Rhône or Australian Shiraz, what tips it?","back":"Black olive, smoked meat, violet and pepper at moderate alcohol lean northern Rhône. Jammy blackberry, chocolate, eucalyptus and vanilla at higher alcohol lean Australia."},{"id":"gt-opepv3yd","front":"Shiraz in Australia: when did it arrive, and which oak is traditional?","back":"In 1832 on most accounts, with vine cuttings brought from Europe. Barossa Shiraz was long aged in American oak, which gives vanilla and coconut; many cellars now use French oak."}],"questions":[{"id":"gt-n5g8vhpk","q":"A guest loved a Barossa Shiraz last week and asks for the same grape in a more peppery, savoury style, with less sweetness of fruit. Which bottle do you bring?","opts":["Châteauneuf-du-Pape","McLaren Vale Shiraz","Saint-Joseph","Mendoza Malbec"],"a":2,"exp":"Saint-Joseph is northern Rhône Syrah, typically peppery and savoury at moderate alcohol. McLaren Vale Shiraz is the same grape in the ripe style the guest already knows, and the other two are led by Grenache and Malbec.","tag":"worlds"},{"id":"gt-jdv5bebg","q":"A blind red is opaque purple, with blackberry, blueberry, violet, cracked black pepper and a note of smoked bacon, on firm tannin and fresh acidity. Which grape is it most likely to be?","opts":["Cabernet Sauvignon","Syrah","Malbec","Zinfandel"],"a":1,"exp":"Black pepper and smoked meat with violet on a firm frame point to Syrah. Malbec shares the violet but lacks the pepper and the meat, and Cabernet Sauvignon shows cassis and cedar."},{"id":"gt-bxbe2zgr","q":"A guest reads that the red Côte-Rôtie on the list holds a little white grape and asks which one. What do you tell her?","opts":["Viognier","Chardonnay","Roussanne","Marsanne"],"a":0,"exp":"Côte-Rôtie may ferment up to 20 per cent Viognier with its Syrah, for perfume. Marsanne and Roussanne are the white grapes allowed in red Hermitage, not in Côte-Rôtie."}],"sources":[{"title":"Wine Australia: Shiraz, stories of Australian wine","url":"https://www.wineaustralia.com/what-s-happening/stories-of-australian-wine/november-2016/shiraz-the-story-of-an-australian-legend"},{"title":"Inter Rhône: Côte-Rôtie, the appellation","url":"https://www.vins-rhone.com/en/cotes-du-rhone-cru-aoc-cote-rotie"},{"title":"Wine South Australia: our vines","url":"https://winesouthaustralia.com/wine-south-australia/our-vines"},{"title":"Wine Grapes, entry on Syrah","url":""}]},
"tempranillo": {"slug":"tempranillo","name":"Tempranillo","colour":"red","kind":"grape","status":"full","mode":"home","court":true,"aliases":[],"notIn":[],"say":"tem-prah-NEE-yoh","lines":{"s10":"Tempranillo is the heart of Rioja and Ribera del Duero: dried cherry, strawberry and leather, often wrapped in the vanilla of long cask age.","s20":"Tempranillo is Spain's signature red, the backbone of Rioja and, as Tinto Fino, of Ribera del Duero. It gives strawberry, dried cherry and leather at medium weight, and years in American oak add vanilla and coconut.","s45":"Tempranillo is Spain's own red, the grape of Rioja on the upper Ebro and, as Tinto Fino, of Ribera del Duero on the high plateau. It tastes of strawberry, red plum and dried cherry, with leather and tobacco, at medium weight with a fresh, fine grip. Then comes the barrel. Traditional Rioja spends years in American oak barrels, which add vanilla, coconut and dill, and the label says how long: Crianza, Reserva, Gran Reserva. Ribera is darker and firmer, more often in French oak. Serve it with roast lamb, chorizo or Manchego. Temprano means early, and the grape earns the name, ripening weeks before most of Spain's other reds.","sale":"Tempranillo has done its waiting for you: a Rioja Reserva ages at least three years, part in barrel and part in bottle, until the cherry softens into leather and tobacco. Pour it with the roast lamb, or for anyone who wants a mellow red tonight."},"glass":{"lead":"Strawberry, red plum and dried cherry with leather and tobacco, often under the vanilla and coconut of American oak, at medium weight.","notes":[{"family":"red fruit","notes":["strawberry","red cherry","red plum"]},{"family":"dried fruit","notes":["dried cherry","dried fig"]},{"family":"black fruit","notes":["blackberry","black cherry"]},{"family":"oak","notes":["vanilla pod","coconut","dill"]},{"family":"earth","notes":["leather","tobacco leaf","dried leaves"]}],"ladder":[{"climate":"moderate","where":"Rioja Alta and Rioja Alavesa, and Ribera del Duero's high plateau","notes":["strawberry","red cherry","red plum","black cherry"]},{"climate":"warm","where":"Toro, Rioja Oriental, La Mancha and the Alentejo","notes":["blackberry","black plum","dried fig"]}],"structure":"Dry. Acidity medium to medium plus, tannin medium plus and firmer in Ribera and Toro, alcohol medium plus, body medium to full."},"worlds":{"home":{"home":"Spain, above all Rioja, where it leads a blend with Garnacha, Graciano and Mazuelo and the law sets its ageing as Crianza, Reserva or Gran Reserva. Ribera del Duero and Toro make it darker and firmer as Tinto Fino and Tinta de Toro, and La Mancha softer as Cencibel.","abroad":"Across the border it is Tinta Roriz in the Douro, for Port and dry reds, and Aragonez in the Alentejo. Australia, southern Oregon and Texas tend to make it juicier, with ripe cherry and plum and softer tannin."}},"service":{"temp":"59 to 63 degrees Fahrenheit, 15 to 17 degrees Celsius, as for any medium red such as Rioja.","glass":"A tall Bordeaux glass, with room to swirl, for Rioja and Ribera alike.","decant":"Consider it for a young Ribera or Toro after a sample; decant an old Gran Reserva gently off its sediment.","age":"Crianza within about five years; a Gran Reserva arrives already aged and can keep for decades more."},"food":{"with":[{"dish":"Roast lamb","why":"the classic match in Rioja and Ribera alike"},{"dish":"Chorizo and grilled sausages","why":"smoky paprika meets the sweet vanilla of the cask"},{"dish":"Aged Manchego","why":"salt and nutty fat soften the grip"},{"dish":"Grilled ribeye","why":"charred fat suits a dark, firm Ribera"}]},"labels":[{"term":"Crianza, Reserva, Gran Reserva","means":"Rioja's ageing ladder for reds: at least two, three and five years, with one, one and two in barrel."},{"term":"Tinto Fino, Tinta del País","means":"Tempranillo's names in Ribera del Duero, where it grows darker and firmer."},{"term":"Tinta de Toro, Cencibel","means":"Tempranillo in Toro, dense and dark, and in La Mancha and Valdepeñas, softer and rounder."},{"term":"Tinta Roriz, Aragonez","means":"Tempranillo in Portugal: Tinta Roriz in the Douro, Aragonez in the Alentejo and Lisboa."},{"term":"Joven, Roble","means":"A young red with no oak, or with a few months in barrel, below Crianza."}],"guest":[{"q":"What is the difference between a Crianza and a Gran Reserva?","a":"Time. A Crianza has aged at least two years, one in barrel. A Gran Reserva has aged at least five, two in barrel and two in bottle, so it is mellower, with tobacco, leather and dried cherry."},{"q":"Why does this Rioja smell of coconut and dill?","a":"That is American oak, the traditional cask of Rioja's older houses. The grape gives the strawberry and cherry; the barrel gives the vanilla, coconut and dill."},{"q":"Is a Ribera del Duero the same grape as a Rioja?","a":"Yes, Tempranillo, called Tinto Fino there. Hot days and cold nights on the high plateau give thicker skins, so Ribera tends to be darker and firmer, with blackberry where Rioja has strawberry."}],"compare":[{"slug":"sangiovese","tell":"Sangiovese is tarter, with sour cherry, tomato leaf and dried oregano; Tempranillo is softer in acidity, with strawberry and, in Rioja, the dill and coconut of American oak."},{"slug":"grenache","tell":"Grenache is paler, softer in tannin and warmer in alcohol, with raspberry, kirsch and white pepper; Tempranillo is deeper and firmer, with tobacco and the vanilla of the cask."},{"slug":"graciano","tell":"Graciano, its partner in the Rioja blend, is deeper in colour and sharper in acidity, with violet and a peppery lift; Tempranillo is rounder, with strawberry and leather."}],"cards":[{"id":"gt-z1sur97z","front":"Tempranillo goes by other names. Which in Ribera, Toro, La Mancha and Portugal?","back":"Tinto Fino or Tinta del País in Ribera del Duero, Tinta de Toro in Toro, Cencibel in La Mancha, Tinta Roriz in the Douro and Aragonez in the Alentejo."},{"id":"gt-nbh758mb","front":"Tempranillo as red Rioja: the minimum ageing of Crianza, Reserva and Gran Reserva?","back":"Crianza two years, one in barrel. Reserva three, one in barrel and six months in bottle. Gran Reserva five, two in barrel and two in bottle."},{"id":"gt-21xosgk4","front":"Tempranillo: what does its name mean, and who are its parents?","back":"Temprano is Spanish for early: it ripens weeks before Garnacha. DNA work published in 2012 found its parents to be Albillo Mayor, a white grape, and Benedicto, a grape now almost lost."}],"questions":[{"id":"gt-4lzq33t1","q":"A red poured blind is garnet at the rim, with dried cherry, strawberry, leather and tobacco under a sweet layer of coconut and dill, medium acidity and medium plus tannin. Which grape and place fit?","opts":["Tempranillo, Rioja","Grenache, Châteauneuf-du-Pape","Sangiovese, Chianti Classico","Nebbiolo, Barolo"],"a":0,"exp":"Dill and coconut point to American oak, the traditional cask of Rioja, over Tempranillo's dried cherry and leather. Chianti Classico, the nearest miss, would be tarter, with tomato leaf and higher acidity."},{"id":"gt-mjjglyx3","q":"A guest who loves Rioja asks for the same grape on the Portuguese page of the list. Which names do you look for on the Douro and Alentejo reds?","opts":["Touriga Nacional or Jaen","Tinta Roriz or Aragonez","Baga or Alfrocheiro","Castelão or Trincadeira"],"a":1,"exp":"Tempranillo is Tinta Roriz in the Douro and Aragonez in the Alentejo. Touriga Nacional and Jaen, the nearest miss, are two other grapes: Jaen is Mencía."},{"id":"gt-3gp2k8z5","q":"A guest asks why the Ribera del Duero tastes darker and firmer than the Rioja beside it, though both are Tempranillo. What is the main reason?","opts":["The law makes it age far longer","It is fermented with whole bunches","The law makes it blend with Garnacha","Hot days and cold nights thicken the skins"],"a":3,"exp":"On Ribera's high plateau, hot days and cold nights give a thicker skinned grape with deeper colour and firmer tannin. Its legal ageing minimums match Rioja's barrel time, so longer ageing is the nearest wrong answer."}],"sources":[{"title":"Rioja Wine: Tempranillo, grape varieties of the designation","url":"https://riojawine.com/en/the-designation/grape-varieties/tempranillo/"},{"title":"University of La Rioja: Genetic origin of the grapevine cultivar Tempranillo","url":"https://investigacion.unirioja.es/documentos/5bbc5cdeb7506047d09b584b"},{"title":"Texas A&M AgriLife: Texas wine grape varieties","url":"https://aggie-horticulture.tamu.edu/wp-content/uploads/sites/13/2020/05/2015-Texas-Wine-Grape-Varieties.pdf"},{"title":"The Oxford Companion to Wine","url":""}]},
"zinfandel": {"slug":"zinfandel","name":"Zinfandel","colour":"red","kind":"grape","status":"full","mode":"contrast","court":true,"aliases":[],"notIn":[],"say":"ZIN-fan-del, in Italy pree-mee-TEE-voh","lines":{"s10":"Zinfandel is the red California made its own: brambly blackberry jam and black pepper with a raisin edge, and Italy calls it Primitivo.","s20":"Zinfandel came from Croatia, but it is at home in the old vineyards of California and, as Primitivo, in the heel of Italy. It tastes of blackberry jam, raspberry and black pepper, with moderate tannin and a warm finish.","s45":"Zinfandel reached California in the Gold Rush, and old, gnarled vines still grow in Lodi, Sonoma and the Sierra Foothills. It tastes of blackberry jam, raspberry and black pepper. Its bunches ripen unevenly, which often leaves a raisin edge. The colour is only medium and the tannin moderate, but the alcohol is high. American oak often adds vanilla and a hint of dill. In Puglia, where it is called Primitivo, it tends to be more savoury, with leather and tobacco. It belongs with barbecue: smoky ribs, a spiced sausage or a burger. DNA finally traced it home to Croatia, where old records call it Tribidrag.","sale":"Smoke and a sweet, sticky glaze want a red with ripe blackberry and a little pepper, and Zinfandel brings both. With the ribs or a spiced sausage, I would serve it a touch cool so the warmth stays in check."},"glass":{"lead":"Brambly blackberry jam and raspberry with black pepper and a raisin edge, on a full, warm body of only medium colour.","notes":[{"family":"black fruit","notes":["blackberry jam","black cherry","black plum"]},{"family":"red fruit","notes":["raspberry","red cherry"]},{"family":"dried fruit","notes":["raisin","dried fig","prune"]},{"family":"spice","notes":["black pepper","clove","cinnamon"]},{"family":"oak","notes":["vanilla pod","dill","coconut"]},{"family":"earth","notes":["tobacco leaf","leather"]}],"ladder":[{"climate":"moderate","where":"Gioia del Colle, on the limestone plateau of the Murge","notes":["raspberry","red cherry","black pepper"]},{"climate":"warm","where":"Manduria, Lodi, Amador, Paso Robles and Dry Creek Valley","notes":["blackberry jam","raisin","prune","dried fig"]}],"structure":"Dry. Acidity medium to medium plus, tannin medium to medium plus, alcohol high, body full."},"worlds":{"contrast":{"old":{"where":"Puglia, as Primitivo: Manduria on the Ionian coast and Gioia del Colle","glass":"Tends to be more savoury and rustic: black plum, black cherry and dried fig with leather and tobacco leaf, and alcohol often nearly as high as California's.","cellar":"Labelled Primitivo with its place. Old vines are often bush vines, and Manduria also makes a sweet red, Dolce Naturale, from late picked or dried grapes."},"new":{"where":"California: Lodi, Sonoma's Dry Creek Valley, Amador and Paso Robles","glass":"Typically riper and jammier: blackberry jam, raspberry and a raisin edge with black pepper, then vanilla and dill from American oak, and high alcohol.","cellar":"Bottled under the grape's own name, often from old head trained vines, some in field blends with Petite Sirah and Carignane; usually aged in oak, often American."},"why":"Both homes are hot, so alcohol runs high on either side. California tends to pick riper, for jammier fruit, and to age in American oak, for vanilla. Puglia's label names the place, California's the grape.","blur":"Some Puglian cellars pick very ripe and use new oak, for a plush Primitivo hard to tell from California, while some California cellars now pick earlier for fresher wines.","call":"Leather, tobacco leaf and a savoury, dry finish lean Puglia; blackberry jam with vanilla and dill up front leans California."}},"service":{"temp":"61 to 64 degrees Fahrenheit, 16 to 18 degrees Celsius; the cooler end keeps the alcohol in check.","glass":"A Bordeaux glass, filled no more than a third.","decant":"Usually unnecessary for a fruity young wine; taste a sample first, and decant an old bottle off its sediment.","age":"Most show well at three to eight years; a few old vine wines keep longer."},"food":{"with":[{"dish":"Barbecued pork ribs","why":"ripe fruit stands up to smoke and a sweet glaze"},{"dish":"Grilled spiced sausages","why":"black pepper in the wine meets the spice"},{"dish":"A burger off the grill","why":"char and fat suit the brambly fruit"},{"dish":"Orecchiette with a lamb ragù","why":"slow cooked meat for a ripe red"}]},"labels":[{"term":"Primitivo","means":"The Italian name of the same grape, at home in Puglia; it is named for ripening early."},{"term":"Primitivo di Manduria","means":"Dry Primitivo from the Ionian coast of Taranto and Brindisi; the sweet one is the Dolce Naturale."},{"term":"Old Vine","means":"A term with no legal definition in the United States, often taken to mean vines of fifty years or more."},{"term":"White Zinfandel","means":"An off dry pink made from the same grape, its juice taken off the skins early; not a separate grape."}],"guest":[{"q":"Is Zinfandel the sweet pink wine?","a":"That is White Zinfandel, an off dry pink made from the same grape. Red Zinfandel is dry and full bodied, with blackberry jam, black pepper and a warm finish."},{"q":"Is Primitivo the same as Zinfandel?","a":"Yes, one grape: DNA work at the University of California, Davis showed it, and traced both to Croatia. Primitivo tends to be the more savoury."},{"q":"Why is Zinfandel so strong?","a":"Its bunches ripen unevenly, so growers wait for the last berries while others turn to raisins, and all that sugar becomes alcohol."},{"q":"Is Zinfandel an American grape?","a":"By long history it is California's, but it came from Croatia, by way of a Vienna nursery and Long Island in the 1820s, and reached California in the Gold Rush."}],"compare":[{"slug":"grenache","tell":"Grenache is paler, with kirsch, strawberry and dried thyme; Zinfandel is darker and jammier, with blackberry, a raisin edge and black pepper."},{"slug":"syrah","tell":"Syrah is darker and more savoury, with smoked meat, black olive and firmer tannin; Zinfandel is jammier, with a raisin edge and only medium colour."},{"slug":"corvina","tell":"Corvina, as Amarone, shares the raisin edge but adds sour cherry and bitter almond; Zinfandel is brambly, with blackberry jam and black pepper."}],"cards":[{"id":"gt-qr72u2xn","front":"Zinfandel: its Italian and Croatian names, and where it began?","back":"Primitivo in Puglia; Tribidrag, also called Crljenak Kaštelanski, in Croatia, where it began. DNA work at the University of California, Davis showed all three are one grape."},{"id":"gt-c39w9z3j","front":"Zinfandel blind: Primitivo from Puglia or California, what tips it?","back":"Leather, tobacco leaf and a savoury, dry finish lean Puglia. Blackberry jam with vanilla and dill from American oak up front leans California. Alcohol runs high in both."},{"id":"gt-onsc4362","front":"Why does Zinfandel so often show a raisin edge and high alcohol?","back":"Its bunches ripen unevenly: some berries shrivel to raisins while others are still catching up, and the sugar of the ripest becomes alcohol."}],"questions":[{"id":"gt-ev5vz8zq","q":"Two glasses of the same grape are poured blind. One shows leather, tobacco leaf and dried fig with a savoury finish; the other blackberry jam, vanilla and dill. Which pair of origins fits, in that order?","opts":["Primitivo di Manduria, then Lodi Zinfandel","Gioia del Colle, then Manduria","Dry Creek Valley, then Paso Robles","Lodi Zinfandel, then Primitivo di Manduria"],"a":0,"exp":"Leather, tobacco and a savoury finish tend to mark Puglian Primitivo; blackberry jam with vanilla and dill from American oak tends to mark California. Two California sites, or two Puglian ones, would not split this way.","tag":"worlds"},{"id":"gt-xwa2ro6x","q":"A guest's friend in Bari recommended Primitivo, and your list holds none. Which bottle on the list is the same grape?","opts":["A Negroamaro from Salice Salentino","A Grenache from McLaren Vale","A Zinfandel from Dry Creek Valley","A Petite Sirah from Napa Valley"],"a":2,"exp":"Primitivo and Zinfandel are one grape, shown by DNA. Petite Sirah often grows beside Zinfandel in old California vineyards but is a separate grape, a child of Syrah, and Negroamaro is Puglia's other red."},{"id":"gt-mvkco7qg","q":"A blind red of only medium colour shows blackberry jam, raspberry and a raisin edge, with black pepper and a warm, high alcohol finish. Which grape fits?","opts":["Syrah","Petite Sirah","Grenache","Zinfandel"],"a":3,"exp":"Jammy, brambly fruit with a raisin edge and high alcohol at only medium colour is Zinfandel. Grenache would be paler, with kirsch and dried thyme; Syrah and Petite Sirah would be darker."}],"sources":[{"title":"Zinfandel Advocates and Producers: the history of Zinfandel","url":"https://zinfandel.org/history/"},{"title":"Wine Folly: Zinfandel, the grape","url":"https://winefolly.com/grapes/zinfandel/"},{"title":"Decanter: America's old vines and their wines","url":"https://www.decanter.com/wine/usa/national-treasures-americas-old-vines-and-their-dynamic-and-delicious-wines/"},{"title":"Wine Grapes, entry on Tribidrag","url":""}]},
"assyrtiko": {"slug":"assyrtiko","name":"Assyrtiko","colour":"white","kind":"grape","status":"full","mode":"home","aliases":[],"notIn":[],"say":"ah-SEER-tee-koh","lines":{"s10":"Assyrtiko is the white of Santorini: lemon, sea salt and wet stone, bone dry, with acidity that makes your mouth water.","s20":"Assyrtiko comes from the volcanic island of Santorini, where the vines are coiled into baskets on the ground to hide from the wind. The wine is bone dry and tastes of lemon, sea salt and crushed rock, with a sharp, mouthwatering finish.","s45":"Assyrtiko is a white grape grown on what is left of a volcano: the hot, dry Greek island of Santorini. The vines stand in ash and pumice, each coiled into a basket on the ground against the wind. The wine tastes of lemon, underripe peach and sea salt, with a stony edge. It is bone dry, and its acidity stays piercing even in that heat. Most sees only steel, and a riper, oaked style is called Nykteri. Assyrtiko is made for oysters, grilled octopus or a whole fish with lemon. The vines grow on their own roots, because the pest that ruined Europe's vineyards cannot live in the ash.","sale":"Assyrtiko is the nearest thing to squeezing a lemon over the plate and adding a pinch of sea salt. Have it with the Gulf oysters tonight, raw or chargrilled, and you will not reach for the lemon."},"glass":{"lead":"Lemon, underripe peach and quince over sea salt and crushed volcanic rock, bone dry, with piercing acidity on a sturdy frame.","notes":[{"family":"citrus","notes":["lemon zest","lemon pith","lime"]},{"family":"stone fruit","notes":["underripe peach","white peach"]},{"family":"orchard fruit","notes":["quince","green apple"]},{"family":"mineral","notes":["sea salt","crushed volcanic rock","struck flint"]},{"family":"age","notes":["beeswax","honey"]}],"ladder":[{"climate":"moderate","where":"Mainland Greece: Macedonia, Thrace and the high Peloponnese","notes":["lemon zest","green apple","white peach"]},{"climate":"warm","where":"Santorini, a hot, dry, windswept island of volcanic ash","notes":["lemon pith","underripe peach","sea salt","struck flint"]}],"structure":"Bone dry. Acidity high and piercing, alcohol medium plus to high, body medium and sturdy; mostly unoaked."},"worlds":{"home":{"home":"Santorini, a hot, dry, windswept island in the Aegean. The vines grow on their own roots in volcanic ash and pumice, woven into low baskets called kouloura against the wind. Dry Santorini is at least 85 per cent Assyrtiko, mostly made in steel; Nykteri is the riper, oaked style, and Vinsanto the sweet wine of sun dried grapes.","abroad":"Across mainland Greece and on other islands it keeps its crispness, with more open fruit and a less dense build. Beyond Greece it is a rarity: Australia's Clare Valley has grown a little since the 2010s."}},"service":{"temp":"45 to 50 degrees Fahrenheit, 7 to 10 degrees Celsius; an oaked Nykteri or an old bottle a little warmer.","glass":"A white wine glass; a wider bowl for Nykteri or a bottle with some age.","decant":"Usually unnecessary. A young, tight Santorini opens with a few minutes of air in the glass.","age":"Three to eight years for dry Santorini, longer than a hot island suggests; Vinsanto far longer."},"food":{"with":[{"dish":"Gulf oysters, raw or chargrilled","why":"the wine's lemon and salt season the oyster"},{"dish":"Grilled octopus with olive oil","why":"piercing acidity against char and oil"},{"dish":"Whole grilled sea bass or bream","why":"it does the work of a squeezed lemon"},{"dish":"Fried calamari or whitebait","why":"acidity cuts through the frying"},{"dish":"Feta with tomato and capers","why":"salt meets salt, and acidity matches the tomato"}]},"labels":[{"term":"Santorini","means":"The island's protected name: a dry white of at least 85 per cent Assyrtiko, with Athiri and Aidani."},{"term":"Nykteri","means":"A riper dry Santorini aged at least three months in oak; the name recalls the old night pressing."},{"term":"Vinsanto","means":"Santorini's sweet wine of sun dried grapes, at least 51 per cent Assyrtiko, cask aged and unfortified; one word."},{"term":"Kouloura","means":"The low basket into which each Santorini vine is woven, sheltering the grapes from wind, sand and sun."}],"guest":[{"q":"How can a wine from such a hot island taste so sharp?","a":"Assyrtiko is the rare white grape that holds its acidity in a hot, dry climate. The island adds the rest: sea wind, old vines and poor volcanic ash keep the wine taut and salty."},{"q":"I usually drink Chablis. Will I like this?","a":"I think so. It has the same lean, stony feel with no oak in the way, but it is saltier and sharper, with more weight behind it."},{"q":"Is Vinsanto the same as the Italian Vin Santo?","a":"No. Vinsanto, one word, is Santorini's sweet wine, from Assyrtiko and Aidani dried in the sun. Vin Santo, two words, is Tuscany's, from other grapes dried indoors. Santorini's is never fortified."}],"compare":[{"slug":"chardonnay","tell":"Chablis, which is Chardonnay, is softer at the edges and less salty, with green apple and chalk; Assyrtiko is sharper and sturdier, with lemon pith and sea salt."},{"slug":"riesling","tell":"Dry Riesling is more perfumed, with lime, jasmine and, with age, petrol on a lighter body; Assyrtiko shows stone and salt in their place, with more alcohol and weight."},{"slug":"albarino","tell":"Albariño is lighter and more perfumed, with white peach and apricot; Assyrtiko is firmer and more austere, with less scent and more salt."}],"cards":[{"id":"gt-ateepldo","front":"Assyrtiko blind: what parts it from Chablis and from dry Riesling?","back":"Salt and stone on a sturdier, stronger frame with piercing acidity. Chablis is softer at the edges and less salty; dry Riesling is lighter and more perfumed, with lime and jasmine and no salt."},{"id":"gt-9k2wkn94","front":"Name Santorini's three wines of Assyrtiko, and what sets each apart.","back":"Dry Santorini, at least 85 per cent Assyrtiko, mostly unoaked. Nykteri, riper, with at least three months in oak. Vinsanto, sweet, from sun dried grapes, at least 51 per cent Assyrtiko, aged two years or more in cask."},{"id":"gt-7nmnavvp","front":"Why do Santorini's Assyrtiko vines grow on their own roots?","back":"Phylloxera cannot live in the island's volcanic ash and pumice, which hold no clay, so the vines were never grafted. Many are very old."}],"questions":[{"id":"gt-n2v3hjsp","q":"A blind white is pale and star bright, bone dry and unoaked: lemon pith, underripe peach, sea salt and crushed rock, with piercing acidity yet real alcohol and weight. Which grape and origin fit it?","opts":["Albariño, Rías Baixas","Chardonnay, Chablis","Riesling, Clare Valley","Assyrtiko, Santorini"],"a":3,"exp":"Piercing acidity with salt and stone on a sturdy, fairly strong frame is Assyrtiko from Santorini. Chablis is softer at the edges and less salty, at lower alcohol."},{"id":"gt-fh8alvqz","q":"A guest orders a glass of Vinsanto from Santorini after dinner and asks how it becomes sweet. What do you tell her?","opts":["The grapes freeze on the vine and are pressed frozen","Grape spirit is added to stop the fermentation","The grapes are dried in the sun before pressing","Noble rot shrivels the grapes on the vine"],"a":2,"exp":"Vinsanto is made from late picked grapes, mostly Assyrtiko, laid in the sun for a week or two and then aged in cask. It is never fortified, so no spirit is added."},{"id":"gt-5ngehhs5","q":"A guest has read that Santorini's vines are woven into baskets that lie on the ground, and asks what the baskets are for. Which answer is right?","opts":["To keep phylloxera away from the roots","To shelter the grapes from wind, sand and sun","To let machines pick the fruit","To hold rainwater around the trunk"],"a":1,"exp":"The kouloura shelters the fruit from the meltemi wind, blown sand and sun. Phylloxera is kept away by the volcanic ash itself, which holds no clay, and not by the basket."}],"sources":[{"title":"Wines of Greece: Assyrtiko","url":"https://winesofgreece.org/varieties/assyrtiko/"},{"title":"Wines of Greece: PDO Santorini","url":"https://winesofgreece.org/pdo/pdo-santorini/"},{"title":"Wine Australia: Clare Valley, the regional notes","url":"https://www.wineaustralia.com/getmedia/b57ce557-52c5-4c85-8b72-bb156e12d09b/AWD_ClareValley_Notes.pdf"},{"title":"The Oxford Companion to Wine, entries on Assyrtiko and Santorini","url":""}]},
"furmint": {"slug":"furmint","name":"Furmint","colour":"white","kind":"grape","status":"full","mode":"home","aliases":[],"notIn":[],"say":"FOOR-mint","lines":{"s10":"Furmint gives Hungary two wines from one fiery grape: a smoky, sharp dry white and the golden, honeyed sweet Tokaji.","s20":"Furmint is the grape of Tokaj, in the volcanic hills of north east Hungary, and dry it tastes of green apple, quince and smoke, with fierce acidity. When a mould called noble rot shrivels the grapes it becomes Tokaji Aszú, a sweet wine of apricot and honey.","s45":"Furmint is all fire. It is a white grape of fierce acidity, at home in Tokaj, in the volcanic hills of north east Hungary. Dry, it tastes of green apple, quince and lime, with a smoky edge and a bright, biting finish. In the autumn mist a mould called noble rot shrivels the grapes, and those berries are steeped in wine to make sweet Tokaji Aszú. It tastes of apricot, honey and saffron. The dry wine suits pork, river fish or a paprika stew, and Aszú wants blue cheese or foie gras. Tokaj ranked its vineyards more than a century before Bordeaux ranked its châteaux.","sale":"Furmint is for anyone who loves dry Riesling or Chablis and wants something new: green apple, smoke and a fierce, clean finish. Tonight I would set a dry one beside the blackened redfish, and keep a small glass of sweet Aszú for the cheese."},"glass":{"lead":"Green apple, quince and lime with a smoky, flinty edge and fierce acidity when dry; apricot, honey and saffron as sweet Tokaji Aszú.","notes":[{"family":"orchard fruit","notes":["green apple","pear skin","quince"]},{"family":"citrus","notes":["lime","lemon zest"]},{"family":"mineral","notes":["struck flint","smoke","salt"]},{"family":"dried fruit","notes":["dried apricot","candied orange peel"]},{"family":"honeyed","notes":["acacia honey","saffron","orange marmalade"]}],"ladder":[{"climate":"cool","where":"Tokaj's cooler north west arm, Somló and Slovenia's Štajerska","notes":["green apple","lime","struck flint"]},{"climate":"moderate","where":"Tokaj's warm south facing slopes and Burgenland around Rust","notes":["ripe pear","quince","apricot"]}],"structure":"Dry to lusciously sweet. Acidity high and fiery at every sweetness, alcohol medium plus when dry and lower in Aszú, body medium, richer when sweet."},"worlds":{"home":{"home":"Tokaj, in the volcanic hills of north east Hungary, where autumn mist off the Bodrog and the Tisza brings noble rot. Dry Furmint is raised in steel or in oak casks on its lees. For Aszú the shrivelled berries are picked one by one, steeped in must or wine and aged at least eighteen months in oak.","abroad":"Elsewhere in Hungary it is dry and smoky on the volcanic hill of Somló. Slovenia grows it as Šipon for taut dry whites, Austria's Burgenland keeps a little around Rust, and Slovakia has its own strip of Tokaj."}},"service":{"temp":"Dry Furmint at 50 to 55 degrees Fahrenheit, 10 to 13 degrees Celsius; Aszú about the same.","glass":"A white wine glass for dry Furmint; a small glass and a small pour for Aszú.","decant":"Usually unnecessary; a young single vineyard dry Furmint can open with a short decant.","age":"Dry Furmint keeps a decade; Aszú far longer, for decades in a sound cellar."},"food":{"with":[{"dish":"Blackened redfish","why":"smoke and sharp acidity meet the paprika crust"},{"dish":"Roast pork or pork belly","why":"fierce acidity cuts through the fat"},{"dish":"Chicken in paprika and cream","why":"the cooking of its own country"},{"dish":"Foie gras, with Aszú","why":"sweetness and acidity against richness"},{"dish":"Blue cheese, with Aszú","why":"sweet against salt, with acidity to finish clean"}]},"labels":[{"term":"Tokaji Aszú","means":"Sweet wine of berries shrivelled by noble rot and steeped in wine; at least 120 grams of sugar a litre."},{"term":"Puttonyos","means":"Once the hods of aszú berries added to a cask; today a sugar band, five or six."},{"term":"Szamorodni","means":"Whole bunches pressed unsorted, as they come: száraz is dry and nutty, édes is sweet."},{"term":"Dűlő","means":"A named single vineyard of Tokaj; the sites were ranked early in the eighteenth century."},{"term":"Šipon","means":"The Slovenian name for Furmint, on taut dry whites from Štajerska."}],"guest":[{"q":"I thought Tokaji was a sweet wine. Is this one dry?","a":"Yes. The same grape makes both, and Tokaj's growers now bottle dry Furmint as well, often from a single vineyard; if the label says Aszú or Szamorodni édes, it is sweet."},{"q":"What does five puttonyos mean on the label?","a":"It is a measure of sweetness. Five puttonyos means at least 120 grams of sugar a litre and six at least 150; the word once counted the hods of shrivelled berries added to each cask."},{"q":"Will the sweet one be heavy or cloying?","a":"No. Furmint's acidity is so high that even a very sweet Aszú finishes fresh. A small glass is enough, and it sits well beside blue cheese."},{"q":"Is Tokaji the same as Tokay Pinot Gris?","a":"No. Alsace once called its Pinot Gris Tokay, and Friuli had a Tocai, but neither had anything to do with Tokaj. Both names have been retired, and Tokaji is the wine of Tokaj, led by Furmint."}],"compare":[{"slug":"riesling","tell":"Dry Riesling is more perfumed, with lime and jasmine and, in age, petrol; Furmint is broader and smokier, with quince and no petrol."},{"slug":"chenin-blanc","tell":"Chenin Blanc shows quince too, but with honey, lanolin and wet wool; Furmint shows smoke and flint in their place, with a fiercer finish."},{"slug":"assyrtiko","tell":"Assyrtiko is saltier and more austere, with lemon pith and little smoke; Furmint is rounder in fruit, with green apple, quince and a smoky edge."}],"cards":[{"id":"gt-9op9g9en","front":"Furmint: where is home, and which two styles does it make there?","back":"Tokaj, in the volcanic hills of north east Hungary. Dry Furmint, smoky and sharp, with green apple and quince; and sweet Tokaji Aszú, from berries shrivelled by noble rot, with apricot, honey and saffron."},{"id":"gt-ne473kbu","front":"Tokaji Aszú: what do five and six puttonyos mean today?","back":"Sugar bands, not a count of hods: five means at least 120 grams of sugar a litre, six at least 150. Three and four were abolished with the 2013 vintage."},{"id":"gt-dty2314d","front":"Dry Furmint blind: what parts it from Riesling and from Chenin Blanc?","back":"Fierce acidity with smoke and flint and no petrol. Dry Riesling is more perfumed, with lime and jasmine; Chenin Blanc shows lanolin and wet wool where Furmint shows smoke."}],"questions":[{"id":"gt-5ql8touq","q":"A blind white is pale lemon and bone dry: green apple, pear skin and quince, a smoky, flinty edge and fierce acidity, with no petrol, no wet wool and no white pepper. Which grape and origin fit it?","opts":["Furmint, Tokaj","Grüner Veltliner, Wachau","Chenin Blanc, Vouvray","Riesling, Mosel"],"a":0,"exp":"Fierce acidity with smoke and flint, and neither petrol nor wet wool, points to dry Furmint. Riesling would be more perfumed, with petrol in age, Chenin Blanc would show lanolin, and Grüner Veltliner white pepper."},{"id":"gt-0ez2yzgk","q":"A guest orders a glass of Tokaji Aszú with the cheese and asks how the grapes for it are gathered. What do you tell him?","opts":["Whole bunches are cut and dried on straw","Ripe bunches are picked by machine at night","Shrivelled berries are picked one by one","Frozen bunches are picked before a winter dawn"],"a":2,"exp":"Aszú berries, shrivelled by noble rot, are picked singly over several passes and then steeped in must or wine. Drying whole bunches on straw makes a straw wine, which Aszú is not."},{"id":"gt-qacnsyz3","q":"A guest wants a bone dry white and points to four Tokaji labels on the list. Which one is the dry wine?","opts":["Tokaji Eszencia","Tokaji Furmint száraz","Tokaji Szamorodni édes","Tokaji Aszú 5 puttonyos"],"a":1,"exp":"Száraz is Hungarian for dry. Aszú and Eszencia are sweet wines from berries shrivelled by noble rot, and édes means sweet, so a Szamorodni édes is sweet as well."}],"sources":[{"title":"Magyar Bor, the wine body of Hungary: Furmint","url":"https://bor.hu/en/furmint/"},{"title":"Magyar Bor, the wine body of Hungary: the Tokaj wine district","url":"https://bor.hu/en/tokaji-wine-district/"},{"title":"Wine Grapes, entry on Furmint","url":""},{"title":"The Oxford Companion to Wine, entries on Furmint and Tokaj","url":""}]},
"semillon": {"slug":"semillon","name":"Sémillon","colour":"white","kind":"grape","status":"full","mode":"contrast","aliases":[],"notIn":[],"say":"seh-mee-YOHN, in Australia SEM-uh-lon","lines":{"s10":"Sémillon is the waxy, lemony grape behind golden Sauternes, and in Australia it makes a lean white that ages to toast and honey.","s20":"Sémillon is Bordeaux's golden grape, the heart of sweet Sauternes and the body in dry white Bordeaux. It tastes of lemon, green apple and beeswax, and when it is made sweet or aged for years it turns to honey, apricot and toast.","s45":"Sémillon leads two lives. In Bordeaux it is the golden grape of Sauternes, where a mould called noble rot shrivels the berries and leaves a sweet wine of honey, apricot and saffron. It is also the body in dry white Bordeaux, beside Sauvignon Blanc. Young and dry it tastes of lemon and beeswax, soft and rounded. Bordeaux often raises it in barrel, while Australia's Hunter Valley picks it early, uses no oak and waits. Pour the dry wines with shellfish or roast chicken, and Sauternes with blue cheese. After ten years a Hunter wine tastes of toast and honey, and it has never touched a barrel.","sale":"Sémillon makes both a dry white of lemon and beeswax and the golden sweetness of Sauternes. A dry one suits the crab or the roast chicken, and Sauternes the blue cheese."},"glass":{"lead":"Lemon, beeswax and lanolin when young and dry, turning to honey, apricot and toast with age or noble rot.","notes":[{"family":"citrus","notes":["lemon","lime"]},{"family":"orchard fruit","notes":["green apple","pear"]},{"family":"stone fruit","notes":["apricot","ripe peach"]},{"family":"honeyed","notes":["honey","beeswax","lanolin","saffron"]},{"family":"age","notes":["toast","roasted nuts","orange marmalade"]},{"family":"oak","notes":["vanilla pod","crème brûlée"]}],"ladder":[{"climate":"moderate","where":"Graves, Pessac-Léognan and Margaret River, made dry","notes":["lemon","beeswax","lanolin"]},{"climate":"warm","where":"The Hunter Valley, picked early to stay lean; the Barossa, riper","notes":["lime","green apple","ripe peach"]}],"structure":"Dry to lusciously sweet. Acidity medium in Bordeaux and high in young Hunter wines, alcohol low in the Hunter to high in Sauternes, body light to full."},"worlds":{"contrast":{"old":{"where":"Bordeaux: Sauternes and Barsac for sweet, Graves and Pessac-Léognan for dry","glass":"Tends to be broad and waxy: lemon, beeswax and lanolin with gentle acidity, usually blended with Sauvignon Blanc. As Sauternes it is lusciously sweet, with honey, apricot and saffron on a full body.","cellar":"Usually blended with Sauvignon Blanc and a little Muscadelle; fermented and aged in barrel, some of it new, for the classed dry whites and for Sauternes."},"new":{"where":"Australia's Hunter Valley, Barossa and Margaret River; Franschhoek in South Africa","glass":"Hunter Valley wines are typically light and bone dry when young, with lime, green apple and searing acidity at low alcohol; with a decade in bottle they turn to toast and honey. The Barossa tends to be riper and fuller.","cellar":"In the Hunter it is picked early, fermented cold in steel and bottled young with no oak; Margaret River blends it with Sauvignon Blanc."},"why":"Bordeaux's misty autumns bring noble rot, and its tradition is the blend and the barrel. The Hunter is warm and humid, so growers pick early for acidity and let the bottle, not oak, do the ageing.","blur":"Margaret River blends it with Sauvignon Blanc as Bordeaux does, the Riverina makes sweet wines from noble rot, and everyday white Bordeaux is often fresh and unoaked.","call":"Wax, lanolin and vanilla from barrels, in a blend, lean Bordeaux; lime, searing acidity and low alcohol, or toast with no oak, lean Hunter Valley."}},"service":{"temp":"Dry wines at 45 to 50 degrees Fahrenheit, 7 to 10 degrees Celsius, barrel aged ones warmer; Sauternes colder.","glass":"A white wine glass for dry wines; a small glass for Sauternes, poured two to three ounces.","decant":"Usually unnecessary; an old bottle of either style is simply poured with care.","age":"Hunter Valley wines keep ten to twenty years or more, Sauternes for decades; most dry blends within five."},"food":{"with":[{"dish":"Crab, scallops or lobster","why":"a waxy, rounded white for sweet shellfish"},{"dish":"Roast chicken with lemon","why":"body to match the bird, lemon to echo"},{"dish":"Roquefort or other blue cheese","why":"with Sauternes: sweetness against salt"},{"dish":"Foie gras or chicken liver pâté","why":"with Sauternes: sweetness and acidity cut the fat"}]},"labels":[{"term":"Sauternes, Barsac","means":"Sweet white Bordeaux from grapes shrivelled by noble rot; Sémillon leads the blend."},{"term":"Graves, Pessac-Léognan","means":"Dry white Bordeaux: Sémillon blended with Sauvignon Blanc, often fermented in barrel."},{"term":"Hunter Valley Semillon","means":"Australia's lean, unoaked, low alcohol style, built to age in bottle; spelt there without the accent."},{"term":"Semillon Sauvignon Blanc","means":"A dry blend in the Bordeaux manner, made above all in Margaret River."}],"guest":[{"q":"Is Sauternes made from Sémillon?","a":"Mostly, yes. Sémillon leads the blend, with Sauvignon Blanc and sometimes a little Muscadelle. Its thin skins welcome noble rot, the mould that shrivels the grapes and concentrates their sugar."},{"q":"Why does this old Australian Semillon taste toasty if it was never in oak?","a":"That is bottle age. Hunter Valley Semillon is picked early and made in steel, and after years in the bottle it develops toast and honey of its own accord."},{"q":"What does Sémillon add to white Bordeaux?","a":"Body and texture. Sauvignon Blanc brings the sharp, grassy lift, and Sémillon rounds it out with a waxy feel and the capacity to age."}],"compare":[{"slug":"sauvignon-blanc","tell":"Sauvignon Blanc is greener and louder, with cut grass, gooseberry and sharper acidity; Sémillon is broader and waxier, with lemon and lanolin."},{"slug":"chenin-blanc","tell":"Chenin Blanc is waxy too, but higher in acidity, with quince and baked apple; Sémillon is softer, with lemon, and toast as it ages."},{"slug":"riesling","tell":"Aged Riesling brings petrol and lime where aged Hunter Valley Sémillon brings toast and honey, and Riesling is the more perfumed when young."}],"cards":[{"id":"gt-sm00dh98","front":"Sémillon: why is it the grape of Sauternes?","back":"Its thin skins and tight bunches welcome noble rot, which shrivels the berries and concentrates sugar. It leads the blend, with Sauvignon Blanc and a little Muscadelle."},{"id":"gt-n1wvsqyr","front":"Sémillon blind: Bordeaux or the Hunter Valley, what tips it?","back":"Wax, lanolin and vanilla from barrels, in a blend with Sauvignon Blanc, lean Bordeaux. Lime, searing acidity and low alcohol, or toast with no oak at all, lean Hunter Valley."},{"id":"gt-1k4y4ldu","front":"Hunter Valley Sémillon: how is it made, and what does age bring?","back":"Picked early at low sugar, fermented cold in steel and bottled young with no oak. After five to ten years in bottle it gains toast and honey."}],"questions":[{"id":"gt-izxzbjof","q":"A blind white is ten years old, deep lemon, bone dry and light, at low alcohol, with lime, toast and honey, high acidity and no vanilla at all. Which origin fits it?","opts":["Sauternes, Bordeaux","Napa Valley, California","Pessac-Léognan, Bordeaux","Hunter Valley, Australia"],"a":3,"exp":"Toast and honey with no barrel note, high acidity and low alcohol in an aged dry white is Hunter Valley Sémillon. Pessac-Léognan would be fuller, with vanilla from barrels, and Sauternes is sweet.","tag":"worlds"},{"id":"gt-025ppuu7","q":"A guest finishing dinner asks for the classic sweet wine of Bordeaux to go with Roquefort. Which do you pour?","opts":["Moscato d'Asti","Sauternes","Vouvray moelleux","Tokaji Aszú"],"a":1,"exp":"Sauternes is the sweet white of Bordeaux, led by Sémillon touched by noble rot, and a classic partner for Roquefort. Vouvray moelleux is Loire Chenin Blanc, and Tokaji Aszú is Hungarian."},{"id":"gt-1195fp04","q":"A dry white Bordeaux blends two main grapes. The Sauvignon Blanc brings sharp, grassy lift. What does the Sémillon bring?","opts":["Body, a waxy texture and the capacity to age","Deep colour and firm tannin","High acidity and a light, lean frame","A perfume of lychee and rose petal"],"a":0,"exp":"Sémillon gives white Bordeaux its body, its waxy texture and much of its capacity to age. High acidity and a lean frame are what the Sauvignon Blanc supplies."}],"sources":[{"title":"Bordeaux Wines: Sémillon","url":"https://www.bordeaux.com/en/grape-varieties/semillon/"},{"title":"Sauternes Barsac: the wines","url":"https://www.sauternes-barsac.com/en/the-wines/"},{"title":"Wine Australia: Semillon","url":"https://www.wineaustralia.com/market-insights/regions-and-varieties/semillon"},{"title":"Wine Australia: Hunter Valley","url":"https://www.wineaustralia.com/market-insights/regions-and-varieties/new-south-wales-wines/hunter-valley"}]},
"melon-de-bourgogne": {"slug":"melon-de-bourgogne","name":"Melon de Bourgogne","colour":"white","kind":"grape","status":"full","mode":"home","aliases":[],"notIn":[],"say":"muh-LOHN duh boor-GON-yuh, the wine moos-kah-DAY","lines":{"s10":"Melon de Bourgogne makes Muscadet, the pale, bone dry, faintly salty white that the Loire pours with its oysters.","s20":"Melon de Bourgogne is the grape of Muscadet, grown where the Loire meets the Atlantic. It is light and bone dry, with green apple, lemon and a bready note from a winter resting on its yeast.","s45":"Melon de Bourgogne has one job, which is Muscadet, made where the Loire meets the Atlantic near Nantes. The grape itself is quiet: green apple, lemon and a little white peach. The wine is pale, light and bone dry, with brisk acidity and a salty finish. Its character comes from resting through the winter on its lees, the spent yeast, which adds a bready note and sometimes a faint prickle. Pour it with oysters, mussels or fried fish. For all its name, it left Burgundy long ago, and it shares both its parents with Chardonnay.","sale":"Melon de Bourgogne makes Muscadet, the wine for oysters: light, salty and bone dry. I would pour it with the raw bar, or with anything fried."},"glass":{"lead":"Green apple, lemon and a little white peach, quiet and pale, with bread dough, oyster shell and a salty finish.","notes":[{"family":"orchard fruit","notes":["green apple","crisp pear"]},{"family":"citrus","notes":["lemon","lemon zest","lime"]},{"family":"mineral","notes":["oyster shell","sea salt","crushed stone"]},{"family":"lees and cellar","notes":["bread dough","fresh yeast"]}],"ladder":[{"climate":"cool","where":"The Pays Nantais in most years, beside the Atlantic","notes":["green apple","lemon zest","oyster shell"]},{"climate":"moderate","where":"The same vineyards in a warm, ripe year","notes":["ripe pear","white peach"]}],"structure":"Bone dry. Acidity high, alcohol low to moderate, body light; often a faint prickle of gas from bottling straight off the lees."},"worlds":{"home":{"home":"The Pays Nantais, where the Loire meets the Atlantic, above all Muscadet Sèvre et Maine. It is fermented cool with no new oak. A wine labelled sur lie spends one winter on its lees and is bottled straight from them. The crus communaux, such as Clisson, Gorges and Le Pallet, stay on theirs seventeen months to two years or more.","abroad":"It rarely leaves home. In California it long passed for Pinot Blanc, until vine scientists put the name right, and a little is bottled under its own name in Oregon's Willamette Valley, in the same lean style."}},"service":{"temp":"45 to 50 degrees Fahrenheit, 7 to 10 degrees Celsius, cold from the ice bucket.","glass":"A white wine glass, small enough to keep the wine cold.","decant":"Usually unnecessary; pour it cold and keep the bottle on ice.","age":"Enjoy most bottles young; sur lie wines hold a few years, and the crus communaux keep far longer."},"food":{"with":[{"dish":"Gulf oysters on the half shell","why":"salt meets salt, and the acidity is the lemon"},{"dish":"Mussels steamed in white wine","why":"a light, briny wine for a briny pot"},{"dish":"Fried fish or calamari","why":"high acidity cuts through the frying"},{"dish":"White fish in beurre blanc","why":"acidity lifts the butter sauce of Nantes"}]},"labels":[{"term":"Muscadet","means":"The wine made from Melon de Bourgogne around Nantes; bone dry, and no relation of Muscat."},{"term":"Sur lie","means":"Kept one winter on its lees, the spent yeast, and bottled straight from them: more texture, a bready note."},{"term":"Sèvre et Maine","means":"The heart of Muscadet, named for two small rivers south east of Nantes."},{"term":"Clisson, Gorges, Le Pallet","means":"Crus communaux: named villages whose wines rest on their lees at least seventeen months, two years in Clisson and Gorges."}],"guest":[{"q":"Is Muscadet the same as Muscat, and is it sweet?","a":"No, on both counts. Muscadet is made from Melon de Bourgogne and is bone dry, light and salty. Muscat is a different, perfumed grape that is often made sweet."},{"q":"What does sur lie on the label mean?","a":"The wine spent the winter on its lees, the spent yeast left after fermentation, and was bottled straight from them. That gives a rounder texture, a bready note and sometimes a faint prickle."},{"q":"Why is it called Melon of Burgundy if it comes from the Loire?","a":"It began in Burgundy, where it was once widely grown, and it has the same parents as Chardonnay. Today it is grown mostly around Nantes."}],"compare":[{"slug":"chardonnay","tell":"Chablis, which is Chardonnay, has more flesh and body, with chalk and green apple; Muscadet is lighter and saltier, with a bready note from its lees."},{"slug":"albarino","tell":"Albariño is salty too, but more aromatic, with white peach and apricot; Melon de Bourgogne is quieter, lighter and more bready."},{"slug":"pinot-grigio","tell":"Italian Pinot Grigio is as light and quiet, but lacks the bread dough and the salty finish that mark Melon de Bourgogne."}],"cards":[{"id":"gt-e7f9mvbb","front":"Melon de Bourgogne: which wine does it make, and where?","back":"Muscadet, in the Pays Nantais, where the Loire meets the Atlantic near Nantes. Muscadet Sèvre et Maine is the heart of it."},{"id":"gt-02vn1ts7","front":"Melon de Bourgogne as Muscadet: what does sur lie on the label promise?","back":"One winter on the lees, the spent yeast, and bottling straight from them in the year after harvest: more texture, a bready note, sometimes a faint prickle."},{"id":"gt-jgdut17f","front":"Melon de Bourgogne: who are its parents, and which grapes share them?","back":"Pinot and Gouais Blanc, the same pair that gave Chardonnay, Gamay and Aligoté. It began in Burgundy and found its home on the Loire."}],"questions":[{"id":"gt-t9pjquli","q":"A blind white is water pale, light and bone dry, with green apple, lemon, bread dough and a salty finish, and a faint prickle on the tongue. Which wine is it most likely to be?","opts":["Muscadet sur lie","Pinot Grigio from the Veneto","Chablis","Albariño from Rías Baixas"],"a":0,"exp":"A quiet, light, salty white with bread dough and a faint prickle is Muscadet aged on its lees. Chablis has more flesh and chalk, and Albariño is more aromatic, with white peach."},{"id":"gt-rx3ly522","q":"A guest sees Muscadet on the list and says she does not care for sweet Muscat. What do you tell her?","opts":["Muscadet is a dry style of the Muscat grape","Muscadet is Muscat grown in the Loire and lightly sweet","Muscadet is bone dry and made from Melon de Bourgogne","Muscadet is a blend of Muscat and Sauvignon Blanc"],"a":2,"exp":"Muscadet is a bone dry Loire white made from Melon de Bourgogne. The name resembles Muscat, but the grape is a different one and the wine is not sweet."},{"id":"gt-ftwgrfcs","q":"A guest wants a Muscadet with more depth, one that has rested on its lees for two years. Which names on the label do you look for?","opts":["Vouvray or Montlouis","Sancerre or Menetou-Salon","Chinon or Bourgueil","Clisson or Gorges"],"a":3,"exp":"Clisson and Gorges are crus communaux of Muscadet Sèvre et Maine, whose wines rest on their lees for about two years. Vouvray and Montlouis are Chenin Blanc, and Sancerre is Sauvignon Blanc."}],"sources":[{"title":"Vins de Nantes: the grape varieties of the Nantes vineyards","url":"https://www.muscadet.fr/en/the-nantes-vineyards/our-grape-varieties/"},{"title":"Institut national de l'origine et de la qualité: cahier des charges, Muscadet Sèvre et Maine","url":"https://extranet.inao.gouv.fr/fichier/PNOCDC-Muscadet-SevreEtMaine.pdf"},{"title":"University of California, Davis, Foundation Plant Services: Melon","url":"https://ngr.ucdavis.edu/fgrdetails.cfm?varietyid=956&showmore=yes"},{"title":"Willamette Valley Wineries Association: grape varieties","url":"https://www.willamettewines.com/trade-resources/grape-varieties/"}]},
"vermentino": {"slug":"vermentino","name":"Vermentino","colour":"white","kind":"grape","status":"full","mode":"home","aliases":["Rolle"],"notIn":[],"say":"vair-men-TEE-noh, in Provence ROLL","lines":{"s10":"Vermentino is the white of the Mediterranean coast: lime, green apple and wild fennel, with a salty, faintly bitter almond finish.","s20":"Vermentino is grown by the sea, from Sardinia and Corsica to Liguria and Provence. It is dry and crisp, with lime, green apple and wild fennel, and it finishes on a pinch of salt and a twist of bitter almond.","s45":"Vermentino lives by the sea. Its home is Sardinia, above all the granite hills of Gallura, and it follows the coast through Corsica, Liguria and Tuscany to Provence, where it is called Rolle. It tastes of lime, green apple and white peach, with a scent of wild fennel. It is dry and medium bodied, and it finishes on salt and a touch of bitter almond. Cellars keep it in steel and sell it young, with no oak. Open it with grilled fish, shellfish or anything with pesto. In Liguria the same grape, with freckled skins, goes by the name Pigato.","sale":"Vermentino brings the sea air to the table: lime, wild fennel and a salty snap. It belongs beside the grilled fish, or anything dressed in pesto."},"glass":{"lead":"Lime, green apple and white peach with wild fennel, crisp and medium bodied, finishing salty with a twist of bitter almond.","notes":[{"family":"citrus","notes":["lime","lemon zest","grapefruit pith"]},{"family":"orchard fruit","notes":["green apple","green pear"]},{"family":"stone fruit","notes":["white peach","nectarine"]},{"family":"herbal","notes":["wild fennel","thyme","rosemary"]},{"family":"nutty","notes":["green almond","bitter almond"]},{"family":"mineral","notes":["sea salt","wet stone"]}],"ladder":[{"climate":"moderate","where":"Liguria and the Colli di Luni, cooled by sea breezes","notes":["lime","green apple","wild fennel"]},{"climate":"warm","where":"Sardinia, Corsica, the Tuscan Maremma, Provence and McLaren Vale","notes":["white peach","ripe pear","grapefruit pith","bitter almond"]}],"structure":"Dry. Acidity medium to medium plus, alcohol medium, body medium, with a salty, faintly bitter finish."},"worlds":{"home":{"home":"Sardinia first, where Vermentino di Gallura on windy granite is the island's only DOCG, then Corsica, Liguria, the Tuscan coast, Provence and the Languedoc. Nearly all of it is fermented cool in steel and bottled young, sometimes rested on its lees for weight: crisp, salty and scented with wild fennel.","abroad":"Australia has taken to it, in McLaren Vale, the Riverland and the King Valley, and California bottles a little. Both keep to the home style: lime and grapefruit, firm acidity and no oak, a touch riper from warm sites."}},"service":{"temp":"45 to 50 degrees Fahrenheit, 7 to 10 degrees Celsius: cold, but not so cold that the scent closes.","glass":"A white wine glass, tapered to hold the scent.","decant":"Unnecessary; open it cold and pour.","age":"Enjoy it young, within two or three years; a Gallura Superiore holds a little longer."},"food":{"with":[{"dish":"Grilled whole fish with lemon and olive oil","why":"its acidity and salt act like the lemon"},{"dish":"Gulf oysters or raw shellfish","why":"a crisp, salty white mirrors the brine"},{"dish":"Pasta with pesto","why":"the Ligurian match: basil meets the wine's fennel"},{"dish":"Fried calamari or small fried fish","why":"crisp acidity cuts through the frying"}]},"labels":[{"term":"Vermentino di Gallura","means":"Sardinia's only DOCG: at least 95 per cent Vermentino from the granite north east; Superiore is fuller."},{"term":"Rolle","means":"The grape's name in Provence and the Languedoc; a little also goes into Provence rosé."},{"term":"Pigato","means":"The same grape by DNA on Liguria's western coast, bottled under its own name; fuller and more savoury."},{"term":"Vermentinu","means":"The Corsican name, on the whites of Patrimonio, Ajaccio and the island's other appellations."}],"guest":[{"q":"Is Rolle the same as Vermentino?","a":"Yes. Rolle is what Provence and the Languedoc call it, Vermentinu is the Corsican name, and Liguria's Pigato is the same grape too. The wine is dry, crisp and a little salty wherever it grows."},{"q":"I like Sauvignon Blanc. Will I like this?","a":"I think so. It is as crisp and dry, but quieter: lime and green apple in place of cut grass and passion fruit, with a salty, slightly bitter almond finish."},{"q":"Why does it taste salty?","a":"Nobody can say for certain. The vines grow in sea wind on poor, stony ground, and the wine has a briny edge that sits well with fish and shellfish."}],"compare":[{"slug":"albarino","tell":"Albariño has more peach and apricot, higher acidity and no almond; Vermentino smells of wild fennel and ends on a twist of bitter almond."},{"slug":"sauvignon-blanc","tell":"Sauvignon Blanc is louder and greener, with cut grass, gooseberry and passion fruit; Vermentino is quieter, saltier and ends on bitter almond."},{"slug":"pinot-grigio","tell":"Italian Pinot Grigio is more neutral, with pear and little scent; Vermentino has more lime, wild fennel and salt."}],"cards":[{"id":"gt-fffuxbnc","front":"Vermentino: what is Sardinia's only DOCG, and what is the ground?","back":"Vermentino di Gallura, raised to DOCG in 1996: at least 95 per cent Vermentino from the windy granite of the island's north east."},{"id":"gt-voazv9b2","front":"Vermentino: what is it called in Provence, in Corsica and in Liguria?","back":"Rolle in Provence and the Languedoc, Vermentinu in Corsica, and Pigato on Liguria's western coast, where it is grown and bottled as a vine of its own."},{"id":"gt-cga65408","front":"Vermentino blind: which finish parts it from Sauvignon Blanc and Albariño?","back":"A salty edge and a twist of bitter almond, on a quieter scent of lime, green apple and wild fennel, with medium body and no oak."}],"questions":[{"id":"gt-b9tk4oyo","q":"A blind white is pale straw, dry, crisp and unoaked, with lime, green apple and wild fennel, a salty edge and a faint bitter almond finish. Which grape is it most likely to be?","opts":["Viognier","Vermentino","Albariño","Sauvignon Blanc"],"a":1,"exp":"A quiet scent of lime and wild fennel, a salty edge and a bitter almond finish point to Vermentino. Albariño shows more peach and apricot and higher acidity, with no almond."},{"id":"gt-agekik8x","q":"A guest in Nice is poured a white Bellet made from Rolle and asks what that grape is called in Italy. What do you answer?","opts":["Cortese","Garganega","Trebbiano","Vermentino"],"a":3,"exp":"Rolle is the Provençal name of Vermentino and the main white grape of Bellet, above Nice. Trebbiano grows in Provence too, as Ugni Blanc, but it is a different grape."},{"id":"gt-d6js1gi7","q":"A guest asks for the Sardinian white of the island's top legal rank to go with her grilled fish. Which wine do you bring?","opts":["Cannonau di Sardegna","Vernaccia di Oristano","Vermentino di Gallura","Vermentino di Sardegna"],"a":2,"exp":"Vermentino di Gallura, from the granite north east, is Sardinia's only DOCG. Vermentino di Sardegna is the lighter DOC made across the whole island."}],"sources":[{"title":"Wine Australia: Six alternative white varieties you can try","url":"https://www.australianwine.com/experience/articles/six-alternative-white-varieties-you-can-try"},{"title":"Discover California Wines: Meet California Vermentino","url":"https://discovercaliforniawines.com/blog/meet-california-vermentino-crisp-bright-and-easy-to-enjoy/"},{"title":"National Institute of Origin and Quality: Bellet, the appellation's rule, cahier des charges","url":"https://extranet.inao.gouv.fr/fichier/AOC-SOMM49-01122011-1-CDC-Bellet.pdf"},{"title":"Wine Grapes: Vermentino","url":""}]},
"verdejo": {"slug":"verdejo","name":"Verdejo","colour":"white","kind":"grape","status":"full","mode":"home","aliases":[],"notIn":[],"say":"vair-DEH-hoh","lines":{"s10":"Verdejo is the bright, fennel scented white of Rueda, on the high plateau of Castile, with lime, grapefruit and a gently bitter finish.","s20":"Verdejo is the white grape of Rueda, on the high plateau of Castile, where it is picked at night to keep it fresh. It tastes of lime, grapefruit and fennel, dry and brisk, and it ends on a faint, pleasing bitterness.","s45":"Verdejo is picked in the dark. It is the white grape of Rueda, on the high, stony plateau north west of Madrid, and the cold night air keeps its scent fresh. It tastes of lime, grapefruit and white peach, with fennel, cut grass and a faintly bitter finish. It is dry and medium bodied, with brisk acidity. Most is made cold in steel and sold young, and a few richer bottles are raised in barrel. It suits grilled fish, asparagus or goat's cheese. For centuries Rueda made a golden, nutty wine from it, aged like Sherry, and the fresh style in your glass is the newcomer.","sale":"Verdejo does what Sauvignon Blanc does and adds fennel and a gently bitter twist. Try it with the asparagus, or with a salad of goat's cheese."},"glass":{"lead":"Lime, grapefruit and white peach with fennel, bay leaf and cut grass, dry and brisk, closing on a faint note of bitter almond.","notes":[{"family":"citrus","notes":["lime","pink grapefruit","lemon zest"]},{"family":"stone fruit","notes":["white peach","nectarine"]},{"family":"herbal","notes":["fennel","bay leaf","cut grass"]},{"family":"nutty","notes":["bitter almond","toasted hazelnut"]}],"ladder":[{"climate":"moderate","where":"Rueda's high plateau, with hot days and cold nights","notes":["lime","pink grapefruit","fennel","cut grass"]},{"climate":"warm","where":"La Mancha, on the warm central plain","notes":["ripe peach","melon rind","cut grass"]}],"structure":"Dry. Acidity high, alcohol moderate, body medium, with a slightly oily feel and a faint bitterness on the finish; rarely any new oak."},"worlds":{"home":{"home":"Rueda, on the high, dry plateau of Castilla y León, where the vines stand on stony gravel and summer nights are cold. The grapes are picked at night and made cold in steel, because the juice browns quickly in heat and air. A bottle that names Verdejo is at least 85 per cent the grape.","abroad":"It has barely left Spain. La Mancha grows it for fresh, grassy whites, and beyond Spain it is a rarity."}},"service":{"temp":"45 to 50 degrees Fahrenheit, 7 to 10 degrees Celsius: well chilled.","glass":"A white wine glass.","decant":"Usually unnecessary; open it cold and pour.","age":"Young, within a year or two; barrel fermented and old vine bottles keep a few years more."},"food":{"with":[{"dish":"Shrimp remoulade","why":"lime and fennel stand up to the mustard"},{"dish":"Grilled white fish with olive oil","why":"brisk acidity and a clean, bitter finish"},{"dish":"Asparagus","why":"its own green, fennel note welcomes a difficult vegetable"},{"dish":"Goat's cheese salad","why":"high acidity matches the tang of the cheese"},{"dish":"Fried calamari","why":"acidity cuts through the frying"}]},"labels":[{"term":"Rueda","means":"The denomination in Castilla y León; a bottle that names Verdejo is at least 85 per cent the grape."},{"term":"Sobre lías","means":"On the lees: a fuller Verdejo kept on its spent yeast."},{"term":"Fermentado en barrica","means":"Barrel fermented: a richer, rounder style that can age."},{"term":"Gran Vino de Rueda","means":"A mark for wines from vineyards over 30 years old, cropped low."},{"term":"Dorado","means":"Rueda's historic golden wine, aged in air until nutty, in the manner of Sherry."}],"guest":[{"q":"Is this like Sauvignon Blanc?","a":"Close. It is as crisp and as green, but where Sauvignon Blanc is all gooseberry and cut grass, Verdejo adds fennel, bay leaf and a faint bitter finish, with a little more body."},{"q":"Why does it finish slightly bitter?","a":"That is the grape's signature, a gentle bitterness like almond skin or fennel. It is meant to be there, and it is part of why the wine sits so well with food."},{"q":"Is Verdejo the same as Verdelho?","a":"No. Verdelho is a different grape, at home on the island of Madeira. Verdejo belongs to Rueda, on the high plateau north west of Madrid."}],"compare":[{"slug":"sauvignon-blanc","tell":"Sauvignon Blanc shows gooseberry and cut grass more loudly, with sharper acidity; Verdejo is a little rounder, with fennel, bay leaf and a bitter finish."},{"slug":"albarino","tell":"Albariño is white peach and apricot with a salty finish and no bite of bay leaf; Verdejo is greener, with lime and fennel."},{"slug":"macabeo","tell":"Macabeo, Rueda's Viura, is quieter and more neutral, with green apple and almond; Verdejo is louder and greener, and more bitter on the finish."}],"cards":[{"id":"gt-yk9vhapo","front":"Verdejo: where is home, and why is it harvested at night?","back":"Rueda, on the high plateau of Castilla y León. The juice browns and loses its scent in heat and air, so the fruit is picked in the cold of night and made cold in steel."},{"id":"gt-kg5x05v1","front":"Verdejo blind: what parts it from Sauvignon Blanc?","back":"Fennel, bay leaf and a faint bitter almond finish on a slightly rounder, oilier body. Sauvignon Blanc shows gooseberry and cut grass more loudly, with sharper acidity."},{"id":"gt-vaule0qy","front":"Rueda, home of Verdejo: what are its two historic aged wines?","back":"Dorado is aged in air, golden and nutty. Pálido is aged under flor, the veil of yeast, and stays pale. Both recall Sherry."}],"questions":[{"id":"gt-bdd6yjex","q":"A blind white is pale straw with green glints, dry and unoaked: lime, grapefruit, fennel and bay leaf, high acidity, a slightly oily feel and a faint bitter finish. Which grape and origin fit it?","opts":["Verdejo, Rueda","Albariño, Rías Baixas","Sauvignon Blanc, Marlborough","Chardonnay, Chablis"],"a":0,"exp":"Fennel, bay leaf and a bitter finish on a slightly oily texture mark Verdejo. Marlborough Sauvignon Blanc would be louder, with passion fruit and gooseberry and no bitter twist."},{"id":"gt-chbedn0e","q":"A guest reads on the back label of her Rueda that the grapes were harvested at night, and asks why. What is the reason?","opts":["Night picking raises the sugar in the grapes","Cool fruit keeps its scent and does not brown","Dew on the grapes lowers the alcohol","The rules forbid picking by day"],"a":1,"exp":"Verdejo browns and loses its aromatics with heat and air, so Rueda picks in the cold of the night and works cold in the cellar. Sugar does not rise at night."},{"id":"gt-cqg0b4gn","q":"A guest orders a Rueda that names Verdejo on its label and asks how much of the grape the bottle must hold. What do you answer?","opts":["All of it, 100 per cent","At least 75 per cent","At least 85 per cent","At least 50 per cent"],"a":2,"exp":"A Rueda that names the grape is at least 85 per cent Verdejo. A Rueda that names no grape needs at least three quarters from the denomination's main grapes, Viura now among them, and may take the rest from other permitted whites such as Palomino."}],"sources":[{"title":"Consejo Regulador Rueda: the grape varieties","url":"https://dorueda.com/variedades-de-uva/"},{"title":"Consejo Regulador Rueda: the history","url":"https://dorueda.com/historia/"},{"title":"Foods and Wines from Spain: Rueda, the denomination","url":"https://www.foodswinesfromspain.com/en/wine/regions/duero-river-valley/rueda-do"},{"title":"Foods and Wines from Spain: fortified wine from Rueda","url":"https://www.foodswinesfromspain.com/en/wine/news/2024/fortfied-wine-rueda"}]},
"godello": {"slug":"godello","name":"Godello","colour":"white","kind":"grape","status":"full","mode":"home","aliases":[],"notIn":[],"say":"goh-DEH-yoh","lines":{"s10":"Godello is the textured white of inland Galicia: ripe pear, white peach and wet stone on a round, medium to full body.","s20":"Godello comes from Valdeorras, a valley of slate hills in inland Galicia, and it almost died out in the 1970s. It gives a dry and rounded white that tastes of pear, white peach and lemon, with a stony finish.","s45":"Godello rewards the Chardonnay drinker who wants something new. It comes from Valdeorras, a valley of slate hills inland in Galicia, in north west Spain. It tastes of ripe pear, white peach and lemon, with a stony, faintly salty finish. It is dry and medium to full in body, with fresh acidity. Some is made crisp in steel, and the richer bottles rest on their spent yeast or in barrel for a creamy feel. It sits well beside roast fish, scallops or chicken. It had almost died out by the 1970s, until a local project replanted the valley from the few vines left.","sale":"Godello came back from a few surviving vines, and today it pours round and stony, with ripe pear and white peach. I would offer it to anyone who usually asks for Chardonnay, with the roast fish or the chicken."},"glass":{"lead":"Ripe pear, white peach and lemon over wet slate, round and medium to full, with a creamy feel where it has rested on its yeast.","notes":[{"family":"orchard fruit","notes":["ripe pear","green apple","quince"]},{"family":"stone fruit","notes":["white peach","nectarine"]},{"family":"citrus","notes":["lemon zest","grapefruit"]},{"family":"herbal","notes":["fennel","aniseed"]},{"family":"mineral","notes":["wet slate","sea salt"]},{"family":"lees and cellar","notes":["fresh cream","bread dough"]}],"ladder":[{"climate":"cool","where":"The high slate slopes of Valdeorras and Ribeira Sacra","notes":["lemon zest","green apple","wet slate"]},{"climate":"moderate","where":"Monterrei, warmer and drier, near the Portuguese border","notes":["ripe pear","white peach","quince"]}],"structure":"Dry. Acidity medium plus, alcohol medium to medium plus, body medium to full, with a round, textured feel and little or no new oak."},"worlds":{"home":{"home":"Valdeorras, a valley of slate slopes on the river Sil in eastern Galicia, with Bierzo, Ribeira Sacra and Monterrei beside it. Nearly lost by the 1970s, it was replanted from the few vines left. It is made fresh in steel, or kept on its lees or in barrel for a broader, creamier wine.","abroad":"In Portugal it is Gouveio, a backbone of the Douro's dry whites and a grape of White Port; it was long called Verdelho there, though it is a different grape from Madeira's Verdelho. It is seldom planted beyond Iberia."}},"service":{"temp":"50 to 55 degrees Fahrenheit, 10 to 13 degrees Celsius; a young, steel made bottle a little cooler.","glass":"A white wine glass; a wider Burgundy bowl for a barrel aged bottle.","decant":"Usually unnecessary; open it and pour.","age":"Fresh styles within three years; lees and barrel aged wines from old vines three to eight."},"food":{"with":[{"dish":"Redfish roasted with butter and lemon","why":"weight for the fish, acidity for the butter"},{"dish":"Seared scallops","why":"a round texture meets the scallop's sweetness"},{"dish":"Roast chicken","why":"body enough for the bird, with no heavy oak"},{"dish":"Octopus with potato and olive oil","why":"the Galician plate, lifted by a stony freshness"}]},"labels":[{"term":"Valdeorras","means":"The grape's home: a Galician denomination of slate slopes along the river Sil."},{"term":"Bierzo","means":"The neighbouring denomination in Castilla y León, upstream on the Sil, which shares the grape."},{"term":"Gouveio","means":"The same grape in Portugal, in the Douro's dry whites and in White Port."},{"term":"Sobre lías","means":"On the lees: kept on its spent yeast for texture and a creamy note."},{"term":"Fermentado en barrica","means":"Fermented in barrel: a broader, richer style of the wine."}],"guest":[{"q":"I like white Burgundy. What would you pour me from Spain?","a":"Godello. It has a similar weight and roundness, with pear, white peach and a stony finish, and the richer bottles are aged on their lees or in barrel in much the same way."},{"q":"How is it different from Albariño?","a":"Albariño is lighter, more scented and saltier, from the coast. Godello comes from the inland valleys and is broader and quieter on the nose, a wine you notice more in the mouth."},{"q":"Is it oaked?","a":"Sometimes, and usually lightly. Most Godello is made in steel; the fuller bottles spend time on their lees or in barrel, which tends to add cream and texture more than vanilla."}],"compare":[{"slug":"chardonnay","tell":"Chardonnay, as white Burgundy, tends to show a plainer stamp of barrel and lees; Godello is quieter there, with pear, fennel and wet slate on a similar round body."},{"slug":"albarino","tell":"Albariño is more aromatic and saltier, lighter and higher in acidity; Godello is broader and stonier, felt more on the palate than the nose."},{"slug":"verdejo","tell":"Verdejo is greener, with lime, cut grass, bay leaf and a bitter fennel finish; Godello is rounder and stonier, with pear and white peach."}],"cards":[{"id":"gt-1zlmqj8o","front":"Godello: where is home, and what is the soil there?","back":"Valdeorras, on the river Sil in eastern Galicia, where slate slopes give the wine its stony edge. It also grows in Bierzo, Ribeira Sacra and Monterrei."},{"id":"gt-m7faxpog","front":"What is Godello called in Portugal, and what does it make there?","back":"Gouveio. It is a backbone of the Douro's dry whites and one of the grapes of White Port."},{"id":"gt-bt5dofs5","front":"Godello almost died out: when, and how was it saved?","back":"By the 1970s little Godello was left in Valdeorras. A local project begun in 1974 studied the grape and replanted the valley from cuttings of the vines that remained."}],"questions":[{"id":"gt-2p8pmj4i","q":"A blind white is straw gold, dry and medium to full, with ripe pear, white peach, fennel and wet slate, medium plus acidity and a faint creaminess but no vanilla. Which grape and origin fit it?","opts":["Albariño, Rías Baixas","Verdejo, Rueda","Sauvignon Blanc, Sancerre","Godello, Valdeorras"],"a":3,"exp":"A broad, stony, quietly scented white with pear and a creamy feel from its lees points to Godello. Albariño would be lighter, more perfumed and saltier, with higher acidity."},{"id":"gt-iipogvmu","q":"A guest who drinks unoaked white Burgundy asks for something Spanish with the same weight and a stony finish. Which bottle do you bring?","opts":["Valdeorras Godello","Rías Baixas Albariño","Rueda Verdejo","Getariako Txakolina"],"a":0,"exp":"Godello gives a round, medium to full white of pear and wet slate, the nearest Spanish match for white Burgundy. Albariño is lighter and more perfumed, and Verdejo is greener."},{"id":"gt-84fx4n7q","q":"On a Portuguese list a guest finds a dry Douro white made from Gouveio and asks what that grape is called in Spain. What do you answer?","opts":["Albariño","Godello","Verdejo","Viura"],"a":1,"exp":"Gouveio is the Portuguese name of Godello, a backbone of the Douro's dry whites. Albariño is the grape Portugal calls Alvarinho, and its home there is Vinho Verde."}],"sources":[{"title":"Foods and Wines from Spain: Valdeorras, the denomination","url":"https://www.foodswinesfromspain.com/en/wine/regions/green-spain/valdeorras-do"},{"title":"Foods and Wines from Spain: Valdeorras, wines as good as gold","url":"https://www.foodswinesfromspain.com/en/wine/articles/2020/may/do-valdeorras-wines-as-good-as-gold"},{"title":"Wines of Portugal: Gouveio","url":"https://www.winesofportugal.com/en/portuguese-wines/grape-varieties/gouveio/"},{"title":"Consejo Regulador Bierzo: the grape varieties","url":"https://www.crdobierzo.es/en/the-bierzo-designation-of-origin/varieties-grapes/"}]},
"savagnin": {"slug":"savagnin","name":"Savagnin (Jura)","colour":"white","kind":"grape","status":"full","mode":"home","aliases":[],"notIn":[],"say":"sah-vah-NYAN","lines":{"s10":"Savagnin is the grape behind vin jaune, the Jura's dry white aged under a veil of yeast until it tastes of walnut and curry.","s20":"Savagnin belongs to the Jura, the hills between Burgundy and Switzerland, where it ripens late and stays sharp. Kept topped up in cask it is lemon and green apple, and left for years under a veil of yeast it becomes vin jaune, tasting of walnut and curry.","s45":"Savagnin makes the white they leave alone in the cask for years. Its home is the Jura, the hills between Burgundy and Switzerland. From a cask kept full it tastes of lemon, green apple and quince, bone dry and sharp. If the cask is never topped up, a veil of yeast grows over the wine, and after six years it is vin jaune. It is golden and still dry, with walnut, curry and a salty tang. Unlike Sherry, it is never strengthened with spirit. Serve it with Comté cheese, morels or chicken in cream. Its squat bottle, the clavelin, holds what is said to be left of a litre.","sale":"Made from Savagnin and left for years under a veil of yeast, vin jaune tastes of walnut and curry, with a long, salty finish. A small glass suits the Comté, or a chicken in cream with morels."},"glass":{"lead":"Lemon, green apple and quince with piercing acidity from a full cask; under a veil of yeast, walnut, curry and bruised apple with a salty tang.","notes":[{"family":"citrus","notes":["lemon zest","candied lemon peel"]},{"family":"orchard fruit","notes":["green apple","quince","bruised apple"]},{"family":"nutty","notes":["walnut","hazelnut","toasted almond"]},{"family":"spice","notes":["curry powder","fenugreek"]},{"family":"mineral","notes":["sea salt","crushed chalk"]}],"ladder":[{"climate":"cool","where":"The Jura's grey marl slopes, topped up or under the veil","notes":["lemon zest","green apple","walnut"]},{"climate":"moderate","where":"Switzerland's Valais, as Heida, in a sunny, dry mountain valley","notes":["candied lemon peel","honey","hazelnut"]}],"structure":"Bone dry and unfortified. Acidity high, alcohol medium plus, body medium to full; fresh when topped up, broader and more savoury under the veil."},"worlds":{"home":{"home":"The Jura, in eastern France, on cool grey marl slopes between Burgundy and Switzerland, where it ripens late and keeps high acidity. Topped up in cask, ouillé, it is fresh and lemony. Left untopped under a veil of yeast it takes on walnut and salt, and after more than six years it is vin jaune, never fortified.","abroad":"Switzerland's Valais grows it high in the Alps as Heida or Païen, a dry, firm white. In Australia, vines planted as Albariño proved in 2009 to be Savagnin, and the wine is now sold under its own name."}},"service":{"temp":"Vin jaune at 57 to 61 degrees Fahrenheit, 14 to 16 degrees Celsius; a topped up Savagnin cooler.","glass":"A white wine glass with a generous bowl; pour vin jaune small, for it goes a long way.","decant":"No need, but open vin jaune a few hours ahead so the scent can unfold.","age":"Vin jaune is sold in its seventh year and keeps for decades; a topped up Savagnin is poured younger."},"food":{"with":[{"dish":"Comté, young or well aged","why":"a nutty cheese beside a nutty wine, from the same hills"},{"dish":"Chicken with morels in cream","why":"the Jura's own dish, often cooked with the wine"},{"dish":"Turtle soup","why":"it does the work of the Sherry in the bowl"},{"dish":"River trout in cream","why":"sharp acidity cuts through the cream"},{"dish":"Walnuts and cured ham","why":"salt and nuts echo the tang of the veil"}]},"labels":[{"term":"Vin jaune","means":"Savagnin aged under a veil of yeast, untopped, for at least sixty months; sold after six years and three months."},{"term":"Château-Chalon","means":"An appellation, not a château: it makes vin jaune and nothing else."},{"term":"Ouillé","means":"Topped up in cask, so no veil forms: fresh, with lemon and green apple."},{"term":"Sous voile","means":"Under the veil: aged untopped beneath a film of yeast, tasting of walnut and salt."},{"term":"Clavelin","means":"The squat 62 centilitre bottle kept by law for vin jaune."}],"guest":[{"q":"Is Savagnin the same as Sauvignon Blanc?","a":"No, though the names sound alike. Savagnin is the Jura's own grape, from the Traminer family and kin to Gewürztraminer. It is sharper and nuttier, with none of Sauvignon Blanc's cut grass."},{"q":"This smells like Sherry. Has it gone off?","a":"No, it is meant to. Vin jaune ages for years under a veil of yeast, as Fino Sherry does, and that gives walnut and curry. Unlike Sherry it is never fortified."},{"q":"I do not care for the nutty style. Is there another Savagnin?","a":"Yes. Ask for one marked ouillé. The cask is kept topped up, so no veil forms, and the wine stays fresh: lemon, green apple and a chalky edge."},{"q":"Why is the bottle so small and squat?","a":"It is the clavelin, 62 centilitres, kept by law for vin jaune. That is said to be what remains of a litre after more than six years in cask."}],"compare":[{"slug":"palomino","tell":"Palomino, as Fino Sherry, also ages under a veil of yeast, but it is fortified, lighter and softer in acidity; Savagnin as vin jaune is sharper, with walnut and curry."},{"slug":"chardonnay","tell":"Jura Chardonnay, sometimes aged under the veil too, is rounder and less pungent; Savagnin has the sharper acidity and the stronger walnut and curry."},{"slug":"chenin-blanc","tell":"Chenin Blanc aged in air shows bruised apple and honey but none of the curry and fenugreek; Savagnin under the veil has both, with a salty tang."}],"cards":[{"id":"gt-ucdtw7nt","front":"Vin jaune: which grape, how long under the veil, and which bottle?","back":"Savagnin alone. At least sixty months untopped under a veil of yeast, sold no sooner than six years and three months after harvest, in the 62 centilitre clavelin. It is never fortified."},{"id":"gt-s29y6tvr","front":"Savagnin ouillé and sous voile: what differs in the cask and the glass?","back":"Ouillé is topped up, so no veil forms: fresh, with lemon and green apple. Sous voile is left untopped under a film of yeast: walnut, curry and a salty tang."},{"id":"gt-b9wqwr1l","front":"Savagnin is not Sauvignon Blanc. Which family does it belong to?","back":"The Traminer family: it is kin to Gewürztraminer, and DNA makes it a likely parent of Sauvignon Blanc. In the Swiss Valais the same grape is called Heida or Païen."}],"questions":[{"id":"gt-t8hfptck","q":"A blind white is deep gold and bone dry, at the strength of a table wine: walnut, curry, bruised apple and a salty tang, with searing acidity. Which wine is it most likely to be?","opts":["Fino Sherry from Jerez","Aged Savennières from the Loire","Vin jaune from the Jura","Meursault from Burgundy"],"a":2,"exp":"Walnut and curry from a veil of yeast, with searing acidity and no added spirit, is vin jaune. Fino is fortified, paler and softer in acidity, and shows no curry note."},{"id":"gt-0e7us0dj","q":"A guest who likes Fino Sherry asks how the vin jaune on the list differs from it in the cellar. Which answer is right?","opts":["Vin jaune is aged with no veil of yeast","Vin jaune is made from dried grapes","Vin jaune is sweetened before bottling","Vin jaune is never fortified"],"a":3,"exp":"Both age under a film of yeast, but vin jaune forms its veil at table wine strength and takes no spirit. Dried grapes make vin de paille, the Jura's sweet wine, not vin jaune."},{"id":"gt-wttpb5uy","q":"A guest asks why the bottle of Château-Chalon you have brought is squat and holds 62 centilitres, not 75. What do you tell her?","opts":["It is the clavelin, kept by law for vin jaune","It is the Jura's bottle for sparkling wine","It is a half bottle, used for sweet wine","It is an old Burgundy measure for village wine"],"a":0,"exp":"Vin jaune must be sold in the clavelin of 62 centilitres, said to be what remains of a litre after its years in cask. A half bottle holds 37.5 centilitres."}],"sources":[{"title":"Comité Interprofessionnel des Vins du Jura: history, land and grape varieties","url":"https://www.jura-vins.com/medias/documents-a-telecharger/docs-utiles/CIVJ_PLQ_Pro_2009_GB_web.pdf"},{"title":"Swiss Wine Valais: Heida or Païen","url":"https://swisswinevalais.ch/en/discover/grape-varieties/heida/"},{"title":"Wine Spectator: Grape identity crisis in Australia","url":"https://www.winespectator.com/articles/grape-identity-crisis-in-australia-4733"},{"title":"Wine Grapes, entry on Savagnin Blanc","url":""}]},
"marsanne": {"slug":"marsanne","name":"Marsanne","colour":"white","kind":"grape","status":"full","mode":"contrast","aliases":[],"notIn":[],"say":"mar-SAHN","lines":{"s10":"Marsanne is the broad, waxy white of Hermitage in the northern Rhône, quiet in scent, with ripe pear, almond and honeysuckle.","s20":"Marsanne is the main white grape of the northern Rhône, around the hill of Hermitage. It is dry, full and waxy, with ripe pear, almond and honeysuckle, and with years in bottle it turns to honey and toasted hazelnut.","s45":"Marsanne does the quiet work in the white wines of the northern Rhône, at Hermitage, Saint-Joseph and Saint-Péray. It is not a perfumed grape. It gives ripe pear, white peach and almond, with a waxy feel, a full body and soft acidity. With years in bottle it deepens to honey and toasted hazelnut. The Rhône often ferments it in barrel with a little Roussanne, while Victoria in Australia tends to make it alone, in steel, with no oak at all. It suits veal in cream, roast pork or crab. Some of Victoria's vines, planted in 1927, still give a crop each year.","sale":"Marsanne trades perfume for texture: ripe pear, almond and a waxy, mouth filling feel. It holds its own beside the roast pork or the veal."},"glass":{"lead":"Ripe pear, white peach and almond with honeysuckle and beeswax, on a full, waxy body with soft acidity.","notes":[{"family":"orchard fruit","notes":["ripe pear","quince"]},{"family":"stone fruit","notes":["white peach","apricot"]},{"family":"floral","notes":["honeysuckle","acacia blossom"]},{"family":"nutty","notes":["almond","marzipan","toasted hazelnut"]},{"family":"honeyed","notes":["beeswax","honey","orange marmalade"]}],"ladder":[{"climate":"moderate","where":"Hermitage, Crozes-Hermitage, Saint-Joseph and Saint-Péray","notes":["ripe pear","white peach","honeysuckle","almond"]},{"climate":"warm","where":"Cassis in Provence, California's Central Coast, Victoria's Goulburn Valley","notes":["honeydew melon","ripe peach","lemon"]}],"structure":"Dry. Acidity low to medium, alcohol medium to high, body full, with an oily, waxy texture."},"worlds":{"contrast":{"old":{"where":"Hermitage, Crozes-Hermitage, Saint-Joseph and Saint-Péray; Cassis; the Valais","glass":"Tends to be broad and waxy: ripe pear, white peach, honeysuckle and almond, with soft acidity and a faint bitter grip. It often closes for some years, then opens to honey and toasted hazelnut.","cellar":"Often blended with a little Roussanne and fermented in barrel or large cask, some of it new; Saint-Péray also makes it sparkling, in the bottle."},"new":{"where":"Victoria's Goulburn Valley and Nagambie Lakes; California's Central Coast","glass":"Victorian wines are typically lighter and unoaked: lemon, honeysuckle and pear when young, delicate and fresh, turning to honey, toast and orange marmalade with bottle age. California tends to be riper, with melon.","cellar":"In Victoria usually bottled alone under its own name, fermented in steel and released young; California often blends it with Roussanne and Viognier in older barrels."},"why":"The Rhône's appellations name a place, and its growers blend the wine and raise it in wood. Victoria has grown Marsanne since the nineteenth century and built its own habit: one grape, steel, and bottle age in place of oak.","blur":"Some Rhône cellars now work in steel for a fresher, earlier wine, and oaked Australian bottlings and California blends can come close to a white Saint-Joseph.","call":"Waxy weight, almond and a touch of vanilla from barrel lean Rhône; lemon and honeysuckle at lighter weight, or toast with no oak, lean Victoria."}},"service":{"temp":"50 to 55 degrees Fahrenheit, 10 to 13 degrees Celsius; a young unoaked wine a little cooler.","glass":"A white wine glass; a wide Burgundy bowl for a mature Hermitage.","decant":"Rarely needed. A mature white Hermitage can open with a gentle decant; taste a sample first.","age":"It often closes after its first few years and opens again after five; white Hermitage can keep for decades."},"food":{"with":[{"dish":"Roast pork or veal in cream","why":"a full, waxy white meets the richness"},{"dish":"Crab or lobster with butter","why":"weight and soft acidity suit sweet shellfish"},{"dish":"Roast chicken with mushrooms","why":"almond and honey in the wine echo the roast"},{"dish":"Redfish with almonds and brown butter","why":"the wine's almond note meets the browned butter"},{"dish":"Comté or another hard mountain cheese","why":"a nutty wine beside a nutty cheese"}]},"labels":[{"term":"Hermitage Blanc","means":"White wine from the hill of Hermitage: mostly Marsanne, often with some Roussanne."},{"term":"Saint-Péray","means":"A northern Rhône appellation for Marsanne and Roussanne alone, made still or sparkling."},{"term":"Ermitage","means":"In the Valais, the Swiss name for Marsanne, often sweet; on a Rhône label, another spelling of Hermitage."},{"term":"Marsanne Roussanne","means":"The classic pair: Marsanne for weight and wax, Roussanne for perfume and acidity."}],"guest":[{"q":"What is in a white Hermitage?","a":"Mostly Marsanne, often with some Roussanne. Marsanne gives the weight and the waxy texture, and Roussanne adds perfume and freshness."},{"q":"This older bottle tastes of honey and nuts. Has it gone off?","a":"No, that is the grape growing up. Marsanne starts quiet, with pear and almond, and with years in bottle it turns to honey, toasted hazelnut and marmalade while staying dry."},{"q":"Is it like Chardonnay?","a":"In weight, yes. It is as full, but softer in acidity and waxier, and it tastes of pear and almond where an oaked Chardonnay tastes of butter and toast."}],"compare":[{"slug":"roussanne","tell":"Roussanne is the more aromatic partner, with chamomile tea, pear and fresher acidity; Marsanne is broader, waxier and nuttier."},{"slug":"viognier","tell":"Viognier is loudly perfumed with apricot and honeysuckle and feels headier; Marsanne is quieter, with pear, almond and beeswax."},{"slug":"chardonnay","tell":"Chardonnay carries more acidity and shows butter and toast from the cellar; Marsanne is waxier and softer, with almond and honeysuckle."}],"cards":[{"id":"gt-dq5g9l43","front":"Marsanne: which northern Rhône appellations make white wine from it?","back":"Hermitage, Crozes-Hermitage, Saint-Joseph and Saint-Péray, often with some Roussanne. Saint-Péray makes it still and sparkling."},{"id":"gt-3u61prkg","front":"Marsanne blind: the Rhône or Victoria, what tips it?","back":"Waxy weight, almond and a touch of vanilla from barrel lean Rhône. Lemon and honeysuckle at lighter weight, or toast and honey with no oak at all, lean Victoria."},{"id":"gt-ose470bq","front":"What does Marsanne give a Rhône white blend, and Roussanne beside it?","back":"Marsanne gives weight, a waxy texture and a reliable crop. Roussanne adds perfume, pear and firmer acidity, and is harder to grow."}],"questions":[{"id":"gt-1yq24wun","q":"A blind white is eight years old, dry and medium bodied, with lemon, honeysuckle, honey and toast, and no vanilla or barrel note at all. Which origin fits it?","opts":["Hermitage, northern Rhône","Nagambie Lakes, Victoria","Napa Valley, California","Condrieu, northern Rhône"],"a":1,"exp":"Unoaked Victorian Marsanne starts with lemon and honeysuckle and gains honey and toast in bottle. White Hermitage would be fuller and waxier, usually with a note from the barrel.","tag":"worlds"},{"id":"gt-yr33lr0u","q":"A guest asks which grape gives a white Hermitage its weight and its waxy texture. Which is it?","opts":["Clairette","Viognier","Roussanne","Marsanne"],"a":3,"exp":"Marsanne gives white Hermitage its weight and waxy texture. Roussanne, its partner, brings perfume and acidity, and Viognier belongs to Condrieu."},{"id":"gt-4yaj8x6d","q":"A Swiss list from the Valais offers a rich white labelled Ermitage. Which grape is in the bottle?","opts":["Silvaner","Pinot Gris","Marsanne","Chasselas"],"a":2,"exp":"Ermitage is the Valais name for Marsanne. Chasselas there is Fendant, Silvaner is Johannisberg and Pinot Gris is Malvoisie."}],"sources":[{"title":"Inter Rhône: Marsanne, the grape variety","url":"https://www.vins-rhone.com/en/marsanne-grape-variety"},{"title":"Inter Rhône: Côtes du Rhône Cru Hermitage","url":"https://www.vins-rhone.com/en/cotes-du-rhone-cru-aoc-hermitage"},{"title":"Wine Australia: Goulburn Valley","url":"https://www.wineaustralia.com/market-insights/regions-and-varieties/victoria-wines/goulburn-valley"},{"title":"Wine Australia: Australia's old vines guide","url":"https://www.australianwine.com/getcontentasset/d2dc5c45-bc97-403c-9c04-e1f836139581/2a6464f5-7547-4f96-aacf-a0c3da1718f8/awd_oldvines_guide.pdf"}]},
"petit-manseng": {"slug":"petit-manseng","name":"Petit Manseng","colour":"white","kind":"grape","status":"full","mode":"home","aliases":[],"notIn":[],"say":"puh-TEE mahn-SENG","lines":{"s10":"Petit Manseng makes sweet Jurançon in the foothills of the Pyrenees: pineapple, apricot and honey, kept fresh by very high acidity.","s20":"Petit Manseng ripens late in Jurançon, in the foothills of the Pyrenees, where its small berries are left to shrivel on the vine into December. The wine is sweet with candied pineapple, apricot and honey, and its sharp acidity makes it finish clean.","s45":"Petit Manseng is still on the vine in December. That is the custom in Jurançon, on steep slopes below the Pyrenees in south west France. A warm, dry wind off the mountains shrivels the small, thick skinned berries, and the sugar gathers as they dry. The wine tastes of candied pineapple, apricot and honey. It is a sweet white, yet so sharp in acidity that it finishes clean. It ferments slowly, often in barrel, and no spirit is added. Bring it out with foie gras, a hard sheep's cheese or a fruit tart. They say the newborn Henri IV of France had his lips wet with a drop of Jurançon.","sale":"Petit Manseng is the sweet wine for people who think they dislike sweet wine: pineapple and honey, then a finish as fresh as lime. A small glass is enough, with the foie gras to begin or with an apricot tart to finish."},"glass":{"lead":"Candied pineapple, apricot and quince with honey and candied orange peel, sweet and full, cut by very high acidity.","notes":[{"family":"tropical fruit","notes":["pineapple","mango","passion fruit"]},{"family":"stone fruit","notes":["apricot","ripe peach"]},{"family":"citrus","notes":["candied orange peel","grapefruit"]},{"family":"orchard fruit","notes":["quince","ripe pear"]},{"family":"honeyed","notes":["acacia honey","beeswax"]}],"ladder":[{"climate":"moderate","where":"Jurançon's steep slopes below the Pyrenees","notes":["pineapple","quince","candied orange peel"]},{"climate":"warm","where":"Virginia's hot, humid Piedmont","notes":["ripe pineapple","mango","honey"]}],"structure":"Off dry to lusciously sweet, with some dry wines. Acidity very high at every sweetness, alcohol medium plus, body medium to full."},"worlds":{"home":{"home":"Jurançon, on steep south facing slopes in the Pyrenean foothills near Pau. Small, thick skinned berries hang into November and December while a warm, dry wind, the foehn, shrivels them; they are picked by hand in several passes. The sweet wine ferments slowly, often in barrel, and is never fortified. Pacherenc du Vic-Bilh grows it too.","abroad":"Virginia has taken it up, because its thick skins and loose bunches resist rot in a humid summer; there it is made dry, off dry or sweet. The Basque Country grows a little on both sides of the border."}},"service":{"temp":"43 to 46 degrees Fahrenheit, 6 to 8 degrees Celsius for the sweet wines; a dry one a little warmer.","glass":"A small glass and a small pour for the sweet wines; a white wine glass for a dry one.","decant":"No. A sweet white wants only to be cold and poured small.","age":"The sweet wines keep ten years and more; a dry one is poured within a few years."},"food":{"with":[{"dish":"Foie gras","why":"sweetness against richness, with acidity to finish clean"},{"dish":"Roquefort or another blue cheese","why":"sweet against salt"},{"dish":"Ossau-Iraty or another hard sheep's cheese","why":"the cheese of its own mountains"},{"dish":"Apricot or pineapple tart","why":"the wine's own fruit, lifted by its acidity"},{"dish":"Shrimp remoulade, with an off dry one","why":"pineapple and sharp acidity against mustard and cayenne"}]},"labels":[{"term":"Jurançon","means":"Unless it says Sec, the sweet wine of the Pyrenean foothills, from Petit and Gros Manseng."},{"term":"Jurançon Sec","means":"The dry wine of the same slopes, built mostly on Gros Manseng."},{"term":"Vendanges Tardives","means":"Late harvest: a richer sweet Jurançon from the two Mansengs alone, picked late after shrivelling on the vine."},{"term":"Moelleux","means":"Sweet, in French: the style of plain Jurançon."},{"term":"Pacherenc du Vic-Bilh","means":"The white wine of the Madiran zone, dry or sweet, from the Mansengs and local grapes."}],"guest":[{"q":"Is this like Sauternes?","a":"It is usually a little less sweet, and brighter. Sauternes owes its saffron to noble rot; here the grapes simply dry on the vine, so you taste pineapple and apricot, with sharper acidity."},{"q":"The label just says Jurançon. Is it sweet or dry?","a":"Sweet. Plain Jurançon is the sweet wine, from gently to richly so, and the dry one says Sec on the label."},{"q":"What is the difference between Petit Manseng and Gros Manseng?","a":"They are two grapes of the same hills. Petit Manseng has smaller, thicker skinned berries that shrivel well, so it leads the sweet wines; Gros Manseng crops more and makes most of the dry Jurançon Sec."}],"compare":[{"slug":"semillon","tell":"Sémillon, as Sauternes, is waxier, with saffron and honey from noble rot and softer acidity; Petit Manseng is brighter, with pineapple and no note of rot."},{"slug":"chenin-blanc","tell":"Sweet Chenin Blanc from the Layon shows quince, honey and lanolin; Petit Manseng is more exotic, with candied pineapple and apricot, and sharper still."},{"slug":"furmint","tell":"Furmint, as Tokaji Aszú, carries the saffron and ginger of noble rot over a smoky edge; Petit Manseng shows no rot, only candied pineapple, apricot and honey."}],"cards":[{"id":"gt-s2xuef3r","front":"Petit Manseng: how does sweet Jurançon become sweet?","back":"By passerillage: the small, thick skinned berries hang into November and December and shrivel on the vine in the foehn, a warm, dry wind off the Pyrenees. Noble rot is not the aim, and no spirit is added."},{"id":"gt-ny7007bp","front":"Why has Virginia taken up Petit Manseng?","back":"Its thick skins and loose bunches resist rot in hot, humid summers, and it holds high acidity at high sugar, so it is made dry, off dry or sweet."},{"id":"gt-k5bbaizs","front":"Petit Manseng blind: what parts sweet Jurançon from Sauternes?","back":"Candied pineapple and apricot with very high acidity, and no saffron from noble rot. Sauternes, from Sémillon, is waxier and softer in acidity, with saffron and honey."}],"questions":[{"id":"gt-3z24wh38","q":"A blind white is deep gold and sweet: candied pineapple, apricot and honey, with very high acidity and none of the saffron of noble rot. Which wine is it most likely to be?","opts":["Jurançon","Tokaji Aszú","Coteaux du Layon","Sauternes"],"a":0,"exp":"Candied pineapple and apricot with very high acidity and no rot character point to Petit Manseng dried on the vine in Jurançon. Sauternes would show the saffron of noble rot and softer acidity."},{"id":"gt-kmh7mojg","q":"A guest orders a bottle labelled simply Jurançon to drink with grilled fish, expecting a dry white. What should you tell him before you open it?","opts":["Jurançon is a red wine made from Tannat","Jurançon is sparkling unless the label says still","Plain Jurançon is sweet; the dry wine says Sec","Jurançon is fortified with grape spirit"],"a":2,"exp":"Plain Jurançon is the sweet wine, from grapes shrivelled on the vine, and the dry one is labelled Jurançon Sec. It is white only and never fortified, so the other answers fail."},{"id":"gt-4kqsn336","q":"A guest enjoys a sweet Jurançon led by Petit Manseng and asks which grape does most of the work in the dry Jurançon Sec. What do you answer?","opts":["Chenin Blanc","Gros Manseng","Sauvignon Blanc","Sémillon"],"a":1,"exp":"Gros Manseng, larger berried and more productive, is the base of Jurançon Sec. Sauvignon Blanc and Sémillon are the grapes of Bordeaux and Bergerac, not of Jurançon."}],"sources":[{"title":"Ministère de l'Agriculture: cahier des charges de l'appellation d'origine contrôlée Jurançon","url":"https://info.agriculture.gouv.fr/boagri/document_administratif-acde35d1-4b35-46b2-8632-c2e7e07405a4/telechargement"},{"title":"Béarn Pyrénées tourism agency: discovering Pau and Jurançon","url":"https://www.tourisme64.com/en/sejours-et-week-end-pays-basque-pyrenees/mansengs-and-flavors/"},{"title":"Virginia Tech viticulture notes: Petit Manseng in Virginia","url":"https://sites.ext.vt.edu/newsletter-archive/viticulture/03januaryfebruary/03januaryfebruary.html"},{"title":"Wine Grapes, entries on Petit Manseng and Gros Manseng","url":""}]},
"xinomavro": {"slug":"xinomavro","name":"Xinomavro","colour":"red","kind":"grape","status":"full","mode":"home","aliases":[],"notIn":[],"say":"ksee-NOH-mah-vroh","lines":{"s10":"Xinomavro is the pale, sharp red of northern Greece, with sour cherry, sun dried tomato and black olive on a firm grip.","s20":"Xinomavro makes the reds of Naoussa and Amyndeon in northern Greece, often set beside Barolo for its grip. It pours pale and turns to brick early, with sour cherry, sun dried tomato and black olive over high acidity and firm tannin.","s45":"Xinomavro belongs to northern Greece, to Naoussa on the slopes of Mount Vermio and to the cold plateau of Amyndeon. It looks pale and turns to brick early. It tastes of sour cherry, sun dried tomato and black olive, over very high acidity and a firm, drying tannin. The old cellars age it for years in large old casks, while newer ones pick a little earlier and handle it more gently. Set it beside roast lamb, a tomato rich braise or wild mushrooms. In Greek the name means acid black: true of the acid, though not of the colour.","sale":"Xinomavro looks and grips like a Barolo, then tastes of Greece: sun dried tomato and black olive in place of tar. I would decant the Naoussa and set it beside anything slow cooked with tomato."},"glass":{"lead":"Sour cherry, sun dried tomato and black olive in a pale garnet glass, over very high acidity and a firm, drying tannin.","notes":[{"family":"red fruit","notes":["sour cherry","raspberry","strawberry"]},{"family":"savoury","notes":["sun dried tomato","black olive"]},{"family":"herbal","notes":["tomato leaf","bay leaf"]},{"family":"dried fruit","notes":["dried cherry","dried fig"]},{"family":"age","notes":["truffle","old leather","tobacco leaf"]}],"ladder":[{"climate":"cool","where":"The Amyndeon plateau, by the lakes of Western Macedonia","notes":["raspberry","strawberry","tomato leaf"]},{"climate":"moderate","where":"Naoussa, on the eastern slopes of Mount Vermio","notes":["sour cherry","sun dried tomato","black olive"]},{"climate":"warm","where":"Goumenissa above the Axios plain, and Rapsani on Olympus","notes":["black cherry","red plum","dried fig"]}],"structure":"Dry. Acidity high, tannin high and drying, alcohol medium plus, body medium; pale garnet, turning to brick early."},"worlds":{"home":{"home":"Northern Greece. Naoussa, on the eastern slopes of Mount Vermio, is Xinomavro alone and red only. Amyndeon, on a colder plateau, makes it red, rosé and sparkling. Goumenissa blends it with Negoska, and Rapsani on Olympus with Krassato and Stavroto.","abroad":"It barely travels: a joint venture of Greek and Chinese wineries planted it in Gansu, in north western China, around 2010; elsewhere it is hardly seen."}},"service":{"temp":"61 to 64 degrees Fahrenheit, 16 to 18 degrees Celsius; a young, modern Naoussa a touch cooler.","glass":"A wide Burgundy bowl, as for Nebbiolo, to gather the perfume.","decant":"Decant a young Naoussa after a taste; an old Grande Reserve gently, off its sediment.","age":"Ten years and more for Naoussa Reserve and Grande Reserve; a young, modern bottle within five."},"food":{"with":[{"dish":"Roast lamb with oregano","why":"fat and protein meet the firm grip"},{"dish":"Beef or lamb braised with tomato","why":"tomato in the dish meets tomato in the wine"},{"dish":"Wild mushrooms","why":"savoury depth beside dried cherry and truffle"},{"dish":"Roast quail or partridge","why":"high acidity lifts lean, gamey meat"}]},"labels":[{"term":"Naoussa","means":"Xinomavro alone, red only, from the eastern slopes of Mount Vermio; a PDO since 1971."},{"term":"Amyndeon, Amynteo","means":"Xinomavro alone from a cold lakeside plateau, as red, rosé or sparkling rosé."},{"term":"Goumenissa","means":"Xinomavro with at least 20 per cent Negoska, a local grape that softens and deepens it."},{"term":"Rapsani","means":"Xinomavro, Krassato and Stavroto in equal parts, from the slopes of Mount Olympus."},{"term":"Reserve, Grande Reserve","means":"Greece's national ageing terms; a Grande Reserve spends at least four years in cask and bottle."}],"guest":[{"q":"What does Xinomavro mean?","a":"Acid black, in Greek. The acid is true to the name; the black is not, since the wine is pale and turns to brick early."},{"q":"Why do people compare it with Barolo?","a":"Both are pale, high in acidity and firm in tannin, and both can age for decades. Xinomavro tastes of sun dried tomato and black olive where Nebbiolo has tar and roses."},{"q":"Is there a lighter Xinomavro?","a":"Amyndeon's, from a colder plateau: paler and fresher, and also made as a dry rosé and a sparkling rosé."}],"compare":[{"slug":"nebbiolo","tell":"Nebbiolo has rose petal and tar; Xinomavro shares the pale colour, acidity and grip but brings sun dried tomato, black olive and bay leaf."},{"slug":"agiorgitiko","tell":"Agiorgitiko, from Nemea in the south, is deeper in colour, softer and plusher; Xinomavro is pale, sharper and more tannic, with sun dried tomato and black olive."},{"slug":"sangiovese","tell":"Sangiovese has dried oregano and a gentler tannin; Xinomavro grips harder, with sharper acidity, sun dried tomato and black olive."}],"cards":[{"id":"gt-mbpj2tys","front":"Xinomavro: what does the name mean, and where is it at home?","back":"Acid black, in Greek. Its home is Macedonia in northern Greece, at Naoussa, Amyndeon and Goumenissa, with Rapsani on Mount Olympus in Thessaly."},{"id":"gt-dr31u1vq","front":"Xinomavro's four appellations: which grapes must each of them hold?","back":"Naoussa, Xinomavro alone, red only. Amyndeon, alone, as red, rosé or sparkling. Goumenissa, with at least 20 per cent Negoska. Rapsani, with Krassato and Stavroto in equal parts."},{"id":"gt-z5ao8x87","front":"Xinomavro blind: which mix of colour, structure and aroma gives it away?","back":"Pale garnet turning to brick early, very high acidity and firm tannin, with sour cherry, sun dried tomato and black olive."}],"questions":[{"id":"gt-j80jxnge","q":"A red poured blind is pale garnet with a brick rim, with sour cherry, sun dried tomato and black olive over searing acidity and firm tannin. Which grape is it?","opts":["Sangiovese","Xinomavro","Agiorgitiko","Nebbiolo"],"a":1,"exp":"Sun dried tomato and black olive on a pale, high acid, tannic frame mark Xinomavro. Nebbiolo, the nearest miss, shares the colour and the grip but brings tar and rose petal instead."},{"id":"gt-y2cukk08","q":"A guest wants a dry Greek rosé made from Xinomavro under a protected appellation. Which name do you look for?","opts":["Goumenissa","Nemea","Naoussa","Amyndeon"],"a":3,"exp":"Amyndeon allows Xinomavro as red, rosé and sparkling rosé. Naoussa, the nearest miss, is Xinomavro too but red only; a rosé from its slopes is a regional PGI wine."},{"id":"gt-n3rrwzg2","q":"A guest asks why the Goumenissa on the list is darker and softer than the Naoussa beside it, though both hold Xinomavro. What is the reason?","opts":["It must hold at least 20 per cent Negoska","It is grown on a cold lakeside plateau","It is aged in new American oak","It is made from dried grapes"],"a":0,"exp":"Goumenissa blends Xinomavro with at least a fifth of Negoska, which brings colour, alcohol and soft tannin. A cold lakeside plateau, the nearest miss, describes Amyndeon, whose wine is paler still."}],"sources":[{"title":"Wines of Greece: Xinomavro","url":"https://winesofgreece.org/varieties/xinomavro/"},{"title":"Decanter: Greek Xinomavro, the experts' choice","url":"https://www.decanter.com/premium/greek-xinomavro-experts-choice-468160/"},{"title":"Decanter: Greece and China joint venture to plant Xinomavro","url":"https://www.decanter.com/wine-news/greece-and-china-joint-venture-53045/"},{"title":"The Oxford Companion to Wine","url":""}]},
"mencia": {"slug":"mencia","name":"Mencía","colour":"red","kind":"grape","status":"full","mode":"home","aliases":[],"notIn":[],"say":"men-THEE-ah, in Portugal zhah-EN","lines":{"s10":"Mencía comes from the slate slopes of north west Spain: raspberry, sour cherry and violet, with a stony freshness and fine tannin.","s20":"Mencía gives Bierzo and Ribeira Sacra, in Spain's green north west, their reds. It is medium bodied and fragrant, with raspberry, violet and a stony note from slate, fresh rather than heavy.","s45":"Mencía grows on the slate of Spain's green north west, in Bierzo and in the canyons of Ribeira Sacra. In the glass it is bright ruby, with raspberry, sour cherry and violet. It feels fresh and stony, medium in body, with fine tannin. Most cellars handle it gently, in steel or older barrels, so the violet scent survives, and old vines give deeper, firmer wines that keep. Pour it slightly cool with roast pork, octopus or charcuterie. In Ribeira Sacra the vines hang on stone terraces so steep that the grapes come up in baskets or on little rack railways.","sale":"Mencía comes from Bierzo, on the pilgrims' road to Santiago: violet and raspberry with a stony freshness that revives any traveller. A light chill suits it, beside the octopus or the roast pork."},"glass":{"lead":"Raspberry, sour cherry and violet over crushed slate, medium bodied and fresh, with fine tannin and a touch of liquorice.","notes":[{"family":"red fruit","notes":["raspberry","sour cherry","red cherry","pomegranate"]},{"family":"black fruit","notes":["black cherry","blackberry"]},{"family":"floral","notes":["violet","dried violet"]},{"family":"spice","notes":["liquorice","black pepper"]},{"family":"mineral","notes":["crushed slate","graphite"]}],"ladder":[{"climate":"cool","where":"Chantada on the Miño and the Sil's north facing terraces","notes":["raspberry","sour cherry","violet"]},{"climate":"moderate","where":"Amandi's south facing slate, Bierzo's basin and the Dão as Jaen","notes":["black cherry","blackberry","liquorice","graphite"]}],"structure":"Dry. Acidity medium plus, tannin medium and fine, alcohol medium, body medium; old vines on slate add depth and grip."},"worlds":{"home":{"home":"The green north west of Spain: Bierzo in León and, in Galicia, Ribeira Sacra, Valdeorras and Monterrei, much of it on slate. Most cellars handle it gently, in steel or used wood, for a fragrant young red; old bush vines on the slopes give deeper, firmer wines with a decade of life.","abroad":"Across the border it is Jaen, a supple, early ripening partner of Touriga Nacional in the reds of the Dão. Beyond Iberia it is seldom seen."}},"service":{"temp":"54 to 59 degrees Fahrenheit, 12 to 15 degrees Celsius; old vine wines a little warmer.","glass":"A Burgundy bowl, which gathers the violet and raspberry perfume.","decant":"Usually unnecessary for a young wine; consider it for an old vine bottling after tasting a sample.","age":"Most within three years; old vine bottlings from slate keep three to ten."},"food":{"with":[{"dish":"Octopus with paprika and olive oil","why":"the Galician classic: fresh acidity meets the oil"},{"dish":"Roast pork or pork belly","why":"bright acidity and fine tannin cut the fat"},{"dish":"Charcuterie and cured sausages","why":"bright raspberry and a gentle grip beside salt"},{"dish":"Blackened redfish","why":"served cool, it meets the spice without crushing the fish"}]},"labels":[{"term":"Bierzo","means":"The basin in León where Mencía leads the reds, at its deepest from old vines on slate."},{"term":"Ribeira Sacra","means":"Galicia's canyons of the Sil and Miño: Mencía on stone terraces worked by hand."},{"term":"Jaen","means":"Mencía's Portuguese name, in the reds of the Dão."},{"term":"Vino de Villa","means":"A Bierzo village wine, the first rung above the plain Bierzo label in the 2017 ladder of place."},{"term":"Viñas viejas","means":"Old vines, a common claim on bottles from the slate slopes."}],"guest":[{"q":"Is Mencía like Pinot Noir?","a":"In weight and freshness, yes: medium bodied, bright and fragrant. It is a little deeper in colour, with liquorice, black pepper and a stony note from slate, so it handles roast pork as well as fish."},{"q":"Is it related to Cabernet Franc?","a":"Growers long thought so, since both can smell of raspberry and violet. DNA says no: it is the same grape as Jaen, the red of Portugal's Dão."},{"q":"Should a red really be served this cool?","a":"A young Mencía, yes, lightly: a few minutes in the ice bucket lifts the raspberry and the freshness. An old vine bottling wants a little more warmth."}],"compare":[{"slug":"cabernet-franc","tell":"Cabernet Franc is leafier, with green bell pepper and tobacco leaf; Mencía shares the raspberry and violet without the green edge, leaning to sour cherry and pomegranate."},{"slug":"pinot-noir","tell":"Pinot Noir is paler and silkier, with forest floor; Mencía is deeper in colour, with liquorice, black pepper and a firmer grip."},{"slug":"gamay","tell":"Gamay is lighter and juicier, often with banana and bubblegum from whole bunch fermentation; Mencía has more colour and tannin, with liquorice and black pepper."}],"cards":[{"id":"gt-415ytteh","front":"Mencía: where is it at home, and on what soil?","back":"North west Spain: Bierzo in León, and Ribeira Sacra, Valdeorras and Monterrei in Galicia, its deepest wines from old vines on slate."},{"id":"gt-ajt2qj41","front":"What is Mencía called in Portugal, and in which region?","back":"Jaen, in the Dão, where it is a supple, early ripening partner of Touriga Nacional, Alfrocheiro and Tinta Roriz."},{"id":"gt-86qddxde","front":"Mencía was long linked to Cabernet Franc. What did DNA show?","back":"No relation. Profiling showed it to be the same grape as Jaen of Portugal's Dão."}],"questions":[{"id":"gt-n7xoxi3r","q":"A red poured blind is bright ruby and medium bodied, with raspberry, sour cherry, violet and a stony note, fresh acidity and fine tannin. It comes from terraces above a Galician river. Which grape is it?","opts":["Garnacha Tintorera","Tempranillo","Mencía","Cabernet Franc"],"a":2,"exp":"Raspberry, violet and a stony freshness at medium weight from Ribeira Sacra mark Mencía. Cabernet Franc, the nearest miss, would show a leafy edge of green bell pepper and is no Galician grape."},{"id":"gt-ekzp7gjp","q":"A guest loved a Dão red led by Jaen and asks for the same grape from Spain. Which wine do you bring?","opts":["A Bierzo red","A Rioja Crianza","A Toro red","A Priorat red"],"a":0,"exp":"Jaen is Mencía, the red of Bierzo and Ribeira Sacra. Rioja Crianza, the nearest miss, is Tempranillo, which the Dão also grows, under the name Tinta Roriz."},{"id":"gt-6xtftjb6","q":"A guest wants a light, fresh Spanish red that can take a slight chill beside a plate of octopus. Which bottle fits?","opts":["Monastrell from Jumilla","Garnacha from Priorat","Tinta de Toro from Toro","Mencía from Ribeira Sacra"],"a":3,"exp":"Mencía from Ribeira Sacra is light to medium bodied, fragrant and fresh, and a light chill suits it. Toro and Jumilla are dense, dark and tannic, and Priorat Garnacha is powerful and warm."}],"sources":[{"title":"Spanish Ministry of Agriculture: Bierzo DO product specification","url":"https://www.mapa.gob.es/images/es/bierzo_2021_07_23_tcm30-210779.pdf"},{"title":"Ribeira Sacra DO: data of the regulatory council","url":"https://ribeirasacra.org/datos-consejo-regulador/en"},{"title":"Falstaff: what is Mencía?","url":"https://falstaff.com/en/grape-varieties/mencia"},{"title":"Wein-Plus glossary: Mencía","url":"https://glossary.wein-plus.eu/mencia"}]},
"aglianico": {"slug":"aglianico","name":"Aglianico","colour":"red","kind":"grape","status":"full","mode":"home","aliases":[],"notIn":[],"say":"ahl-YAH-nee-koh","lines":{"s10":"Aglianico is the slow, dark red of southern Italy's mountains: black cherry, tar and smoke on a tannin that takes years to soften.","s20":"Aglianico makes Taurasi in Campania and Aglianico del Vulture in Basilicata, mountain reds of the south. It brings black cherry, tar and smoke, with high acidity and a firm tannin that wants a decanter, a braise or ten years.","s45":"Aglianico is southern Italy's mountain red, the grape of Taurasi in the hills of Campania and of Vulture, an extinct volcano in Basilicata. It tastes of black cherry and black plum, with tar, leather and a smoky edge. It is full bodied, with high acidity and firm, drying tannin. Taurasi rests three years before release, at least one in wood, in large old casks or small new barrels that add vanilla. Australia and California grow a little, riper and rounder. Give it roast lamb, braised goat or aged hard cheese. In the high vineyards it is picked in November, sometimes after the first snow on the peaks.","sale":"Aglianico hangs on the vine into November in the mountains of the south, and it keeps that cold freshness under its dark cherry and smoke. I would give the Taurasi an hour of air and bring it with the lamb."},"glass":{"lead":"Black cherry, black plum and dried fig over tar, leather and smoke, on a full body with high acidity and a firm, drying tannin.","notes":[{"family":"black fruit","notes":["black cherry","black plum","blackberry"]},{"family":"red fruit","notes":["sour cherry","red plum"]},{"family":"dried fruit","notes":["dried fig","prune"]},{"family":"earth","notes":["tar","leather","tobacco leaf"]},{"family":"mineral","notes":["volcanic ash","iron"]},{"family":"oak","notes":["vanilla pod","coffee"]}],"ladder":[{"climate":"moderate","where":"The high hills of Irpinia and the slopes of Monte Vulture","notes":["sour cherry","black cherry","tar","iron"]},{"climate":"warm","where":"The lower Sannio, McLaren Vale and the Riverland","notes":["black plum","blackberry","dried fig"]}],"structure":"Dry. Acidity high, tannin high and drying, alcohol medium plus, body full; deep ruby, turning garnet at the rim early."},"worlds":{"home":{"home":"Campania and Basilicata. In Campania it makes Taurasi in the high, cold hills of Irpinia, at least 85 per cent Aglianico and three years in the cellar, and Aglianico del Taburno near Benevento. In Basilicata it is Aglianico del Vulture, from the ash and tuff of an extinct volcano.","abroad":"Australia grows a little, in McLaren Vale, the Riverland and Heathcote, and so does California; these wines tend to be riper and rounder, the acidity still bright."}},"service":{"temp":"61 to 64 degrees Fahrenheit, 16 to 18 degrees Celsius, a cool room temperature.","glass":"A tall Bordeaux glass, with room to swirl.","decant":"Yes for a young Taurasi or Vulture, after a taste; an older bottle gently, to leave its sediment.","age":"Taurasi and Vulture Superiore ten to twenty five years; younger Irpinia Aglianico usually within five."},"food":{"with":[{"dish":"Roast or braised lamb","why":"fat and protein soften the firm grip"},{"dish":"Goat braised with tomato","why":"acidity meets the tomato, tannin the meat"},{"dish":"Pasta with a long cooked meat ragù","why":"a rich sauce for a wine of high acidity"},{"dish":"Aged caciocavallo or pecorino","why":"salt and fat round off the tannin"}]},"labels":[{"term":"Taurasi","means":"Aglianico from the hills of Irpinia in Campania, at least 85 per cent, aged three years with one in wood."},{"term":"Aglianico del Vulture","means":"Basilicata's Aglianico from Monte Vulture; Superiore is the DOCG tier, wholly Aglianico and aged three years."},{"term":"Aglianico del Taburno","means":"Aglianico from the Taburno massif near Benevento, at least 85 per cent; a DOCG since 2011."},{"term":"Campi Taurasini","means":"Younger Aglianico from the Taurasi communes, released sooner under the wider Irpinia DOC."},{"term":"Riserva","means":"Longer ageing: four years for Taurasi with eighteen months in wood, five for Vulture Superiore."}],"guest":[{"q":"Why do people call it the Barolo of the South?","a":"For its structure: high acidity and a firm tannin that want years, as Nebbiolo does. The wine differs: deeper in colour, with black cherry and smoke where Barolo shows rose petal and red cherry."},{"q":"Is Taurasi the same wine as Aglianico del Vulture?","a":"The same grape from two regions. Taurasi grows in the hills of Campania on clay and limestone with volcanic layers; Vulture grows on the ash and tuff of an extinct volcano in Basilicata."},{"q":"Will it be too tannic for tonight?","a":"A young one grips. An hour in a decanter and a rich dish of lamb or a slow ragù settle it; a bottle ten years old has already softened."}],"compare":[{"slug":"nebbiolo","tell":"Nebbiolo is paler, with rose petal and red cherry; Aglianico is deeper in colour and darker in fruit, with black cherry, black plum and smoke."},{"slug":"sagrantino","tell":"Sagrantino is blacker and grips even harder, with incense, cocoa and dried violet; Aglianico carries more acidity and a smoky, iron edge."},{"slug":"nero-d-avola","tell":"Nero d'Avola is softer and rounder, with mulberry, liquorice and gentle tannin; Aglianico grips harder, with higher acidity, tar and smoke."}],"cards":[{"id":"gt-vuqa9siw","front":"Aglianico: which two southern wines does it make, and in which regions?","back":"Taurasi, in the hills of Irpinia in Campania, and Aglianico del Vulture, on an extinct volcano in Basilicata. Aglianico del Taburno, near Benevento, is a third."},{"id":"gt-kfrp9j0f","front":"Taurasi: how much Aglianico must it hold, and how long before release?","back":"At least 85 per cent Aglianico, aged three years with at least one in wood. The Riserva ages four years, eighteen months of them in wood."},{"id":"gt-5ilgjp8l","front":"Why is Aglianico picked so late, and when does its harvest fall?","back":"It buds early and ripens late, and its hard tannin needs the whole season. In Irpinia and on Vulture the harvest runs into late October and November."}],"questions":[{"id":"gt-5l0e1eed","q":"A red poured blind is deep ruby, with black cherry, tar and smoke, high acidity and a firm, drying tannin. The guest says it is from southern Italy. Which grape is it?","opts":["Nerello Mascalese","Aglianico","Primitivo","Nero d'Avola"],"a":1,"exp":"High acidity with a hard, drying tannin, tar and smoke point to Aglianico. Nero d'Avola, the nearest miss, is softer and rounder, with ripe black cherry and gentler tannin."},{"id":"gt-ekufmp9z","q":"A guest who loves Taurasi asks for the same grape from another region, grown on the slopes of an extinct volcano. Which wine do you bring?","opts":["Montefalco Sagrantino","Etna Rosso","Cirò Rosso","Aglianico del Vulture"],"a":3,"exp":"Aglianico del Vulture is Aglianico from Monte Vulture in Basilicata. Etna Rosso, the nearest miss, is volcanic too but is Nerello Mascalese, from an active volcano in Sicily."},{"id":"gt-qe75i80y","q":"A table has ordered slow roasted lamb and asks for a southern Italian red with the grip to meet it. Which bottle do you bring?","opts":["Etna Rosato","Cerasuolo di Vittoria","Taurasi","Lambrusco di Sorbara"],"a":2,"exp":"Taurasi is Aglianico, with high acidity and a firm, drying tannin that the lamb's fat and protein soften. Cerasuolo di Vittoria, the nearest miss, is a southern red too, but light and soft in tannin."}],"sources":[{"title":"Federdoc: the wine denominations of Campania","url":"https://www.federdoc.com/new/regioni/en/Campania.pdf"},{"title":"AIS: production rules of the DOCG Aglianico del Vulture Superiore","url":"https://aisitalia.it/Disciplinari/Basilicata/DOCG/Aglianico%20del%20Vulture%20Superiore.pdf"},{"title":"Wine Companion: what is Aglianico","url":"https://winecompanion.com.au/resources/varieties/aglianico"},{"title":"The Oxford Companion to Wine","url":""}]},
"blaufrankisch": {"slug":"blaufrankisch","name":"Blaufränkisch","colour":"red","kind":"grape","status":"full","mode":"home","aliases":[],"notIn":[],"say":"BLOW-fren-kish, in Hungary KAYK-frahn-kosh, in Germany LEM-bair-ger","lines":{"s10":"Blaufränkisch is the serious red of Austria's Burgenland: black cherry, blackberry and cracked black pepper on bright acidity and a firm grip.","s20":"Blaufränkisch is central Europe's dark, peppery red, at home in Burgenland on Austria's eastern edge. It tastes of black cherry and blackberry with cracked black pepper, and its bright acidity and firm tannin let it age for years.","s45":"Blaufränkisch is at home in Burgenland, the eastern edge of Austria along the Hungarian border, and it is the country's red with backbone. It tastes of black cherry and blackberry with a note of cracked black pepper, on bright acidity, firm tannin and a medium to full body. The lighter wines are raised in steel or large old casks and the fuller ones in oak barrels, with less new wood than a generation ago. Bring it out with roast duck, venison or a goulash rich with paprika. Hungary calls the same grape Kékfrankos and Germany calls it Lemberger, so you may have met it under another name.","sale":"You may know Blaufränkisch as Lemberger or Kékfrankos: black cherry and cracked pepper, kept bright by a cool climate's acidity. I would pour it with the roast duck or the venison."},"glass":{"lead":"Black cherry and blackberry with cracked black pepper and a cool edge of iron, on bright acidity and firm tannin.","notes":[{"family":"black fruit","notes":["black cherry","blackberry","elderberry"]},{"family":"red fruit","notes":["sour cherry","cranberry","redcurrant"]},{"family":"spice","notes":["cracked black pepper","clove"]},{"family":"mineral","notes":["iron","graphite","wet slate"]},{"family":"oak","notes":["vanilla pod","cocoa"]}],"ladder":[{"climate":"cool","where":"Sopron by Lake Fertő, Württemberg and the Finger Lakes","notes":["sour cherry","cranberry","cracked black pepper"]},{"climate":"moderate","where":"Burgenland, above all the warm clay basin of Mittelburgenland","notes":["black cherry","blackberry","cocoa"]}],"structure":"Dry. Acidity high, tannin medium plus and firm, alcohol medium, body medium to full."},"worlds":{"home":{"home":"Burgenland, on Austria's border with Hungary. Mittelburgenland, on deep clay, gives the darker, broader wines; the Leithaberg, on limestone and schist, and the Eisenberg, on iron rich slate, give tauter, more peppery ones. Over the border, Sopron in Hungary makes it as Kékfrankos, often a shade lighter, and Eger and Szekszárd build their Bikavér blends on it.","abroad":"Germany grows it as Lemberger in Württemberg, from light and fruity to firm and tannic. Washington State and New York's Finger Lakes bottle it under either name. Slovenia knows it as Modra Frankinja and Slovakia as Frankovka."}},"service":{"temp":"59 to 63 degrees Fahrenheit, 15 to 17 degrees Celsius: a touch cooler than a Cabernet.","glass":"A Burgundy bowl for the perfume and the pepper; a Bordeaux glass for a dark, oaked Reserve.","decant":"Consider it for a young, firm single vineyard wine, after tasting a sample.","age":"Five to fifteen years for the serious wines; the lighter ones drink well young."},"food":{"with":[{"dish":"Roast duck","why":"bright acidity cuts the fat, pepper meets the skin"},{"dish":"Venison or wild boar","why":"black cherry and an iron edge suit game"},{"dish":"Goulash or a paprika stew","why":"the dish of its home, paprika meeting pepper"},{"dish":"Grilled lamb chops","why":"firm tannin takes the char and the fat"}]},"labels":[{"term":"Kékfrankos","means":"The Hungarian name: the signature red of Sopron and the backbone of the Bikavér blends of Eger and Szekszárd."},{"term":"Lemberger","means":"The German name, at home in Württemberg and used in Washington and New York too."},{"term":"Mittelburgenland","means":"The Burgenland origin that admits this grape alone; the growers call it Blaufränkischland."},{"term":"Leithaberg, Eisenberg","means":"Two more Burgenland origins led by the grape: limestone and schist, and iron rich slate."}],"guest":[{"q":"I like Syrah. Is this anything like it?","a":"It is a near neighbour. You get the same cracked black pepper and blackberry, but Blaufränkisch is lighter on its feet, with brighter acidity and none of Syrah's smoked meat and black olive."},{"q":"Lemberger? Isn't that the cheese?","a":"No, the cheese is Limburger. Lemberger is the German name of this grape, and the likeness is one reason some American growers now print Blaufränkisch on the label."},{"q":"Will it keep, or should we drink it now?","a":"Both. A simple bottle is ready on release. A single vineyard wine from Burgenland softens over five to fifteen years and turns towards violet and graphite."}],"compare":[{"slug":"zweigelt","tell":"Zweigelt, its offspring, is softer and juicier, with sour cherry and medium tannin; Blaufränkisch is darker, more peppery, higher in acidity and firmer."},{"slug":"syrah","tell":"Syrah is fuller and meatier, with black olive and smoked meat beside its pepper; Blaufränkisch is leaner, with higher acidity and a sour cherry edge."},{"slug":"cabernet-franc","tell":"Cabernet Franc shows green bell pepper, pencil shavings and raspberry; Blaufränkisch has cracked black pepper instead, with black cherry and brighter acidity."}],"cards":[{"id":"gt-iyr4tmab","front":"Blaufränkisch: its name in Hungary, and its name in Germany?","back":"Kékfrankos in Hungary, where Sopron is called its capital and it is the backbone of Egri Bikavér. Lemberger in Germany, grown chiefly in Württemberg."},{"id":"gt-5e4l0618","front":"Blaufränkisch: name the three Burgenland origins led by it, with their soils.","back":"Mittelburgenland, on deep clay: dark and broad. Leithaberg, on limestone and schist: taut and stony. Eisenberg, on iron rich slate: peppery and firm. Rosalia admits it beside Zweigelt."},{"id":"gt-vts3ctim","front":"Blaufränkisch blind: what gives it away, and what is it mistaken for?","back":"Black cherry and cracked black pepper on high acidity and firm tannin. Syrah is meatier, with black olive; Zweigelt is softer and juicier."}],"questions":[{"id":"gt-4st18u5s","q":"A deep ruby red poured blind shows black cherry, blackberry and a clear note of cracked black pepper, with high acidity, firm tannin and no smoked meat or olive. The taster calls central Europe. Which grape fits?","opts":["Cabernet Franc","Zweigelt","Blaufränkisch","Syrah"],"a":2,"exp":"Pepper, black cherry, high acidity and firm tannin mark Blaufränkisch. Zweigelt, the nearest wrong answer, is softer and juicier, and Syrah would be meatier, with black olive."},{"id":"gt-hkbz6ww5","q":"A guest back from Budapest asks for the grape he drank there as Kékfrankos. The list holds four Austrian reds. Which do you bring?","opts":["Blaufränkisch","Saint Laurent","Blauburgunder","Zweigelt"],"a":0,"exp":"Kékfrankos is Hungary's name for Blaufränkisch. Zweigelt is its offspring, a crossing with Saint Laurent, and Blauburgunder is Austria's name for Pinot Noir."},{"id":"gt-5m6lfy6o","q":"A guest wants a Blaufränkisch in the dark, ripe, broad style that comes from deep clay in a sheltered basin. Which origin do you look for?","opts":["Eisenberg","Mittelburgenland","Neusiedlersee","Leithaberg"],"a":1,"exp":"Mittelburgenland's deep clay and sheltered warmth give the darker, broader wines. Eisenberg, on iron rich slate, and the Leithaberg, on limestone and schist, are tauter, and the Neusiedlersee is Zweigelt country."}],"sources":[{"title":"Austrian Wine: Blaufränkisch","url":"https://www.austrianwine.com/our-wine/grape-varieties/red-wine/blaufraenkisch"},{"title":"Austrian Wine: Mittelburgenland DAC","url":"https://www.austrianwine.com/our-wine/strategy-for-origin-marketing/dac-districtus-austriae-controllatus/dac-regions/mittelburgenland-dac"},{"title":"Wines of Germany: Lemberger","url":"https://www.winesofgermany.com/our-wine/grape-varieties/grape-variety/89/lemberger"},{"title":"Wine Enthusiast: the rise of Blaufränkisch wine","url":"https://www.wineenthusiast.com/2019/01/09/the-rise-of-blaufrankisch-wine/"}]},
"nerello-mascalese": {"slug":"nerello-mascalese","name":"Nerello Mascalese","colour":"red","kind":"grape","status":"full","mode":"home","aliases":[],"notIn":[],"say":"neh-REL-loh mah-skah-LEH-zeh","lines":{"s10":"Nerello Mascalese is the red of Mount Etna, pale as a Pinot Noir, with sour cherry, blood orange and a smoky edge.","s20":"Nerello Mascalese grows on the black lava slopes of Mount Etna in Sicily, often from old bush vines. It pours pale, with sour cherry, wild strawberry and blood orange, and grips with high acidity and a fine, dusty tannin.","s45":"Nerello Mascalese climbs Mount Etna on terraces of black lava, past a thousand metres above the Sicilian sea. Many of the vines are old bushes, never grafted. It looks pale, like a Pinot Noir, and tastes of sour cherry, wild strawberry and blood orange, with a smoky, ashy edge. The acidity is high and the tannin fine but firm. Most is aged gently, often in large old casks, so the place speaks louder than the wood. Pour it with grilled tuna, wild mushrooms or roast lamb. Mascali, a town below the volcano's eastern slope, gave the grape its name.","sale":"Nerello Mascalese has the perfume of a Pinot Noir and the grip of a Nebbiolo, with a smoky edge from the volcano. It wants a slight chill, beside the grilled tuna or the mushrooms."},"glass":{"lead":"Sour cherry, wild strawberry and blood orange with rose and volcanic ash, pale garnet, on high acidity and a fine, dusty tannin.","notes":[{"family":"red fruit","notes":["sour cherry","red cherry","wild strawberry"]},{"family":"citrus","notes":["blood orange","orange peel"]},{"family":"floral","notes":["rose petal","dried rose"]},{"family":"herbal","notes":["dried thyme","dried oregano"]},{"family":"mineral","notes":["volcanic ash","smoke","iron"]},{"family":"age","notes":["dried mushroom","old leather"]}],"ladder":[{"climate":"cool","where":"The high north slope of Etna, from Randazzo to Castiglione di Sicilia","notes":["sour cherry","redcurrant","dried rose","volcanic ash"]},{"climate":"moderate","where":"Etna's lower south east slope and the hills of Faro","notes":["red cherry","wild strawberry","blood orange"]}],"structure":"Dry. Acidity high, tannin medium plus, fine and dusty, alcohol medium, body medium; pale garnet, often translucent."},"worlds":{"home":{"home":"Mount Etna in Sicily, on terraces of black lava from about 400 to over 1,000 metres, with many old bush vines on their own roots. Etna Rosso is at least 80 per cent Nerello Mascalese, often aged in large old casks, and may name its contrada. Faro, above Messina, is its other home.","abroad":"It hardly leaves Sicily: a little grows across the strait in Calabria, and almost nowhere else."}},"service":{"temp":"59 to 63 degrees Fahrenheit, 15 to 17 degrees Celsius; a young bottle at the cool end.","glass":"A wide Burgundy bowl, to gather the rose and red cherry perfume.","decant":"Consider it for a young contrada wine after a taste; it opens much as a Nebbiolo does.","age":"Five to fifteen years for Etna Rosso from a named contrada; simpler bottles usually within five."},"food":{"with":[{"dish":"Grilled tuna or swordfish","why":"fine tannin and high acidity suit a meaty fish"},{"dish":"Wild mushrooms or a mushroom risotto","why":"smoke and dried thyme meet the mushrooms' depth"},{"dish":"Roast lamb with rosemary","why":"acidity and a firm grip cut the fat"},{"dish":"Roast duck","why":"sour cherry and acidity lift the rich meat"}]},"labels":[{"term":"Etna Rosso","means":"At least 80 per cent Nerello Mascalese, with Nerello Cappuccio up to a fifth, from the slopes of Etna."},{"term":"Contrada","means":"A named district of Etna, often one lava flow of one age, printed on the label like a cru."},{"term":"Nerello Cappuccio","means":"The darker, softer partner grape, allowed to a fifth of Etna Rosso for colour and flesh."},{"term":"Faro","means":"A red led by Nerello Mascalese from the hills above Messina, at Sicily's north eastern tip."},{"term":"Etna Rosato, Etna Spumante","means":"A rosé, and a sparkling wine made in the bottle, both from Nerello Mascalese."}],"guest":[{"q":"It is so pale. Is it a light wine?","a":"Pale, but not slight. Nerello Mascalese carries little colour, yet high acidity and a fine, firm tannin give it a grip much like a Nebbiolo or a mountain Pinot Noir."},{"q":"Does the volcano change the taste?","a":"Many tasters find a smoky, ashy edge. The height matters as much: vineyards up to over 1,000 metres give cool nights, a late harvest and bright acidity."},{"q":"What does contrada mean on the label?","a":"A named district on the slopes of Etna, often one lava flow of one age, so it works much like a cru in Burgundy."}],"compare":[{"slug":"pinot-noir","tell":"Pinot Noir is silkier, with forest floor and softer tannin; Nerello Mascalese has a firmer, dustier grip, with blood orange and volcanic ash."},{"slug":"nebbiolo","tell":"Nebbiolo has tar and a fiercer, drying tannin; Nerello Mascalese is gentler in grip, with blood orange, dried thyme and volcanic ash."},{"slug":"nero-d-avola","tell":"Nero d'Avola is deep in colour, soft and round, with mulberry and liquorice; Nerello Mascalese is pale and taut, with sour cherry, blood orange and high acidity."}],"cards":[{"id":"gt-nu8hzbp0","front":"Nerello Mascalese: where is it at home, and how high do its vines climb?","back":"Mount Etna in Sicily, on terraces of black lava from about 400 to over 1,000 metres. Faro, above Messina, is a second home."},{"id":"gt-4yf9f5kh","front":"Etna Rosso: what share of Nerello Mascalese, and which partner grape?","back":"At least 80 per cent Nerello Mascalese, with Nerello Cappuccio up to a fifth for colour and flesh. Etna became Sicily's first DOC in 1968."},{"id":"gt-1qejtb3a","front":"Nerello Mascalese: what has DNA shown of its parents, and whence its name?","back":"DNA work points to Sangiovese and Mantonico Bianco, a Calabrian white, as its parents. The name comes from Mascali, below Etna's eastern slope."}],"questions":[{"id":"gt-iwjjd5al","q":"A red poured blind is pale garnet, with sour cherry, blood orange and an ashy, smoky edge, high acidity and a fine, dusty tannin. It is Italian. Which grape is it?","opts":["Nero d'Avola","Nerello Mascalese","Montepulciano","Nebbiolo"],"a":1,"exp":"Pale colour, blood orange and ash on high acidity and a fine, dusty tannin point to Nerello Mascalese. Nebbiolo, the nearest miss, is as pale but brings tar and a fiercer, drying grip."},{"id":"gt-2ba1mplk","q":"A guest asks what the word contrada means on a bottle of Etna Rosso. What do you tell her?","opts":["A wine made from old bush vines","A wine aged four years before release","A sparkling wine made in the bottle","A named district on one lava flow, like a cru"],"a":3,"exp":"A contrada is a named district on Etna's slopes, often one lava flow of one age, printed like a cru. Longer ageing is marked by Riserva, the nearest miss, not by contrada."},{"id":"gt-aau4n3k0","q":"A guest who loves red Burgundy wants an Italian red with the same pale colour and lift, and a volcanic edge. Which wine do you bring?","opts":["Etna Rosso","Montefalco Sagrantino","Cerasuolo di Vittoria","Taurasi"],"a":0,"exp":"Etna Rosso, Nerello Mascalese from the slopes of Etna, is pale, high in acidity and ashy. Cerasuolo di Vittoria, the nearest miss, is light too but grows on red sand and is softer and fruitier."}],"sources":[{"title":"AIS: production rules of the DOC Etna","url":"https://aisitalia.it/Disciplinari/Sicilia/DOC/Etna.pdf"},{"title":"Molecular Biotechnology, through PubMed: the parentage of Nerello Mascalese and related southern Italian grapes","url":"https://www.pubmed.gov/23001829/"},{"title":"Gambero Rosso: rare wines, Nerello Mascalese between Calabria and Sicily","url":"https://www.gamberorosso.it/vini/vini-rari/vini-rari-il-ponte-tra-calabria-e-sicilia-si-chiama-nerello-mascalese"},{"title":"Wine Grapes","url":""}]},
"touriga-nacional": {"slug":"touriga-nacional","name":"Touriga Nacional","colour":"red","kind":"grape","status":"full","mode":"home","aliases":[],"notIn":[],"say":"too-REE-gah nah-see-oo-NAHL","lines":{"s10":"Touriga Nacional is the backbone of Port and the Douro's dry reds: blackberry and violet with a lift of bergamot.","s20":"Touriga Nacional is the red grape at the heart of Port and of Portugal's dry reds, from the Douro and the Dão. It is deep purple and full, with blackberry and violet, a lift of bergamot and a firm, velvety tannin.","s45":"Touriga Nacional gives Port its backbone, and it leads many of Portugal's dry reds. It was born in the Dão, they say near the village of Tourigo, and made its name in the Douro. The glass is nearly black, with blackberry, violet and a lift of bergamot. It is full in body, with a firm, velvety tannin. Its small, thick skinned berries give that depth, and time in French oak rounds it out. The Dão makes it finer and more perfumed, the Douro fuller. It suits roast lamb, game or hard cheese. Bordeaux now trials it as its summers grow hotter.","sale":"Touriga Nacional is Port's own grape made dry: all the blackberry and violet, none of the sweetness, with a bergamot lift that keeps it bright. Decanted now, it will be ready for the lamb or the game."},"glass":{"lead":"Blackberry, blueberry and violet with a lift of bergamot, nearly opaque and full bodied, over a firm, velvety tannin.","notes":[{"family":"black fruit","notes":["blackberry","black plum","cassis"]},{"family":"floral","notes":["violet","rockrose"]},{"family":"citrus","notes":["bergamot","orange peel"]},{"family":"mineral","notes":["slate","schist"]},{"family":"oak","notes":["dark chocolate","cedar"]}],"ladder":[{"climate":"moderate","where":"The Dão's granite plateau and Bairrada","notes":["violet","bergamot","blackberry"]},{"climate":"warm","where":"The Douro's schist, the Alentejo and Rutherglen","notes":["black plum","cassis","dark chocolate"]}],"structure":"Dry. Acidity medium plus, tannin high and velvety, alcohol medium plus, body full, and nearly opaque in colour."},"worlds":{"home":{"home":"Portugal. The Dão claims its birth near Tourigo, and on that granite plateau it is finer and more perfumed; in the Douro's schist it is fuller and leads both Port and the dry reds. Since the 1990s it has spread south to Lisboa, the Tejo and the Alentejo.","abroad":"Rutherglen in Australia grows it for vintage fortified wine with Shiraz and Durif, South Africa's Little Karoo for fortified and dry reds, and Bordeaux has allowed it on trial since 2021."}},"service":{"temp":"61 to 64 degrees Fahrenheit, 16 to 18 degrees Celsius, as for any full red or Vintage Port.","glass":"A tall Bordeaux glass for the dry wines; a small tulip for Port.","decant":"Consider it for a young dry red after tasting a sample; decant a Vintage Port off its crust.","age":"The serious dry reds keep ten years and more; Vintage Port lasts for decades."},"food":{"with":[{"dish":"Roast lamb or kid","why":"firm tannin meets fat and herbs"},{"dish":"Venison or wild boar","why":"blackberry and a firm grip suit dark, rich meat"},{"dish":"Braised beef cheeks","why":"slow cooked richness softens the tannin"},{"dish":"Stilton, with a Vintage Port","why":"the Port's sweet black plum against salty blue cheese"}]},"labels":[{"term":"Douro","means":"The Port valley's dry wines, often blends led by Touriga Nacional and Touriga Franca."},{"term":"Dão","means":"The granite plateau around Viseu, where Touriga Nacional is finer, often blended with Jaen and Alfrocheiro."},{"term":"Vintage Port","means":"Port from one declared year, bottled young and unfiltered; it throws a crust and is decanted."},{"term":"Vinhas velhas","means":"Old vines, often a mixed field blend in which Touriga Nacional is one grape of many."}],"guest":[{"q":"Is this the grape they make Port from?","a":"Yes, one of the main ones. This wine fermented all the way to dry, so you have the blackberry and violet of Port without its sweetness or the added grape spirit."},{"q":"Why does it smell faintly of orange peel?","a":"That lift is typical of Touriga Nacional: bergamot and orange peel over the blackberry and violet. It keeps a full, dark wine smelling bright."},{"q":"Which is better, the Douro or the Dão?","a":"Different, not better. The Douro's hot schist gives a fuller, darker wine; the Dão's cooler granite plateau gives more perfume, finer tannin and a little less weight."}],"compare":[{"slug":"touriga-franca","tell":"Touriga Franca, its child, is lighter and juicier, with rose petal and pomegranate; Touriga Nacional is darker and firmer, with violet and bergamot."},{"slug":"syrah","tell":"Syrah brings cracked black pepper, black olive and smoked meat; Touriga Nacional has no such savoury side, and bergamot and orange peel lift its blackberry."},{"slug":"malbec","tell":"Malbec shares the violet and the dark colour but lacks the bergamot lift, and its tannin is softer and plusher than Touriga Nacional's firm grip."}],"cards":[{"id":"gt-spra21dq","front":"Touriga Nacional: which two Portuguese regions are home, and how do they differ?","back":"The Dão, where it is said to have been born and is finer and more perfumed, and the Douro, where it is fuller and leads Port and the dry reds."},{"id":"gt-w6ata3ii","front":"Touriga Nacional blind: which aromas give it away beside its blackberry?","back":"Violet with a lift of bergamot and orange peel, sometimes rockrose, over deep blackberry and black plum and a firm, velvety tannin."},{"id":"gt-v780rx97","front":"Why did Touriga Nacional nearly vanish, and what brought it back?","back":"Its small berries give very low yields, so growers chose other grapes after phylloxera. Research in the Douro in the 1970s and 1980s named it among five favoured Port grapes."}],"questions":[{"id":"gt-qh6eiuul","q":"A dry red poured blind is nearly opaque and full bodied, with blackberry, violet and a striking lift of bergamot, a firm, velvety tannin and fresh acidity. Which grape is it?","opts":["Touriga Nacional","Petit Verdot","Malbec","Cabernet Sauvignon"],"a":0,"exp":"Violet with a bergamot lift over deep blackberry marks Touriga Nacional. Malbec, the nearest miss, shares the violet and the colour but not the bergamot, and its tannin is softer."},{"id":"gt-wz7nghdv","q":"A guest who loves Vintage Port asks for a dry red led by the same main grape. Which bottle do you bring?","opts":["An Alentejo red led by Alicante Bouschet","A Bairrada red led by Baga","A Setúbal red led by Castelão","A Douro red led by Touriga Nacional"],"a":3,"exp":"Touriga Nacional is a main Port grape and leads many dry Douro reds. Baga, the nearest miss, is Bairrada's own grape and plays no part in Port."},{"id":"gt-spa3kjjv","q":"A guest notices Touriga Nacional among the grapes Bordeaux has allowed on trial since 2021 and asks why it was chosen. What do you tell him?","opts":["It is a parent of Cabernet Sauvignon","It ripens early in cool, wet years","It copes with heat and drought","It gives a pale, light red"],"a":2,"exp":"Bordeaux admitted it on trial against a warming climate, and in the Douro it thrives where heat and drought are the hazards. Ripening early in cool years, the nearest wrong answer, is not its strength."}],"sources":[{"title":"Stellenbosch University: Touriga Nacional and environment interaction in the Little Karoo","url":"https://scholar.sun.ac.za/handle/10019.1/2877"},{"title":"Wine Companion: Touriga Nacional, varietals and styles","url":"https://winecompanion.com.au/resources/varietals/touriga-nacional"},{"title":"Vine to Wine Circle: Touriga Nacional","url":"https://www.vinetowinecircle.com/en/castas_post/nacional-touriga"},{"title":"The Oxford Companion to Wine","url":""}]},
"carignan": {"slug":"carignan","name":"Carignan","colour":"red","kind":"grape","status":"full","mode":"contrast","aliases":["Carignane"],"notIn":[],"say":"kah-reen-YAHN, in Spain kah-ree-NYEH-nah","lines":{"s10":"Carignan is the old vine red of the Languedoc: black raspberry and cranberry, dried thyme, and a bite of acidity and tannin together.","s20":"Carignan is a Mediterranean red that keeps high acidity and firm tannin even in great heat. Old vines in the Languedoc, Spain and Sardinia give it depth: black raspberry, cranberry and dried thyme, in a wine made for rich, rustic food.","s45":"Carignan was long the workhorse of the Languedoc, and old hillside vines have made its name. It tastes of black raspberry, cranberry and dried cherry, with dried thyme and liquorice. It is deep in colour, with high acidity and firm, rustic tannin. Many cellars ferment the berries whole in a sealed vat, which softens that grip. The Languedoc and Spain tend to be savoury, while old vines in Chile and California are often juicier and more openly fruity. Give it cassoulet, grilled sausages or lamb. Spain, where it most likely began, calls it Cariñena or Mazuelo, and old vine growers in Chile share one name for theirs, Vigno.","sale":"Carignan bites back: black raspberry, dried thyme and a freshness that keeps a rich, slow cooked dish lively. With the cassoulet, the duck or the sausages, it is the bottle I would bring."},"glass":{"lead":"Black raspberry, cranberry and dried cherry with dried thyme, liquorice and a note of iron, deep in colour, high in acidity and tannin.","notes":[{"family":"red fruit","notes":["cranberry","sour cherry","red plum"]},{"family":"black fruit","notes":["black raspberry","blackberry","black plum"]},{"family":"dried fruit","notes":["dried cherry","prune"]},{"family":"herbal","notes":["dried thyme","rosemary","bay leaf"]},{"family":"spice","notes":["liquorice","black pepper"]},{"family":"mineral","notes":["iron","graphite"]}],"ladder":[{"climate":"moderate","where":"Maule in Chile and the high hills of Corbières","notes":["cranberry","damson","black raspberry"]},{"climate":"warm","where":"Roussillon, Priorat, Sulcis in Sardinia and Contra Costa","notes":["black plum","dried cherry","liquorice"]}],"structure":"Dry. Acidity high, tannin high and rustic, alcohol medium plus, body medium to full."},"worlds":{"contrast":{"old":{"where":"Languedoc and Roussillon; Priorat, Montsant and Rioja; Sulcis in Sardinia","glass":"Tends to be savoury and firm: black raspberry, cranberry and dried cherry with dried thyme, liquorice and iron, high acidity and rustic tannin. Old vine Priorat and Sulcis are typically darker, riper and denser.","cellar":"Mostly a partner in blends with Grenache, Syrah and Mourvèdre; in the Languedoc often fermented whole, by carbonic maceration, to soften the tannin. Rioja uses a little, as Mazuelo."},"new":{"where":"Maule in Chile; Mendocino, Sonoma and Contra Costa in California","glass":"Typically juicier and purer in fruit: damson, dark cherry and cranberry with a little dried thyme, the same high acidity and a firm but finer grip.","cellar":"Usually bottled under the grape's name, from old, dry farmed bush vines: in Maule some as VIGNO, in California often from old mixed vineyards with Zinfandel."},"why":"Carignan ripens late and keeps its acidity, so both worlds grow it in heat. The Old World blends it and names the place. Chile and California bottle old vines, once used for bulk wine, alone under the grape's name.","blur":"Old vine Carignan from the Languedoc, Roussillon or Priorat is now often bottled alone and can be as bright as Maule, and warm California sites give dense, savoury wines.","call":"Iron and a rustic, savoury grip lean Old World; purer, juicier damson and dark cherry lean Maule or California. Both keep high acidity."}},"service":{"temp":"59 to 63 degrees Fahrenheit, 15 to 17 degrees Celsius: a little cool keeps the fruit bright.","glass":"A Bordeaux glass, or a universal glass for a lighter, juicy style.","decant":"Consider it for a young, firm old vine wine after tasting a sample; a light, juicy style needs none.","age":"Most within five years; old vine wines reward five to fifteen."},"food":{"with":[{"dish":"Cassoulet","why":"acidity and grip to stand beside beans, duck and sausage"},{"dish":"Grilled sausages or merguez","why":"smoke, fat and paprika meet a rustic red"},{"dish":"Roast or braised lamb with herbs","why":"the wine's dried thyme echoes the dish"},{"dish":"Charcuterie and hard sheep's milk cheese","why":"salt and fat ease the firm tannin"}]},"labels":[{"term":"Carignane","means":"The spelling used in California, with a final e, for the same grape."},{"term":"Cariñena, Mazuelo, Samsó","means":"Its Spanish names: Mazuelo in Rioja, Samsó in Catalonia; Cariñena is also a place in Aragon."},{"term":"Carignano del Sulcis","means":"Carignan of south west Sardinia, much of it from old, ungrafted bush vines in coastal sand."},{"term":"VIGNO","means":"A name shared by growers in Maule, Chile, for Carignan from old vines farmed without irrigation."},{"term":"Vieilles Vignes","means":"Old vines, in French: no age is fixed by law, but with Carignan it is the phrase to look for."}],"guest":[{"q":"I have never heard of Carignan. What is it like?","a":"A deep, tangy Mediterranean red: black raspberry and cranberry with dried thyme, bright acidity and a firm grip. If you enjoy Côtes du Rhône or Chianti, you will feel at home."},{"q":"Why do the labels make so much of old vines?","a":"Young Carignan crops heavily and can taste thin and harsh. Old vines give far less fruit, so the wine has the depth to carry its acidity and tannin."},{"q":"Is Cariñena a grape or a place?","a":"Both. It is a town and a wine region in Aragon, and it is also a Spanish name for this grape, which Rioja calls Mazuelo."}],"compare":[{"slug":"grenache","tell":"Grenache is paler, softer and sweeter fruited, with kirsch and low acidity; Carignan is darker, with cranberry, high acidity and firm tannin."},{"slug":"mourvedre","tell":"Mourvèdre is darker and meatier, with leather and game and only medium acidity; Carignan is tangier, with cranberry and black raspberry."}],"cards":[{"id":"gt-7lx3wmmn","front":"Carignan: its names in Rioja, Catalonia, Sardinia and California?","back":"Mazuelo in Rioja and Samsó in Catalonia, with Cariñena elsewhere in Spain; Carignano in Sardinia; Carignane, with a final e, in California."},{"id":"gt-ta0fu11f","front":"What does the name VIGNO on a Chilean label promise?","back":"Carignan from Maule, from vines at least thirty years old, head trained and farmed without irrigation, aged two years before sale."},{"id":"gt-q99tzvgg","front":"Carignan blind: Old World or New World, what does a taster lean on?","back":"Iron, liquorice and a rustic, savoury grip lean the Languedoc, Spain or Sardinia. Purer, juicier damson and dark cherry on a finer grip lean Maule or California. Both keep high acidity."}],"questions":[{"id":"gt-n8mnsk3b","q":"Two reds from old Carignan vines are poured blind, both high in acidity. The first shows black raspberry with iron, liquorice and a rustic, savoury grip; the second is juicier and purer, with damson and dark cherry on a finer grip. Which pair of origins fits, in that order?","opts":["Maule, then Sulcis","Maule, then Corbières","Mendocino, then Priorat","Corbières, then Maule"],"a":3,"exp":"Iron and a rustic, savoury grip tend to mark the Languedoc. Juicier, purer damson and dark cherry on a finer grip tend to mark old vines in Maule, so the reverse order does not fit.","tag":"worlds"},{"id":"gt-47kcpc6x","q":"A guest sees the word Mazuelo on the back label of a Rioja and asks what it is. What do you tell her?","opts":["Grenache, the grape Spain calls Garnacha","Graciano, a perfumed grape native to Rioja","Carignan, used in small shares for acidity and tannin","Tempranillo, under its local Rioja name"],"a":2,"exp":"Mazuelo is the Rioja name for Carignan, a tannic, acidic grape blended in small shares. Tempranillo leads Rioja under its own name, and Garnacha and Graciano are different grapes."},{"id":"gt-i5pyrxi3","q":"A blind red is deep ruby with a purple rim, with black raspberry, cranberry, dried thyme and liquorice. The acidity is high and the tannin is high and rustic. Which grape is it most likely to be?","opts":["Mourvèdre","Carignan","Grenache","Merlot"],"a":1,"exp":"High acidity with high, rustic tannin and tangy black raspberry and cranberry point to Carignan. Grenache is paler and low in acidity, and Mourvèdre is meatier with only medium acidity."}],"sources":[{"title":"Decanter: Corbières, a profile of the appellation","url":"https://www.decanter.com/premium/corbieres-profile-and-wines-406475/"},{"title":"Napa Valley Wine Academy: Chile's Carignan renaissance","url":"https://napavalleywineacademy.com/chiles-carignan-renaissance/"},{"title":"The Oxford Companion to Wine, entry on Carignan","url":""},{"title":"Wine Grapes, entry on Mazuelo","url":""}]},
"mourvedre": {"slug":"mourvedre","name":"Mourvèdre / Monastrell","colour":"red","kind":"grape","status":"full","mode":"contrast","aliases":[],"notIn":[],"say":"moor-VED-ruh, in Spain moh-nahs-TREL","lines":{"s10":"Mourvèdre is the dark, meaty red of Bandol on the Provence coast, blackberry and leather on a firm grip, known in Spain as Monastrell.","s20":"Mourvèdre is a late ripening black grape that needs real heat, at home in Bandol and, as Monastrell, in south eastern Spain. It gives blackberry, black plum and a gamey, leathery depth, with firm tannin that asks for food or for time.","s45":"Mourvèdre ripens very late, so it only thrives where the summer is long and hot. Its French home is Bandol, on the Provence coast, and Spain, where it began, calls it Monastrell. It is nearly black, tasting of blackberry and black plum, with leather and a gamey, meaty note. It is full bodied, with a firm, drying grip. Bandol ages it at least eighteen months in wood, and tends to be sterner than the riper wines of Spain, California or Australia. Choose it for braised lamb, grilled sausages or game. Bandol growers say it wants its head in the sun and its feet in the water.","sale":"Mourvèdre suits a table that likes its red dark and a little wild: blackberry, leather and a gamey depth. Have it with the lamb or the game, and give it time in the glass."},"glass":{"lead":"Blackberry and black plum under leather, black pepper and a gamey, meaty depth, nearly black, full and firmly tannic.","notes":[{"family":"black fruit","notes":["blackberry","black plum"]},{"family":"dried fruit","notes":["raisin","dried fig"]},{"family":"savoury","notes":["game","cured meat"]},{"family":"spice","notes":["black pepper","liquorice"]},{"family":"herbal","notes":["dried thyme","rosemary","bay leaf"]},{"family":"earth","notes":["leather","truffle","forest floor"]}],"ladder":[{"climate":"moderate","where":"Bandol, where the sea tempers the heat","notes":["blackberry","leather","dried thyme","black pepper"]},{"climate":"warm","where":"Jumilla, Yecla and Alicante, the Barossa and Paso Robles","notes":["blackberry jam","raisin","liquorice","dark chocolate"]}],"structure":"Dry. Acidity medium, tannin high and gripping, alcohol medium plus to high, body full."},"worlds":{"contrast":{"old":{"where":"Bandol and the southern Rhône; Jumilla, Yecla and Alicante as Monastrell","glass":"Bandol tends to be stern and savoury: blackberry and black plum under leather, game, dried thyme and black pepper, with high, gripping tannin that needs years. Spanish Monastrell is usually riper, with blackberry jam, dried fig and higher alcohol.","cellar":"Bandol red is at least half Mourvèdre, with Grenache and Cinsault, and spends eighteen months or more in wood, by tradition large old casks."},"new":{"where":"Barossa and McLaren Vale as Mataro; Contra Costa and Paso Robles; Washington","glass":"Typically riper and fuller in fruit: blackberry and black plum with dark chocolate and liquorice, at higher alcohol, the meaty note there but less to the fore. On a list it is most often the third grape of a GSM.","cellar":"Mostly blended with Grenache and Shiraz or Syrah; a few wines from very old vines are bottled alone, as Mataro or Mourvèdre."},"why":"Mourvèdre ripens very late, so both worlds plant it in heat. Bandol's law asks for a high share of the grape and long ageing in wood, hence firm wines for keeping. Elsewhere it is picked riper and mostly blended.","blur":"Jumilla sits in the Old World yet gives ripe, sweet fruited Monastrell, and old vine wines from Contra Costa or the Barossa can be as firm and gamey as Bandol.","call":"Dried thyme, leather and a stern grip with fresher fruit lean Bandol; blackberry jam, dried fig and higher alcohol lean Spain or the New World."}},"service":{"temp":"61 to 64 degrees Fahrenheit, 16 to 18 degrees Celsius; Bandol rosé cold, as a white.","glass":"A Bordeaux glass for the red; a white wine glass for Bandol rosé.","decant":"Consider it for a young Bandol after tasting a sample; air can lift a closed, gamey first scent.","age":"Bandol rewards ten years and more; most Monastrell and New World blends are ready within five."},"food":{"with":[{"dish":"Braised lamb shoulder","why":"richness and long cooking to match the grip"},{"dish":"Venison, wild boar or duck","why":"the wine's own gamey note meets the meat"},{"dish":"Grilled sausages and smoked meats","why":"fat and salt ease the firm tannin"},{"dish":"Shrimp remoulade, with Bandol rosé","why":"a rosé with the weight for mustard and paprika"}]},"labels":[{"term":"Monastrell","means":"The grape's Spanish name, on wines from Jumilla, Yecla and Alicante."},{"term":"Mataro","means":"The old name in Australia and California; many labels there now say Mourvèdre."},{"term":"Bandol","means":"A Provence place name: the red is at least half Mourvèdre, and the rosé holds it too."},{"term":"GSM","means":"Grenache, Syrah and Mourvèdre: in the blend Mourvèdre brings colour, grip and a savoury depth."}],"guest":[{"q":"Why does this wine smell a little gamey?","a":"That is Mourvèdre. It often carries a meaty, leathery scent, strongest when the wine is young and closed, and a little air usually lets the blackberry come forward."},{"q":"Is Monastrell the same grape as Mourvèdre?","a":"Yes. Monastrell is its Spanish name, and Spain is where the grape began. Australia and California long called it Mataro."},{"q":"Is Bandol only a rosé?","a":"No. Bandol is known for rosé, but its red is the serious one: at least half Mourvèdre, aged eighteen months in wood and made to keep for ten years or more."}],"compare":[{"slug":"syrah","tell":"Syrah is more polished and peppery, with violet and black olive; Mourvèdre is gamier and more rustic, with leather and a heavier grip."},{"slug":"grenache","tell":"Grenache is far paler and softer, with strawberry and kirsch; Mourvèdre is nearly black, with blackberry, leather and high tannin."},{"slug":"carignan","tell":"Carignan is higher in acidity, with cranberry and black raspberry; Mourvèdre is darker and meatier, with only medium acidity."}],"cards":[{"id":"gt-i0e4u249","front":"Mourvèdre: its names in Spain and in Australia, and its French home?","back":"Monastrell in Spain; Mataro in Australia and California. Its French home is Bandol, on the Provence coast."},{"id":"gt-sqphk29q","front":"Bandol red: how much Mourvèdre, and how long in wood?","back":"At least 50 per cent Mourvèdre, with Grenache and Cinsault, and at least eighteen months in wood, by tradition large old casks."},{"id":"gt-ohnbvccp","front":"Mourvèdre blind: Bandol or a riper origin, what tips it?","back":"Dried thyme, leather and a stern grip with fresher fruit lean Bandol. Blackberry jam, dried fig, chocolate and higher alcohol lean Jumilla or the New World."}],"questions":[{"id":"gt-xk6x1h7t","q":"A guest who enjoys a ripe Barossa blend of Grenache, Shiraz and Mataro asks for the Mataro grape from the Old World, in a firmer, more savoury style made for keeping. Which bottle do you bring?","opts":["Cahors","Madiran","Bandol","Cornas"],"a":2,"exp":"Bandol red is at least half Mourvèdre, the grape Australia calls Mataro, and is typically stern, savoury and long lived. Cornas is Syrah, Cahors is Malbec and Madiran is Tannat.","tag":"worlds"},{"id":"gt-ivrx0ycz","q":"A blind red is nearly black and full, with blackberry, leather, dried thyme and a gamey, meaty note, on high, gripping tannin and medium acidity. Which grape is it most likely to be?","opts":["Mourvèdre","Grenache","Syrah","Carignan"],"a":0,"exp":"Leather and game on a nearly black, heavily tannic wine point to Mourvèdre. Syrah would show more pepper and violet with a finer grip, Carignan has higher acidity and Grenache is far paler."},{"id":"gt-ogqdeqy8","q":"A guest orders a Spanish red labelled Monastrell from Jumilla and asks what to expect in the glass. What do you tell him?","opts":["Medium bodied, with cassis, cedar and green bell pepper","Dark, ripe and full, with blackberry and firm tannin","Light and gently sparkling, with strawberry and some sweetness","Pale and light, with sour cherry and high acidity"],"a":1,"exp":"Monastrell is Mourvèdre, and in hot Jumilla it gives dark, ripe, full wines with firm tannin. A pale, tart wine or one of cassis and cedar describes other grapes."}],"sources":[{"title":"Inter Rhône: Mourvèdre, the grape variety","url":"https://www.vins-rhone.com/en/mourvedre-grape-variety"},{"title":"Hachette Vins: the Bandol appellation","url":"https://www.hachette-vins.com/tout-sur-le-vin/appellations-vins/284/bandol"},{"title":"Wine Australia: Mataro, stories of Australian wine","url":"https://www.wineaustralia.com/whats-happening/Stories-of-Australian-Wine/April-2017/Mataro"},{"title":"Wine Grapes, entry on Monastrell","url":""}]},
"barbera": {"slug":"barbera","name":"Barbera","colour":"red","kind":"grape","status":"full","mode":"contrast","aliases":[],"notIn":[],"say":"bar-BEH-rah","lines":{"s10":"Barbera is Piedmont's everyday red, deep in colour and juicy with sour cherry, its acidity high and its tannin gentle.","s20":"Barbera is Piedmont's red for the dinner table, from the hills of Asti and Alba. It is dark and juicy, with sour cherry and black plum, mouthwatering acidity and very little tannin.","s45":"Barbera breaks the rule that a dark red must be a hard one. It is a red of Piedmont, at home in the hills of Asti and Alba. It pours deep purple and tastes of sour cherry and black plum, with mouthwatering acidity and very little tannin. Much of it is made simply, while the Barbera of Nizza often spends time in small oak barrels that add vanilla and a firmer frame. California Barbera, from the Sierra Foothills, tends to be riper and fuller, yet just as bright. It loves tomato sauce, salumi and pizza. In Piedmont it is the family's daily bottle, opened while the Barolo waits.","sale":"Barbera pours as dark as a serious red and drinks as juicy as a light one, sour cherry with almost no tannin. A Barbera d'Asti, a touch cool, is what I would pour with the pasta or the charcuterie."},"glass":{"lead":"Sour cherry and black plum in a deep purple glass, with mouthwatering acidity and soft, low tannin.","notes":[{"family":"red fruit","notes":["sour cherry","red plum"]},{"family":"black fruit","notes":["black cherry","blackberry","black plum"]},{"family":"herbal","notes":["balsamic","dried thyme"]},{"family":"oak","notes":["vanilla pod","clove","toast"]}],"ladder":[{"climate":"moderate","where":"The hills of Asti, Nizza and Alba, and the Monferrato","notes":["sour cherry","red plum","violet"]},{"climate":"warm","where":"The Sierra Foothills and Lodi in California, and McLaren Vale","notes":["ripe black cherry","blackberry","plum jam"]}],"structure":"Dry. Acidity high to very high, tannin low to medium, alcohol medium plus, body medium; deep in colour for so soft a red."},"worlds":{"contrast":{"old":{"where":"Piedmont's Asti, Nizza, Alba and Monferrato; Gutturnio in the Piacenza hills","glass":"Tends to be sharp and juicy: sour cherry, red plum and dried thyme over very high acidity, little tannin and medium body.","cellar":"Everyday Barbera is often kept in steel or large old casks; Nizza and Superiore spend months in wood, often small French barrels."},"new":{"where":"California's Sierra Foothills and Lodi; Australia's McLaren Vale","glass":"Typically riper and rounder: black cherry, blackberry and plum jam, with higher alcohol and often vanilla from oak, the acidity still bright beneath.","cellar":"Labelled by the grape and usually aged in oak barrels, a share of them new; much of California's crop once went into blends."},"why":"Barbera holds its acidity even in heat, so warm New World sites ripen it to darker fruit and more alcohol while it stays fresh. Piedmont's rules tie it to Asti, Nizza or Alba and their ageing laws.","blur":"A warm year's Nizza or Barbera d'Alba in new French barrels can match California's plush vanilla; the cooler Adelaide Hills and King Valley make fresher, less jammy Barbera.","call":"Sour cherry, dried thyme and a sharp, juicy finish lean Piedmont; jammy black cherry, vanilla and higher alcohol lean California or Australia."}},"service":{"temp":"59 to 63 degrees Fahrenheit, 15 to 17 degrees Celsius; a young, unoaked one a little cooler.","glass":"A Burgundy bowl, or a standard red wine glass for an everyday bottle.","decant":"Usually unnecessary; a young, barrel aged Nizza may open with air after a taste.","age":"Three to eight years: everyday Barbera sooner, Nizza and Superiore at the long end."},"food":{"with":[{"dish":"Pasta with a tomato sauce","why":"the wine's acidity matches the tomato's own"},{"dish":"Salumi and cured meats","why":"acidity cuts the fat, and low tannin spares the salt"},{"dish":"Pizza with sausage","why":"juicy fruit and no hard edges beside tomato and cheese"},{"dish":"Chicken and andouille gumbo","why":"bright acidity and soft tannin beside the smoky sausage"}]},"labels":[{"term":"Barbera d'Asti","means":"The DOCG of the hills around Asti, at least 90 per cent Barbera; Superiore means longer ageing, partly in wood."},{"term":"Nizza","means":"Barbera alone from around Nizza Monferrato, its own DOCG since 2014, aged 18 months, 6 in wood."},{"term":"Barbera d'Alba","means":"The Langhe's Barbera, from the hills around Alba; often plusher and fuller than Barbera d'Asti."},{"term":"Gutturnio","means":"A wine, not a grape: Barbera with Croatina from the Piacenza hills of Emilia."}],"guest":[{"q":"Is Barbera anything like Barolo?","a":"Same hills, different grape. Barolo is Nebbiolo, pale with a fierce grip. Barbera is darker and juicier, soft in tannin and bright in acidity, the bottle Piedmont opens while its Barolo ages."},{"q":"Why does this red make my mouth water?","a":"That is Barbera's high acidity. With so little tannin, the freshness is what you feel, and it is why the wine suits tomato sauces and cured meats."},{"q":"Is the California one very different?","a":"Usually riper and fuller, with black cherry, more alcohol and more vanilla from oak barrels, but the same bright acidity runs through it."}],"compare":[{"slug":"dolcetto","tell":"Dolcetto is the low acid one, soft and round, with a little more tannin and a bitter almond finish; Barbera is sharper, juicier and gentler in grip."},{"slug":"nebbiolo","tell":"Nebbiolo from the same hills is the opposite: pale garnet with high tannin; Barbera is deep purple, with high acidity and little tannin."},{"slug":"sangiovese","tell":"Sangiovese shares the high acidity but adds tomato leaf and a dusty, firmer tannin; Barbera is deeper in colour, juicier and softer in grip."}],"cards":[{"id":"gt-vtqx78gy","front":"Barbera blind: what upside down structure gives it away?","back":"Deep colour with very high acidity and low tannin: the reverse of Nebbiolo, which is pale with high tannin, from the same hills."},{"id":"gt-b76y34a4","front":"Barbera, Old World or New World: what does a blind taster lean on?","back":"Sour cherry, dried thyme and a sharp, juicy finish lean Piedmont. Jammy black cherry, vanilla and higher alcohol lean California or Australia."},{"id":"gt-34s6i4u7","front":"Nizza: which grape, since when its own DOCG, and how long aged?","back":"Barbera alone. Nizza became its own DOCG in 2014; it ages at least 18 months, 6 of them in wood, and Riserva 30 months with 12."}],"questions":[{"id":"gt-3z3nj40g","q":"Two glasses of Barbera are poured blind. One shows sour cherry, dried thyme and a sharp, juicy finish; the other jammy black cherry, vanilla and higher alcohol. Which pair of origins fits, in that order?","opts":["Lodi, then McLaren Vale","Amador County, then Asti","Alba, then Nizza","Asti, then Amador County"],"a":3,"exp":"Sharp sour cherry fruit at moderate ripeness tends to mark Piedmont; riper, jammier fruit, vanilla from oak and more alcohol tend to mark a warm California site. The reversed pair swaps the two.","tag":"worlds"},{"id":"gt-5gmt9p4c","q":"A red poured blind is deep purple, with sour cherry and black plum, very high acidity and almost no tannin. Which Piedmont grape is it?","opts":["Dolcetto","Barbera","Nebbiolo","Freisa"],"a":1,"exp":"Deep colour, very high acidity and low tannin is Barbera's signature. Dolcetto, the nearest wrong option, is the low acid grape with firmer tannin, and Nebbiolo is pale with high tannin."},{"id":"gt-h9gptgxo","q":"A guest wants a Piedmont red for salumi and a tomato pasta: fresh, juicy and with almost no tannin. Which do you pour?","opts":["Barbera d'Asti","Dolcetto d'Alba","Barolo","Ghemme"],"a":0,"exp":"Barbera d'Asti brings the high acidity tomato and cured meat ask for, with little tannin. Dolcetto d'Alba, the nearest miss, is softer in acidity and firmer in tannin; Barolo and Ghemme are Nebbiolo and grip hard."}],"sources":[{"title":"Il Nizza: the production regulations of the Nizza DOCG","url":"https://ilnizza.net/en/production-regulations"},{"title":"University of California, Davis, Foundation Plant Services: Barbera fact sheet","url":"https://devo.ucdavis.edu/sites/g/files/dgvnsk1961/files/files/page/Barbera%20Fact%20Sheet.pdf"},{"title":"Lodi Wine: Barbera from young and old vine Lodi plantings","url":"https://www.lodiwine.com/blog/Round-up-of-Barbara-bottlings-from-both-young-and-old-vine-Lodi-plantings"},{"title":"Wine Selectors: the state of play, Barbera in Australia","url":"https://wineselectors.com.au/selector-magazine/wine/state-of-play-barbera"}]},
"corvina": {"slug":"corvina","name":"Corvina (Valpolicella)","colour":"red","kind":"grape","status":"full","mode":"home","aliases":[],"notIn":[],"say":"kor-VEE-nah","lines":{"s10":"Corvina is the grape of Valpolicella and Amarone: sour cherry and bitter almond, light and tart, or dried and deep.","s20":"Corvina is the main grape of Valpolicella, north of Verona, where fresh it makes a pale, tart red of sour cherry and bitter almond. Dried for months before pressing, the same grapes become Amarone, dark and warming.","s45":"Corvina makes a pale, tart red for lunch and a dark, warming Amarone for winter. It is the main grape of Valpolicella, north of Verona. Fermented fresh, it gives sour cherry and cranberry with a bitter almond finish, lively acidity and little tannin. Dried in airy lofts for three or four months, the same grapes make Amarone: raisined black cherry, fig and cinnamon. Pour the young wine cool with charcuterie, and Amarone with braised beef or aged cheese. The story goes that Amarone began as a cask of sweet Recioto, forgotten until it had fermented dry.","sale":"With Corvina one grape can carry the whole evening, from a cool, tart red to a dried, deep Amarone. Start with a Valpolicella beside the charcuterie and keep the Amarone for the braised beef or the cheese."},"glass":{"lead":"Sour cherry and bitter almond, pale and tart when fresh, with raisined black cherry, fig and cinnamon when the grapes are dried.","notes":[{"family":"red fruit","notes":["sour cherry","cranberry","red cherry"]},{"family":"black fruit","notes":["black cherry","black plum"]},{"family":"dried fruit","notes":["raisin","dried fig","prune"]},{"family":"nutty","notes":["bitter almond","cherry stone"]},{"family":"spice","notes":["cinnamon","clove"]}],"ladder":[{"climate":"cool","where":"The higher hill vineyards, cooled at night by air off the Lessini","notes":["sour cherry","cranberry","bitter almond"]},{"climate":"moderate","where":"The lower, warmer slopes running down towards Verona","notes":["red cherry","black cherry","cherry stone"]}],"structure":"Dry. Fresh Valpolicella: acidity high, tannin low, alcohol medium, body light. Amarone: alcohol high, body full, tannin medium, acidity still fresh."},"worlds":{"home":{"home":"The hills north of Verona: Valpolicella, where it leads a blend with Rondinella, and Bardolino on Lake Garda. One blend makes four wines by method: fresh Valpolicella, Ripasso refermented on Amarone skins, and Amarone and Recioto from grapes dried for months.","abroad":"It rarely leaves the Veneto. A few vineyards in Argentina's Andean foothills and in Australia grow it, some of the grapes dried in the Veronese manner."}},"service":{"temp":"Valpolicella 54 to 59, Amarone 61 to 64 degrees Fahrenheit; 12 to 15 and 16 to 18 degrees Celsius.","glass":"A standard red wine glass for Valpolicella; a large Burgundy bowl for Amarone.","decant":"Not for fresh Valpolicella; consider it for a young Amarone, and decant an old one off its sediment.","age":"Valpolicella young, within two or three years; Amarone ten to twenty years, or more."},"food":{"with":[{"dish":"Charcuterie and soft cheeses","why":"served cool, tart cherry lifts the salt and fat"},{"dish":"Grilled tuna or salmon, with Bardolino","why":"a light, low tannin red that spares the fish"},{"dish":"Beef braised in Amarone","why":"raisined fruit and weight match the long braise"},{"dish":"Aged hard cheese","why":"salt and crystals beside Amarone's dried fruit"}]},"labels":[{"term":"Valpolicella Classico","means":"From the five historic communes in the west of the zone: fresh, pale and tart."},{"term":"Ripasso","means":"Valpolicella refermented on the skins left from Amarone or Recioto: fuller and rounder."},{"term":"Amarone della Valpolicella","means":"Dry red from grapes dried for months, at least 14 per cent alcohol, aged two years at least."},{"term":"Recioto della Valpolicella","means":"The sweet wine of the same dried grapes, its fermentation stopped with sugar left."},{"term":"Bardolino, Chiaretto","means":"Light Corvina reds from Lake Garda's eastern shore; Chiaretto is the pale rosé."}],"guest":[{"q":"Is Amarone a sweet wine?","a":"No, it is dry, though rich and warming. Its name comes from amaro, bitter, to set it apart from Recioto, the sweet wine of the same dried grapes."},{"q":"What does Ripasso mean on the label?","a":"Passed over again: young Valpolicella is refermented on the skins left from Amarone, which gives it more body, colour and raisined fruit. It sits between the two in weight."},{"q":"Why is Amarone so strong?","a":"The grapes dry for months and lose about a third of their weight, so the sugar is concentrated. Fermented dry, that sugar gives at least 14 per cent alcohol, usually more."}],"compare":[{"slug":"gamay","tell":"Gamay matches fresh Valpolicella's pale colour and tart cherry fruit, often with banana; Corvina gives itself away with a bitter almond, cherry stone finish."},{"slug":"zinfandel","tell":"Zinfandel shares Amarone's raisined richness and warmth, with blackberry jam and black pepper; Amarone has more sour cherry and a bitter cherry stone finish."},{"slug":"sangiovese","tell":"Sangiovese is firmer, with dusty tannin and tomato leaf; fresh Corvina is lighter and softer in grip, with a bitter almond finish."}],"cards":[{"id":"gt-2i2w158k","front":"Corvina: which four wines does Valpolicella make from one blend?","back":"Valpolicella, fresh; Ripasso, refermented on the skins of dried grapes; Amarone, dry, from dried grapes; and Recioto, sweet, from dried grapes."},{"id":"gt-96nfu4gj","front":"Corvina for Amarone: how long do the grapes dry, and what do they lose?","back":"Three to four months in a drying loft, losing about a third of their weight, which concentrates sugar, acid and glycerol."},{"id":"gt-r5ka8z3x","front":"Corvina blind: what tell do fresh Valpolicella and Amarone share?","back":"A bitter almond, cherry stone finish over sour cherry: fresh and pale in Valpolicella, raisined and dense in Amarone."}],"questions":[{"id":"gt-gh5bv1m2","q":"A dry red poured blind is dark and warming, with raisined black cherry, dried fig, cinnamon and a bitter cherry stone finish. Which wine is it?","opts":["Recioto della Valpolicella","Zinfandel from Lodi","Amarone della Valpolicella","Barolo"],"a":2,"exp":"Dried grape richness with cherry stone bitterness and cinnamon, fermented dry, is Amarone. Recioto, the nearest wrong option, comes from the same dried grapes but is sweet."},{"id":"gt-2v9t2qnw","q":"A guest finds plain Valpolicella too light and Amarone too heavy, and asks for a Corvina red between the two in weight. Which do you suggest?","opts":["Valpolicella Ripasso","Bardolino Classico","Recioto della Valpolicella","Chiaretto di Bardolino"],"a":0,"exp":"Ripasso is Valpolicella refermented on the skins left from Amarone, which adds body and raisined fruit. Recioto, the nearest miss, is sweet, and Bardolino is lighter still."},{"id":"gt-5pmrhpc0","q":"A guest reading an Amarone back label asks which grape leads the blend. Which do you name?","opts":["Molinara","Rondinella","Sangiovese","Corvina"],"a":3,"exp":"Corvina leads at 45 to 95 per cent, and Corvinone may stand in for up to half of it. Rondinella, the nearest miss, is the reliable second grape at 5 to 30 per cent."}],"sources":[{"title":"Australian Wine Research Institute: Corvina","url":"https://awri.com.au/files/attachment/s2112"},{"title":"Wine Folly: Corvina","url":"https://winefolly.com/grapes/corvina"},{"title":"Wine Insiders: Amarone wine","url":"https://wineinsiders.com/wine-101/amarone-wine"},{"title":"The Oxford Companion to Wine","url":""}]},
"sagrantino": {"slug":"sagrantino","name":"Sagrantino","colour":"red","kind":"grape","status":"full","mode":"home","aliases":[],"notIn":[],"say":"sah-grahn-TEE-noh","lines":{"s10":"Sagrantino is the near black red of Montefalco in Umbria: blackberry, incense and cocoa on a grip few wines can match.","s20":"Sagrantino is the grape of Montefalco in Umbria, made dry or as a sweet passito. It is near black, with blackberry, prune and incense, and a tannin so firm it wants years in bottle and a rich dish beside it.","s45":"Sagrantino grows almost nowhere but Montefalco, a hill town in Umbria, the green heart of Italy. Its thick skins give a near black wine of blackberry, black cherry and prune, with incense, cocoa and dried violet. It is full bodied, and its tannin grips harder than almost any grape you will meet. It must age more than three years before release, a year of that in wood, and it rewards ten years in bottle. Open it for wild boar, a grilled steak or aged pecorino. Until the dry wine arrived in the 1970s, it was made sweet, from grapes dried on racks for feast days.","sale":"Sagrantino grows almost nowhere but Montefalco, one hill town in Umbria, and it pours near black, with blackberry and incense. Opened early, it stands up to the wild boar or the steak."},"glass":{"lead":"Blackberry, black cherry and prune with incense, cocoa and dried violet, near opaque and full, on a tannin that grips like few others.","notes":[{"family":"black fruit","notes":["blackberry","black cherry","black plum"]},{"family":"dried fruit","notes":["prune","dried fig"]},{"family":"floral","notes":["violet","dried violet"]},{"family":"spice","notes":["incense","clove","cinnamon"]},{"family":"oak","notes":["cocoa","vanilla pod","espresso"]}],"ladder":[{"climate":"moderate","where":"The higher ridge of Montefalco and the King Valley","notes":["blackberry","black cherry","dried violet"]},{"climate":"warm","where":"The lower slopes towards Bevagna, Heathcote and McLaren Vale","notes":["black plum","prune","cocoa"]}],"structure":"Dry. Acidity medium plus, tannin high and mouth coating, alcohol medium plus to high, body full; deep purple, nearly opaque."},"worlds":{"home":{"home":"Montefalco in Umbria, with Bevagna, Gualdo Cattaneo, Castel Ritaldi and Giano dell'Umbria. Montefalco Sagrantino is the grape alone, dry or passito, the dry wine aged 37 months with 12 in wood. A share of it gives grip to Montefalco Rosso, a Sangiovese blend.","abroad":"Australia grows a little, in McLaren Vale, Heathcote and the King Valley, and California a few rows; these tend to be riper and fleshier, the grip still firm."}},"service":{"temp":"61 to 64 degrees Fahrenheit, 16 to 18 degrees Celsius; the passito a little cooler.","glass":"A large Bordeaux glass, which gives the dense wine room to breathe.","decant":"Often helps a young secco: taste a sample, then decant. An old bottle only to leave its sediment.","age":"Ten years and more, and even then it grips; Montefalco Rosso drinks far sooner."},"food":{"with":[{"dish":"Wild boar ragù or braised boar","why":"a rich, gamey meat for a fierce grip"},{"dish":"Grilled steak or roast lamb","why":"protein and fat soften the tannin"},{"dish":"Aged pecorino","why":"salt and fat round off the grip"},{"dish":"Dark chocolate, with the passito","why":"sweet blackberry and tannin meet bitter cocoa"}]},"labels":[{"term":"Montefalco Sagrantino","means":"Sagrantino alone from Montefalco and four neighbouring communes; a DOCG since 1992, dry or passito."},{"term":"Secco","means":"Dry; printed to set the dry wine apart from the passito."},{"term":"Passito","means":"A sweet red from bunches dried on racks for weeks, the original wine of Montefalco."},{"term":"Montefalco Rosso","means":"A Sangiovese led red with a share of Sagrantino for grip; a different, lighter wine."},{"term":"Sagrantino di Montefalco","means":"The older form of the same DOCG's name."}],"guest":[{"q":"Why does this wine make my whole mouth feel dry?","a":"That is tannin, and Sagrantino's thick skins hold more of it than almost any grape. Fat and protein in the food, a decanter and years in bottle all soften it."},{"q":"Is Montefalco Rosso the same wine?","a":"No. Montefalco Rosso is mostly Sangiovese with a share of Sagrantino, lighter and ready sooner. Montefalco Sagrantino is the grape alone, made to age."},{"q":"Is Sagrantino a sweet wine?","a":"It can be. For centuries it was made sweet from dried grapes, and the dry wine came only in the 1970s. Secco on the label means dry, passito means sweet."}],"compare":[{"slug":"tannat","tell":"Tannat matches the grip with cassis, blueberry and iron; Sagrantino brings black cherry preserve, incense and dried violet."},{"slug":"aglianico","tell":"Aglianico carries higher acidity, with tar and smoke; Sagrantino is blacker and denser, with incense, cocoa and dried violet."},{"slug":"sangiovese","tell":"Sangiovese, which leads Montefalco Rosso, is paler, with sour cherry and dried oregano and a gentler grip; Sagrantino is near black and far more tannic."}],"cards":[{"id":"gt-r3latgw2","front":"Montefalco Sagrantino: what must it hold, and how long does it age?","back":"Sagrantino alone. The dry wine ages at least 37 months, 12 of them in wood and 4 in bottle; the passito shares the DOCG."},{"id":"gt-o98mvufg","front":"Sagrantino: how was it made before the 1970s, and what is passito?","back":"Sweet. Bunches were dried on racks for weeks and made into passito for feast days; dry Sagrantino is a wine of the 1970s."},{"id":"gt-eg3c2gg8","front":"Montefalco Rosso and Montefalco Sagrantino: how do the two wines differ?","back":"Montefalco Rosso is a Sangiovese led blend with a share of Sagrantino, ready sooner. Montefalco Sagrantino is the grape alone, a DOCG made to age."}],"questions":[{"id":"gt-user02jq","q":"A red poured blind is near opaque, with blackberry, prune, incense and cocoa, and a tannin that coats the whole mouth. The bottle is from Umbria. Which grape is it?","opts":["Sangiovese","Montepulciano","Aglianico","Sagrantino"],"a":3,"exp":"Near opaque colour, incense and cocoa on a tannin like few others point to Sagrantino of Montefalco. Sangiovese, Umbria's other red, is paler, with sour cherry and a gentler grip."},{"id":"gt-qzncgs8j","q":"A guest wants the sweet red of Montefalco to finish dinner. Which word on the label do you look for?","opts":["Superiore","Passito","Riserva","Secco"],"a":1,"exp":"Passito marks the sweet wine made from bunches dried on racks for weeks. Secco, the nearest miss, marks the dry wine of the same DOCG."},{"id":"gt-74lifvxv","q":"A guest enjoys the Montefalco Rosso by the glass and asks how the Montefalco Sagrantino on the list differs. What is the main difference?","opts":["It comes from another region of Italy","It is the sweet version of the Rosso","It is Sagrantino alone, not a Sangiovese blend","It is aged without any wood"],"a":2,"exp":"Montefalco Sagrantino is the grape alone, dense and tannic, aged 37 months. Montefalco Rosso, from the same hills, is led by Sangiovese with a share of Sagrantino."}],"sources":[{"title":"Consorzio Tutela Vini Montefalco: Montefalco Sagrantino DOCG and Montefalco DOC rules","url":"https://www.consorziomontefalco.it"},{"title":"Decanter: Montefalco Sagrantino on the rise","url":"https://www.decanter.com/premium/montefalco-sagrantino-406638/"},{"title":"Wine Companion: grape varieties and wine styles, Italian reds","url":"https://winecompanion.com.au/resources/varieties/italian-reds"},{"title":"Wine Grapes","url":""}]},
"tannat": {"slug":"tannat","name":"Tannat","colour":"red","kind":"grape","status":"full","mode":"contrast","aliases":[],"notIn":[],"say":"tah-NAHT, in France tah-NAH","lines":{"s10":"Tannat is the dark red of Madiran in south west France and the national grape of Uruguay: blackberry, liquorice and a firm grip.","s20":"Tannat comes from Madiran, in the hills of Gascony, and few grapes carry more tannin. It is nearly black, with blackberry, black plum and liquorice, and Uruguay makes a rounder, softer version of it.","s45":"Tannat is a grape of real grip, nearly black and firmly tannic, at home in Madiran, in Gascony. It tastes of blackberry, black plum and cassis, with liquorice and a note of dark chocolate. It is full bodied and fresh, and its tannin dries the mouth as strong tea does. Madiran tames it with time in oak barrels and tiny doses of oxygen, while Uruguay's mild sea air usually gives a rounder, softer wine. It wants rich food: duck confit, cassoulet or a fatty grilled steak. A Basque settler planted it in Uruguay in the 1870s, and for generations the country called it by his name, Harriague.","sale":"Tannat came to Uruguay with a Basque settler and became the national grape: blackberry, liquorice and a firm, fresh grip. I would decant it first and pour it with the ribeye or the duck."},"glass":{"lead":"Blackberry, black plum and cassis with liquorice, smoke and dark chocolate, opaque and full, on very firm, drying tannin.","notes":[{"family":"black fruit","notes":["blackberry","black plum","cassis"]},{"family":"blue fruit","notes":["blueberry","damson"]},{"family":"spice","notes":["liquorice","cracked black pepper"]},{"family":"earth","notes":["iron","leather","tobacco leaf"]},{"family":"oak","notes":["dark chocolate","wood smoke","coffee"]}],"ladder":[{"climate":"moderate","where":"Madiran and Irouléguy in France; Canelones and Maldonado in Uruguay","notes":["blackberry","cassis","raspberry","iron"]},{"climate":"warm","where":"Cafayate in Salta, northern Uruguay and Paso Robles","notes":["ripe blackberry","black plum","liquorice"]}],"structure":"Dry. Acidity medium plus, tannin very high and firm, alcohol medium plus, body full."},"worlds":{"contrast":{"old":{"where":"Madiran, Irouléguy and Saint-Mont in south west France","glass":"Tends to be firmer and more savoury: blackberry, cassis and black plum with iron, leather, smoke and liquorice, fresh acidity and a wall of drying tannin when young. Time brings it round.","cellar":"Tannat leads the blend, with Cabernet Franc, Cabernet Sauvignon and Fer beside it; long soaks on the skins, oak, and often small doses of oxygen to soften the tannin."},"new":{"where":"Uruguay, in Canelones and Maldonado; Cafayate in Salta; Paso Robles in California","glass":"Typically rounder and more fruit led: blackberry, black plum and blueberry with liquorice and dark chocolate, the tannin still firm but riper and finer. Maldonado's granite hills tend to give fresher wines than the clay of Canelones.","cellar":"Bottled under the grape's name, alone or blended with Merlot or Cabernet Franc to soften it; some cellars use oak or small doses of oxygen, and rosé is made too."},"why":"Madiran keeps Tannat in the lead by law and soaks it long, so the wine is stern. Uruguay is humid and moderated by the sea, and climate and gentler handling give rounder tannin. France labels the place, Uruguay the grape.","blur":"Modern Madiran, picked riper and softened with oxygen, can be supple when young, and the high desert of Cafayate gives Tannat as dark and tannic as Madiran's.","call":"Iron, leather and a stern, drying grip lean Madiran; blueberry, riper blackberry and rounder, finer tannin lean Uruguay."}},"service":{"temp":"61 to 64 degrees Fahrenheit, 16 to 18 degrees Celsius: cool room temperature, since warmth makes the alcohol show.","glass":"A large Bordeaux glass.","decant":"Consider it for a young wine after tasting a sample; an old Madiran may be decanted off its sediment.","age":"Many Uruguayan wines are ready young; Madiran from a good year keeps ten years and more."},"food":{"with":[{"dish":"Cassoulet","why":"the Gascon match: beans, sausage and duck fat meet the grip"},{"dish":"Duck confit, duck breast or rack of lamb","why":"fat and salt make firm tannin feel smoother"},{"dish":"Ribeye or beef from the grill","why":"Uruguay's own table: charred, fatty beef and a firm red"},{"dish":"Hard sheep's milk cheese from the Pyrenees","why":"salt and fat ease the tannin"}]},"labels":[{"term":"Madiran","means":"A red only appellation of Gascony, led by Tannat with the two Cabernets and Fer beside it."},{"term":"Irouléguy","means":"The French Basque appellation: firm reds and rosés of Tannat and Cabernet Franc, from terraces near Spain."},{"term":"Harriague","means":"The old name for Tannat in Uruguay, after the Basque settler who planted it."},{"term":"Tannat Merlot","means":"A common Uruguayan blend: the Merlot rounds off the tannin."}],"guest":[{"q":"I have never heard of Tannat. What is it close to?","a":"Think of Cabernet Sauvignon with more colour and more grip, or a firmer, drier Malbec. It comes from Gascony in south west France, and Uruguay has made it its national red."},{"q":"Is the one from Uruguay different from the French?","a":"Usually rounder. Uruguay is humid and near the sea, and its Tannat tends to show riper blackberry, blueberry and finer tannin. Madiran is the sterner wine, and the one to keep."},{"q":"Will it soften if we give it time?","a":"Air can open the aroma within the hour, and years in the cellar soften the grip. Tonight the surest help is the food: fat and salt make the tannin feel smoother."}],"compare":[{"slug":"malbec","tell":"Malbec is plusher and softer, with a lift of violet; Tannat is firmer and drier, with higher acidity and far more tannin."},{"slug":"sagrantino","tell":"Sagrantino matches Tannat's grip but smells of black cherry preserve, dried violet and incense; Tannat is cassis, iron, leather and smoke."},{"slug":"cabernet-sauvignon","tell":"Cabernet Sauvignon shows cedar and a leafy edge of green bell pepper; Tannat is darker and more tannic, with liquorice and iron."}],"cards":[{"id":"gt-sxy6207h","front":"Tannat: its French home, and the grapes blended with it there?","back":"Madiran, in Gascony: red only and led by Tannat, with Cabernet Franc, Cabernet Sauvignon and Fer beside it. Irouléguy and Saint-Mont grow it too."},{"id":"gt-6qdtl2x6","front":"Tannat blind: Madiran or Uruguay, what tips it?","back":"Iron, leather and a stern, drying grip lean Madiran. Blueberry, riper blackberry and rounder, finer tannin lean Uruguay."},{"id":"gt-d86163jd","front":"What method did Madiran develop in the early 1990s to soften Tannat?","back":"Micro-oxygenation: tiny, measured doses of oxygen fed into the wine in tank, to soften the tannin and fix the colour. It is now used far beyond Madiran."}],"questions":[{"id":"gt-77j89fv7","q":"A blind red is opaque purple black, with blackberry, black plum and blueberry, liquorice and dark chocolate. The tannin is high but ripe and fine, and the fruit is generous. The table agrees it is Tannat. Which origin fits it?","opts":["Canelones, Uruguay","Colchagua, Chile","Madiran, France","Irouléguy, France"],"a":0,"exp":"Blueberry, generous fruit and rounder, finer tannin tend to mark Uruguay. Madiran and Irouléguy are typically sterner, with iron and leather, and Colchagua is known for Carmenère and Cabernet, not Tannat.","tag":"worlds"},{"id":"gt-nhqsxxzw","q":"A table orders a young Madiran with the duck breast, and one guest fears the wine will be too tannic for her. What is the sound thing to tell her?","opts":["Chilling it well will hide the tannin","The duck's fat and salt will smooth the grip","Tannat loses its tannin once it is bottled","Decanting will take the tannin out"],"a":1,"exp":"Fat and salt soften the impression of tannin, which is why Gascony pours Madiran with duck. Decanting can open the aroma but removes no tannin, and a cold tannic red feels harder, not softer."},{"id":"gt-4x9nuijr","q":"A guest sees Tannat Merlot beside a Uruguayan red on the list and asks why the two grapes are blended. What do you say?","opts":["Merlot adds the colour that Tannat lacks","The law of Uruguay asks for a second grape","Merlot's soft fruit rounds off Tannat's firm tannin","Merlot raises the acidity of the blend"],"a":2,"exp":"Tannat has colour, acidity and tannin to spare, so growers blend in Merlot to soften it. It is short of neither colour nor acidity, and Uruguay has no rule that asks for a second grape."}],"sources":[{"title":"INAO: cahier des charges of the appellation Madiran","url":"https://extranet.inao.gouv.fr/fichier/2-CdC-AOC-Madiran.pdf"},{"title":"Wine Grapes, entry on Tannat","url":""},{"title":"Decanter: Understanding Uruguay","url":"https://www.decanter.com/sponsored/south-america-guide/understanding-uruguay-432540/"},{"title":"Wine Enthusiast: Tannat, the dark prince of Paso Robles wine","url":"https://www.wineenthusiast.com/culture/wine/tannat-paso-robles-wine/"}]},
"pinotage": {"slug":"pinotage","name":"Pinotage","colour":"red","kind":"grape","status":"full","mode":"home","aliases":[],"notIn":[],"say":"PEE-noh-tahzh","lines":{"s10":"Pinotage was born in South Africa: a dark, smoky red with black plum, mulberry and a firm grip made for the grill.","s20":"Pinotage was bred at the Cape in 1925, a crossing of Pinot Noir and Cinsault. It tastes little like either: black plum, mulberry and wood smoke on a full, firm frame, a natural red for grilled meat.","s45":"Pinotage belongs to South Africa: it was bred at Stellenbosch in 1925, and few other countries grow it at all. It makes a dark, full red that tastes of black plum, mulberry and wood smoke, with firm tannin and a warm finish. The classic style is aged in oak barrels and built to keep, and a popular modern one uses heavily toasted oak to give a clear taste of coffee and chocolate. It belongs beside a fire: pour it with grilled lamb, barbecued ribs or smoked sausage. Ask where the name comes from and you have its parents: Pinot Noir, and Cinsault, which the Cape used to call Hermitage.","sale":"Pinotage is the taste of one place: South Africa bred it, and its smoky black plum is the flavour of the Cape. It wants anything off the grill, and lamb above all."},"glass":{"lead":"Black plum, mulberry and blackberry under wood smoke and a sweet edge of tar, full bodied, with firm tannin.","notes":[{"family":"black fruit","notes":["black plum","mulberry","blackberry"]},{"family":"red fruit","notes":["red cherry","raspberry"]},{"family":"oak","notes":["wood smoke","coffee","dark chocolate"]},{"family":"earth","notes":["tar","rooibos tea","leather"]}],"ladder":[{"climate":"cool","where":"Walker Bay and the Hemel-en-Aarde, by the cold sea","notes":["red cherry","raspberry","mulberry"]},{"climate":"warm","where":"Paarl, and Bottelary and the warm valley floors of Stellenbosch","notes":["black plum","blackberry","tar","wood smoke"]}],"structure":"Dry. Acidity medium, tannin medium plus to high, alcohol medium plus to high, body medium to full."},"worlds":{"home":{"home":"South Africa, and above all Stellenbosch, where it was bred in 1925. Old bush vines on granite and shale give the dark, smoky classic, fermented in open vats and aged in oak barrels. Heavily toasted oak makes the coffee style, cooler coastal sites a lighter, red cherry one, and it anchors the Cape Blend.","abroad":"It has barely left the Cape. New Zealand, California, Israel and Brazil each grow a little, and a bottle from any of them is a rarity on a list."}},"service":{"temp":"61 to 64 degrees Fahrenheit, 16 to 18 degrees Celsius; a lighter, unoaked one a little cooler.","glass":"A Bordeaux glass for the dark, oaked wines; a Burgundy bowl suits the lighter style.","decant":"Consider it for a young, firm bottle, after tasting a sample.","age":"Three to ten years; the firm old vine wines of Stellenbosch keep longer."},"food":{"with":[{"dish":"Lamb chops or sausage off the grill","why":"smoke in the wine meets smoke from the fire"},{"dish":"Barbecued ribs or brisket","why":"ripe plum takes a sweet, smoky glaze"},{"dish":"Venison or other game","why":"firm tannin and black plum suit lean, rich meat"},{"dish":"Bobotie or a mild, fragrant curry","why":"the Cape's own table, ripe fruit against gentle spice"}]},"labels":[{"term":"Cape Blend","means":"A red blend built on Pinotage, generally 30 to 70 per cent of it; a trade term, not a law."},{"term":"Bush vine","means":"A low vine grown without wires, often old and unirrigated, giving a small crop of concentrated fruit."},{"term":"Coffee Pinotage","means":"A style, not a place: heavily toasted oak gives a plain taste of coffee and chocolate."},{"term":"Wine of Origin","means":"South Africa's seal of place, in force since 1973; Stellenbosch, Paarl and Swartland are districts under it."}],"guest":[{"q":"Is it a kind of Pinot Noir?","a":"Only by birth. Pinot Noir is one parent and Cinsault the other, but Pinotage is darker, fuller and smokier than either, and closer at the table to a Syrah."},{"q":"I had one years ago that smelled of paint and banana. Are they all like that?","a":"No. That came from fermenting too hot and too fast. Careful growers now ferment it cooler and press early, and a good one tastes of black plum, mulberry and smoke."},{"q":"Does this one really taste of coffee?","a":"It can. Some cellars use heavily toasted oak to bring out coffee and chocolate on purpose. If you would rather taste the fruit, I will point you to a classic one."}],"compare":[{"slug":"syrah","tell":"Syrah is peppery and meaty, with black olive; Pinotage has no pepper, and shows mulberry, tar and a sweet smoke like rooibos tea."},{"slug":"zinfandel","tell":"Zinfandel is jammier and sweeter in fruit, with raisin at the edges and softer tannin; Pinotage is smokier and firmer, with tar."},{"slug":"cinsault","tell":"Cinsault, its parent, is pale, light and perfumed, with strawberry and soft tannin; Pinotage is far darker and fuller, with smoke and a firm grip."}],"cards":[{"id":"gt-3l3gjdgq","front":"Pinotage: who crossed it, where, when, and from which two grapes?","back":"Abraham Perold, at Stellenbosch, in 1925, from Pinot Noir and Cinsault, which the Cape then called Hermitage: hence the name. The name first appeared on a label in 1961."},{"id":"gt-fqvrsxna","front":"Pinotage: what is a Cape Blend, and is it a legal term?","back":"A red blend built on Pinotage, generally 30 to 70 per cent of it, with Cabernet Sauvignon, Merlot or Shiraz. It is a trade term with no law behind it."},{"id":"gt-xi5qbcnc","front":"Pinotage blind: what gives it away, and which two grapes mislead?","back":"Mulberry, black plum, tar and a sweet smoke like rooibos tea, sometimes banana. Syrah is peppery and meaty instead; Zinfandel is jammier, with raisin."}],"questions":[{"id":"gt-olvzg45o","q":"A deep ruby red poured blind is full and firm, with black plum, mulberry, tar and a sweet, smoky note like rooibos tea, and no black pepper. Which grape and origin fit?","opts":["Syrah from the Northern Rhône","Zinfandel from Lodi","Malbec from Mendoza","Pinotage from Stellenbosch"],"a":3,"exp":"Mulberry, tar and rooibos smoke with no pepper mark Pinotage. Syrah, the nearest wrong answer, would show cracked black pepper and smoked meat, and Zinfandel is jammier."},{"id":"gt-x5w6rn49","q":"A guest asks how Pinotage came by its name. Which answer is right?","opts":["It crosses Pinot Noir with Syrah from the hill of Hermitage","It crosses Pinot Noir with Cinsault, once called Hermitage","It is Pinot Noir grown in the heat of the Cape","It is an old Cape name for the grape Pinot Meunier"],"a":1,"exp":"Pinotage is a 1925 crossing of Pinot Noir and Cinsault, which the Cape called Hermitage. The Hermitage in the name is that local alias, not Syrah from the Rhône hill."},{"id":"gt-tdbv53h2","q":"A guest loved a Pinotage that tasted plainly of coffee and chocolate, and asks how the cellar did it. What do you tell him?","opts":["Heavily toasted oak","Long ageing in bottle","A hot, fast fermentation","Grapes dried before pressing"],"a":0,"exp":"Coffee and chocolate come from heavily toasted oak, used on purpose in this style. A hot, fast fermentation, the nearest wrong answer, gives Pinotage banana and varnish instead."}],"sources":[{"title":"Wines of South Africa: red wine varieties","url":"https://www.wosa.co.za/The-Industry/Varieties-and-Styles/Red-Wine-Varieties/"},{"title":"Wines of South Africa: wine styles","url":"https://www.wosa.co.za/The-Industry/Varieties-and-Styles/Wine-Styles/"},{"title":"Pinotage Association: the father of Pinotage","url":"https://pinotage.co.za/?p=373"},{"title":"Wine Enthusiast: what is Pinotage","url":"https://www.wineenthusiast.com/varietal/pinotage/"}]},
"carmenere": {"slug":"carmenere","name":"Carmenère","colour":"red","kind":"grape","status":"full","mode":"contrast","aliases":[],"notIn":[],"say":"kar-men-AIR","lines":{"s10":"Carmenère is Chile's signature red, a lost grape of Bordeaux: dark and soft, with black plum, roasted red pepper and a pinch of paprika.","s20":"Carmenère came to Chile from Bordeaux in the 1850s and was grown there as Merlot until 1994. It is a soft, dark red with black plum and blackberry, a streak of roasted pepper and paprika, and gentle tannin.","s45":"Carmenère is the grape Bordeaux gave up and Chile kept, grown in the warm valleys of Colchagua and Maipo. It tastes of black plum and blackberry, with roasted red pepper, paprika and a savoury note like soy. It is round and soft, with gentle tannin. It ripens weeks after Merlot, and picked too soon it tastes green. Chile ages it in French oak, while the rare Italian bottles are usually lighter and leafier. Set it beside grilled pork, smoked sausage or anything cooked with peppers. Chile grew it as Merlot for over a century, until a French vine expert told the two apart in 1994.","sale":"Carmenère hid in Chile as Merlot for over a century, and it tastes like Merlot with a dash of paprika and roasted pepper. It belongs with the smoked sausage or the grilled pork."},"glass":{"lead":"Black plum and blackberry with roasted red pepper, paprika and a savoury touch of soy, on a round, medium to full body with soft tannin.","notes":[{"family":"black fruit","notes":["black plum","blackberry","black cherry"]},{"family":"red fruit","notes":["raspberry","red plum"]},{"family":"herbal","notes":["roasted red pepper","green bell pepper","tomato leaf"]},{"family":"spice","notes":["paprika","green peppercorn"]},{"family":"savoury","notes":["soy","hoisin"]},{"family":"oak","notes":["cocoa","coffee"]}],"ladder":[{"climate":"moderate","where":"The Veneto and Friuli in north east Italy","notes":["raspberry","red plum","green bell pepper","cut grass"]},{"climate":"warm","where":"Colchagua, Peumo in Cachapoal and the floor of the Maipo","notes":["black plum","blackberry","roasted red pepper","paprika"]}],"structure":"Dry. Acidity medium, tannin medium and soft edged, alcohol medium plus, body medium plus to full."},"worlds":{"contrast":{"old":{"where":"North east Italy, in the Veneto and Friuli; a little in Bordeaux","glass":"Tends to be lighter and leafier: raspberry and red plum with a clear streak of green bell pepper and cut grass, medium body and fresher acidity. The Berici hills of the Veneto tend to give riper, darker wines.","cellar":"Long grown and bottled as Cabernet Franc, usually for drinking young with little or no new oak; now also labelled under its own name."},"new":{"where":"Chile: Colchagua, Cachapoal and Maipo in the Central Valley","glass":"Typically dark, round and ripe: black plum and blackberry with roasted red pepper, paprika, soy and cocoa, soft tannin and medium acidity. Picked early, it shows green bell pepper and tomato leaf.","cellar":"Picked late, often into May, then aged in French oak; bottled alone under the grape's name or blended with Cabernet Sauvignon for a firmer frame."},"why":"Chile's long, dry autumn lets this late grape hang until its green note fades. North east Italy is cooler and wetter at harvest, so the wine stays leafier. In Bordeaux it set fruit poorly and was not replanted after phylloxera.","blur":"A Chilean wine picked early or from a cool year can be as green as any from Italy, and ripe wines from the Berici hills come close to Chile.","call":"Black plum, paprika, soy and cocoa on a soft, full frame lean Chile; raspberry, cut grass and a lighter, fresher body lean north east Italy."}},"service":{"temp":"59 to 63 degrees Fahrenheit, 15 to 17 degrees Celsius: a touch cool, so the fruit stays fresh.","glass":"A Bordeaux glass.","decant":"Seldom needed for a soft young wine; a firm, oaked blend may open with air after a sample.","age":"Most are ready on release; the serious wines of Apalta and Peumo keep five to twelve years."},"food":{"with":[{"dish":"Grilled pork chop or pork belly","why":"soft tannin and ripe plum suit rich pork"},{"dish":"Lamb with cumin or smoked paprika","why":"the wine's own paprika note echoes the seasoning"},{"dish":"Stuffed peppers or a pepper stew","why":"roasted pepper in the glass meets pepper on the plate"},{"dish":"Beef and onion empanadas","why":"the Chilean match: a savoury filling and a soft red"}]},"labels":[{"term":"Carmenere","means":"The usual spelling on a Chilean label, without the accent: the same grape."},{"term":"Peumo, Apalta","means":"Two warm sites of central Chile, in Cachapoal and Colchagua, where the grape ripens fully."},{"term":"Gran Reserva","means":"In Chile a legal term that asks a little more alcohol than the minimum, not a rank of quality."},{"term":"Colli Berici Carmenere","means":"A wine of the Veneto, in north east Italy, that names the grape on its label."}],"guest":[{"q":"Is this like a Merlot?","a":"It is as soft and round as Merlot, and Chile sold it as Merlot for years. Carmenère adds a savoury edge that Merlot lacks: roasted pepper, paprika and a touch of soy."},{"q":"Why does it taste a little of green pepper?","a":"The grape carries that note by nature, as its parent Cabernet Franc does. Given a long, warm autumn it ripens into roasted red pepper and paprika; picked too soon it stays green."},{"q":"Is it French or Chilean?","a":"Born in Bordeaux, at home in Chile. Bordeaux all but gave it up after the vine pest phylloxera in the nineteenth century; Chile had taken cuttings in the 1850s and kept it."}],"compare":[{"slug":"merlot","tell":"Merlot has the same soft, plummy body but none of Carmenère's savoury streak: no roasted pepper, paprika or soy."},{"slug":"cabernet-franc","tell":"Cabernet Franc, its parent, is lighter and brighter, with redcurrant, pencil shavings and higher acidity; Carmenère is darker, fuller and softer."},{"slug":"cabernet-sauvignon","tell":"Cabernet Sauvignon is firmer and more drying, with cassis and cedar; Carmenère is softer, with roasted red pepper, paprika and soy."}],"cards":[{"id":"gt-i2bw4nlz","front":"Carmenère: the three dates of its story in Chile?","back":"Cuttings came from Bordeaux in the 1850s. A French vine expert told it apart from Merlot on 24 November 1994. Chile entered it in the official register in 1998."},{"id":"gt-z4c68iwe","front":"Carmenère blind: Chile or north east Italy, what tips it?","back":"Black plum, paprika, soy and cocoa on a soft, full frame lean Chile. Raspberry, cut grass and a lighter, fresher body lean the Veneto or Friuli."},{"id":"gt-wxnaunvf","front":"Why does Carmenère taste green when picked with the Merlot beside it?","back":"It ripens weeks after Merlot, often into May in Chile. Picked early it keeps a hard note of green bell pepper, which full ripeness turns to roasted red pepper and paprika."}],"questions":[{"id":"gt-x665f712","q":"A blind red is ruby and medium in body, with raspberry, red plum, cut grass and green bell pepper, fresh acidity and no mark of new oak. The table agrees it is Carmenère. Which origin fits it?","opts":["Colchagua, Chile","Napa Valley, California","Friuli, Italy","Mendoza, Argentina"],"a":2,"exp":"A lighter, leafy Carmenère with fresh acidity and no new oak tends to mark north east Italy. Colchagua is typically darker and rounder, with black plum, paprika and cocoa from French oak.","tag":"worlds"},{"id":"gt-yrwmpmtl","q":"A guest remembers the Chilean Merlot of the early 1990s as oddly green and leafy, and asks why a bottle today tastes so different. What do you tell him?","opts":["The wines were shipped before they were ready","The wines were aged in unseasoned oak","Chile's valleys were cooler in those years","Much of it was Carmenère, picked too early"],"a":3,"exp":"Until 1994 much Chilean Merlot was Carmenère, which ripens weeks later. Picked with the Merlot it kept a hard green pepper note. The weather and the oak were not the cause."},{"id":"gt-cfj8j0xy","q":"A guest finds the same leafy note of green pepper in a Chinon and in a Chilean Carmenère, and asks whether the two grapes are related. What do you tell her?","opts":["Cabernet Franc is a parent of Carmenère","Carmenère is a parent of Cabernet Franc","They are unrelated, and the note comes from oak","They are one grape under two names"],"a":0,"exp":"Carmenère is a natural offspring of Cabernet Franc, the grape of Chinon, and it shares its parent's note of green pepper. The two are separate grapes, and oak gives no such note."}],"sources":[{"title":"Wine Grapes, entry on Carmenère","url":""},{"title":"The Oxford Companion to Wine, entries on Carmenère and Cabernet Franc","url":""},{"title":"Decanter: From Bordeaux to Chile, International Carmenère Day","url":"https://www.decanter.com/wine/from-bordeaux-to-chile-celebrating-a-happy-accident-this-international-carmenere-day-569482/"},{"title":"Decanter: The curious case of Carmenère in Italy","url":"https://www.decanter.com/premium/the-curious-case-of-carmenere-in-italy-562898"}]},
"roussanne": {"slug":"roussanne","name":"Roussanne","colour":"white","kind":"grape","status":"thin","mode":"home","aliases":[],"notIn":[],"say":"roo-SAHN, in Savoie bair-zhuh-ROHN","lines":{"s10":"Roussanne is the scented white of the Rhône: ripe pear, honeysuckle and chamomile tea on a rich, silky body that stays fresh.","s20":"Roussanne is Marsanne's partner in the white wines of the Rhône, and the finer scented of the two. It smells of ripe pear, honeysuckle and chamomile tea, and it is full and silky with enough acidity to age.","s45":"Roussanne tests the patience of growers in the white Rhône, and repays it. It grows from Hermitage down to Châteauneuf-du-Pape, and it climbs into Savoie under the name Bergeron. It smells of ripe pear, honeysuckle and chamomile tea. It is full and silky, with more freshness than its partner, Marsanne. The Rhône tends to blend it and raise it in barrel, while California's Central Coast usually makes it riper and more honeyed. It flatters crab, roast veal or a mushroom risotto. A good many California vines sold as Roussanne in the 1990s turned out to be Viognier.","sale":"Roussanne is hard to grow, and one glass shows why growers bother: honeyed pear and honeysuckle, held up by a fresh finish. Try it with the crab or the roast veal."},"glass":{"lead":"Ripe pear, honeysuckle and chamomile tea with a touch of honey, on a full, silky body kept fresh by its acidity.","notes":[{"family":"orchard fruit","notes":["ripe pear","yellow apple"]},{"family":"stone fruit","notes":["apricot","white peach"]},{"family":"floral","notes":["honeysuckle","iris","hawthorn blossom","chamomile tea"]},{"family":"honeyed","notes":["acacia honey","beeswax"]},{"family":"nutty","notes":["roasted almond","hazelnut"]}],"ladder":[{"climate":"cool","where":"Chignin-Bergeron in Savoie, on sunny limestone scree","notes":["apricot","white peach","wet stone"]},{"climate":"moderate","where":"Hermitage, Saint-Joseph and Saint-Péray","notes":["ripe pear","honeysuckle","chamomile tea"]},{"climate":"warm","where":"Châteauneuf-du-Pape, the Languedoc, California's Central Coast","notes":["baked pear","acacia honey","roasted almond"]}],"structure":"Dry. Acidity medium, fresher than Marsanne's, alcohol medium to high, body full, with a silky, slightly oily texture."},"worlds":{"home":{"home":"The Rhône, where it is mostly blended: with Marsanne in white Hermitage and Saint-Joseph, with Grenache Blanc and Clairette in white Châteauneuf-du-Pape, often in barrel. It brings ripe pear, honeysuckle and chamomile tea and a fresh finish. Savoie bottles it alone as Chignin-Bergeron, rich yet fresh, with apricot and honey.","abroad":"California's Central Coast, Washington and the Swartland grow it, mostly for Rhône style blends with Marsanne, Viognier and Grenache Blanc. Some is bottled alone, typically riper and broader, with baked pear, acacia honey and roasted almond."}},"service":{"temp":"50 to 55 degrees Fahrenheit, 10 to 13 degrees Celsius: too cold and the scent and the texture hide.","glass":"A white wine glass with a generous bowl; a Burgundy bowl for a rich, mature wine.","decant":"Rarely. A young, closed white Rhône can open with a short decant; taste a sample first.","age":"Most within three to six years; white Hermitage and serious Châteauneuf whites keep ten years or more."},"food":{"with":[{"dish":"Crab cakes or crab in butter","why":"silky weight for sweet, rich crabmeat"},{"dish":"Roast veal or pork with cream","why":"body to match, freshness to cut the cream"},{"dish":"Mushroom risotto","why":"honey and roasted almond suit the mushrooms"},{"dish":"Roast chicken with herbs","why":"the tea and honeysuckle scent echo the herbs"}]},"labels":[{"term":"Bergeron, Chignin-Bergeron","means":"Roussanne's name in Savoie, and the cru made from it alone, on limestone scree."},{"term":"Châteauneuf-du-Pape Blanc","means":"The southern Rhône white: Roussanne with Grenache Blanc, Clairette and Bourboulenc. Marsanne is not permitted there."},{"term":"White Rhône blend","means":"Roussanne with Marsanne, Viognier or Grenache Blanc, wherever in the world it is made."}],"guest":[{"q":"I always see Roussanne next to Marsanne. Are they alike?","a":"They are partners. Marsanne gives weight and a waxy feel, and Roussanne brings the perfume, the freshness and much of the capacity to age. Roussanne is the harder of the two to grow."},{"q":"What does it taste like next to a Chardonnay?","a":"As full, but more scented: ripe pear, honeysuckle and a note like chamomile tea, with a silky, slightly oily feel and usually less obvious oak."},{"q":"What is Bergeron on this Savoie list?","a":"It is Roussanne. Savoie calls the grape Bergeron, and Chignin-Bergeron is its own cru there: rich yet fresh, with apricot and honey."}],"compare":[{"slug":"marsanne","tell":"Marsanne is broader, waxier and nuttier, with lower acidity; Roussanne is the more scented, with chamomile tea and a fresher finish."},{"slug":"viognier","tell":"Viognier is headier and louder, all apricot and honeysuckle on low acidity; Roussanne is subtler, with pear, chamomile tea and more freshness."},{"slug":"grenache-blanc","tell":"Grenache Blanc is plainer in scent, with green apple, fennel and a warm finish; Roussanne is more perfumed and more honeyed."}],"profile":{"c":"w","st":"Deep straw to gold · Body full · Acid medium · Alcohol medium to high · Dry · Silky, slightly oily texture","fruit":"Ripe pear, apricot, white peach; baked pear and yellow apple from warm sites","other":"Honeysuckle, iris, hawthorn blossom and chamomile tea; acacia honey and beeswax; roasted almond and hazelnut with age","tell":"A full, silky white of pear, honeysuckle and chamomile tea, with a fresher finish than its weight suggests","regions":"Hermitage, Crozes-Hermitage, Saint-Joseph and Saint-Péray with Marsanne; Châteauneuf-du-Pape blanc; Savoie as Bergeron (Chignin-Bergeron); California's Central Coast; Washington; the Swartland","confuse":"Marsanne is waxier, nuttier and lower in acid; Viognier is louder, with apricot and less acid; oaked Chardonnay shows butter and toast and no tea note"},"cards":[{"id":"gt-hbvlz8n5","front":"Roussanne: what is it called in Savoie, and which cru is its own?","back":"Bergeron. Chignin-Bergeron is made from Roussanne alone, grown on limestone scree: rich yet fresh, with apricot and honey."},{"id":"gt-cc2whea0","front":"Roussanne abroad: where is it grown, and how is it mostly used?","back":"California's Central Coast, Washington and the Swartland, mostly in Rhône style blends with Marsanne, Viognier and Grenache Blanc. Some is bottled alone, riper and more honeyed than at home."},{"id":"gt-kxiwebxy","front":"Which white grapes may join Roussanne in a Châteauneuf-du-Pape blanc?","back":"Grenache Blanc, Clairette, Bourboulenc, Picpoul and Picardan. Marsanne and Viognier are not among the appellation's permitted grapes."}],"questions":[{"id":"gt-jxt3u2de","q":"A blind white from Saint-Joseph is full and silky, with ripe pear, honeysuckle and chamomile tea and a fresh finish. Which grape most likely brings the scent?","opts":["Viognier","Roussanne","Marsanne","Grenache Blanc"],"a":1,"exp":"Pear, honeysuckle and chamomile tea with a fresh finish mark Roussanne, the scented half of the northern Rhône's white blend. Marsanne, its partner, is broader, waxier and nuttier, and Viognier would smell loudly of apricot."},{"id":"gt-s3zj51st","q":"A guest in a Savoie restaurant orders a Chignin-Bergeron and asks which grape it is made from. What do you tell him?","opts":["Altesse","Jacquère","Roussanne","Chasselas"],"a":2,"exp":"Bergeron is Savoie's name for Roussanne, and Chignin-Bergeron is made from it alone. Plain Chignin is chiefly Jacquère, and Altesse is the grape of Roussette de Savoie."},{"id":"gt-1m50nbsn","q":"A guest who knows the white grapes of the northern Rhône asks what the Châteauneuf-du-Pape blanc on the list can hold. Which pair may that appellation use?","opts":["Roussanne and Marsanne","Roussanne and Viognier","Marsanne and Viognier","Roussanne and Grenache Blanc"],"a":3,"exp":"Roussanne and Grenache Blanc are both on the list Châteauneuf-du-Pape permits, with Clairette and Bourboulenc. Marsanne and Viognier are not, though both make white wine in the northern Rhône."}],"sources":[{"title":"Inter Rhône: Roussanne, the grape variety","url":"https://www.vins-rhone.com/en/roussanne-grape-variety"},{"title":"Châteauneuf-du-Pape: the appellation's rule, cahier des charges","url":"https://www.chateauneuf.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/10/CDC-Chateauneuf-du-Pape.pdf"},{"title":"Capstone California: Roussanne, history","url":"https://capstonecalifornia.com/study-guides/varieties/white/roussanne/history"},{"title":"Rhône Rangers: more about Roussanne","url":"https://rhonerangers.org/wine-education/white-grapes/more-about-roussanne"}]},
"garganega": {"slug":"garganega","name":"Garganega","colour":"white","kind":"grape","status":"thin","mode":"home","aliases":[],"notIn":[],"say":"gar-GAH-neh-gah","lines":{"s10":"Garganega is the grape of Soave, from the hills east of Verona: lemon, white peach and a clean finish of bitter almond.","s20":"Garganega makes Soave, the dry white of the hills east of Verona. It tastes of lemon, white peach and ripe pear with a finish of bitter almond, and the hillside wines of Soave Classico add a stony depth.","s45":"Garganega goes into every glass of Soave, and it grows on hills of dark volcanic rock and pale limestone east of Verona. It tastes of lemon, white peach and ripe pear, and it finishes on a note of bitter almond. It is dry and light to medium in body, fresh without being sharp. Most of it is made in steel tanks and bottled young, with no oak to hide the fruit. Pour it with risotto, lake fish or white asparagus. Dried on racks through the winter, the same grape becomes Recioto di Soave, a golden sweet wine that tastes of honey.","sale":"Garganega from the hills of Soave suits a creamy plate: lemon and white peach to lift it, bitter almond to close it. The risotto is its natural partner, and so is the redfish."},"glass":{"lead":"Lemon, white peach and ripe pear with acacia blossom, soft and dry, closing on bitter almond and a stony edge.","notes":[{"family":"citrus","notes":["lemon zest","orange zest"]},{"family":"orchard fruit","notes":["ripe pear","yellow apple"]},{"family":"stone fruit","notes":["white peach","apricot"]},{"family":"floral","notes":["acacia blossom","elderflower"]},{"family":"nutty","notes":["bitter almond","marzipan"]},{"family":"mineral","notes":["wet stone","crushed chalk","a smoky edge"]}],"ladder":[{"climate":"moderate","where":"The hills of Soave Classico and Gambellara","notes":["lemon zest","white peach","bitter almond","wet stone"]},{"climate":"warm","where":"Sicily, where it is called Grecanico","notes":["grapefruit","green apple","white peach","sea salt"]}],"structure":"Dry. Acidity medium to medium plus, brighter from the hills, alcohol moderate, body light to medium; Recioto di Soave, from dried grapes, is sweet and full."},"worlds":{"home":{"home":"The hills and plain east of Verona, in the Veneto. Soave must be at least 70 per cent Garganega, with Trebbiano di Soave or Chardonnay for the rest, and Gambellara next door grows the same grape. The vines are trained high on pergolas and ripen late. Soave Classico, from hills of volcanic basalt and limestone, is the wine that keeps.","abroad":"It scarcely leaves Italy. In Sicily the same vine is called Grecanico Dorato, as DNA work has shown, and it makes crisp, lemony dry whites there. Outside Italy it is a rarity."}},"service":{"temp":"45 to 50 degrees Fahrenheit, 7 to 10 degrees Celsius; a Soave Classico at the warmer end.","glass":"A white wine glass; a small glass for the sweet Recioto di Soave.","decant":"No. A light white with no oak needs only to be cold and freshly opened.","age":"Most within two or three years; Soave Classico from the hills can keep ten."},"food":{"with":[{"dish":"Risotto with peas or spring vegetables","why":"a gentle wine beside a gentle, creamy dish"},{"dish":"Lake fish or trout, simply cooked","why":"lemon and almond season the fish"},{"dish":"White asparagus with egg","why":"a mild white with no oak suits asparagus"},{"dish":"Grilled redfish with lemon","why":"a light white leaves the fish its sweetness"},{"dish":"Almond tart, with Recioto di Soave","why":"the sweet wine echoes the almond"}]},"labels":[{"term":"Soave","means":"A town and its wine zone east of Verona; the wine is at least 70 per cent Garganega."},{"term":"Soave Classico","means":"From the historic hill zone around Soave and Monteforte d'Alpone, on volcanic basalt and limestone."},{"term":"Soave Superiore","means":"A separate DOCG for hillside Soave made to stricter rules; not the same thing as Classico."},{"term":"Recioto di Soave","means":"Sweet golden wine from Garganega dried on racks for months; the Veneto's first DOCG, in 1998."},{"term":"Grecanico","means":"Sicily's name for the same vine, in full Grecanico Dorato."}],"guest":[{"q":"Is Soave a grape or a place?","a":"A place: a town east of Verona and the hills around it. The grape is Garganega, which must make up at least 70 per cent of the wine."},{"q":"I remember Soave as thin and plain. Has it changed?","a":"That was mostly wine from the heavily cropped plain. Ask for Soave Classico from the hills, where old vines on volcanic rock and limestone give white peach, almond and a stony depth."},{"q":"What is that slightly bitter taste at the end?","a":"Bitter almond, the mark of the grape. It is meant to be there, and it is one reason Soave sits so well beside rice, fish and anything creamy."}],"compare":[{"slug":"pinot-grigio","tell":"Pinot Grigio, its neighbour, is lighter and more neutral, with pear and a fainter almond; Garganega has white peach, more weight and a firm bitter almond finish."},{"slug":"verdicchio","tell":"Verdicchio is sharper and leaner, with higher acidity, lemon and green almond; Garganega is softer and rounder, with white peach."},{"slug":"cortese","tell":"Cortese, as Gavi, is leaner and more lemony, with green apple and a stony finish; Garganega is softer, with white peach and more almond."}],"profile":{"c":"w","st":"Pale lemon, gold as Recioto · Body light to medium · Acid medium to medium plus · Alcohol moderate · Dry, or sweet from dried grapes","fruit":"Lemon zest, white peach, ripe pear; yellow apple and apricot from limestone and from riper sites","other":"Bitter almond and marzipan, acacia blossom and elderflower; a chalky or smoky finish from the hills; honey in Recioto di Soave","tell":"A soft, dry Italian white of lemon and white peach that ends on bitter almond, with no oak: Soave, and from the hills a stony depth","regions":"Soave and Soave Classico, Gambellara and the Custoza blend in the Veneto; Sicily as Grecanico Dorato","confuse":"Pinot Grigio is lighter and more neutral; Verdicchio is sharper, with higher acidity; Cortese is leaner and more lemony; Garganega is rounder, with white peach and bitter almond"},"cards":[{"id":"gt-llm2fwpz","front":"Garganega: which wine does it make, and how much must the blend hold?","back":"Soave, from the hills and plain east of Verona. At least 70 per cent Garganega, with Trebbiano di Soave or Chardonnay allowed for the rest."},{"id":"gt-79bfh88n","front":"What is Garganega called in Sicily, and how do we know?","back":"Grecanico Dorato. DNA work published in 2008 found the two names to belong to one vine."},{"id":"gt-drtl79bo","front":"Garganega dried on racks through the winter makes which sweet wine?","back":"Recioto di Soave: golden and honeyed, from the same hills as dry Soave. It was the Veneto's first DOCG, in 1998."}],"questions":[{"id":"gt-ppgajh2r","q":"A blind white is pale lemon and dry, with lemon, white peach and ripe pear, no oak, medium acidity and a finish of bitter almond. Which wine is it most likely to be?","opts":["Soave Classico","Petit Chablis","Gavi di Gavi","Pouilly-Fumé"],"a":0,"exp":"Medium acidity, white peach and a bitter almond finish with no oak point to Garganega from Soave. Gavi would be leaner and sharper, with green apple, and Pouilly-Fumé and Petit Chablis sharper still."},{"id":"gt-t9ef0q0b","q":"A guest reading an Italian list asks what sets Soave Classico apart from a wine labelled only Soave. What do you tell her?","opts":["It is aged longer in oak before release","It is made from Garganega with no other grape","It comes from the historic hill zone around the town","It is made from grapes dried on racks"],"a":2,"exp":"Classico names the original hill zone around Soave and Monteforte d'Alpone, on volcanic basalt and limestone. It is no rule of oak or of ageing, and dried grapes make Recioto di Soave."},{"id":"gt-vmgc7q71","q":"At the end of dinner a guest asks for a sweet wine made from Garganega, the grape of the dry white she enjoyed. Which do you bring?","opts":["Recioto della Valpolicella","Recioto di Soave","Vin Santo","Moscato d'Asti"],"a":1,"exp":"Recioto di Soave is Garganega dried on racks and made sweet. Recioto della Valpolicella is its red neighbour, from Corvina, and Moscato d'Asti and Vin Santo come from other grapes."}],"sources":[{"title":"Consorzio Tutela Vini Soave: the grapes","url":"https://www.ilsoave.com/en/the-grapes/"},{"title":"Consorzio Tutela Vini Soave: the soils","url":"https://www.ilsoave.com/en/soils/"},{"title":"Associazione Italiana Sommelier: the production rules of Soave DOC","url":"https://aisitalia.it/Disciplinari/Veneto/DOC/Soave.pdf"},{"title":"Vitis, 2008: Sangiovese and Garganega are two key varieties of the Italian grapevine assortment evolution","url":"https://ojs.openagrar.de/index.php/VITIS/article/view/4185"}]},
"cortese": {"slug":"cortese","name":"Cortese","colour":"white","kind":"grape","status":"thin","mode":"home","aliases":[],"notIn":[],"say":"kor-TEH-zeh","lines":{"s10":"Cortese gives Piedmont its crisp white, Gavi: bone dry, with lemon, green apple and a clean, stony finish.","s20":"Cortese is the grape of Gavi, from the hills of south east Piedmont that look towards the Ligurian sea. It is light, bone dry and sharp, with lemon, green apple and a stony finish, a wine made to be poured cold with fish.","s45":"Cortese makes Gavi, a crisp white from the south east corner of Piedmont, a region known first for its red wine. It tastes of lemon, green apple and pear, with a stony finish and a faint note of almond. It is light and bone dry, and its acidity is high and bright. The winemaking is plain: steel tanks, no oak and an early bottling, though a little is made sparkling. Serve it cold with oysters, raw fish or a plate of fried seafood. Gavi sits just over the hills from Genoa, and its wine has long gone to the fish restaurants of that coast.","sale":"Cortese makes Gavi, the cold, bone dry white that has long gone to the fish restaurants of Genoa's coast. I would open it with the oysters and keep the bottle on the table through the fried fish."},"glass":{"lead":"Lemon zest, green apple and pear, light and bone dry, with acacia blossom and a stony, faintly almond finish.","notes":[{"family":"citrus","notes":["lemon zest","lime"]},{"family":"orchard fruit","notes":["green apple","ripe pear"]},{"family":"floral","notes":["acacia blossom","hawthorn"]},{"family":"nutty","notes":["bitter almond","fresh almond"]},{"family":"mineral","notes":["wet stone","crushed chalk"]}],"ladder":[{"climate":"cool","where":"The higher hills of Gavi, towards the Apennines","notes":["lemon zest","green apple","wet stone"]},{"climate":"moderate","where":"The lower slopes of Gavi and the Alto Monferrato","notes":["ripe pear","white peach","fresh almond"]}],"structure":"Bone dry. Acidity high, alcohol moderate, body light; a little is made gently or fully sparkling."},"worlds":{"home":{"home":"The south east corner of Piedmont: eleven communes of the province of Alessandria around the town of Gavi, on clay and limestone a short way from the Ligurian coast. Gavi is Cortese alone, mostly still, made in steel and bottled young, with frizzante, spumante and Riserva versions. The grape also grows in the Alto Monferrato and the Colli Tortonesi.","abroad":"It hardly travels. Beyond Piedmont it has a part in the Custoza blend near Lake Garda, and outside Italy it is scarcely grown."}},"service":{"temp":"45 to 50 degrees Fahrenheit, 7 to 10 degrees Celsius: well chilled.","glass":"A white wine glass; a tulip for the sparkling versions.","decant":"No. It is a light, crisp white, poured cold and fresh.","age":"Enjoy most within two or three years; a Riserva is built to keep a few years longer."},"food":{"with":[{"dish":"Gulf oysters and raw shellfish","why":"high acidity and a stony finish meet the brine"},{"dish":"Raw fish, as crudo or carpaccio","why":"a light wine leaves delicate fish alone"},{"dish":"Fried seafood","why":"sharp acidity cuts through the frying"},{"dish":"Pasta with basil pesto","why":"the dish of Genoa next door wants a crisp white"}]},"labels":[{"term":"Gavi, Cortese di Gavi","means":"One wine under either name: Cortese alone, from eleven communes around the town of Gavi."},{"term":"Gavi di Gavi","means":"In full Gavi del Comune di Gavi: grapes grown in the commune of Gavi itself, not a higher rank."},{"term":"Riserva","means":"Gavi from lower yields, aged at least a year before release, six months of it in bottle."},{"term":"Frizzante, Spumante","means":"Gently sparkling and fully sparkling Gavi, both allowed beside the still wine."}],"guest":[{"q":"What grape is Gavi made from?","a":"Cortese, and nothing else. Gavi is the town, in the south east of Piedmont, and the rules allow no other grape in the wine."},{"q":"What does Gavi di Gavi mean? Is it a step up from Gavi?","a":"It means the grapes grew in the commune of Gavi itself, one of eleven in the zone. It tells you where the wine is from, not how good it is; the grower matters more."},{"q":"I usually order Pinot Grigio. Will I like this?","a":"Very likely. It is just as light and dry, with a little more bite: lemon, green apple and a clean, stony finish. It is at its happiest beside shellfish."}],"compare":[{"slug":"arneis","tell":"Arneis, Piedmont's other white, is rounder and softer, with pear, almond and lower acidity; Cortese is leaner and sharper, with lemon and green apple."},{"slug":"pinot-grigio","tell":"Pinot Grigio is as light but softer and more neutral, with pear; Cortese has higher acidity, more lemon and a stony finish."},{"slug":"garganega","tell":"Garganega, as Soave, is softer and rounder, with white peach and a bitter almond finish; Cortese is leaner and more lemony, with green apple."}],"profile":{"c":"w","st":"Pale straw · Body light · Acid high · Alcohol moderate · Bone dry, still or sparkling","fruit":"Lemon zest, lime, green apple, ripe pear; a hint of white peach from warmer slopes and years","other":"Acacia blossom and hawthorn, a faint bitter almond, a stony finish; no oak as a rule","tell":"A light, bone dry, high acid Italian white of lemon and green apple with a stony finish and little perfume: Gavi","regions":"Gavi in south east Piedmont; Cortese dell'Alto Monferrato and Colli Tortonesi; a part of the Custoza blend by Lake Garda","confuse":"Arneis is rounder, with pear and lower acidity; Pinot Grigio is softer and more neutral; Garganega is rounder, with white peach and bitter almond"},"cards":[{"id":"gt-siyi00pe","front":"Cortese: which wine does it make, and where is the town?","back":"Gavi, also labelled Cortese di Gavi: Cortese alone, from eleven communes of the province of Alessandria in south east Piedmont, near the Ligurian border. A DOCG since 1998."},{"id":"gt-j34nanlz","front":"What does Gavi di Gavi on a label of Cortese mean?","back":"That the grapes grew in the commune of Gavi itself: in full, Gavi del Comune di Gavi. It names a place, not a higher rank."},{"id":"gt-vfzu9mcr","front":"Cortese blind: what gives a glass of Gavi away?","back":"A light, bone dry white with high acidity: lemon, green apple and a stony finish, no oak and little perfume."}],"questions":[{"id":"gt-3ie47upw","q":"A guest orders a dozen raw oysters and asks for a light, bone dry white from Piedmont with plenty of acidity. Which bottle do you bring?","opts":["Erbaluce di Caluso Passito","Gavi","Roero Arneis","Moscato d'Asti"],"a":1,"exp":"Gavi, from Cortese, is light, bone dry and high in acidity, a natural match for raw shellfish. Roero Arneis is rounder and softer, and Moscato d'Asti and a passito are sweet."},{"id":"gt-4bo7ktfg","q":"A blind white is pale and bone dry, light in body with high acidity: lemon, green apple and a stony finish, no oak and little perfume. Which grape is it most likely to be?","opts":["Cortese","Viognier","Gewürztraminer","Arneis"],"a":0,"exp":"Light body, high acidity, lemon and green apple with a stony finish point to Cortese from Gavi. Arneis is rounder, with lower acidity, and Viognier and Gewürztraminer are loudly perfumed."},{"id":"gt-5mbekruc","q":"A guest points to Gavi di Gavi on the list and asks whether it is a higher grade than the plain Gavi beside it. What do you tell her?","opts":["No: it marks the gently sparkling version","Yes: it must come from older vines","Yes: it is the Riserva, aged a year longer","No: it names grapes grown in the commune of Gavi"],"a":3,"exp":"Gavi di Gavi, in full Gavi del Comune di Gavi, names fruit from the commune of Gavi, one of eleven in the zone. It is a place, not a rank; the Riserva is the wine held a year before release."}],"sources":[{"title":"Associazione Italiana Sommelier: the production rules of Gavi or Cortese di Gavi DOCG","url":"https://aisitalia.it/Disciplinari/Piemonte/DOCG/Gavi%20o%20Cortese%20di%20Gavi.pdf"},{"title":"Italian Wine Central: Gavi DOCG","url":"https://italianwinecentral.com/denomination/gavi-docg/"},{"title":"The Oxford Companion to Wine","url":""}]},
"glera": {"slug":"glera","name":"Glera","colour":"white","kind":"grape","status":"thin","mode":"home","aliases":[],"notIn":[],"say":"GLEH-rah","lines":{"s10":"Glera is the grape of Prosecco: light, bright bubbles that taste of green apple, pear and white peach.","s20":"Glera is the grape behind Prosecco, from the hills north of Venice. It makes a light sparkling wine of green apple, pear and acacia blossom, soft and easy from the first sip.","s45":"Glera is the grape of Prosecco, grown in the Veneto and Friuli in the north east of Italy. It tastes of green apple, ripe pear and white peach, with a scent of acacia blossom. The wine is light in body and gentle in alcohol, and often just off dry. Its bubbles are made in a sealed tank, not in the bottle, which keeps that fresh fruit in front. Champagne tastes of bread and time, and Prosecco tastes of the grape. Pour it before dinner, with cured ham and melon, or with anything fried. Until 2009 the grape itself was called Prosecco, and the name now belongs to the place.","sale":"Glera, as Prosecco, is the easy way to begin: green apple, pear and soft bubbles that ask nothing of you. I would open the evening with it, beside the fried seafood or the ham."},"glass":{"lead":"Green apple, ripe pear and white peach with acacia blossom, light and fresh, often with a touch of sweetness.","notes":[{"family":"orchard fruit","notes":["green apple","ripe pear","yellow apple"]},{"family":"stone fruit","notes":["white peach","apricot"]},{"family":"floral","notes":["acacia blossom","wisteria","honeysuckle"]}],"ladder":[{"climate":"cool","where":"The steep hills of Conegliano Valdobbiadene and Asolo","notes":["green apple","white peach","wisteria"]},{"climate":"moderate","where":"The plains of the Veneto and Friuli","notes":["yellow apple","ripe pear"]}],"structure":"Dry to off dry, with medium plus acidity, a light body and low to moderate alcohol; a soft, frothy sparkle."},"worlds":{"home":{"home":"The north east of Italy: the Veneto and Friuli Venezia Giulia under the name Prosecco, with the steep hills of Conegliano Valdobbiadene and Asolo at its heart. The second fermentation happens in a sealed tank, so the wine keeps its green apple and pear freshness and is sold young.","abroad":"It rarely leaves home. Australia grows it and still calls the grape Prosecco, a name Europe keeps for the Italian zone, so a bottle labelled Glera is seldom seen."}},"service":{"temp":"43 to 46 degrees Fahrenheit, 6 to 8 degrees Celsius, straight from the ice bucket.","glass":"A tulip shaped sparkling glass or a white wine glass; a narrow flute hides the perfume.","decant":"No. Open it cold and pour gently in two motions to keep the sparkle.","age":"Enjoy it young, within a year or two of release; freshness is the point."},"food":{"with":[{"dish":"Prosciutto and melon","why":"soft fruit and bubbles against salt and sweetness"},{"dish":"Fried seafood","why":"bubbles and acidity cut through the frying"},{"dish":"Shrimp remoulade","why":"a touch of sweetness cools the mustard and cayenne"},{"dish":"Light starters and soft cheeses","why":"a gentle wine that does not crowd the plate"}]},"labels":[{"term":"Prosecco","means":"A place name since 2009, not a grape: sparkling Glera from the Veneto and Friuli."},{"term":"Extra Dry","means":"Sweeter than Brut, not drier: a soft, slightly sweet style long traditional for Prosecco."},{"term":"Spumante, Frizzante","means":"Spumante is fully sparkling; Frizzante is gently sparkling, with a softer fizz."},{"term":"Conegliano Valdobbiadene","means":"The steep hill zone, labelled Prosecco Superiore; Rive names a single hillside village, Cartizze one prized hill."},{"term":"Col Fondo","means":"Refermented in the bottle and sold cloudy, on its yeast."}],"guest":[{"q":"Is Prosecco the same thing as Champagne?","a":"No. Champagne is from France, from other grapes, and gets its bubbles in the bottle, which gives bread and toast. Prosecco is Glera from Italy, made in a tank to keep its fresh apple and pear."},{"q":"I asked for a dry one. Why does Extra Dry taste a little sweet?","a":"The names run backwards. Brut is the drier style and Extra Dry carries a little more sugar, so ask for Brut if you want it crisp."},{"q":"The label only says Prosecco. What is Glera?","a":"Glera is the grape. It was itself called Prosecco until 2009, when the name was kept for the place and its wines."}],"compare":[{"slug":"chardonnay","tell":"Chardonnay in Champagne gives lemon, chalk and brioche from years on its lees; Glera stays with green apple, pear and acacia blossom, lighter and softer."},{"slug":"macabeo","tell":"Macabeo is a grape of Cava, which is made in the bottle like Champagne and tastes drier, with toast and almond; Glera is softer and more openly fruity."},{"slug":"muscat-blanc","tell":"Moscato d'Asti is sweeter, lower in alcohol and loudly grapey, with orange blossom; Glera is drier and far quieter in scent."}],"profile":{"c":"w","st":"Pale straw with a fine, frothy mousse · Body light · Acid medium plus · Alcohol low to moderate · Dry to off dry, usually sparkling","fruit":"Green apple, ripe pear, white peach; yellow apple and apricot from warmer sites","other":"Acacia blossom, wisteria and honeysuckle; little yeast character, since the bubbles are made in a tank; often a touch of sweetness","tell":"Light and softly sparkling, with green apple, pear and acacia blossom and no bready note: tank method fruit, not bottle age","regions":"Prosecco in the Veneto and Friuli Venezia Giulia; Conegliano Valdobbiadene and Asolo in the hills","confuse":"Champagne and Cava show brioche and toast from bottle ageing; Moscato d'Asti is sweeter and more grapey; Glera sits between, fresh and scented with acacia"},"cards":[{"id":"gt-aeroikhr","front":"Glera: which wine does it make, and how are the bubbles made?","back":"Prosecco, from the Veneto and Friuli Venezia Giulia. The second fermentation takes place in a sealed tank, the Martinotti or Charmat method, which keeps the fruit fresh."},{"id":"gt-r5iy2opd","front":"On a Prosecco label, which is sweeter: Brut or Extra Dry?","back":"Extra Dry. Brut holds less than 12 grams of sugar a litre; Extra Dry holds 12 to 17, and Dry 17 to 32."},{"id":"gt-193vqupx","front":"How much Glera must a Prosecco hold, and what happened in 2009?","back":"At least 85 per cent. In 2009 the grape, until then called Prosecco, was renamed Glera, and Prosecco became a protected place name."}],"questions":[{"id":"gt-kqrkdswm","q":"A guest wants bubbles with no hint of sweetness, and the list offers one Prosecco in four styles. Which do you pour?","opts":["Extra Dry","Dry","Demi-Sec","Brut"],"a":3,"exp":"Of the four, Brut holds the least sugar, under 12 grams a litre. Extra Dry and Dry are both sweeter than Brut, despite their names, and Demi-Sec is sweeter still."},{"id":"gt-3jtns0l1","q":"A sparkling wine poured blind is pale, light and softly fizzy, all green apple, pear and acacia blossom, with no bread or toast. Which is it most likely to be?","opts":["Champagne","Franciacorta","Prosecco","Cava"],"a":2,"exp":"Green apple, pear and acacia with no bready note point to the tank method and Glera. Champagne, Cava and Franciacorta are made in the bottle and show brioche or toast."},{"id":"gt-4lzz8kud","q":"A guest asks why the front label says Prosecco and the back label says Glera. What is the difference?","opts":["Prosecco is the place and the wine; Glera is the grape","Glera is the village; Prosecco is the grape","Prosecco is sparkling; Glera is the still version","Glera is a sweeter style of Prosecco"],"a":0,"exp":"Since 2009 Prosecco has been a protected place name for wines of the Veneto and Friuli Venezia Giulia, and the grape, once called Prosecco too, is Glera."}],"sources":[{"title":"Prosecco DOC Consortium: the production regulation","url":"https://www.prosecco.wine/en/prosecco/production-regulation/"},{"title":"Prosecco DOC Consortium: the types and their sugar levels","url":"https://www.prosecco.wine/en/prosecco/discover/types/"},{"title":"Conegliano Valdobbiadene Prosecco Superiore: wine and food matching","url":"https://www.prosecco.it/en/conegliano-valdobbiadene-prosecco-superiore-docg/wine-and-food-matching/"},{"title":"Wine Australia: Prosecco, what is the deal","url":"https://www.wineaustralia.com/news/articles/prosecco-what-is-the-deal"}]},
"silvaner": {"slug":"silvaner","name":"Silvaner","colour":"white","kind":"grape","status":"thin","mode":"home","aliases":[],"notIn":[],"say":"sil-VAH-ner, in Germany zil-VAH-ner","lines":{"s10":"Silvaner is Franken's dry white, bottled in a round flask: pear, green apple and fresh hay, gentle and made for the table.","s20":"Silvaner is a dry German white, at home in Franken and Rheinhessen. It is gentle and savoury, with pear, green apple and a scent of fresh hay, and it is the wine Germans pour with white asparagus.","s45":"Silvaner is what Germany pours when the white asparagus arrives. It makes a dry white, at home in Franken, around Würzburg, and in Rheinhessen. It tastes of pear, green apple and honeydew melon, with a scent of fresh hay and a savoury note like damp earth. The acidity is gentle and the body medium, so nothing shouts. Growers make it in steel or big old casks, with no new oak. Besides asparagus, pour it with river fish, shellfish or a plate of ham. In Franken it comes in the Bocksbeutel, a flat, round flask that is the region's trademark.","sale":"When the white asparagus arrives, Germany reaches for Silvaner, with its pear, green apple and savoury finish. I would pour it with the asparagus, or with any simply cooked fish."},"glass":{"lead":"Pear, green apple and honeydew melon with fresh hay and a savoury, damp earth finish, dry, gentle and unoaked.","notes":[{"family":"orchard fruit","notes":["ripe pear","green apple","quince"]},{"family":"stone fruit","notes":["white peach","yellow plum"]},{"family":"herbal","notes":["fresh hay","cut grass"]},{"family":"earth","notes":["damp earth","celery root"]},{"family":"nutty","notes":["sweet almond","bitter almond"]}],"ladder":[{"climate":"cool","where":"Franken, Rheinhessen, Alsace and Alto Adige's Isarco Valley","notes":["green apple","fresh hay","cut grass"]},{"climate":"moderate","where":"The sunny, dry Valais in Switzerland, as Johannisberg","notes":["ripe pear","honeydew melon","sweet almond"]}],"structure":"Dry. Acidity medium, gentler than Riesling's, alcohol moderate, body medium; a soft, savoury feel with no new oak."},"worlds":{"home":{"home":"Germany, in two regions. Franken, around Würzburg on the river Main, makes it dry and firm on shell limestone and bottles it in the Bocksbeutel. Rheinhessen grows it widely too. It came from Austria, and the first record of it in Germany is from Castell in Franken, in 1659.","abroad":"Alsace spells it Sylvaner and makes a light, fresh white; one grand cru, Zotzenberg, admits it. Alto Adige grows it high in the Isarco Valley, and the Swiss Valais calls it Johannisberg, a rounder wine with an almond note."}},"service":{"temp":"45 to 50 degrees Fahrenheit, 7 to 10 degrees Celsius: cool, not icy, so the gentle scent can show.","glass":"A white wine glass; nothing wider is needed.","decant":"Usually unnecessary: pour it cool and fresh.","age":"Most are for enjoying young, within a few years; the firmest dry wines of Franken keep longer."},"food":{"with":[{"dish":"White asparagus with butter or hollandaise","why":"gentle acidity and a savoury note suit the spears"},{"dish":"Trout and other river fish","why":"a mild wine that does not cover delicate fish"},{"dish":"Gulf oysters and other shellfish","why":"quiet fruit leaves room for the brine"},{"dish":"Ham, sausages and cold cuts","why":"soft, savoury fruit beside salt and smoke"}]},"labels":[{"term":"Sylvaner","means":"The spelling in Alsace, Alto Adige and Switzerland; the same grape."},{"term":"Bocksbeutel","means":"The flat, round flask of Franken, also allowed in two corners of Baden."},{"term":"Johannisberg","means":"The name for Silvaner in the Swiss canton of Valais; it is not Riesling."},{"term":"Zotzenberg","means":"An Alsace grand cru whose rules admit Sylvaner, by exception."}],"guest":[{"q":"What is that odd round bottle?","a":"It is the Bocksbeutel, the traditional flask of Franken. The shape is the region's trademark, so when you see it you can expect a dry Franconian wine, very often Silvaner."},{"q":"I have never heard of Silvaner. What is it like?","a":"Think of something close to Pinot Blanc but more savoury: dry, with pear and green apple, soft acidity and no oak. It is a wine for food more than for sipping alone."},{"q":"Is it sweet?","a":"Rarely. Franken makes it properly dry, and most of what you will meet on a list is dry too."}],"compare":[{"slug":"muller-thurgau","tell":"Müller-Thurgau is lighter and more scented, with elderflower, a faint nutmeg note and softer acidity; Silvaner is firmer, with pear, hay and a savoury finish."},{"slug":"riesling","tell":"Riesling has piercing acidity, lime and often some sweetness; Silvaner is gentler, dry and savoury, with pear and fresh hay."},{"slug":"pinot-blanc","tell":"Pinot Blanc is rounder, with ripe apple and almond; Silvaner is leaner and more savoury, with green apple, hay and damp earth."}],"profile":{"c":"w","st":"Pale straw with green tints · Body medium · Acid medium · Alcohol moderate · Dry; no new oak","fruit":"Ripe pear, green apple and quince; white peach and honeydew melon from warmer sites","other":"Fresh hay and cut grass, damp earth and celery root; a savoury, gently bitter finish; sweet almond in the Valais","tell":"A gentle, dry, savoury white: pear and green apple with fresh hay and damp earth, medium acidity and no loud perfume","regions":"Franken (in the Bocksbeutel) and Rheinhessen in Germany; Alsace as Sylvaner, with Zotzenberg; Alto Adige's Isarco Valley; the Valais as Johannisberg","confuse":"Riesling has higher acidity and lime; Müller-Thurgau is lighter, with elderflower and nutmeg; Pinot Blanc is rounder, with almond"},"cards":[{"id":"gt-8tnvuog3","front":"Silvaner: which German region is its home, and in what bottle?","back":"Franken, around Würzburg, where it is made dry and bottled in the flat, round Bocksbeutel. Rheinhessen grows it widely too."},{"id":"gt-2ion422j","front":"Silvaner: who are its parents, and when did it reach Germany?","back":"A natural cross of Traminer and Österreichisch Weiss, from Austria. It was first planted in Germany, on record, at Castell in Franken, on 6 April 1659."},{"id":"gt-sfuqcv4k","front":"Under what names does Silvaner appear in Alsace and in the Valais?","back":"Sylvaner in Alsace, where the grand cru Zotzenberg admits it, and Johannisberg in the Swiss Valais."}],"questions":[{"id":"gt-rsf8yyyi","q":"A guest points to a squat, flat, round flask on the shelf of German wines and asks what is likely to be inside. What do you tell him?","opts":["Sparkling wine from the Rheingau","Dry Silvaner from Franken","Sweet Riesling from the Mosel","Red wine from the Ahr"],"a":1,"exp":"The Bocksbeutel is the flask of Franken, whose signature is dry Silvaner. Mosel Riesling comes in a tall, slim bottle."},{"id":"gt-z1ccpm5v","q":"White asparagus with hollandaise is on the menu, and a guest wants a quiet, dry white that will not fight it. Which grape do you suggest?","opts":["Torrontés","Viognier","Silvaner","Gewürztraminer"],"a":2,"exp":"Silvaner's gentle acidity and quiet, savoury fruit make it Germany's own match for asparagus. Gewürztraminer, Viognier and Torrontés are loudly perfumed and would cover the dish."},{"id":"gt-kho6pygo","q":"A blind white is dry and gentle: pear, green apple and fresh hay, a savoury finish, medium acidity and neither oak nor perfume. Which grape is it most likely to be?","opts":["Riesling","Sauvignon Blanc","Gewürztraminer","Silvaner"],"a":3,"exp":"Quiet pear and hay with a savoury finish and only medium acidity point to Silvaner. Riesling would show piercing acidity and lime, and Sauvignon Blanc a loud scent of grapefruit and cut grass."}],"sources":[{"title":"Wines of Germany: Silvaner","url":"https://www.winesofgermany.com/our-wine/grape-varieties/grape-variety/108/silvaner"},{"title":"Wines of Germany: highlights of German wine culture, Castell","url":"https://www.winesofgermany.com/our-regions/highlight-of-wine-culture/149/castell"},{"title":"Vins d'Alsace: Sylvaner","url":"https://www.vinsalsace.com/en/gouts-et-couleurs/cepages/frais-et-secs/sylvaner-dalsace/"},{"title":"Alto Adige Wines: Sylvaner","url":"https://www.altoadigewines.com/en/wine-varieties/sylvaner/22-12646.html"}]},
"pinot-blanc": {"slug":"pinot-blanc","name":"Pinot Blanc","colour":"white","kind":"grape","status":"thin","mode":"home","aliases":[],"notIn":[],"say":"PEE-noh BLAHN, in Germany VICE-boor-goon-der, in Italy PEE-noh BYAHN-koh","lines":{"s10":"Pinot Blanc is the quiet one of the Pinot family, a gentle dry white of apple, pear and a hint of almond.","s20":"Pinot Blanc grows in Alsace, Germany and the north of Italy, where it goes by Weissburgunder or Pinot Bianco. It is a soft, dry white of ripe apple, pear and almond, rounder and milder than most, and a friend to simple food.","s45":"Pinot Blanc never raises its voice. It is the pale member of the Pinot family, grown in Alsace, in Germany as Weissburgunder and in the north of Italy as Pinot Bianco. It tastes of ripe apple, pear and a little almond, and it is dry, soft and medium in body. Growers mostly use steel or old casks, and in Alsace it is the main grape of the sparkling wine. It suits quiche, river fish or white asparagus. For generations growers planted it mixed with Chardonnay and took the two for one grape.","sale":"Pinot Blanc shows the gentle side of the Pinot family, with ripe apple, pear and a hint of almond. Open a bottle with the quiche or the lighter fish, or simply to start."},"glass":{"lead":"Ripe apple, pear and white peach with a hint of almond, soft and rounded, on gentle acidity.","notes":[{"family":"orchard fruit","notes":["ripe apple","pear","quince"]},{"family":"citrus","notes":["lemon","lemon zest"]},{"family":"stone fruit","notes":["white peach","ripe peach"]},{"family":"floral","notes":["apple blossom","acacia blossom"]},{"family":"nutty","notes":["almond","green walnut","hazelnut"]},{"family":"lees and cellar","notes":["bread dough","brioche"]}],"ladder":[{"climate":"cool","where":"Alto Adige's high slopes, Alsace and the Willamette Valley","notes":["green apple","lemon zest","white peach","acacia blossom"]},{"climate":"moderate","where":"Baden, the Pfalz and Alto Adige's lower, warmer sites","notes":["ripe apple","ripe pear","hazelnut"]}],"structure":"Dry. Acidity medium, alcohol moderate, body medium; soft and rounded, a touch fuller where it is raised in cask."},"worlds":{"home":{"home":"Alsace, Germany, the north of Italy and Austria. In Alsace it is often blended with Auxerrois and is the main grape of white Crémant d'Alsace. Germany and Austria call it Weissburgunder and make it dry, sometimes in cask. Alto Adige, as Pinot Bianco, grows it high for apple, pear and a sleek, fresh line.","abroad":"It has travelled little. Oregon's Willamette Valley and California's Central Coast bottle some, mostly in a fresh style that strays little from the quiet model of home."}},"service":{"temp":"45 to 50 degrees Fahrenheit, 7 to 10 degrees Celsius; a cask aged wine a little warmer.","glass":"A white wine glass; a tulip for Crémant d'Alsace.","decant":"Usually unnecessary; serve it cool and fresh.","age":"Enjoy it young, within two to four years; a few cask aged wines keep longer."},"food":{"with":[{"dish":"Quiche Lorraine or onion tart","why":"soft fruit and gentle acidity suit eggs and cream"},{"dish":"White asparagus with hollandaise","why":"a mild wine that does not fight the asparagus"},{"dish":"Pan fried redfish or trout","why":"gentle fruit and no new oak beside delicate fish"},{"dish":"Roast chicken or veal","why":"a rounded body matches white meat"}]},"labels":[{"term":"Weissburgunder","means":"The German and Austrian name, white Burgundian; dry as a rule."},{"term":"Pinot Bianco","means":"The Italian name, met above all from Alto Adige and Friuli."},{"term":"Klevner","means":"An old Alsace name for Pinot Blanc, still seen on some labels."},{"term":"Crémant d'Alsace","means":"Alsace's bottle fermented sparkling wine; Pinot Blanc is the main grape of the white."}],"guest":[{"q":"Is Pinot Blanc like Pinot Grigio?","a":"They are kin in the Pinot family. Pinot Blanc is softer and rounder, with ripe apple and almond; Italian Pinot Grigio is usually lighter, crisper and more neutral."},{"q":"I like Chardonnay but not the oak. Would this suit me?","a":"It may well. Pinot Blanc has a similar rounded feel and apple fruit, but it is milder and is seldom put in new oak."},{"q":"What is Weissburgunder?","a":"It is the German and Austrian name for Pinot Blanc: the same grape, usually made dry, with apple, pear and a hint of almond."}],"compare":[{"slug":"chardonnay","tell":"Chardonnay has more body and a firmer line, and often cream or toast from the cellar; Pinot Blanc is milder and softer, with apple and almond."},{"slug":"pinot-grigio","tell":"Pinot Gris from Alsace is richer and oilier, with spiced pear and a faint bitterness; Pinot Blanc is lighter, plainer and fresher."},{"slug":"aligote","tell":"Aligoté is sharper and more lemony, with higher acidity; Pinot Blanc is softer and rounder, with ripe apple and almond."}],"profile":{"c":"w","st":"Pale lemon · Body medium · Acid medium · Alcohol moderate · Dry; also the base of Crémant d'Alsace","fruit":"Ripe apple, pear and quince, white peach; lemon and lemon zest from high, cool sites","other":"Almond, hazelnut and green walnut, apple blossom, acacia blossom; bread dough with age or in sparkling wine; seldom any new oak","tell":"Soft, round and mild: apple, pear and almond on gentle acidity with no loud perfume, a quieter, lighter wine than Chardonnay","regions":"Alsace (often with Auxerrois, and in Crémant d'Alsace), Germany and Austria (Weissburgunder), Alto Adige and Friuli (Pinot Bianco), Willamette Valley","confuse":"Chardonnay has more body and often cream or toast; Pinot Gris is richer and oilier, with spiced pear; Aligoté is sharper and more lemony"},"cards":[{"id":"gt-z000vkna","front":"Pinot Blanc: its German and Italian names, and its family?","back":"Weissburgunder in Germany and Austria, Pinot Bianco in Italy. It is a white berried mutation within the Pinot family, kin to Pinot Noir and Pinot Gris."},{"id":"gt-5vxp5c5y","front":"What part does Pinot Blanc play in Alsace?","back":"It makes a soft, dry white, often blended with Auxerrois and sold as Pinot Blanc, and it is the main grape of white Crémant d'Alsace."},{"id":"gt-t8lsr6x5","front":"Pinot Blanc in the glass: what gives it away?","back":"Ripe apple, pear and almond, a soft, rounded feel and gentle acidity, with no loud perfume and seldom any new oak: a quieter, lighter wine than Chardonnay."}],"questions":[{"id":"gt-eqf6p8m5","q":"A German list offers a dry white called Weissburgunder from Baden. A guest asks which grape that is. What do you answer?","opts":["Silvaner","Chardonnay","Pinot Blanc","Pinot Gris"],"a":2,"exp":"Weissburgunder, white Burgundian, is the German name for Pinot Blanc. Pinot Gris is Grauburgunder in German, and Chardonnay and Silvaner are sold under their own names."},{"id":"gt-31qsmp7y","q":"A blind white is pale and dry, with ripe apple, pear and a hint of almond, a soft, rounded feel, gentle acidity and no trace of a barrel. Which grape fits it?","opts":["Pinot Blanc","Sauvignon Blanc","Riesling","Gewürztraminer"],"a":0,"exp":"Soft apple, pear and almond on gentle acidity with no loud perfume points to Pinot Blanc. Sauvignon Blanc and Riesling have far sharper acidity, and Gewürztraminer smells of lychee and rose petal."},{"id":"gt-8hb1gj9k","q":"A guest enjoys her glass of white Crémant d'Alsace and asks which grape leads most of it. Which do you name?","opts":["Muscat","Pinot Blanc","Gewürztraminer","Riesling"],"a":1,"exp":"Pinot Blanc is the main grape of white Crémant d'Alsace, chosen for its freshness and soft fruit. Riesling, Gewürztraminer and Muscat are Alsace's aromatic grapes, met mostly as still wines under their own names."}],"sources":[{"title":"Vins d'Alsace: Pinot Blanc d'Alsace","url":"https://www.vinsalsace.com/en/tastes-and-colours/grapes-varieties/vins-da/pinot-blanc-dalsace/"},{"title":"Wines of Germany: Pinot Blanc","url":"https://www.winesofgermany.com/our-wine/grape-varieties/grape-variety/110/pinot-blanc"},{"title":"Alto Adige Wines: Pinot Blanc from Alto Adige","url":"https://www.altoadigewines.com/en/wine-varieties/pinot-blanc"},{"title":"University of California, Davis, Foundation Plant Services: The Pinots","url":"https://fps.ucdavis.edu/grapebook/winebook.cfm?chap=PinotNoir"}]},
"palomino": {"slug":"palomino","name":"Palomino","colour":"white","kind":"grape","status":"thin","mode":"home","aliases":[],"notIn":[],"say":"pah-loh-MEE-noh","lines":{"s10":"Palomino is the plain white grape that becomes dry Sherry, from pale, salty Fino to dark, walnut scented Oloroso.","s20":"Palomino is the white grape of dry Sherry, grown on chalk around Jerez in southern Spain. It is mild by itself, and the cellar turns it into Fino, pale and salty under a film of yeast, or Oloroso, dark and nutty from years in air.","s45":"Palomino says very little on its own, and that is why Sherry is made from it. It grows on white chalk around Jerez, in the far south of Spain. The young wine is mild: green apple, a little almond, soft acidity. Then the cellar takes over. Aged under a film of yeast called flor it becomes Fino, pale and bone dry with a salty tang. Aged in air it becomes Oloroso, dark and full of walnut. Pour a cold Fino with olives, almonds or fried fish. Nearly every bottle is a blend of many years, drawn from a stack of casks called a solera.","sale":"Palomino becomes Fino, bone dry and salty, with almond and fresh bread, and a cold glass of it makes you hungry. I would pour it before dinner with olives and almonds, and bring an Amontillado when the turtle soup arrives."},"glass":{"lead":"As Fino, almond, bread dough and green apple with a salty tang, bone dry and light; as Oloroso, walnut, tobacco leaf and old leather.","notes":[{"family":"orchard fruit","notes":["green apple","bruised apple"]},{"family":"nutty","notes":["almond","hazelnut"]},{"family":"lees and cellar","notes":["bread dough","fresh yeast"]},{"family":"mineral","notes":["sea brine","crushed chalk"]},{"family":"age","notes":["walnut","tobacco leaf","old leather"]}],"ladder":[{"climate":"moderate","where":"Tenerife's high volcanic slopes, as Listán Blanco","notes":["lemon peel","struck flint","wood smoke"]},{"climate":"warm","where":"The chalk hills of Jerez and Sanlúcar","notes":["green apple","almond","sea brine"]}],"structure":"Dry. Acidity low. As Fino, light bodied, with alcohol a little above a table wine's; as Oloroso, full bodied and high in alcohol. Traditionally fortified."},"worlds":{"home":{"home":"Jerez, in Andalusia, on albariza, a white chalk that holds the winter rain through a rainless summer. The grape is picked early and fermented dry, then traditionally fortified. Under flor it becomes Fino, or Manzanilla at Sanlúcar; in air, Oloroso; Amontillado has known both. Most are blended down through the casks of a solera.","abroad":"On the Canary Islands it is Listán Blanco, and on Tenerife's volcanic slopes it gives dry, smoky table wines. Inland Spain long grew it for volume, from Rueda to Galicia."}},"service":{"temp":"43 to 46 degrees Fahrenheit, 6 to 8 degrees Celsius, for Fino and Manzanilla; Amontillado and Oloroso cool, not cold.","glass":"A copita or a small tulip; a white wine glass shows a Fino well.","decant":"Usually unnecessary. Keep an open Fino in the fridge and finish it within days.","age":"Bottled ready to drink. Pour Fino and Manzanilla young and fresh; Amontillado and Oloroso keep for years."},"food":{"with":[{"dish":"Olives, salted almonds and cured ham","why":"a cold Fino echoes the salt and the almond"},{"dish":"Fried fish and shellfish","why":"Fino or Manzanilla, dry and salty, cuts the frying"},{"dish":"Turtle soup","why":"a dry Amontillado suits a rich soup or consommé"},{"dish":"Mushrooms or roast chicken","why":"the hazelnut of an Amontillado meets the roast"},{"dish":"Braised oxtail or game","why":"Oloroso has the weight and the walnut depth"}]},"labels":[{"term":"Fino","means":"Aged under flor, the veil of yeast: pale and bone dry, with almond and bread dough."},{"term":"Manzanilla","means":"A Fino style aged only in the sea air of Sanlúcar de Barrameda: lighter and saltier."},{"term":"Amontillado","means":"Begun under flor and finished in air: amber and dry, with hazelnut."},{"term":"Oloroso","means":"Aged in air from the start: dark, dry and full, with walnut and leather."},{"term":"Listán Blanco","means":"The grape's name on the Canary Islands, where it makes dry table wines."}],"guest":[{"q":"Is Sherry always sweet?","a":"No. Most Sherry made from Palomino is bone dry: Fino, Manzanilla, Amontillado and Oloroso. The sweet ones are sweetened blends such as Cream, or wines of sun dried Pedro Ximénez."},{"q":"What is flor?","a":"A film of yeast that grows on the surface of the wine in the cask. It keeps the air off, so a Fino stays pale and fresh, and it gives the taste of almond and bread dough."},{"q":"How long will an open bottle keep?","a":"A Fino or Manzanilla keeps a few days in the fridge, so treat it like a white wine. An Amontillado or Oloroso has already lived with air and keeps for weeks."}],"compare":[{"slug":"pedro-ximenez","tell":"Pedro Ximénez is the sweet one, black and syrupy from sun dried grapes, all raisin and fig; Palomino makes the dry Sherries, from pale Fino to dark Oloroso."},{"slug":"savagnin","tell":"Savagnin, as vin jaune, also ages under a veil of yeast, but it is unfortified and sharply acid, with walnut and curry; Palomino, as Fino, is lighter, softer and saltier."}],"profile":{"c":"w","st":"Pale straw as Fino to mahogany as Oloroso · Body light to full · Acid low · Alcohol raised, traditionally by fortification · Bone dry","fruit":"Green apple when young, bruised apple under flor; little fruit of its own, which is what the cellar wants","other":"Almond, bread dough and sea brine under flor; hazelnut and tobacco leaf in Amontillado; walnut and old leather in Oloroso","tell":"A plain, low acid base that the cellar defines: almond and brine under flor, walnut in air, and the warmth of a wine traditionally fortified","regions":"Jerez, Sanlúcar de Barrameda and El Puerto de Santa María; the Canary Islands as Listán Blanco; Rueda and Galicia in small part","confuse":"Savagnin as vin jaune is unfortified and far higher in acidity, with walnut and curry; Pedro Ximénez is black, sweet and raisined"},"cards":[{"id":"gt-ri23jf84","front":"Palomino: where is home, and what is the white soil called?","back":"Jerez, in Andalusia, with Sanlúcar de Barrameda and El Puerto de Santa María. The soil is albariza, a white chalky marl that holds winter rain through the dry summer."},{"id":"gt-5woqgodk","front":"What does Palomino become under flor, and what in air?","back":"Under flor, the veil of yeast, it becomes Fino, or Manzanilla at Sanlúcar: pale and bone dry. Aged in air it becomes Oloroso: dark and dry, with walnut. Amontillado has had both."},{"id":"gt-l4i0iljj","front":"What is Palomino called on the Canary Islands, and what does it make?","back":"Listán Blanco. On volcanic soils, above all on Tenerife, it makes dry, unfortified whites with a smoky, flinty edge."}],"questions":[{"id":"gt-iq30hzo8","q":"A guest wants a bone dry Sherry, served cold, with olives and fried fish before dinner. Which style do you pour?","opts":["Fino","Cream","Oloroso","Pedro Ximénez"],"a":0,"exp":"Fino is Palomino aged under flor: pale, bone dry and salty, served cold as an aperitif. Oloroso is dry too, but dark and full and served cool, and Cream is sweetened."},{"id":"gt-dx988sug","q":"A blind wine is pale straw, bone dry and light, with almond, bread dough, green apple and a salty tang, low acidity and a warmth of alcohol above a table wine's. Which is it?","opts":["Chablis","Vin jaune from the Jura","Muscadet sur lie","Fino Sherry"],"a":3,"exp":"Almond, bread dough and brine with low acidity and added strength mark a Fino. Vin jaune also ages under a veil of yeast, but it is unfortified and sharply acid, with walnut and curry."},{"id":"gt-fwq327yv","q":"A guest asks why her Fino is pale and fresh while the Oloroso beside it is brown, though both are Palomino. What made the difference?","opts":["Fino is from white grapes, Oloroso from black","Fino aged under flor, Oloroso in contact with air","Oloroso had caramel added for colour","Fino is bottled within a year of the harvest"],"a":1,"exp":"Flor, a veil of yeast, shields a Fino from oxygen, so it stays pale. Oloroso is traditionally fortified higher, which keeps flor away, and years in air turn it brown, with walnut."}],"sources":[{"title":"Sherry Wines, the regulatory council: Palomino, the defining grape of Sherry","url":"https://www.sherry.wine/news/palomino-the-defining-grape-of-sherry"},{"title":"Sherry Wines, the regulatory council: viticulture","url":"https://www.sherry.wine/sherry-wine/production/viticulture"},{"title":"Sherry Wines, the regulatory council: Fino","url":"https://www.sherry.wine/sherry-wine/dry-sherry-wines/fino"},{"title":"Sherry Wines, the regulatory council: a guide to matching food with Sherry","url":"https://www.sherry.wine/documents/127/sherry_wines_eating_in_good_company_pairing_guide.pdf"}]},
"cinsault": {"slug":"cinsault","name":"Cinsault","colour":"red","kind":"grape","status":"thin","mode":"contrast","aliases":[],"notIn":[],"say":"san-SOH","lines":{"s10":"Cinsault is the pale, perfumed red of the Mediterranean: wild strawberry, raspberry and rose petal, soft enough to serve cool.","s20":"Cinsault is a grape of the hot south of France, at ease in heat and drought. It gives pale, soft wines of strawberry and raspberry, and it is the quiet partner in many Provence rosés and southern French reds.","s45":"Cinsault has spent most of its life in other grapes' company, softening the red blends and filling the rosés of southern France. On its own it is pale and perfumed, with wild strawberry, raspberry and rose petal, light in body and gentle in tannin. It is rarely given new oak. France mostly blends it or presses it for rosé, while South Africa and Chile now bottle old vines alone as light, fragrant reds. Pour it cool with charcuterie, grilled tuna or a tomato salad. Chile planted it in its southern hills after the earthquake of 1939, and those vines, now old, are bottled alone at last.","sale":"Cinsault is a grape behind the pale Provence rosé you already know, and as a red it keeps that same strawberry freshness. Give it a light chill and pour it with grilled tuna or the charcuterie."},"glass":{"lead":"Wild strawberry, raspberry and red cherry with rose petal and a hint of thyme, pale, light and soft on the tongue.","notes":[{"family":"red fruit","notes":["wild strawberry","raspberry","red cherry","redcurrant"]},{"family":"floral","notes":["rose petal","violet"]},{"family":"herbal","notes":["thyme","black tea"]}],"ladder":[{"climate":"moderate","where":"The granite hills of Itata, near the coast of southern Chile","notes":["raspberry","redcurrant","rose petal"]},{"climate":"warm","where":"The Languedoc, Provence, the southern Rhône and the Bekaa","notes":["ripe strawberry","red cherry","thyme"]}],"structure":"Dry. Acidity medium, tannin low and soft, alcohol medium, body light to medium; pale in colour."},"worlds":{"contrast":{"old":{"where":"The Languedoc, Provence and the southern Rhône; Lebanon's Bekaa Valley","glass":"Usually met in a blend or as rosé: pale pink, with strawberry, red cherry and a dry, fresh finish. As a red it tends to be soft and light, with a scent of thyme.","cellar":"Mostly blended with Grenache, Syrah, Mourvèdre or Carignan, or pressed early for rosé; fermented in tank, with little or no new oak."},"new":{"where":"South Africa's Swartland and Stellenbosch; Itata in southern Chile","glass":"Typically bottled alone as a pale, fragrant red: raspberry, wild strawberry and rose petal, bright and light, with modest alcohol and fine, gentle tannin. Old vines tend to add depth without weight.","cellar":"Old, unirrigated bush vines, often picked early; whole bunches fermented in concrete, old casks or clay jars, with no new oak, and bottled young."},"why":"In France it was planted to soften blends and make rosé, and the label names a place. South Africa and Chile inherited old, dry farmed vines, which growers now bottle alone under the grape's name.","blur":"The Languedoc now bottles old vine Cinsault alone too, light and chilled, and South Africa still blends much of it into fuller reds with Syrah and Grenache.","call":"A dry, pale rosé or a thyme scented blend leans France; a pale, perfumed red bottled alone leans the Cape or Chile."}},"service":{"temp":"54 to 59 degrees Fahrenheit, 12 to 15 degrees Celsius, as a red; colder still as a rosé.","glass":"A Burgundy bowl for the perfume of a red bottled alone; a white wine glass for rosé.","decant":"Usually unnecessary for a light, aromatic red.","age":"Young, within three or four years; a few old vine reds reward five or more."},"food":{"with":[{"dish":"Charcuterie and cured ham","why":"soft tannin and fresh fruit against salt and fat"},{"dish":"Grilled tuna or salmon","why":"a light red with little tannin to fight the fish"},{"dish":"Ratatouille or stuffed tomatoes","why":"soft fruit suits ripe tomato and olive oil"},{"dish":"Mezze with grilled aubergine and lamb","why":"the table of the Bekaa, perfume meeting smoke"},{"dish":"Shrimp remoulade, with a rosé","why":"a dry, cold rosé cools the mustard and cayenne"}]},"labels":[{"term":"Cinsaut","means":"The same grape spelt without the L, as French officials and many Cape labels write it."},{"term":"Côtes de Provence","means":"The appellation of most Provence rosé, pale and dry, in which Cinsault often joins Grenache."},{"term":"Hermitage","means":"The Cape's old name for Cinsault, no kin to the Rhône hill; it gave Pinotage its ending."},{"term":"Secano Interior","means":"A Chilean label term kept for dry farmed País and Cinsault from the southern coastal hills."}],"guest":[{"q":"I only know Cinsault from rosé. What is it like as a red?","a":"Much the same in spirit: pale, light and fragrant, with wild strawberry and very little tannin. Drink it as you would a rosé, lightly chilled."},{"q":"Why is this red so pale? Is something wrong with it?","a":"Nothing at all. Cinsault has large, juicy berries, so there is more juice to each skin and less colour and tannin to draw out. The pale colour is the grape."},{"q":"The label says old vines. Does that matter?","a":"With this grape it does. Young, heavily cropped Cinsault can be thin. Old vines grown without irrigation give far fewer grapes, and the wine has more scent and depth for it."}],"compare":[{"slug":"grenache","tell":"Grenache is fuller and warmer in alcohol, with kirsch, raspberry jam and white pepper; Cinsault is paler still, lighter and fresher, with less alcohol and more perfume."},{"slug":"pinot-noir","tell":"Pinot Noir has brighter acidity, a longer finish and forest floor beneath its cherry; Cinsault is softer and simpler, with strawberry and a warmer, thyme scented feel."},{"slug":"gamay","tell":"Gamay is tarter, with high acidity and often banana from whole bunch fermentation; Cinsault is softer, lower in acidity and warmer in feel."}],"profile":{"c":"r","st":"Pale ruby · Body light to medium · Acid medium · Tannin low and soft · Alcohol medium","fruit":"Wild strawberry, raspberry, red cherry, redcurrant; riper strawberry in the heat of the Midi","other":"Rose petal, violet, thyme and black tea; little or no new oak; often blended, or pressed early for pale, dry rosé","tell":"Pale and perfumed, strawberry and rose petal on soft tannin and modest acidity: light as Pinot Noir, but warmer and simpler","regions":"The Languedoc, Provence and the southern Rhône; Lebanon's Bekaa; South Africa's Swartland and Stellenbosch; Itata in Chile; Puglia as Ottavianello","confuse":"Grenache is fuller and higher in alcohol; Pinot Noir has brighter acidity and forest floor; Gamay is tarter, often with banana"},"cards":[{"id":"gt-fui0tbtd","front":"Cinsault: what was it called at the Cape, and what was bred from it?","back":"Hermitage. In 1925 it was crossed with Pinot Noir at Stellenbosch to give Pinotage, whose name joins the two."},{"id":"gt-t6kdwnp6","front":"Cinsault on a list: Old World or New World, what tips it?","back":"A dry, pale rosé or a thyme scented blend leans France. A pale, perfumed red bottled alone from old bush vines leans South Africa or Itata in Chile."},{"id":"gt-hmjrwfxj","front":"Cinsault: when did Chile plant it, and where?","back":"Just after the Chillán earthquake of 1939, in the dry farmed southern hills around Itata. The label term Secano Interior is kept for it and for País."}],"questions":[{"id":"gt-ebo6kyb1","q":"Two pale wines of Cinsault are poured. One is a dry rosé blended with Grenache; the other is a perfumed, lightly chilled red from old bush vines, labelled with the grape alone. Which pair of origins is the more typical, in that order?","opts":["Itata, then the southern Rhône","Stellenbosch, then the Languedoc","Provence, then the Swartland","The Swartland, then Provence"],"a":2,"exp":"A Grenache and Cinsault rosé is the classic of Provence, and old bush vine Cinsault bottled alone belongs to the Swartland's revival. The reversed pair, the nearest wrong answer, swaps the two traditions.","tag":"worlds"},{"id":"gt-lk7pxlm6","q":"A guest sees Hermitage on an old South African label and expects the grape of the Rhône hill. Which grape did the Cape mean by that name?","opts":["Cinsault","Syrah","Pinotage","Grenache"],"a":0,"exp":"At the Cape, Hermitage was the old name for Cinsault, which is why its crossing with Pinot Noir was named Pinotage. Syrah, the nearest wrong answer, is the grape of the Rhône hill."},{"id":"gt-h9sz9g2y","q":"A guest asks for a red to drink cool with grilled tuna, with as little tannin as the list allows. The list offers four grapes of the south of France. Which do you pour?","opts":["Syrah","Mourvèdre","Carignan","Cinsault"],"a":3,"exp":"Cinsault's large, juicy berries give a pale, soft red with little tannin, which takes a chill and suits fish. Carignan, Syrah and Mourvèdre are darker and firmer, and turn hard when cold."}],"sources":[{"title":"Wines of South Africa: red wine varieties","url":"https://www.wosa.co.za/The-Industry/Varieties-and-Styles/Red-Wine-Varieties/"},{"title":"Decanter: reassessing Cinsault","url":"https://www.decanter.com/wine/cinsault/cin-city-reassessing-cinsault-the-unsung-hero-finally-having-its-moment/"},{"title":"Wine and Spirits Magazine: true south, the Secano Interior of Chile","url":"https://www.wineandspiritsmagazine.com/?p=1630"},{"title":"wein.plus glossary: Ottavianello","url":"https://glossary.wein.plus/ottavianello"}]},
"petit-verdot": {"slug":"petit-verdot","name":"Petit Verdot","colour":"red","kind":"grape","status":"thin","mode":"home","aliases":[],"notIn":[],"say":"puh-TEE vair-DOH","lines":{"s10":"Petit Verdot adds colour and grip to red Bordeaux in small doses, and alone it is inky, with blackberry and violet.","s20":"Petit Verdot is a late ripening Bordeaux grape, used a few per cent at a time to deepen the colour and firm the finish of a blend. Bottled alone, from somewhere warmer, it is inky and full, with blackberry, violet and plenty of tannin.","s45":"Petit Verdot means the little green one, because in Bordeaux it ripens so late that some years it never quite does. So the Médoc uses it as seasoning: a few per cent of the blend, for colour, perfume and grip. Tasted alone it is blackberry, black plum and violet, with a pinch of liquorice. It is inky and full, with firm tannin and bright acidity. Warmer places ripen it more surely, and Virginia, Spain and Australia bottle it alone, aged in oak. It takes on braised short rib, lamb shank or a hard, aged cheese. In America it may go into a Meritage, a blend whose name rhymes with heritage.","sale":"Petit Verdot is for the guest who finds Cabernet too polite: inky, violet scented and gripping. I would decant it and pour it with the short rib, or with anything slow cooked and rich."},"glass":{"lead":"Blackberry, black plum and blueberry with violet and liquorice, inky and full, on firm tannin and bright acidity.","notes":[{"family":"black fruit","notes":["blackberry","black plum","black cherry"]},{"family":"blue fruit","notes":["blueberry","mulberry"]},{"family":"floral","notes":["violet","lavender"]},{"family":"spice","notes":["liquorice","cracked black pepper"]},{"family":"oak","notes":["cocoa","coffee","vanilla pod"]}],"ladder":[{"climate":"moderate","where":"The Médoc and Graves, and Margaret River","notes":["tart blackberry","violet","cracked black pepper"]},{"climate":"warm","where":"Virginia, Australia's Riverland, Castilla-La Mancha and Jumilla, California","notes":["ripe black plum","blackberry jam","blueberry","liquorice"]}],"structure":"Dry. Acidity medium plus, tannin high and firm, alcohol medium plus to high, body full."},"worlds":{"home":{"home":"Bordeaux, above all the gravel of the Médoc, where it is among the last grapes picked and goes into the blend a few per cent at a time, beside Cabernet Sauvignon and Merlot, for colour, violet perfume and tannin. In a cool year it may not ripen, so a guest seldom meets it alone there.","abroad":"Warmer, sunnier places ripen it more surely and bottle it alone: Virginia, California, Australia's Riverland and central Spain. There it is inky and full, with ripe blackberry and blueberry, violet and firm tannin, usually aged in oak."}},"service":{"temp":"61 to 64 degrees Fahrenheit, 16 to 18 degrees Celsius: cool room temperature.","glass":"A tall Bordeaux glass, with room to swirl.","decant":"Consider it for a young wine made from the grape alone, after tasting a sample.","age":"Firm when young; a well made wine of the grape alone softens over five to ten years."},"food":{"with":[{"dish":"Braised short rib","why":"richness that takes the tannin and the weight"},{"dish":"Lamb shank","why":"slow cooked meat softens a firm, dark wine"},{"dish":"Venison or other game","why":"blackberry and liquorice suit strong meat"},{"dish":"Aged hard cheese","why":"salt and fat round off the grip"}]},"labels":[{"term":"Bordeaux blend","means":"Cabernet Sauvignon and Merlot with Cabernet Franc and often a little Petit Verdot, wherever it is made."},{"term":"Meritage","means":"An American name for blends of the Bordeaux grapes, Petit Verdot among them; it rhymes with heritage."},{"term":"Petit Verdot, alone","means":"The grape named on the front label, as Virginia, Australia and Spain bottle it: dark, full and firm."}],"guest":[{"q":"I have only seen Petit Verdot in small print on a Bordeaux label. What does it do?","a":"It is the seasoning. A few per cent deepens the colour, adds a scent of violet and firms the finish, much as a pinch of pepper lifts a dish."},{"q":"Why is this bottle all Petit Verdot?","a":"It comes from somewhere warmer than Bordeaux, where the grape ripens fully. On its own it is dark, full and firm, so it suits rich, slow cooked meat."},{"q":"Is it the same as Petite Sirah?","a":"No, only the names are alike. Petite Sirah is a different grape, at home in California. Petit Verdot is from Bordeaux, with a perfume of violet and a tight, fresh grip."}],"compare":[{"slug":"cabernet-sauvignon","tell":"Cabernet Sauvignon shows cassis, cedar and a leafy edge; Petit Verdot is darker and more scented, with violet, blueberry and a harder, fresher grip."},{"slug":"malbec","tell":"Malbec shares the purple colour and the violet but is plusher and softer; Petit Verdot is firmer, with higher acidity and more tannin."},{"slug":"syrah","tell":"Syrah brings smoked meat and black olive; Petit Verdot shares its dark colour, violet and pepper but has no meaty note, and its tannin is firmer."}],"profile":{"c":"r","st":"Opaque purple to black · Body full · Acid medium plus · Tannin high, firm · Alcohol medium plus to high","fruit":"Blackberry, black plum, black cherry and blueberry; riper and jammier from warm sites","other":"Violet and lavender, liquorice and cracked black pepper, cocoa and coffee from oak, leather with age; inky colour and a firm, drying finish","tell":"Inky purple, violet perfume and blackberry on high tannin with bright acidity: darker and more scented than Cabernet Sauvignon, firmer than Malbec","regions":"Bordeaux's Médoc and Graves, in small shares of the blend; bottled alone in Virginia, California, Australia's Riverland and central Spain","confuse":"Cabernet Sauvignon has cassis, cedar and a leafy edge; Malbec is plusher and softer; Syrah adds smoked meat and black olive; Petite Sirah is another grape, of California"},"cards":[{"id":"gt-i4uzy7pa","front":"Petit Verdot: what does its name mean, and why?","back":"The little green one. In Bordeaux it ripens so late that in a cool year the berries may stay green, one reason it is only a small share of the blend."},{"id":"gt-fwc0juoa","front":"What does a few per cent of Petit Verdot add to a Bordeaux blend?","back":"Deep colour, a scent of violet, a note of liquorice, and firm tannin with fresh acidity: seasoning for Cabernet Sauvignon and Merlot."},{"id":"gt-oy91fghz","front":"Where is Petit Verdot bottled alone, and why there?","back":"Virginia, California, Australia's Riverland and central Spain: places warm and sunny enough to ripen a grape that Bordeaux often cannot."}],"questions":[{"id":"gt-lg4ns1p2","q":"A blind red is opaque purple black. It shows blackberry, blueberry and violet with liquorice, and a firm, drying grip with bright acidity; there is no cassis, no cedar and no smoked meat. Which grape is it most likely to be?","opts":["Cabernet Sauvignon","Merlot","Syrah","Petit Verdot"],"a":3,"exp":"Inky colour, violet and blackberry on high tannin with bright acidity point to Petit Verdot. Cabernet Sauvignon would show cassis and cedar, Syrah smoked meat, and Merlot is softer."},{"id":"gt-bcmqc1by","q":"A guest reads 3 per cent Petit Verdot on the back of a bottle of Margaux and asks why so little is used. What do you tell him?","opts":["It is added only to sweeten the wine","It ripens late and is strong, so a little is enough","It is too pale to be used in a larger share","It is a white grape, added only for its scent"],"a":1,"exp":"Petit Verdot ripens late in Bordeaux and is deeply coloured and tannic, so a few per cent seasons the blend. It is a black grape, dark and dry, and adds neither paleness nor sweetness."},{"id":"gt-5h92v8mx","q":"A guest wants a bottle made from Petit Verdot alone. Under which heading of the list are you likeliest to find one?","opts":["Pauillac","Saint-Émilion","Virginia","Chinon"],"a":2,"exp":"Virginia is warm enough to ripen Petit Verdot and bottles it alone. In Pauillac it is a small share of a Cabernet blend, and Saint-Émilion and Chinon are made from Merlot and Cabernet Franc."}],"sources":[{"title":"Bordeaux Wines: Petit Verdot, a small grape with serious character","url":"https://www.bordeaux.com/en/grape-varieties/petit-verdot/"},{"title":"Médoc wine council: the grape varieties","url":"https://www.medoc-bordeaux.com/en/appellations-en/grape-varieties/"},{"title":"Virginia Wine: Petit Verdot","url":"https://www.virginiawine.org/varietals/petit-verdot"},{"title":"Wine Australia: Riverland, a jewel in Australia's wine crown","url":"https://www.wineaustralia.com/whats-happening/stories-of-australian-wine/june-2016/riverland"}]},
"nero-d-avola": {"slug":"nero-d-avola","name":"Nero d’Avola","colour":"red","kind":"grape","status":"thin","mode":"home","aliases":[],"notIn":[],"say":"NEH-roh DAH-voh-lah","lines":{"s10":"Nero d'Avola is Sicily's deep, generous red, with black cherry and liquorice on a soft, round tannin.","s20":"Nero d'Avola is named for the town of Avola, near Syracuse in Sicily. It pours deep and ripe, with black cherry, mulberry and liquorice, a full body and gentle tannin that suit grilled meat and tomato.","s45":"In Sicily, Nero d'Avola is the red on every table, grown across the island and most at home in the south east. It is deep in colour, with black cherry, mulberry and a touch of liquorice. The body is full and the tannin soft and round. Much of it is bottled young for that fruit, while wines from Pachino and Noto, on limestone by the sea, are firmer and keep longer. Set it beside pasta with aubergine and tomato, grilled sausage or lamb chops. Growers in Australia's warm, dry regions have taken it up for the way it copes with the heat.","sale":"Nero d'Avola is deep and generous like a Shiraz, only softer and rounder, with ripe black cherry and liquorice. I would bring it when the table shares grilled sausage and a pasta with aubergine."},"glass":{"lead":"Black cherry, black plum and mulberry with liquorice, deep ruby and full, on a soft, round tannin.","notes":[{"family":"black fruit","notes":["black cherry","black plum","mulberry"]},{"family":"dried fruit","notes":["prune","dried fig"]},{"family":"spice","notes":["liquorice","cinnamon"]},{"family":"earth","notes":["tobacco leaf","leather"]},{"family":"oak","notes":["dark chocolate","vanilla pod"]}],"ladder":[{"climate":"moderate","where":"The high inland hills of Contea di Sclafani","notes":["black cherry","red cherry","liquorice"]},{"climate":"warm","where":"Vittoria, Noto and Pachino, the west of Sicily, the Riverland","notes":["black plum","mulberry","prune","dried fig"]}],"structure":"Dry. Acidity medium, tannin medium and round, alcohol medium plus to high, body medium plus to full; deep ruby to purple."},"worlds":{"home":{"home":"Sicily, all over the island, with the south east around Vittoria, Noto and Pachino at its heart. Since 2017 a bottle that names the grape must be a DOC, most often Sicilia DOC. With Frappato it makes Cerasuolo di Vittoria, the island's one DOCG.","abroad":"Australia has taken it up for the way it copes with heat and drought, above all in McLaren Vale and the Riverland, and makes it as a bright, fruit led red."}},"service":{"temp":"59 to 63 degrees Fahrenheit, 15 to 17 degrees Celsius; Cerasuolo di Vittoria a shade cooler.","glass":"A Bordeaux glass for a full Nero d'Avola; a Burgundy bowl for perfumed Cerasuolo di Vittoria.","decant":"Usually unnecessary for a young, soft bottle; an older or oaked one may open with a short decant.","age":"Most within two to five years; a structured bottle from Pachino or Noto can keep longer."},"food":{"with":[{"dish":"Pasta alla Norma, with aubergine and tomato","why":"ripe black cherry meets sweet tomato and aubergine"},{"dish":"Grilled sausage with fennel","why":"soft tannin and black cherry for fat and fennel"},{"dish":"Grilled lamb chops","why":"ripe fruit and a round grip for charred meat"},{"dish":"Seared tuna, with Cerasuolo di Vittoria","why":"a lighter red with soft tannin for meaty fish"}]},"labels":[{"term":"Sicilia DOC","means":"The island wide DOC, and since the 2017 vintage the usual home of a bottle that names Nero d'Avola."},{"term":"Cerasuolo di Vittoria","means":"Sicily's one DOCG: Nero d'Avola, 50 to 70 per cent, with Frappato. A red, not a rosé."},{"term":"Calabrese","means":"An older Sicilian name for Nero d'Avola, still met on some labels."},{"term":"Noto, Eloro","means":"DOCs of the south eastern tip near Syracuse, where Nero d'Avola grows on limestone by the sea."},{"term":"Terre Siciliane","means":"The island's IGT; since 2017 it may not print Nero d'Avola on the label."}],"guest":[{"q":"Is Avola a place?","a":"A small town on the coast south of Syracuse, in the south east of Sicily, that gave the grape its name. The wine comes from all over the island."},{"q":"Is it like a Shiraz?","a":"A cousin in spirit: deep, ripe and generous. Nero d'Avola is softer and rounder, with gentler tannin, and without Shiraz's pepper and smoked meat."},{"q":"Will a red work with the tuna?","a":"A light one will. Cerasuolo di Vittoria, Nero d'Avola with Frappato, brings strawberry, perfume and soft tannin that sit well with a meaty fish."}],"compare":[{"slug":"syrah","tell":"Syrah carries cracked black pepper, black olive and smoked meat over firmer tannin; Nero d'Avola has none of the meat or olive, and a softer, rounder grip."},{"slug":"nerello-mascalese","tell":"Nerello Mascalese, from Etna, is pale and high in acidity, with sour cherry and volcanic ash; Nero d'Avola is deep in colour, softer and rounder, with mulberry and liquorice."},{"slug":"aglianico","tell":"Aglianico grips far harder, with high acidity, tar and smoke; Nero d'Avola is softer and rounder, with mulberry and liquorice."}],"profile":{"c":"r","st":"Deep ruby to purple · Body medium plus to full · Acid medium · Tannin medium, soft and round · Alcohol medium plus to high · Dry","fruit":"Black cherry, black plum, mulberry; prune and dried fig from hot sites","other":"Liquorice, tobacco leaf and leather; dark chocolate and vanilla from new oak; a soft, round texture","tell":"Deep colour and ripe black cherry, plum and mulberry with liquorice, on soft, round tannin and a full body","regions":"Sicily: Vittoria, Noto and Pachino in the south east, the west and the interior hills; McLaren Vale and the Riverland in Australia","confuse":"Syrah carries pepper, black olive and smoked meat; Nerello Mascalese is pale and high in acid; Aglianico grips far harder"},"cards":[{"id":"gt-k0z33v2f","front":"Nero d'Avola: where does its name come from, and what is its older name?","back":"Avola, a small town near Syracuse in the south east of Sicily. Its older Sicilian name is Calabrese."},{"id":"gt-faa1zpwv","front":"Cerasuolo di Vittoria: which grapes, in what shares, and what colour?","back":"Nero d'Avola, 50 to 70 per cent, with Frappato, 30 to 50: a red, not a rosé, and Sicily's one DOCG, since 2005."},{"id":"gt-kd5pwv3v","front":"Nero d'Avola on a label: what has the 2017 vintage changed?","back":"Terre Siciliane IGT may no longer print Nero d'Avola or Grillo. A bottle that names either must be a DOC, most often Sicilia DOC, or a local one such as Noto or Vittoria."}],"questions":[{"id":"gt-msosij80","q":"A red poured blind is deep ruby, with black cherry, mulberry and liquorice, soft, round tannin and a full body. The guest says it comes from a Mediterranean island. Which grape is it?","opts":["Grenache","Nero d'Avola","Nerello Mascalese","Sagrantino"],"a":1,"exp":"Deep colour and ripe black cherry and mulberry on soft, round tannin point to Nero d'Avola. Nerello Mascalese, the nearest miss and Sicilian too, is pale, with sour cherry and high acidity."},{"id":"gt-v25yfjp3","q":"A guest wants a Sicilian red that still holds Nero d'Avola but is lighter and more perfumed than a varietal bottle. Which wine do you bring?","opts":["Etna Rosato","Etna Rosso","Cerasuolo di Vittoria","Marsala Superiore Riserva"],"a":2,"exp":"Cerasuolo di Vittoria blends Nero d'Avola with 30 to 50 per cent Frappato, which brings strawberry, perfume and lift. Etna Rosso, the nearest miss, is lighter too but is Nerello Mascalese."},{"id":"gt-tc4moy4v","q":"A guest sees Calabrese on the back label of a Sicilian red and asks which grape that is. What do you answer?","opts":["Nero d'Avola","Frappato","Nerello Cappuccio","Gaglioppo"],"a":0,"exp":"Calabrese is an older Sicilian name for Nero d'Avola. Gaglioppo, the nearest miss, is the red grape of Calabria itself, the grape of Cirò."}],"sources":[{"title":"AIS: production rules of the DOC Sicilia","url":"https://aisitalia.it/Disciplinari/Sicilia/DOC/Sicilia.pdf"},{"title":"Wine Companion: Nero d'Avola","url":"https://winecompanion.com.au/resources/varietals/nero-davola"},{"title":"Decanter: Italian grape varieties in Australia","url":"https://www.decanter.com/wine-reviews-tastings/australian-wine-italian-grape-varieties-383478"},{"title":"The Oxford Companion to Wine","url":""}]},
"dolcetto": {"slug":"dolcetto","name":"Dolcetto","colour":"red","kind":"grape","status":"thin","mode":"home","aliases":[],"notIn":[],"say":"dohl-CHET-toh","lines":{"s10":"Dolcetto is the soft, dark red Piedmont drinks young: black plum and blackberry with a dry, bitter almond finish.","s20":"Dolcetto is the early ripening red of Piedmont, grown around Dogliani and Alba on the cooler slopes Nebbiolo will not take. It is dark and dry, with black plum, blackberry and a bitter almond finish, soft in acidity and drunk young.","s45":"Dolcetto ripens early and drinks early. It is a red of Piedmont, at home around Dogliani and Alba on the cooler slopes that Nebbiolo will not take. In the glass it is deep purple, with black plum and blackberry. It is soft in acidity, with a little grip and a dry, bitter almond finish. Most is kept in steel and bottled young, while the fruit is fresh. Have it slightly cool with salumi, agnolotti or a mushroom pizza. Little sweet one is what Dolcetto means, though the wine is dry: the name is thought to describe the grapes, low in acid and sweet off the vine.","sale":"Dolcetto is dark, soft and ready now, a red that asks for no patience and no decanter. With the agnolotti or a mushroom dish, a glass of Dogliani served slightly cool is my choice."},"glass":{"lead":"Black plum and blackberry in a deep purple glass, soft in acidity, with a little grip and a bitter almond finish.","notes":[{"family":"black fruit","notes":["black plum","blackberry","black cherry"]},{"family":"spice","notes":["liquorice","anise"]},{"family":"nutty","notes":["bitter almond","almond skin"]}],"ladder":[{"climate":"cool","where":"The higher, cooler Langhe slopes and Dogliani","notes":["black cherry","blackberry","bitter almond"]},{"climate":"moderate","where":"The lower Monferrato hills around Ovada and Acqui","notes":["black plum","ripe blackberry","liquorice"]}],"structure":"Dry. Acidity low to medium, tannin medium with a slightly bitter edge, alcohol medium, body medium; deep purple ruby in colour."},"worlds":{"home":{"home":"Piedmont, above all the higher, cooler slopes of the Langhe, where it ripens weeks before Nebbiolo. Dogliani and Diano d'Alba in the Langhe and Ovada in the Alto Monferrato are its DOCGs, and Dolcetto d'Alba the everyday bottle. It is usually kept fresh in steel and drunk within a few years.","abroad":"Liguria calls it Ormeasco, around Pornassio, where it makes a firm red and a rosé called Sciac-trà. Beyond Italy it is rare: a little in Australia and the United States."}},"service":{"temp":"59 to 63 degrees Fahrenheit, 15 to 17 degrees Celsius; a touch cooler for a young bottle.","glass":"A standard red wine glass; a young Dolcetto needs no great bowl.","decant":"Usually unnecessary: it is made to be poured young and fresh.","age":"Within two or three years; Dogliani from good sites keeps a few more."},"food":{"with":[{"dish":"Agnolotti with butter and sage","why":"soft black cherry beside the meat filling and butter"},{"dish":"Salumi and cured meats","why":"gentle acidity and blackberry beside salt and fat"},{"dish":"Mushroom pizza","why":"bitter almond and black plum sit well with mushrooms"},{"dish":"Grilled sausages","why":"soft tannin and ripe black plum meet fat and char"}]},"labels":[{"term":"Dogliani","means":"Dolcetto alone from the hills around Dogliani, south of Barolo, a DOCG since 2005."},{"term":"Dolcetto d'Alba","means":"The everyday Dolcetto of the hills around Alba, made to drink young."},{"term":"Diano d'Alba","means":"A Dolcetto DOCG of one village near Alba, in the Langhe."},{"term":"Ormeasco di Pornassio","means":"Dolcetto's Ligurian name and wine; Sciac-trà is its rosé."}],"guest":[{"q":"Dolcetto means little sweet one. Is it a sweet wine?","a":"No, it is dry. The name is thought to describe the grapes, which are low in acid and taste sweet off the vine."},{"q":"How is it different from the Barbera?","a":"Both are dark, everyday reds of Piedmont. Barbera is sharp and juicy with little tannin; Dolcetto is softer in acidity, with a little more grip and a bitter almond finish."},{"q":"Do I need to let it breathe?","a":"No. It is made to be poured young and fresh, and a few minutes in the glass is all it needs."}],"compare":[{"slug":"barbera","tell":"Barbera is as dark but sharply acidic, with less tannin; Dolcetto is softer in acidity and rounder, with a dry, bitter almond finish."},{"slug":"gamay","tell":"Gamay is paler and tarter, with raspberry and sometimes banana; Dolcetto is darker, softer in acidity and firmer, with black plum and bitter almond."},{"slug":"nebbiolo","tell":"Nebbiolo is pale garnet with high acidity and fierce tannin; Dolcetto is deep purple, low in acidity, soft and ready young."}],"profile":{"c":"r","st":"Deep purple ruby · Body medium · Acid low to medium · Tannin medium, with a bitter edge · Alcohol medium · Dry","fruit":"Black plum, blackberry, black cherry; riper black plum from the lower, warmer hills","other":"Bitter almond on the finish, liquorice and anise; little or no oak, since most is kept in steel and drunk young","tell":"Deep colour, soft acidity and a dry, bitter almond finish: dark like Barbera, but rounder and lower in acid","regions":"Piedmont: Dogliani, Diano d'Alba, Dolcetto d'Alba, Ovada and Acqui; Liguria as Ormeasco di Pornassio","confuse":"Barbera is as dark but sharply acidic with less tannin; Gamay is paler and tarter; Nebbiolo is pale and far firmer"},"cards":[{"id":"gt-kwxsylei","front":"Dolcetto: what does the name mean, and is the wine sweet?","back":"Little sweet one. The wine is dry; the name is thought to describe the grapes, low in acid and sweet off the vine."},{"id":"gt-qz59qjdn","front":"Dolcetto: its two DOCGs in the Langhe, and its Ligurian name?","back":"Dogliani and Diano d'Alba. In Liguria, around Pornassio, it is Ormeasco, with a rosé called Sciac-trà."},{"id":"gt-s15f4odw","front":"Dolcetto blind: which structure and finish set it apart from Barbera?","back":"Deep colour like Barbera, but low to medium acidity, a little more tannin and a dry, bitter almond finish."}],"questions":[{"id":"gt-tvn8weyt","q":"A red poured blind is deep purple and soft, with black plum and blackberry, low acidity, some grip and a bitter almond finish. Which Piedmont grape is it?","opts":["Dolcetto","Nebbiolo","Barbera","Grignolino"],"a":0,"exp":"Deep colour with low acidity and a bitter almond finish is Dolcetto. Barbera, the nearest wrong option, is as dark but sharply acidic, with less tannin; Nebbiolo is pale and fiercely tannic."},{"id":"gt-x54ukkse","q":"A guest translates Dolcetto on the list as little sweet one and asks for something dry instead. What do you tell her?","opts":["It is a sweet sparkling red from Acqui","It is sweet, made from dried grapes","It is off dry, with a little sugar left","It is dry; the name describes the grapes"],"a":3,"exp":"Dolcetto is a dry red; the name is thought to describe grapes low in acid and sweet off the vine. The sweet sparkling red of Acqui, the nearest miss, is Brachetto."},{"id":"gt-jtwo4ttp","q":"On a Ligurian wine list a guest finds Ormeasco di Pornassio and asks which grape is in the bottle. What do you answer?","opts":["Barbera","Dolcetto","Vermentino","Rossese"],"a":1,"exp":"Ormeasco is Liguria's name for Dolcetto, grown in the hills of Pornassio as a firm red and a rosé called Sciac-trà. Rossese, the nearest miss, is a different Ligurian red."}],"sources":[{"title":"Ente Turismo Langhe Monferrato Roero: the Dolcetto grape","url":"https://www.visitlmr.it/en/vitigni/dolcetto"},{"title":"Langhe.net: Dogliani DOCG","url":"https://langhe.net/en/wines/dogliani-docg/"},{"title":"Falstaff: grape varieties, Dolcetto","url":"https://www.falstaff.com/en/grape-varieties/dolcetto"},{"title":"The Oxford Companion to Wine","url":""}]},
"zweigelt": {"slug":"zweigelt","name":"Zweigelt","colour":"red","kind":"grape","status":"thin","mode":"home","aliases":[],"notIn":[],"say":"TSVY-gelt","lines":{"s10":"Zweigelt is the juicy red of Austria: sour cherry and a pinch of black pepper, soft on the tongue and happy to be served cool.","s20":"Zweigelt is an Austrian red, a crossing of two older local grapes made in 1922. It is juicy and soft, with sour cherry, raspberry and a little black pepper, and it likes a light chill.","s45":"Zweigelt grows all across Austria, from the banks of the Danube to the Hungarian border. It tastes of sour cherry and raspberry with a pinch of black pepper, and it is juicy and medium bodied, with fresh acidity and soft tannin. Most of it is made in steel tanks and sold young, while the fuller wines from around Lake Neusiedl rest in oak barrels and pick up a little vanilla. Chill it lightly for schnitzel, sausages or roast chicken. It is a young grape, a crossing made near Vienna in 1922 from two older local reds, Blaufränkisch and Saint Laurent.","sale":"Zweigelt puts sour cherry and a snap of black pepper into a red light enough for a schnitzel. Lightly chilled, it suits the roast chicken or anything fried."},"glass":{"lead":"Sour cherry and raspberry with a pinch of cracked black pepper, juicy and medium bodied, on fresh acidity and soft tannin.","notes":[{"family":"red fruit","notes":["sour cherry","raspberry","redcurrant"]},{"family":"black fruit","notes":["black cherry","blackberry"]},{"family":"spice","notes":["cracked black pepper","liquorice"]},{"family":"oak","notes":["vanilla pod","cocoa"]}],"ladder":[{"climate":"cool","where":"Lower Austria: the Weinviertel and the Kamptal","notes":["sour cherry","redcurrant","cracked black pepper"]},{"climate":"moderate","where":"Burgenland around Lake Neusiedl, and Carnuntum","notes":["black cherry","blackberry","liquorice"]}],"structure":"Dry. Acidity medium to medium plus, tannin medium and soft, alcohol moderate, body medium."},"worlds":{"home":{"home":"Austria, where it is grown across the country's wine regions. The warm shore of Lake Neusiedl in Burgenland and the slopes of Carnuntum, east of Vienna, give the fullest wines, some of them aged in small oak barrels. Lower Austria's are lighter, made in steel and sold young. It is also pressed for rosé.","abroad":"Hungary, the Czech Republic and Slovakia grow it next door, mostly for light reds and rosé. Further off it has taken to places with hard winters: the Okanagan Valley in British Columbia and Hokkaido in Japan."}},"service":{"temp":"54 to 59 degrees Fahrenheit, 12 to 15 degrees Celsius: lightly chilled, an oaked Reserve a little warmer.","glass":"A plain red wine glass; a Burgundy bowl flatters a fuller, barrel aged bottle.","decant":"Usually unnecessary: it is made to be opened and poured.","age":"Young, within three or four years; a Reserve from Burgenland or Carnuntum keeps five to ten."},"food":{"with":[{"dish":"Wiener schnitzel","why":"fresh acidity cuts the fried crumb"},{"dish":"Sausages, cold cuts and dark bread","why":"the Viennese wine tavern table, fruit against salt"},{"dish":"Roast chicken","why":"soft tannin leaves the bird alone"},{"dish":"Goulash or chicken in paprika","why":"the pepper in the wine meets the paprika"}]},"labels":[{"term":"Blauer Zweigelt","means":"The grape's longer name on some Austrian labels: the same wine."},{"term":"Rotburger","means":"A second legal name. The breeder was a National Socialist, and some in Austria want his name off the grape."},{"term":"Neusiedlersee","means":"The lakeside origin in Burgenland whose red is Zweigelt; a Reserve blend holds at least 60 per cent."},{"term":"Zweigeltrebe","means":"The grape's name on Czech and Japanese labels: Zweigelt vine, nothing more."}],"guest":[{"q":"I usually drink Pinot Noir. Will I like this?","a":"I think so. It is a little darker and juicier, with sour cherry and a peppery snap where Pinot Noir has silk and forest floor, and it is just as easy with food."},{"q":"It smells so fruity. Is it sweet?","a":"No, it is dry. Ripe sour cherry and raspberry can read as sweetness on the nose, but there is no sugar left in the wine and the finish is fresh."},{"q":"How on earth do I say it?","a":"TSVY-gelt. The Z is said as ts and the W as v, in two syllables with the weight on the first."}],"compare":[{"slug":"blaufrankisch","tell":"Blaufränkisch, its parent, is darker and firmer, with blackberry, more black pepper, higher acidity and real tannin; Zweigelt is softer, juicier and ready sooner."},{"slug":"gamay","tell":"Gamay is paler and lighter, with violet and often a candied banana note; Zweigelt is darker, with a peppery snap and a touch more tannin."},{"slug":"pinot-noir","tell":"Pinot Noir is paler and silkier, with forest floor under its red cherry; Zweigelt is purple at the rim, juicier and plainer, with a peppery finish."}],"profile":{"c":"r","st":"Ruby with a violet tint · Body medium · Acid medium to medium plus · Tannin medium and soft · Alcohol moderate","fruit":"Sour cherry, raspberry, redcurrant; black cherry and blackberry in the fuller wines of Burgenland and Carnuntum","other":"Cracked black pepper and a touch of liquorice; vanilla and cocoa where the wine has seen small oak barrels; mostly unoaked and sold young","tell":"Juicy sour cherry with a peppery snap, soft tannin and a violet tinted rim: darker than Gamay, softer than Blaufränkisch","regions":"Austria throughout: Neusiedlersee and Carnuntum for the fuller wines, Lower Austria for the lighter; Hungary, the Czech Republic and Slovakia","confuse":"Blaufränkisch is darker, firmer and more peppery; Gamay is paler and lighter, often with banana; St Laurent is silkier and more perfumed"},"cards":[{"id":"gt-eet7nzrk","front":"Zweigelt: which two grapes were crossed, where, and in what year?","back":"Blaufränkisch and Saint Laurent, crossed by Fritz Zweigelt at Klosterneuburg, near Vienna, in 1922. Rotburger is its second legal name."},{"id":"gt-z7x2u9n3","front":"Zweigelt: which lakeside Austrian origin is built on it?","back":"Neusiedlersee, in Burgenland, a protected origin since 2012. Its Reserve may be a blend, with at least 60 per cent Zweigelt."},{"id":"gt-td4j2gby","front":"Zweigelt against Blaufränkisch in a blind glass: what parts them?","back":"Zweigelt is softer and juicier: sour cherry, medium tannin, made to drink young. Blaufränkisch is darker, with blackberry, more black pepper, higher acidity and firm tannin, and it keeps."}],"questions":[{"id":"gt-dtzqcih1","q":"An Austrian red poured blind is ruby with a violet tint, juicy and medium bodied, with sour cherry, raspberry and a pinch of black pepper, fresh acidity and soft tannin. Which grape is it most likely to be?","opts":["Cabernet Sauvignon","Nebbiolo","Blaufränkisch","Zweigelt"],"a":3,"exp":"Juicy sour cherry, a little pepper and soft tannin point to Zweigelt. Blaufränkisch, its parent and the nearest wrong answer, is darker and firmer, with higher acidity and real grip."},{"id":"gt-n1h7lg2u","q":"A guest reads Rotburger on an Austrian label and asks what grape that is. What do you tell her?","opts":["Zweigelt, under its second legal name","Blaufränkisch, as Germany names it","Pinot Noir, as Austria names it","Saint Laurent, under an old name"],"a":0,"exp":"Rotburger is a legal synonym for Zweigelt in Austria. Blaufränkisch is Lemberger in Germany, and Pinot Noir is Blauburgunder in Austria."},{"id":"gt-hr5rdnr6","q":"A guest who enjoyed a light Zweigelt asks for a fuller one, from where the grape ripens most fully. Which Austrian origin do you look for on the list?","opts":["Wachau","Traisental","Neusiedlersee","Weststeiermark"],"a":2,"exp":"The warm shore of Lake Neusiedl is the origin built on Zweigelt and gives its fullest reds. The Wachau and Traisental are white wine country, and Weststeiermark is known for Schilcher rosé."}],"sources":[{"title":"Austrian Wine: Zweigelt","url":"https://www.austrianwine.com/our-wine/grape-varieties/red-wine/zweigelt"},{"title":"Austrian Wine: Grape varieties, a small guide","url":"https://www.austrianwine.com/fileadmin/user_upload/PDF/Broschueren/Rebsortenbroschuere_2019_EN.pdf"},{"title":"wein.plus glossary: Zweigelt","url":"https://glossary.wein.plus/zweigelt"},{"title":"Yoichi Town, Hokkaido: wine tourism, the wineries","url":"https://www.town.yoichi.hokkaido.jp/wine-tourism/en/winery/index.html"}]},
"meunier": {"slug":"meunier","name":"Meunier","colour":"red","kind":"grape","status":"new","mode":"home","aliases":["Pinot Meunier","Schwarzriesling"],"notIn":[],"say":"muh-NYAY, or PEE-noh muh-NYAY","lines":{"s10":"Meunier is Champagne's third grape, a black one, and it gives the blend ripe pear, red apple and an early, easy charm.","s20":"Meunier is the third grape of Champagne, a black grape pressed gently for white juice. It brings ripe pear, yellow plum and a soft, round middle, and it makes a Champagne ready to enjoy young.","s45":"Meunier is the third grape of Champagne, beside Chardonnay and Pinot Noir. It is a black grape, and its home is the Marne Valley, where it buds late and so suffers less from spring frost. In the glass it gives ripe pear, yellow plum and a little wild strawberry, with a soft, round feel. Most of it is pressed gently for white juice and blended, to fill out the middle of a Champagne that is ready young. A few growers bottle it alone. Pour it with fried chicken, mushrooms or a plate of charcuterie. Its name means miller, for the white down that dusts its leaves like flour.","sale":"Meunier is the grape that makes Champagne easy to love on the first sip: ripe pear, soft bubbles, nothing sharp. Start the table with a glass of it and the fried oysters."},"glass":{"lead":"Ripe pear, yellow plum and wild strawberry on a soft, round frame, with brioche from its time on the lees.","notes":[{"family":"orchard fruit","notes":["ripe pear","red apple","yellow apple"]},{"family":"stone fruit","notes":["yellow plum","apricot"]},{"family":"red fruit","notes":["wild strawberry","raspberry"]},{"family":"lees and cellar","notes":["brioche","bread dough"]},{"family":"age","notes":["hazelnut","honey","dried mushroom"]}],"ladder":[{"climate":"cool","where":"Champagne's Marne Valley, England, Tasmania and the Willamette Valley","notes":["ripe pear","red apple","redcurrant"]},{"climate":"moderate","where":"Württemberg's sheltered Neckar valley, as a soft still red","notes":["sour cherry","wild strawberry"]}],"structure":"Made as Champagne it is dry, with high acidity, moderate alcohol, a light to medium body and barely any tannin."},"worlds":{"home":{"home":"Champagne, above all the clay soils of the western Marne Valley, where late budding makes it less prone to spring frost. It is pressed gently for white juice and blended with Chardonnay and Pinot Noir, to give ripe pear and a round middle to wines made to be enjoyed young. A few growers bottle it alone.","abroad":"England grows it for sparkling wine made the Champagne way, and so do Tasmania and Oregon's Willamette Valley. In Germany it is Schwarzriesling or Müllerrebe, mostly in Württemberg, and is made as a light still red."}},"service":{"temp":"46 to 50 degrees Fahrenheit, 8 to 10 degrees Celsius, as Champagne.","glass":"A tulip shaped sparkling glass or a white wine glass, so the fruit can show.","decant":"Rarely, and only if the guest asks; the bubbles are the point.","age":"Wines led by Meunier tend to mature sooner than those led by Chardonnay or Pinot Noir; enjoy them young."},"food":{"with":[{"dish":"Gulf oysters, raw or fried","why":"acidity and bubbles cut the brine and the batter"},{"dish":"Fried chicken","why":"bubbles and acidity lift salt and fat"},{"dish":"Mushroom tart","why":"the wine's soft fruit sits well with mushrooms"},{"dish":"Charcuterie and pâté","why":"round fruit beside cured, salty pork"}]},"labels":[{"term":"Blanc de Noirs","means":"White Champagne from black grapes alone: Pinot Noir, Meunier or both."},{"term":"Pinot Meunier","means":"The older name for the same grape; Champagne's own trade body writes Meunier."},{"term":"Schwarzriesling","means":"The German name, black Riesling, though it is no relation of Riesling; also Müllerrebe."},{"term":"Vallée de la Marne","means":"The Marne Valley, the part of Champagne where Meunier is most at home."}],"guest":[{"q":"I only know Chardonnay and Pinot Noir. What is Meunier?","a":"It is the third grape of Champagne, a black grape and a natural mutation of Pinot Noir. It gives a blend its soft, ripe pear fruit, and many Champagnes include it."},{"q":"If the grape is black, why is the wine white?","a":"The colour is in the skins and the juice runs clear. Champagne presses whole bunches gently and takes the juice away before the skins can tint it."},{"q":"Is a Champagne with a lot of Meunier a lesser wine?","a":"No, a different one. It tends to be rounder and more open when young and to mature sooner, which is what many tables want."}],"compare":[{"slug":"pinot-noir","tell":"Pinot Noir gives Champagne more body, structure and red cherry depth and ages longer; Meunier is softer, rounder and ready sooner."},{"slug":"chardonnay","tell":"Chardonnay brings Champagne lemon, chalk and long life; Meunier brings ripe pear, yellow plum and a supple middle."}],"profile":{"c":"r","st":"Black grape, mostly pressed white for sparkling wine · Body light to medium · Acid high · Tannin negligible · Alcohol moderate · Dry","fruit":"Ripe pear, red apple, yellow plum; wild strawberry and raspberry in rosé and still red","other":"Brioche and bread dough from lees ageing, hazelnut and dried mushroom with age; a soft, supple texture","tell":"The round, forward middle of a Champagne blend: soft and ready young, where Pinot Noir is firmer and Chardonnay leaner","regions":"Champagne (the Marne Valley above all), England, Württemberg in Germany (as Schwarzriesling), Tasmania, Willamette Valley","confuse":"Pinot Noir is firmer, with more red cherry and structure; Chardonnay is leaner, with lemon and chalk"},"targets":["champagne","england","wurttemberg","tasmania","willamette-valley"],"cards":[{"id":"gt-gzy1gokk","front":"Meunier: which region is home, and what does it add to a blend?","back":"Champagne, above all the Marne Valley. It adds ripe pear and yellow plum fruit and a soft, round middle, in wines ready to enjoy young."},{"id":"gt-c1cwml9h","front":"Why does Meunier suit the frost prone clay soils of the Marne Valley?","back":"It buds later than Chardonnay and Pinot Noir, so spring frost harms it less, and it copes with soils that hold more clay."},{"id":"gt-cbvbchgv","front":"What does the name Meunier mean, and what is the grape's German name?","back":"Miller: its young leaves and shoot tips look dusted with flour. In Germany it is Schwarzriesling or Müllerrebe, grown mostly in Württemberg."}],"questions":[{"id":"gt-0esfxtk0","q":"A guest points to Blanc de Noirs on a Champagne label and asks which grapes it can be made from. What do you answer?","opts":["Chardonnay alone","Chardonnay and Pinot Blanc","Glera and Pinot Noir","Pinot Noir and Meunier"],"a":3,"exp":"Blanc de Noirs is white Champagne from black grapes alone, which means Pinot Noir, Meunier or both. Chardonnay alone would be Blanc de Blancs."},{"id":"gt-3hztiwm9","q":"A sparkling wine is poured blind: ripe pear and yellow plum, soft and round, open and easy while still young. Which Champagne grape most likely leads the blend?","opts":["Pinot Noir","Pinot Blanc","Meunier","Chardonnay"],"a":2,"exp":"Supple wines of ripe pear and yellow plum that show early are the mark of Meunier. Chardonnay tends to be leaner and more chalky, and Pinot Noir firmer and more structured."},{"id":"gt-6ft6do3b","q":"A German wine list offers a light red called Schwarzriesling from Württemberg. Which grape is in the bottle?","opts":["Trollinger","Meunier","Blaufränkisch","Riesling"],"a":1,"exp":"Schwarzriesling, also Müllerrebe, is the German name for Meunier. Despite the name it is no relation of Riesling; it is a mutation of Pinot Noir."}],"sources":[{"title":"Comité Champagne: Champagne and its grape varieties","url":"https://www.champagne.fr/en/about-champagne/a-great-blended-wine/champagne-and-its-grape-varieties"},{"title":"Comité Champagne: Champagne Blanc de Noirs","url":"https://www.champagne.fr/en/champagne-tasting/choosing-your-champagne/blanc-de-noirs"},{"title":"Wines of Germany: Schwarzriesling, the black Riesling","url":"https://www.winesofgermany.com/our-wine/grape-varieties/grape-variety/93/black-riesling"},{"title":"Vitis International Variety Catalogue: Pinot Meunier","url":"https://www.vivc.de/index.php?r=passport%2Fview&id=9278"}]},
"petite-sirah": {"slug":"petite-sirah","name":"Petite Sirah","colour":"red","kind":"grape","status":"new","mode":"home","aliases":["Durif"],"notIn":[],"say":"puh-TEET sih-RAH, in Australia DUR-if","lines":{"s10":"Petite Sirah is the inky red of California: blueberry, blackberry and black pepper, with a firm grip that calls for rich food.","s20":"Petite Sirah is a grape of its own, raised in France as Durif and at home in California. It is nearly black in the glass, with blueberry, blackberry and dark chocolate, full in body and firm in tannin.","s45":"Petite Sirah is not a small Syrah, nor Syrah at all. It is a separate grape, a child of Syrah, at home in California, where it long grew among the Zinfandel. It is nearly black in the glass and tastes of blueberry, blackberry and black pepper, with dark chocolate behind. It is full bodied, with a great deal of firm tannin. Most is aged in oak, which adds vanilla and mocha. It wants rich food: braised short rib, barbecue or a burger with blue cheese. A French nurseryman named Durif raised it in the nineteenth century, and Australia still sells it under his name.","sale":"Petite Sirah is neither petite nor Syrah: it is an inky red of blueberry, blackberry and black pepper. Have it with the barbecue or a burger with blue cheese, and let me give it some air first."},"glass":{"lead":"Blueberry, blackberry and black plum with black pepper and dark chocolate, nearly black, full bodied and thick with firm tannin.","notes":[{"family":"blue fruit","notes":["blueberry","boysenberry"]},{"family":"black fruit","notes":["blackberry","black plum"]},{"family":"spice","notes":["black pepper","liquorice"]},{"family":"oak","notes":["vanilla pod","mocha","dark chocolate"]}],"ladder":[{"climate":"moderate","where":"The coastal ranges: Mendocino and cooler Sonoma sites","notes":["blueberry","black pepper","black tea"]},{"climate":"warm","where":"Napa Valley, Lodi, the Central Valley and Rutherglen","notes":["blackberry jam","prune","dark chocolate"]}],"structure":"Dry. Acidity medium to medium plus, tannin high, alcohol medium plus to high, body full."},"worlds":{"home":{"home":"California, where vines called Petite Sirah have grown since the 1880s, long as a partner of Zinfandel in old mixed vineyards. It is now bottled alone from Napa, Sonoma and Lodi: inky, full and firmly tannic, with blueberry, blackberry and black pepper, usually aged in oak.","abroad":"France, where it was raised as Durif, has all but given it up. Rutherglen in Australia has grown it since 1908, as Durif, in a dense, ripe style, and Mexico grows it in Baja California."}},"service":{"temp":"61 to 64 degrees Fahrenheit, 16 to 18 degrees Celsius, as for any full, tannic red.","glass":"A tall Bordeaux glass, with room to swirl.","decant":"Consider it for a young wine after tasting a sample; air can open the fruit behind the tannin.","age":"Most within five years; firm, concentrated wines keep ten years and more."},"food":{"with":[{"dish":"Braised short rib","why":"richness to meet a great deal of tannin"},{"dish":"Barbecued brisket or ribs","why":"smoke and char suit the dark, peppery fruit"},{"dish":"Steak or a burger with blue cheese","why":"fat and salt ease the grip"},{"dish":"Aged hard cheese","why":"salt and fat round off firm tannin"}]},"labels":[{"term":"Durif","means":"The grape's first name, after the French nurseryman who raised it; the name Australia uses."},{"term":"Field blend","means":"An old vineyard of several grapes grown and picked together; Petite Sirah is often among them, with Zinfandel."},{"term":"Rutherglen","means":"A warm district of north east Victoria, in Australia, where the grape is sold as Durif."}],"guest":[{"q":"Is Petite Sirah a lighter kind of Syrah?","a":"No, and it is not light at all. It is a separate grape, a cross of Syrah and Peloursin, and it is usually darker, fuller and more tannic than Syrah."},{"q":"Why is it called petite?","a":"The name is thought to come from the berries, which are small. Small berries mean a lot of skin to a little juice, and the skin is where the colour and the tannin come from."},{"q":"Is Durif a different wine?","a":"It is the same grape. Durif is its first, French name, after the man who raised it, and Australia still uses it, above all in Rutherglen."},{"q":"Is it really a French grape?","a":"By birth, yes: it was raised in a French nursery. France has almost none now, and DNA work at the University of California in the 1990s showed that most of California's old Petite Sirah vines are that same grape."}],"compare":[{"slug":"syrah","tell":"Syrah, its parent, is more savoury and perfumed, with black olive, smoked meat and violet; Petite Sirah is inkier and more tannic, with little of that savour."},{"slug":"zinfandel","tell":"Zinfandel is paler and jammier, with raspberry, a raisin edge and softer tannin; Petite Sirah is nearly black and far more tannic."},{"slug":"malbec","tell":"Malbec is as dark but plusher, with violet and soft, round tannin; Petite Sirah is firmer and more peppery, with a far heavier grip."}],"profile":{"c":"r","st":"Opaque purple-black, staining the glass · Body full · Acid medium to medium plus · Tannin high · Alcohol medium plus to high · Dry","fruit":"Blueberry, blackberry, black plum; jammier, with prune, from hot sites","other":"Black pepper, liquorice, dark chocolate and black tea; vanilla and mocha from oak; thick, chewy tannin","tell":"Inky and staining, with blueberry, black pepper and dark chocolate on a wall of firm tannin: darker and more gripping than Syrah or Zinfandel","regions":"California (Napa, Sonoma, Mendocino, Lodi, the Central Valley), Rutherglen in Australia as Durif, Baja California in Mexico","confuse":"Syrah is more savoury, with black olive, smoked meat and violet; Zinfandel is paler and jammier, with softer tannin; Malbec is plusher, with violet"},"targets":["california","napa-sonoma","rutherglen","mexico"],"cards":[{"id":"gt-yii84pg8","front":"Petite Sirah: which two grapes are its parents, and its other name?","back":"Syrah and Peloursin. It was raised in France in the nineteenth century by a nurseryman named Durif, and Durif is its other name."},{"id":"gt-o5csq2j4","front":"Where does Australia grow Petite Sirah, and under which name?","back":"Rutherglen, in north east Victoria, where it has grown since 1908 and is sold as Durif: dense, ripe and tannic."},{"id":"gt-nl5uhwwu","front":"Petite Sirah blind: what sets it apart from Syrah and Zinfandel?","back":"It is inkier and more tannic than either: blueberry, black pepper and dark chocolate on a full, chewy frame. Syrah is more savoury; Zinfandel is paler and jammier."}],"questions":[{"id":"gt-026kh95l","q":"A guest who finds Syrah too heavy sees Petite Sirah on the list and hopes it is a lighter version. What is true of the wine?","opts":["It is a separate grape, darker and more tannic","It is Syrah picked early for a lighter style","It is Syrah from young vines","It is a rosé made from Syrah"],"a":0,"exp":"Petite Sirah is Durif, a cross of Syrah and Peloursin, and it is usually inkier and more tannic than Syrah. The name describes small berries, not a light wine or an early picked Syrah."},{"id":"gt-5dlseump","q":"A blind red is opaque purple and stains the glass, with blueberry, blackberry, black pepper and dark chocolate. It is full bodied, with very firm tannin and no raisin or smoked meat. Which grape is it most likely to be?","opts":["Zinfandel","Petite Sirah","Syrah","Cabernet Sauvignon"],"a":1,"exp":"Inky colour, blueberry, black pepper and very firm tannin point to Petite Sirah. Zinfandel is paler with a raisin edge, Syrah shows smoked meat and olive, and Cabernet Sauvignon shows cassis and cedar."},{"id":"gt-hv30ldrr","q":"An Australian list offers a Rutherglen Durif, and a guest asks which grape she would know it as in California. Which is it?","opts":["Syrah","Zinfandel","Petite Sirah","Carignane"],"a":2,"exp":"Durif is the first name of Petite Sirah, kept in Australia, where Rutherglen has grown it since 1908. Syrah is one of its parents, not the same grape."}],"sources":[{"title":"Winemakers of Rutherglen: history and heritage","url":"https://winemakers.com.au/history-and-heritage/"},{"title":"Wine Folly: Petite Sirah, the grape","url":"https://winefolly.com/grapes/petite-sirah/"},{"title":"Wine Grapes, entry on Durif","url":""},{"title":"The Oxford Companion to Wine, entry on Petite Sirah","url":""}]},
"graciano": {"slug":"graciano","name":"Graciano","colour":"red","kind":"grape","status":"new","mode":"home","aliases":[],"notIn":[],"say":"grah-thee-AH-noh","lines":{"s10":"Graciano is Rioja's seasoning grape: a little of it brings deep colour, sharp freshness and a scent of violet to the blend.","s20":"Graciano is an old red of Rioja, used in small shares to give a blend colour, acidity and perfume. Alone it is deep purple and lively, with blackberry, violet and a cool touch of mint.","s45":"Graciano is used like salt in Rioja: a little lifts the whole blend. A native of Rioja, grown there and in Navarra, it ripens late and bears small crops. In the glass it is deep purple, with blackberry, black cherry and violet. The acidity is sharp, with a cool note of mint and a firm grip. Blenders add a few per cent to the long aged Riojas for colour and freshness, and a few cellars bottle it alone. Try it with lamb chops, venison or a hard sheep's cheese. Growers once pulled it out for easier vines, and it nearly vanished before Rioja brought it back.","sale":"Graciano is usually a small part of a Rioja blend, and on its own it shows violet, blackberry and a fresh, firm bite. I would open it with the lamb chops for a table that wants something new from a region it already trusts."},"glass":{"lead":"Blackberry, black cherry and violet in a deep purple glass, with liquorice, a cool note of mint and sharp, fresh acidity.","notes":[{"family":"black fruit","notes":["blackberry","black cherry","mulberry"]},{"family":"red fruit","notes":["red cherry","raspberry"]},{"family":"floral","notes":["violet","dried violet"]},{"family":"spice","notes":["liquorice","black pepper","clove"]},{"family":"herbal","notes":["mint","menthol"]}],"ladder":[{"climate":"moderate","where":"Rioja Alta and Rioja Alavesa, on warmer, sheltered plots","notes":["red cherry","raspberry","violet","mint"]},{"climate":"warm","where":"Rioja Oriental, Navarra and South Australia","notes":["blackberry","black cherry","mulberry","liquorice"]}],"structure":"Dry. Acidity high, tannin medium plus to high, alcohol medium to medium plus, body medium plus, and deep in colour."},"worlds":{"home":{"home":"Rioja, with Navarra beside it, where it ripens late and yields little. A few per cent in a Reserva or Gran Reserva brings colour, acidity and violet that help the wine keep for decades. It nearly vanished as growers chose easier vines, and its revival now gives a few varietal bottlings too.","abroad":"Portugal knows it as Tinta Miúda, a minor grape of Lisboa, Corsica as Minustellu and Sardinia as Cagnulari. Australia grows a little, mostly in South Australia, as a deep coloured varietal or with Tempranillo."}},"service":{"temp":"59 to 63 degrees Fahrenheit, 15 to 17 degrees Celsius, as for Rioja and other medium reds.","glass":"A tall Bordeaux glass, with room to swirl.","decant":"Consider it for a young varietal Graciano after tasting a sample; it can be tight and firm.","age":"Varietal wines open over five to ten years; its acidity helps a Gran Reserva blend keep for decades."},"food":{"with":[{"dish":"Grilled lamb chops","why":"sharp acidity and grip cut the fat"},{"dish":"Venison or other game","why":"blackberry and firm tannin suit lean, dark meat"},{"dish":"Braised oxtail","why":"fresh acidity lifts a rich, slow cooked sauce"},{"dish":"Hard sheep's cheese","why":"salt and fat soften the firm grip"}]},"labels":[{"term":"Tempranillo Graciano","means":"A two grape blend named on the label: Tempranillo leads, Graciano adds colour and lift."},{"term":"Tinta Miúda","means":"Graciano's Portuguese name, a minor grape in the reds of Lisboa."},{"term":"Cagnulari","means":"Graciano's name in Sardinia, mostly in the north west of the island."},{"term":"Minustellu","means":"Graciano's Corsican name, one of the old island grapes rescued in recent decades."}],"guest":[{"q":"I have never heard of Graciano. What is it?","a":"A native red of Rioja that growers almost lost because it bears such small crops. A few per cent in a Rioja blend adds colour, freshness and a scent of violet, and a few cellars bottle it alone."},{"q":"Why is there so little of it?","a":"It ripens late and yields little, so growers pulled it out for easier vines. Rioja and Navarra have replanted it, but it is still a small share of most blends."},{"q":"Will a young varietal Graciano be too firm tonight?","a":"It is fresh and firm when young. With a fatty cut of lamb and a little air it softens, and I can pour you a taste first."}],"compare":[{"slug":"tempranillo","tell":"Tempranillo is rounder and softer in acidity, with strawberry and leather; Graciano is deeper in colour and sharper, with violet, mint and liquorice."},{"slug":"carignan","tell":"Carignan, Mazuelo in Rioja, shares the acidity and tannin but brings cranberry, dried thyme and an iron note; Graciano is more perfumed, with violet and mint."},{"slug":"mourvedre","tell":"Mourvèdre is gamey and meaty, with leather on a fuller, warmer frame; Graciano is fresher and more perfumed, with violet and mint."}],"profile":{"c":"r","st":"Deep purple, nearly opaque · Body medium plus · Acid high · Tannin medium plus to high · Alcohol medium to medium plus · Dry","fruit":"Blackberry, black cherry and mulberry; red cherry and raspberry from cooler sites","other":"Violet, liquorice, black pepper and clove, with a cool lift of mint","tell":"Deep colour and sharp acidity with violet and mint: the perfume and bite a little Graciano gives a Rioja blend","regions":"Rioja and Navarra; Lisboa as Tinta Miúda, Corsica as Minustellu, Sardinia as Cagnulari; a little in South Australia","confuse":"Tempranillo is softer and rounder, with strawberry and leather; Carignan is as sharp but brings cranberry, dried thyme and iron, not violet"},"targets":["rioja","navarra","spain","lisboa","corsica"],"cards":[{"id":"gt-ry1zfmyd","front":"Graciano: what does a few per cent of it add to a Rioja blend?","back":"Deep colour, fresh acidity and a scent of violet, which help Reserva and Gran Reserva wines keep for many years."},{"id":"gt-zos2kl2o","front":"Why did Graciano nearly disappear from Rioja's vineyards?","back":"It ripens late and yields little, so growers replaced it with easier vines. A revival has brought it back, in blends and in a few varietal wines."},{"id":"gt-ksdbri00","front":"Graciano travels under other names. Which in Portugal, Corsica and Sardinia?","back":"Tinta Miúda in Portugal, chiefly in Lisboa; Minustellu in Corsica; Cagnulari in Sardinia."}],"questions":[{"id":"gt-7seealz6","q":"A varietal red from Rioja is poured blind: deep purple, with blackberry, violet, mint and liquorice, sharp acidity and a firm grip. Which grape is it?","opts":["Mazuelo","Garnacha","Tempranillo","Graciano"],"a":3,"exp":"Deep colour, sharp acidity, violet and mint mark Graciano. Mazuelo, Rioja's Carignan and the nearest miss, is as firm but brings cranberry, dried thyme and an iron note, not violet."},{"id":"gt-8eje9nhn","q":"A guest reading a Portuguese list asks which grape in an Alenquer red is the same as Rioja's Graciano. Which name do you point to?","opts":["Touriga Franca","Tinta Miúda","Castelão","Tinta Roriz"],"a":1,"exp":"Tinta Miúda is Graciano under its Portuguese name, a minor grape of Lisboa. Tinta Roriz, the nearest miss, is a Rioja grape too, but it is Tempranillo."},{"id":"gt-0gaj6ci1","q":"A guest reading the back label of a Rioja Gran Reserva asks why its small share of Graciano was nearly lost from the vineyards. What do you tell him?","opts":["It ripens late and bears small crops","It ripens too early for the valley","It cannot take long ageing in oak","The law long banned it from Rioja"],"a":0,"exp":"Graciano ripens late and yields little, so growers replaced it with easier vines until a revival. Ripening early, the nearest wrong answer, is Tempranillo's trait and gave it its name."}],"sources":[{"title":"Rioja Wine: Graciano, grape varieties of the designation","url":"https://riojawine.com/en/the-designation/grape-varieties/graciano/"},{"title":"Rioja Wine: discover the Rioja red grape varieties","url":"https://riojawine.com/en/blog/discover-the-rioja-red-grape-variety/"},{"title":"Wine Australia: alternative varieties guide","url":"https://www.australianwine.com/getContentAsset/ab598bc4-6da1-4d83-8f07-a637e836df72/2a6464f5-7547-4f96-aacf-a0c3da1718f8/AWD_AlternativeVarieties_Guide.pdf"},{"title":"Wein-Plus glossary: Graciano","url":"https://glossary.wein.plus/graciano"}]},
"aligote": {"slug":"aligote","name":"Aligoté","colour":"white","kind":"grape","status":"new","mode":"home","aliases":[],"notIn":[],"say":"ah-lee-goh-TAY","lines":{"s10":"Aligoté is Burgundy's other white grape: lean, lemony and bright, the wine that built the Kir and now stands alone.","s20":"Aligoté is Burgundy's second white grape, often planted at the top and the foot of Chardonnay's slopes. It gives a light, dry wine of lemon and green apple with a sharp, clean finish, the classic partner for blackcurrant liqueur in a Kir.","s45":"Many people have drunk Aligoté without knowing it, poured over blackcurrant liqueur as a Kir. It grows across Burgundy beside Chardonnay, often at the top and the foot of the slopes. On its own it tastes of lemon, green apple and a little acacia blossom. It is light, dry and brisk, with more bite than Chardonnay. Most of it sees steel, not new oak, and is sold young. Try it with oysters, snails in garlic butter or a slice of parsleyed ham. One village name, Bouzeron, is kept for it alone, and its golden strain has thin skins that turn gold as they ripen.","sale":"Aligoté is white Burgundy without the weight: lemon, green apple and a clean, sharp finish. I would pour it with anything in garlic butter, or with a little cassis as a Kir to open the evening."},"glass":{"lead":"Lemon, green apple and acacia blossom, lean and brisk, with a touch of hazelnut in riper wines.","notes":[{"family":"citrus","notes":["lemon","lemon zest"]},{"family":"orchard fruit","notes":["green apple","crisp pear"]},{"family":"floral","notes":["acacia blossom","hawthorn"]},{"family":"nutty","notes":["hazelnut","fresh almond"]},{"family":"mineral","notes":["wet stone","crushed chalk"]}],"ladder":[{"climate":"cool","where":"Burgundy's hilltops and Hautes Côtes in most years","notes":["lemon zest","green apple","wet stone"]},{"climate":"moderate","where":"Bouzeron's marl slopes, and warm years across Burgundy","notes":["ripe pear","white peach","hazelnut"]}],"structure":"Dry. Acidity high, alcohol moderate, body light to medium; leaner and sharper than the Chardonnay grown beside it."},"worlds":{"home":{"home":"Burgundy, where it has grown since the seventeenth century and has held its own regional name, Bourgogne Aligoté, since 1937. It is often planted at the top and foot of the slopes and made in steel for drinking young. Bouzeron, on the Côte Chalonnaise, is the one village name reserved for it, and its golden strain gives a rounder wine.","abroad":"It travelled east more than west: Bulgaria, Romania, Moldova, Ukraine and Russia all grow it. In the rest of the wine world it is a rarity."}},"service":{"temp":"45 to 50 degrees Fahrenheit, 7 to 10 degrees Celsius; Bouzeron a touch warmer.","glass":"A white wine glass; a Kir is built in the same glass, cassis first.","decant":"Usually unnecessary; serve it cold and fresh.","age":"Enjoy it young, within two or three years; Bouzeron can keep a little longer."},"food":{"with":[{"dish":"Snails in garlic and parsley butter","why":"sharp acidity cuts the butter and garlic"},{"dish":"Gougères and other cheese pastries","why":"a brisk glass before dinner, as Burgundy pours it"},{"dish":"Parsleyed ham or charcuterie","why":"acidity refreshes after salt and fat"},{"dish":"Oysters or grilled fish","why":"the lemon in the wine does the lemon's work"}]},"labels":[{"term":"Bourgogne Aligoté","means":"The regional Burgundy name for the grape, its own since 1937; white wine only."},{"term":"Bouzeron","means":"A village of the Côte Chalonnaise whose wine is Aligoté alone, and tends to be rounder."},{"term":"Aligoté Doré","means":"Golden Aligoté, the thin skinned strain of Bouzeron that turns gold as it ripens."},{"term":"Kir","means":"Crème de cassis topped with Bourgogne Aligoté; named for a mayor of Dijon."},{"term":"Crémant de Bourgogne","means":"Burgundy's sparkling wine, in which Aligoté may join Chardonnay and Pinot Noir."}],"guest":[{"q":"Is this the wine that goes into a Kir?","a":"Yes. A Kir is crème de cassis topped with Bourgogne Aligoté, whose sharpness balances the sweet liqueur. With Champagne it becomes a Kir Royale. The wine is well worth a glass on its own."},{"q":"How is it different from Chardonnay?","a":"It is lighter, leaner and sharper, with lemon and green apple, and it is seldom aged in new oak. Think of it as Burgundy's brisk white for the start of the meal."},{"q":"What is Bouzeron?","a":"A village in the Côte Chalonnaise, in the south of Burgundy, and the only village name kept for Aligoté. Its wines tend to be rounder, with a note of hazelnut."}],"compare":[{"slug":"chardonnay","tell":"Chardonnay is rounder and fuller, and often shows cream or toast from the cellar; Aligoté is leaner and sharper, with lemon and green apple."},{"slug":"melon-de-bourgogne","tell":"Melon de Bourgogne, as Muscadet, is lighter still, with a salty, bready note from its lees; Aligoté has more lemon and more bite."},{"slug":"pinot-blanc","tell":"Pinot Blanc is softer and rounder, with ripe apple and almond at gentler acidity; Aligoté is sharper and more lemony."}],"profile":{"c":"w","st":"Pale straw gold with green tints · Body light to medium · Acid high · Alcohol moderate · Dry","fruit":"Lemon, lemon zest and green apple; ripe pear and white peach in Bouzeron and in warm years","other":"Acacia blossom, hawthorn and wet stone; a touch of hazelnut in riper wines; seldom any new oak","tell":"Lean, brisk white Burgundy of lemon and green apple with no cream or toast: lighter and sharper than Chardonnay","regions":"Burgundy: Bourgogne Aligoté across the region, Bouzeron in the Côte Chalonnaise; also Bulgaria, Romania, Moldova and Ukraine","confuse":"Chardonnay is rounder, with cream or toast from the cellar; Muscadet is lighter, saltier and bready; Pinot Blanc is softer, with almond"},"targets":["burgundy","france","eastern-europe"],"cards":[{"id":"gt-3p29i5z6","front":"Aligoté: where is it at home, and which village is kept for it?","back":"Burgundy, as Bourgogne Aligoté, a regional name of its own since 1937. Bouzeron, in the Côte Chalonnaise, is the one village name reserved for the grape."},{"id":"gt-8ryl0467","front":"What goes into a Kir, and why does Aligoté suit it?","back":"Crème de cassis topped with Bourgogne Aligoté. The wine is dry, light and high in acidity, so it balances the sweet blackcurrant liqueur."},{"id":"gt-2jgjgzbs","front":"Aligoté against Chardonnay in the glass: what parts them?","back":"Aligoté is lighter, leaner and higher in acidity, with lemon and green apple and seldom any new oak. Chardonnay is rounder and often shows cream or toast."}],"questions":[{"id":"gt-g078fdl8","q":"A guest orders a Kir before dinner and asks which wine traditionally goes over the cassis. Which do you name?","opts":["Mâcon-Villages","Champagne","Bourgogne Aligoté","Petit Chablis"],"a":2,"exp":"A Kir is crème de cassis topped with Bourgogne Aligoté, whose high acidity balances the sweet liqueur. With Champagne it becomes a Kir Royale, and Petit Chablis and Mâcon-Villages are Chardonnay."},{"id":"gt-gz4vef7v","q":"A guest wants a white Burgundy that is not Chardonnay. Which village name on the list is made from Aligoté alone?","opts":["Rully","Montagny","Saint-Véran","Bouzeron"],"a":3,"exp":"Bouzeron, in the Côte Chalonnaise, is the one village name reserved for Aligoté. The white wines of Rully, Montagny and Saint-Véran are all Chardonnay."},{"id":"gt-wplpmf5d","q":"Two white Burgundies are poured side by side. One is light and lean, all lemon and green apple, with sharp acidity and no trace of a barrel; the other is rounder, with cream and toasted hazelnut. Which grape made the first?","opts":["Aligoté","Sauvignon Blanc","Chardonnay","Pinot Blanc"],"a":0,"exp":"Lean, sharp and lemony with no barrel note is the mark of Aligoté. Chardonnay is the rounder glass beside it, and Burgundy's Sauvignon Blanc, from Saint-Bris, would smell of cut grass and gooseberry."}],"sources":[{"title":"Bourgogne Wines: Bourgogne Aligoté","url":"https://www.bourgogne-wines.com/wine-and-terroir/bourgogne-and-its-appellations/bourgogne-aligote,2458,9253.html?args=Y29tcF9pZD0yMjc4JmFjdGlvbj12aWV3RmljaGUmaWQ9MjI4Jnw%3D"},{"title":"Bourgogne Wines: Bouzeron","url":"https://www.bourgogne-wines.com/wine-and-terroir/bourgogne-and-its-appellations/bouzeron,2458,9253.html?args=Y29tcF9pZD0yMjc4JmFjdGlvbj12aWV3RmljaGUmaWQ9MjU3Jnw%3D"},{"title":"Plantgrape, the French catalogue of vine varieties: Aligoté","url":"https://www.plantgrape.fr/en/varieties/fruit-varieties/7"},{"title":"Wine of Moldova: international grape varieties in Moldova","url":"https://wineofmoldova.com/en/international-grape-varieties-in-moldova/"}]},
"arneis": {"slug":"arneis","name":"Arneis","colour":"white","kind":"grape","status":"new","mode":"home","aliases":[],"notIn":[],"say":"ar-NAYSS","lines":{"s10":"Arneis is Piedmont's soft, dry white from the Roero hills: ripe pear, camomile and a gentle twist of almond.","s20":"Arneis comes from Roero, the sandy hills across the river from Barolo in Piedmont. It is a dry, rounded white of ripe pear, white peach and camomile, soft in acidity, with a faint bitterness of almond on the finish.","s45":"Arneis is said to mean little rascal in the dialect of Piedmont, and growers say the vine earns the name. Its home is Roero, the sandy hills across the river Tanaro from Barolo. It makes a dry white that tastes of ripe pear, white peach and camomile, with a faint bitterness of almond at the end. It is rounded, medium in body and soft in acidity. Made in steel without oak, it is meant to be enjoyed young. It suits cured meats, cold veal in tuna sauce or fresh pasta in butter. It once grew among the Nebbiolo vines and softened the red, which earned it the nickname white Barolo.","sale":"Arneis is what Piedmont pours before the Barolo: a dry, rounded white of ripe pear and camomile, soft where others are sharp. Start the table with it, beside the cured meats or the veal."},"glass":{"lead":"Ripe pear and white peach with camomile and acacia blossom, round and soft, ending on a faint bitter almond.","notes":[{"family":"orchard fruit","notes":["ripe pear","green apple"]},{"family":"stone fruit","notes":["white peach","apricot"]},{"family":"floral","notes":["camomile","acacia blossom","honeysuckle"]},{"family":"nutty","notes":["bitter almond","marzipan"]},{"family":"age","notes":["honey","hazelnut"]}],"ladder":[{"climate":"moderate","where":"Roero's sandy hills in Piedmont","notes":["ripe pear","white peach","camomile","bitter almond"]},{"climate":"warm","where":"A hot year or a late harvest in Roero","notes":["apricot","baked pear","honey"]}],"structure":"Dry. Acidity medium at most and often soft, alcohol moderate, body medium, with a rounded, faintly oily feel."},"worlds":{"home":{"home":"Roero, the sandy hills on the left bank of the Tanaro, across the river from Barolo and Barbaresco in Piedmont. Roero Arneis must be at least 95 per cent Arneis. It is made in steel, without oak, to be enjoyed young, and a little is made sparkling. Under the wider Langhe name, from either bank, it is Langhe Arneis.","abroad":"It has travelled a little. Australia grows it in Victoria and New South Wales, and Sonoma in California and Oregon's Willamette Valley have some, typically made in the same fresh, pear scented way."}},"service":{"temp":"45 to 50 degrees Fahrenheit, 7 to 10 degrees Celsius; too cold and the perfume closes.","glass":"A white wine glass.","decant":"No. A fresh white with no oak is poured straight from a cold bottle.","age":"Enjoy it within two or three years; its soft acidity does not favour long keeping."},"food":{"with":[{"dish":"Cold veal in tuna sauce","why":"the Piedmont match: a soft wine for a creamy sauce"},{"dish":"Cured meats and antipasti","why":"round pear fruit beside salt and fat"},{"dish":"Fresh egg pasta with butter and sage","why":"almond and pear sit well with butter"},{"dish":"Soft, young cheeses","why":"gentle acidity does not fight the cream"}]},"labels":[{"term":"Roero Arneis","means":"Arneis, at least 95 per cent, from the Roero hills north of Alba; a DOCG since 2004."},{"term":"Roero","means":"On its own, the zone's red wine, made from Nebbiolo; the white adds the word Arneis."},{"term":"Langhe Arneis","means":"The same grape under the wider Langhe name, which takes in the Langhe hills and much of Roero."},{"term":"Spumante","means":"A sparkling Roero Arneis, which the same rules allow."}],"guest":[{"q":"I have never heard of Arneis. What is it like?","a":"A dry, rounded white from Piedmont, the home of Barolo. Think ripe pear and white peach with a scent of camomile, soft in acidity, and a little almond at the end."},{"q":"Is it oaked?","a":"Rarely. It is made in steel to keep its pear and camomile, and its roundness comes from the grape, not from a barrel."},{"q":"The list also shows a Roero with no grape named. Is that the same wine?","a":"No. Roero on its own is the red, made from Nebbiolo. The white is labelled Roero Arneis, and it comes from the same sandy hills."}],"compare":[{"slug":"cortese","tell":"Cortese, as Gavi, is leaner and sharper, with lemon, green apple and high acidity; Arneis is rounder and softer, with ripe pear and almond."},{"slug":"pinot-blanc","tell":"Pinot Blanc is as soft but plainer, with apple and a creamy feel; Arneis is more scented, with ripe pear, camomile and a bitter almond finish."},{"slug":"vermentino","tell":"Vermentino is brisker and saltier, with lime and wild fennel; Arneis is softer and rounder, with ripe pear and camomile."}],"profile":{"c":"w","st":"Pale straw · Body medium · Acid medium minus to medium · Alcohol moderate · Dry, round and faintly oily","fruit":"Ripe pear, white peach, apricot; green apple from cooler sites and years","other":"Camomile, acacia blossom and honeysuckle; a faint bitter almond on the finish; no oak as a rule; honey and hazelnut with a few years","tell":"A rounded, softly scented dry white of ripe pear and camomile with gentle acidity and a bitter almond finish: Roero Arneis","regions":"Roero and the Langhe in Piedmont; a little in Australia, Sonoma and Oregon's Willamette Valley","confuse":"Cortese is leaner and sharper; Pinot Blanc is plainer and less scented; Vermentino is saltier and brisker; Viognier is far more perfumed and fuller"},"targets":["piedmont","italy","australia","willamette-valley","napa-sonoma"],"cards":[{"id":"gt-wo0ygufj","front":"Arneis: where is it at home, and what does the label say?","back":"Roero, the sandy hills on the left bank of the Tanaro, north of Alba in Piedmont. The wine is Roero Arneis, a DOCG since 2004, at least 95 per cent Arneis."},{"id":"gt-39l5w58l","front":"Why was Arneis once nicknamed white Barolo?","back":"It was planted among the Nebbiolo vines, and a little was blended into the red to soften it. Nebbiolo Bianco is another of its old names."},{"id":"gt-xj54sljb","front":"Arneis blind: what parts it from Cortese, Piedmont's other white?","back":"Arneis is rounder and softer, with ripe pear, camomile, bitter almond and gentle acidity. Cortese, as Gavi, is lean and sharp, with lemon and green apple."}],"questions":[{"id":"gt-qoh3aqdj","q":"A list shows two wines side by side, Roero and Roero Arneis. A guest asks how they differ. What do you tell her?","opts":["Roero is dry; Roero Arneis is the sweet version","Roero is the young wine; Roero Arneis is aged longer","They are one white wine under two names","Roero is a red from Nebbiolo; Roero Arneis is the white"],"a":3,"exp":"Roero on its own is the zone's red, made from Nebbiolo. The white carries the grape's name, Roero Arneis. It is no rule of age or of sweetness: both wines are dry."},{"id":"gt-4c4ebx40","q":"A blind white from Piedmont is dry, round and soft, with gentle acidity: ripe pear, white peach and camomile, a faint bitter almond finish and no oak. Which wine is it most likely to be?","opts":["Moscato d'Asti","Gavi di Gavi","Roero Arneis","Erbaluce di Caluso"],"a":2,"exp":"Soft acidity with ripe pear, camomile and bitter almond points to Arneis. Gavi would be leaner and sharper, with lemon and green apple, and Moscato d'Asti is sweet and lightly sparkling."},{"id":"gt-n51z6kzk","q":"A guest orders cold veal in tuna sauce to begin and asks for a soft, dry white from the dish's own region. Which do you suggest?","opts":["Fiano di Avellino","Roero Arneis","Soave Classico","Verdicchio di Matelica"],"a":1,"exp":"Vitello tonnato is a dish of Piedmont, and Roero Arneis is that region's rounded, gentle white. Soave is from the Veneto, Verdicchio from the Marche and Fiano from Campania."}],"sources":[{"title":"Regione Piemonte: the production rules of Roero DOCG","url":"https://www.regione.piemonte.it/governo/bollettino/abbonati/2009/38/attach/com01.pdf"},{"title":"Langhe.net: Roero Arneis DOCG","url":"https://langhe.net/en/wines/docg-roero-arneis/"},{"title":"Visit Langhe Monferrato Roero: Roero Arneis DOCG","url":"https://www.visitlmr.it/en/vini/roero-arneis-docg"},{"title":"The Oxford Companion to Wine","url":""}]},
"fiano": {"slug":"fiano","name":"Fiano","colour":"white","kind":"grape","status":"new","mode":"home","aliases":[],"notIn":[],"say":"fee-AH-noh","lines":{"s10":"Fiano is the waxy, nutty white of Campania's high hills: ripe pear, honeysuckle and toasted hazelnut, with real weight.","s20":"Fiano belongs to Irpinia, the cool hills around Avellino, inland from Naples. It is a dry white with body and a waxy feel, and it tastes of ripe pear, honeysuckle and hazelnut, turning to honey and toast with age.","s45":"Fiano is a southern white that behaves like a northern one. Its home is Irpinia, the cool, high hills inland from Naples. It tastes of ripe pear, honeysuckle and toasted hazelnut, with a trace of smoke. It is dry, medium to full in body and a little waxy, with fresh acidity. Most ferments in steel and rests on its yeast for texture, and it keeps: in ten years it turns to honey and toast. It has the weight for roast chicken, mushroom pasta or buffalo mozzarella. The Romans are said to have named it for the bees its sweet grapes drew, and the label may still say Apianum.","sale":"Fiano carries a main course without any help from new oak: ripe pear, hazelnut and a trace of smoke. I would serve it cool but not ice cold, with the roast chicken or a mushroom pasta."},"glass":{"lead":"Ripe pear and honeysuckle over toasted hazelnut and a trace of smoke, dry, waxy and medium to full in body.","notes":[{"family":"orchard fruit","notes":["ripe pear","yellow apple"]},{"family":"floral","notes":["honeysuckle","acacia blossom"]},{"family":"nutty","notes":["toasted hazelnut","almond"]},{"family":"mineral","notes":["a trace of smoke","wet stone"]},{"family":"honeyed","notes":["acacia honey","beeswax"]}],"ladder":[{"climate":"moderate","where":"The high hills of Irpinia, around Avellino","notes":["ripe pear","honeysuckle","toasted hazelnut","a trace of smoke"]},{"climate":"warm","where":"The Campanian coast, Puglia, Sicily and McLaren Vale","notes":["apricot","nectarine","honeydew melon"]}],"structure":"Dry. Acidity medium plus and fresh, alcohol moderate to medium plus, body medium to full, with a waxy, faintly oily texture."},"worlds":{"home":{"home":"Irpinia, the high hills around Avellino in Campania, 400 to 700 metres up, with cold winters and an October harvest. Fiano di Avellino must be at least 85 per cent Fiano, from twenty-six communes. It is made mostly in steel and rested on its lees, a little in used oak, and it gains honey and toast over a decade.","abroad":"Elsewhere in the south of Italy it grows on the Campanian coast, in Puglia and Sicily, with riper apricot and nectarine. In Australia it has found a second home in McLaren Vale, where it keeps its acidity in the heat."}},"service":{"temp":"50 to 55 degrees Fahrenheit, 10 to 13 degrees Celsius: cellar cool, not ice cold.","glass":"A white wine glass; a wider bowl for an older bottle.","decant":"Rarely. A mature or tightly wound bottle can open with a little air; taste a sample first.","age":"Good young, and a white that keeps: Fiano di Avellino can gain honey and toast over ten years."},"food":{"with":[{"dish":"Roast chicken","why":"the wine's weight matches the roast"},{"dish":"Pasta with mushrooms","why":"hazelnut and smoke meet the mushrooms"},{"dish":"Buffalo mozzarella","why":"Campania's own cheese: freshness against cream"},{"dish":"Crawfish étouffée","why":"body and fresh acidity carry the rich sauce"},{"dish":"Grilled swordfish or tuna","why":"a meaty fish wants a white with body"}]},"labels":[{"term":"Fiano di Avellino","means":"The DOCG of the hills around Avellino: at least 85 per cent Fiano, from twenty-six communes."},{"term":"Apianum","means":"The wine's Latin name, from the word for bee; the rules allow it on the label."},{"term":"Irpinia Fiano","means":"Fiano from the wider province of Avellino, outside the stricter rules of the DOCG."},{"term":"Riserva","means":"Fiano di Avellino held back longer before release, at least a year."},{"term":"Fiano Minutolo","means":"A separate, muscat scented grape of Puglia, properly Minutolo, despite the shared name."}],"guest":[{"q":"Is this a light white or a rich one?","a":"Between the two, and nearer rich. Fiano has body and a waxy feel, with ripe pear, honeysuckle and hazelnut, but it has fresh acidity and no new oak, so it does not feel heavy."},{"q":"I taste nuts and a little smoke. Is there oak in this?","a":"Usually none, or only old casks. The hazelnut and the trace of smoke belong to the grape and its hills, and they deepen as the wine ages."},{"q":"Can a white from so far south really be fresh?","a":"Yes. The vineyards sit high in the hills inland from Naples, where the nights are cold and the harvest is late, so the wine keeps its acidity."}],"compare":[{"slug":"chardonnay","tell":"Chardonnay without oak is cleaner and more neutral, with lemon and green apple; Fiano is waxier and more scented, with honeysuckle, hazelnut and a trace of smoke."},{"slug":"verdicchio","tell":"Verdicchio is leaner and sharper, with lemon, green almond and a bitter finish; Fiano is broader and waxier, with ripe pear and hazelnut."},{"slug":"vermentino","tell":"Vermentino is lighter and saltier, with lime and wild fennel; Fiano has more body and a waxy feel, with hazelnut and honey."}],"profile":{"c":"w","st":"Pale to medium lemon · Body medium to full · Acid medium plus · Alcohol moderate to medium plus · Dry, waxy in texture","fruit":"Ripe pear, yellow apple; apricot, nectarine and melon from the coast, Puglia, Sicily and McLaren Vale","other":"Honeysuckle and acacia blossom, toasted hazelnut and almond, a trace of smoke; acacia honey, beeswax and toast with age; usually no new oak","tell":"A dry southern Italian white with weight and a waxy feel, pear and honeysuckle over hazelnut and smoke, yet fresh in acidity: Fiano di Avellino","regions":"Fiano di Avellino and Irpinia in Campania; the Campanian coast, Puglia and Sicily; McLaren Vale in Australia","confuse":"Greco di Tufo is tauter, sharper and flintier; Chardonnay without oak is more neutral; Verdicchio is leaner, with a bitter almond finish; Fiano Minutolo is a separate, muscat scented grape"},"targets":["campania","puglia","sicily","mclaren-vale","italy","australia"],"cards":[{"id":"gt-gjpxlijk","front":"Fiano: where is it at home, and what are the rules?","back":"Irpinia in Campania. Fiano di Avellino, a DOCG since 2003, is at least 85 per cent Fiano from twenty-six communes around Avellino."},{"id":"gt-0odkg36i","front":"What does Apianum on a label of Fiano di Avellino mean?","back":"It is the wine's Latin name, from apis, the bee: the grape's sweetness is said to have drawn bees. The rules allow it on the label."},{"id":"gt-ou111h7j","front":"Fiano blind against Greco di Tufo, its neighbour: what parts them?","back":"Fiano is waxy and nutty, with pear, honeysuckle, hazelnut and weight. Greco di Tufo is tauter and higher in acidity, with bitter almond and a flinty edge."}],"questions":[{"id":"gt-sflcmfbf","q":"A blind Italian white is dry and medium to full in body, waxy in feel, with ripe pear, honeysuckle, toasted hazelnut and a trace of smoke, fresh acidity and no new oak. Which wine is it most likely to be?","opts":["Gavi di Gavi","Pinot Grigio delle Venezie","Fiano di Avellino","Soave Classico"],"a":2,"exp":"Weight, a waxy feel, hazelnut and smoke point to Fiano from Irpinia. Gavi and Pinot Grigio are lighter and leaner, and Soave is softer, with white peach and bitter almond."},{"id":"gt-euj29yf5","q":"A guest sees the word Apianum on a bottle from Campania and asks which grape it points to. What do you answer?","opts":["Fiano","Coda di Volpe","Greco","Falanghina"],"a":0,"exp":"Apianum is the Latin name allowed on Fiano di Avellino, from the word for bee. Greco di Tufo and Falanghina del Sannio are Campania's other white names and carry no such term."},{"id":"gt-il8ojkar","q":"A guest wants a white with enough body for roast chicken and mushrooms but dislikes the taste of new oak. Which bottle do you suggest?","opts":["White Rioja Reserva","Fiano di Avellino","Napa Valley Chardonnay","Meursault"],"a":1,"exp":"Fiano di Avellino takes its weight and waxy texture from the grape, usually with no new oak. Meursault, Napa Chardonnay and white Rioja Reserva are typically raised in barrel."}],"sources":[{"title":"Associazione Italiana Sommelier: the production rules of Fiano di Avellino DOCG","url":"https://aisitalia.it/Disciplinari/Campania/DOCG/Fiano%20di%20Avellino.pdf"},{"title":"Regione Campania: the production rules of Fiano di Avellino DOCG","url":"https://agricoltura.regione.campania.it/pubblicazioni/pdf/DOCGFIA1.PDF"},{"title":"Italian Wine Central: Fiano di Avellino DOCG","url":"https://italianwinecentral.com/denomination/fiano-di-avellino-docg/"},{"title":"The Oxford Companion to Wine","url":""}]},
"grenache-blanc": {"slug":"grenache-blanc","name":"Grenache Blanc","colour":"white","kind":"grape","status":"new","mode":"home","aliases":["Garnacha Blanca"],"notIn":[],"say":"gruh-NASH BLAHN, in Spain gar-NAH-chah BLAHN-kah","lines":{"s10":"Grenache Blanc is the white form of Grenache: green apple, pear and fennel on a full, round body with a warm finish.","s20":"Grenache Blanc is the pale skinned sibling of red Grenache, grown across the south of France and north east Spain. It gives a full, round white of green apple, pear and fennel, usually in a blend and now more often alone.","s45":"Grenache Blanc gives the white blends of the Mediterranean their body, from Terra Alta in Catalonia to Châteauneuf-du-Pape in the Rhône. It tastes of green apple, ripe pear and mandarin, with a hint of fennel. It is full and round, with soft acidity and a warm finish. France mostly blends it for weight, while Terra Alta and California's Central Coast often bottle it alone, and cold nights in Paso Robles keep it crisp. Pour it with roast chicken, grilled fish or garlic shrimp. It began as red Grenache: a vine whose berries lost their colour, and growers kept it.","sale":"Grenache Blanc is red Grenache that lost its colour and kept its body: green apple, pear and a twist of fennel. With the grilled fish or the garlic shrimp, its weight stands up to olive oil and garlic."},"glass":{"lead":"Green apple, ripe pear and mandarin with fennel and a touch of honeysuckle, on a full, round body with a warm finish.","notes":[{"family":"orchard fruit","notes":["green apple","ripe pear","quince"]},{"family":"citrus","notes":["mandarin","lemon zest"]},{"family":"stone fruit","notes":["white peach","apricot"]},{"family":"spice","notes":["fennel seed","aniseed","liquorice"]},{"family":"floral","notes":["honeysuckle","acacia blossom"]},{"family":"nutty","notes":["bitter almond","walnut"]}],"ladder":[{"climate":"moderate","where":"Terra Alta's high plateau; the cooler western hills of Paso Robles","notes":["green apple","mandarin","white peach"]},{"climate":"warm","where":"Châteauneuf-du-Pape, Roussillon, Priorat and the Swartland","notes":["ripe pear","quince","aniseed","bitter almond"]}],"structure":"Dry. Acidity low to medium, alcohol high, body full, with a round, slightly oily texture."},"worlds":{"home":{"home":"The southern Rhône, Roussillon, the Languedoc and Catalonia, where heat ripens it to a full, soft white of ripe pear, quince and fennel with a bitter almond finish. Mostly it is blended for weight, in steel or old casks; Terra Alta bottles it alone, fresher from its high plateau, and Roussillon also fortifies it.","abroad":"California's Central Coast, above all Paso Robles, often bottles it alone, typically crisper, with green apple and mandarin, as cold nights hold its acidity. In the Swartland and Paarl it joins Chenin Blanc in Rhône style white blends."}},"service":{"temp":"50 to 55 degrees Fahrenheit, 10 to 13 degrees Celsius; a crisp unoaked bottle a little cooler.","glass":"A white wine glass; a wider bowl for an oak aged or older wine.","decant":"Usually unnecessary; open it and pour.","age":"Most within two to four years, while the fruit is fresh; aged Rivesaltes is a different, fortified wine."},"food":{"with":[{"dish":"Grilled redfish with olive oil and herbs","why":"body for the oil, fennel to meet the herbs"},{"dish":"Garlic shrimp or prawns","why":"a round white that stands up to garlic"},{"dish":"Roast chicken with lemon","why":"weight for the bird, apple and mandarin to lift it"},{"dish":"Paella or saffron rice with shellfish","why":"body and soft acidity suit saffron and shellfish"}]},"labels":[{"term":"Garnacha Blanca, Garnatxa Blanca","means":"The grape's Spanish and Catalan names, on bottles from Terra Alta, Priorat and Rioja."},{"term":"Terra Alta Garnatxa Blanca","means":"A guarantee mark of the Terra Alta region for whites made wholly from this grape."},{"term":"Châteauneuf-du-Pape Blanc","means":"The southern Rhône white, where Grenache Blanc gives weight beside Clairette, Roussanne and Bourboulenc."},{"term":"Rivesaltes Ambré","means":"A fortified sweet wine of Roussillon from white grapes, Grenache Blanc among them, aged until amber and nutty."}],"guest":[{"q":"Is this related to the red Grenache?","a":"Yes, it is the same vine in a paler skin. Grenache Blanc is a colour mutation of red Grenache, and it shares its liking for heat and its generous body and alcohol."},{"q":"I usually drink Chardonnay. What is this like?","a":"Similar in weight, with no butter. Expect green apple, pear and a little fennel, a round feel and a warm finish, usually with little or no new oak."},{"q":"Is Garnacha Blanca the same grape?","a":"The same. Garnacha Blanca is its Spanish name and Garnatxa Blanca its Catalan one, and Terra Alta in Catalonia is where you will most often see it bottled alone."}],"compare":[{"slug":"roussanne","tell":"Roussanne is more perfumed, with honeysuckle and chamomile tea and a fresher finish; Grenache Blanc is plainer, broader and warmer, with green apple and fennel."},{"slug":"viognier","tell":"Viognier is loudly scented with apricot and honeysuckle; Grenache Blanc is quiet on the nose, with green apple, pear and fennel."},{"slug":"macabeo","tell":"Macabeo, the Viura of Rioja, is lighter and leaner, with less alcohol; Grenache Blanc is fuller, rounder and warmer on the finish."}],"profile":{"c":"w","st":"Pale straw to gold · Body full · Acid low to medium · Alcohol high · Dry, or fortified and sweet as Rivesaltes","fruit":"Green apple, ripe pear, mandarin, white peach; quince from warm sites","other":"Fennel, aniseed and liquorice, honeysuckle, a bitter almond finish; walnut where it is aged in air; a round, slightly oily feel","tell":"A full, round, warm white that is quiet on the nose: green apple, pear and fennel, with a faint bitter almond finish","regions":"Southern Rhône (Châteauneuf-du-Pape blanc), Roussillon and the Languedoc; Terra Alta, Priorat and Rioja as Garnacha Blanca; California's Central Coast; the Swartland","confuse":"Roussanne and Viognier are more perfumed; Macabeo is lighter and leaner; Marsanne is waxier, with almond and honeysuckle; Chardonnay has more acidity and a cellar accent"},"targets":["rhone","languedoc-roussillon","priorat","rioja","central-coast","swartland","paarl"],"cards":[{"id":"gt-gz08ck2o","front":"Grenache Blanc: what is its Spanish name, and its Catalan stronghold?","back":"Garnacha Blanca, in Catalan Garnatxa Blanca. Terra Alta, inland in the south of Catalonia, bottles it alone under a guarantee mark of its own."},{"id":"gt-shwm3n0y","front":"In the Swartland, which grape leads the white blends Grenache Blanc joins?","back":"Chenin Blanc, with Clairette, Roussanne and Viognier beside it, fermented and aged in old casks or concrete: waxy, nutty and dry, the Cape's answer to a white Châteauneuf."},{"id":"gt-sjmlvrgy","front":"How is Grenache Blanc related to red Grenache, and when did it reach California?","back":"It is a colour mutation of red Grenache. It reached California in 1992 as cuttings from Châteauneuf-du-Pape, and was bottled there under its own name about ten years later."}],"questions":[{"id":"gt-izlqfcid","q":"A guest orders a white Priorat, rich and stony with ripe pear and fennel, and asks which grape leads the blend. Which do you name?","opts":["Garnacha Blanca","Parellada","Macabeu","Pedro Ximénez"],"a":0,"exp":"Garnacha Blanca is the lead white grape of Priorat, for rich, stony, textured whites. Macabeu and Pedro Ximénez are its partners there, and Parellada belongs to the Penedès and Cava."},{"id":"gt-9b1lxxiw","q":"A guest asks which grape gives a white Châteauneuf-du-Pape much of its weight and roundness. Which is it?","opts":["Picpoul","Grenache Blanc","Sauvignon Blanc","Chenin Blanc"],"a":1,"exp":"Grenache Blanc brings body, roundness and alcohol to the southern Rhône's white blends. Picpoul is permitted there too, but it is grown for sharp acidity, not for weight."},{"id":"gt-bmyszsfn","q":"After dinner a guest orders a fortified sweet wine from Roussillon, made from white grapes and aged for years in contact with air until nutty. Which is it?","opts":["Muscat de Beaumes-de-Venise","Banyuls Rimage","Maury Grenat","Rivesaltes Ambré"],"a":3,"exp":"Rivesaltes Ambré is made from white grapes such as Grenache Blanc and aged in air until amber and nutty. Rimage and Grenat are young, fruit led reds, and Beaumes-de-Venise is in the Rhône."}],"sources":[{"title":"Inter Rhône: Grenache blanc, the grape variety","url":"https://www.vins-rhone.com/en/grenache-blanc-grape-variety"},{"title":"Catalan Institute of Vine and Wine: Terra Alta, the rule of the denomination","url":"https://incavi.gencat.cat/content/dam/incavi/home/normativa/plecs-de-condicions-de-les-do-catalanes/do-terra-alta/dop-terra-alta-modificacio-plec-condicions.pdf"},{"title":"Foods and Wines from Spain: Garnacha Blanca goes solo at last","url":"https://www.foodswinesfromspain.com/en/wine/articles/2018/march/garnacha-blanca-goes-solo-at-last"},{"title":"Rhône Rangers: more about Grenache Blanc","url":"https://rhonerangers.org/wine-education/white-grapes/more-about-grenache-blanc"}]},
"macabeo": {"slug":"macabeo","name":"Macabeo","colour":"white","kind":"grape","status":"new","mode":"home","aliases":["Viura","Macabeu"],"notIn":[],"say":"mah-kah-BEH-oh, in Rioja vee-OO-rah","lines":{"s10":"Macabeo is the quiet white grape of Cava and, as Viura, of white Rioja: green apple, pear and a note of almond.","s20":"Macabeo is a white grape of northern Spain, called Viura in Rioja, and it is one of the three grapes of Cava. It gives a fresh, gentle wine of green apple, pear and lemon, and a golden, nutty one after years in cask.","s45":"Macabeo stands behind two Spanish wines you know. It is one of the three grapes of Cava, from Catalonia, and in Rioja, as Viura, it leads the white wine. It tastes of green apple, pear and lemon, with a hint of almond. It is dry and fairly light, with gentle acidity. As Cava it gets its bubbles in the bottle, the way Champagne does, and the still wines are mostly made fresh in steel. Fried fish, cured ham and grilled vegetables all suit it. A few old cellars in Rioja still age it for years in cask, until it turns golden and tastes of hazelnut and beeswax.","sale":"Macabeo is one of the three grapes of Cava, a sparkling wine made in the bottle as Champagne is, dry and fresh with green apple. Pour it with anything salty or fried, and save an aged white Rioja for the roast chicken."},"glass":{"lead":"Green apple, pear and lemon with camomile and bitter almond, light and gentle; with cask age, hazelnut and beeswax.","notes":[{"family":"orchard fruit","notes":["green apple","ripe pear","yellow apple"]},{"family":"citrus","notes":["lemon zest","grapefruit"]},{"family":"floral","notes":["camomile","acacia blossom"]},{"family":"nutty","notes":["bitter almond","hazelnut"]},{"family":"lees and cellar","notes":["bread dough","toast"]},{"family":"age","notes":["beeswax","honey","toffee"]}],"ladder":[{"climate":"moderate","where":"Rioja Alta and the central basin of the Penedès","notes":["green apple","lemon zest","camomile"]},{"climate":"warm","where":"La Mancha, the coastal Penedès and Roussillon","notes":["ripe pear","yellow apple","bitter almond"]}],"structure":"Dry. Acidity medium, brighter from Rioja's higher ground and gentler from warm sites, alcohol moderate, body light to medium; as Cava, a fine and steady sparkle."},"worlds":{"home":{"home":"Northern Spain. In Catalonia it is one of the three grapes of Cava, with Xarel·lo and Parellada, picked early and fermented a second time in the bottle. In Rioja, as Viura, it leads the white wines: mostly fresh from steel, and in the old style aged for years in cask until golden and nutty.","abroad":"Across the Pyrenees, in Roussillon, it is Macabeu, a grape of the dry white blends and of the amber, fortified Rivesaltes. It is rarely grown outside Spain and the south of France."}},"service":{"temp":"43 to 46 degrees Fahrenheit, 6 to 8 degrees Celsius, as Cava; a cask aged white Rioja warmer.","glass":"A tulip for Cava; a white wine glass for still wines, a wider bowl for cask aged Rioja.","decant":"No for Cava. An old, cask aged white Rioja can open with a little air; taste first.","age":"Cava and young whites within two or three years; cask aged white Rioja can keep ten years and more."},"food":{"with":[{"dish":"Fried fish or calamari","why":"bubbles and freshness cut through the frying"},{"dish":"Cured ham and salted almonds","why":"a dry, gentle wine beside salt and fat"},{"dish":"Grilled vegetables with romesco","why":"almond in the sauce meets almond in the wine"},{"dish":"Roast chicken","why":"an aged white Rioja brings weight and a nutty depth"}]},"labels":[{"term":"Viura","means":"The grape's name in Rioja, Navarra and Rueda."},{"term":"Cava","means":"Spanish sparkling wine made in the bottle, classically from Macabeo, Xarel·lo and Parellada."},{"term":"Rioja Blanco","means":"White Rioja, led by Viura: fresh and young, or aged in oak as Crianza, Reserva or Gran Reserva."},{"term":"Macabeu","means":"The grape's Catalan and French name, in Roussillon's dry whites and in Rivesaltes."},{"term":"Brut Nature","means":"Cava with under three grams of sugar a litre and none added: drier than Brut."}],"guest":[{"q":"Is Cava just Spanish Champagne?","a":"It is made the same way, with the second fermentation in the bottle, but from Spanish grapes, Macabeo among them. It tends to taste of green apple and almond, a little softer than Champagne."},{"q":"The label says Viura. Is that Macabeo?","a":"Yes, the same grape. Rioja calls it Viura and Catalonia calls it Macabeo, so the still white of one region and the sparkling wine of the other share a grape."},{"q":"Why is this white Rioja so golden?","a":"It was aged for years in oak casks, the traditional way. Slow contact with air turns it gold and gives hazelnut, beeswax and honey. It is meant to taste like that; it is not spoiled."}],"compare":[{"slug":"glera","tell":"Glera, as Prosecco, gets its bubbles in a tank and tastes of pear and acacia blossom, soft and often off dry; Macabeo, as Cava, is drier, with toast and almond."},{"slug":"grenache-blanc","tell":"Grenache Blanc is fuller, rounder and warmer on the finish; Macabeo, the Viura of Rioja, is lighter and leaner, with less alcohol."},{"slug":"verdejo","tell":"Verdejo is louder and greener, with lime, fennel and a bitter finish; Macabeo is quieter and more neutral, with green apple and almond."}],"profile":{"c":"w","st":"Pale lemon, gold with cask age · Body light to medium · Acid medium · Alcohol moderate · Dry, still or sparkling","fruit":"Green apple, ripe pear, lemon zest; yellow apple from warm sites","other":"Camomile, acacia blossom and bitter almond; bread dough and toast as Cava; hazelnut, beeswax and honey after long cask ageing","tell":"A quiet, gentle white of green apple and almond: the freshness in a Cava blend, or the golden, nutty frame of an aged white Rioja","regions":"Catalonia (Cava and Penedès); Rioja, Navarra and Rueda as Viura; La Mancha; Roussillon as Macabeu","confuse":"Verdejo is greener and more bitter; Grenache Blanc is fuller and warmer; Glera, as Prosecco, is softer, with acacia blossom; Chardonnay is weightier, with butter or vanilla from the cellar"},"targets":["penedes","rioja","navarra","rueda-toro","la-mancha","languedoc-roussillon","spain"],"cards":[{"id":"gt-divfqujl","front":"Macabeo: what is it called in Rioja, and what does it do there?","back":"Viura. It is the main grape of white Rioja: fresh and neutral when young, golden, nutty and waxy after long ageing in oak."},{"id":"gt-hpykel5h","front":"Which three grapes make the classic Cava blend, and what is Macabeo's part?","back":"Macabeo, Xarel·lo and Parellada. Macabeo brings fruit and apple freshness. The wine ferments a second time in the bottle and rests at least nine months on its lees."},{"id":"gt-ujiwpe0k","front":"Macabeo blind, as Cava: what parts it from Prosecco?","back":"Toast and almond from time on its lees in the bottle, and a drier finish. Prosecco, made in a tank from Glera, is softer, with pear and acacia blossom and no bready note."}],"questions":[{"id":"gt-l5nc965p","q":"A guest who enjoyed a white Rioja labelled Viura asks which sparkling wine is made from the same grape. What do you bring?","opts":["Crémant d'Alsace","Prosecco","Cava","Franciacorta"],"a":2,"exp":"Viura is Rioja's name for Macabeo, one of the three classic grapes of Cava with Xarel·lo and Parellada. Prosecco is made from Glera, and Franciacorta mostly from Chardonnay and Pinot Noir."},{"id":"gt-unhtba1k","q":"A blind white is deep gold and dry, with hazelnut, beeswax, honey and dried apple over bright acidity, the mark of years in old oak casks. Which wine is it most likely to be?","opts":["White Rioja Gran Reserva","Vinho Verde Alvarinho","Rueda Verdejo","Rías Baixas Albariño"],"a":0,"exp":"Gold colour with hazelnut, beeswax and honey points to Viura aged for years in cask, the old style of white Rioja. Albariño and Verdejo are pale, fresh and usually unoaked."},{"id":"gt-h148sqf6","q":"A guest asks how Cava gets its bubbles. What do you tell him?","opts":["Carbon dioxide added before bottling","One fermentation finished in the bottle","A second fermentation in a sealed tank","A second fermentation in the bottle"],"a":3,"exp":"Cava is made by the traditional method: a second fermentation in the bottle and at least nine months on its lees. The sealed tank is the method of Prosecco."}],"sources":[{"title":"Cava Regulatory Board: the authorised grape varieties","url":"https://www.cava.wine/en/origin-cava/authorised-grape-varieties/"},{"title":"Consejo Regulador Rioja: Viura","url":"https://riojawine.com/en/the-designation/grape-varieties/viura/"},{"title":"Foods and Wines from Spain: the rebirth of white Rioja","url":"https://www.foodswinesfromspain.com/en/wine/articles/2020/july/white-wines-rioja"}]},
"muller-thurgau": {"slug":"muller-thurgau","name":"Müller-Thurgau","colour":"white","kind":"grape","status":"new","mode":"home","aliases":[],"notIn":[],"say":"MEW-ler TOOR-gow","lines":{"s10":"Müller-Thurgau is Germany's easy white: light and soft, with apple, peach and elderflower, and a faint whiff of nutmeg.","s20":"Müller-Thurgau is a German crossing, bred in 1882 to ripen early in a cool climate. It makes a light, soft white for drinking young, with apple, white peach and a gentle Muscat scent.","s45":"Müller-Thurgau was made on purpose. A Swiss botanist bred it in Germany in 1882, hoping for Riesling's flavour in a vine that ripens early. It grows all over Germany, and high in the Italian Alps. It tastes of apple and white peach, with a scent of elderflower and a faint note of nutmeg. It is light and soft, with mild acidity, and it is made in steel and bottled young. Pour it cold, with salads, mild cheese or a simple plate of fish. For a century its parents were thought to be Riesling and Silvaner, until DNA named a table grape, Madeleine Royale, in Silvaner's place.","sale":"Müller-Thurgau is the uncomplicated one: light, soft and gently scented, with nothing to puzzle over. I would pour it cold at the start, with the salads and the lighter plates, for anyone who finds Riesling too sharp."},"glass":{"lead":"Apple, white peach and elderflower with a faint nutmeg note, light, soft and mild, made to be enjoyed young.","notes":[{"family":"orchard fruit","notes":["yellow apple","ripe pear","Muscat grape"]},{"family":"stone fruit","notes":["white peach","apricot"]},{"family":"floral","notes":["elderflower","lilac"]},{"family":"spice","notes":["nutmeg","mace"]}],"ladder":[{"climate":"cool","where":"Alto Adige's high slopes, Franken, Rheinhessen and the Mosel","notes":["green apple","lemon","fresh hay"]},{"climate":"moderate","where":"Baden and the Pfalz, Germany's warmer south west","notes":["yellow apple","white peach","ripe pear"]}],"structure":"Dry to off dry. Acidity mild, low to medium; alcohol low to moderate; body light to medium. No oak."},"worlds":{"home":{"home":"Germany, across the country, and widely in Rheinhessen, Baden and Franken. Bred at Geisenheim in 1882 by Hermann Müller, of the Swiss canton of Thurgau, it ripens early where Riesling struggles. Most is made in steel, light and soft, and sold young; dry wines are often labelled Rivaner.","abroad":"Alto Adige and Trentino grow it high in the mountains for a fresher, more scented wine. Switzerland calls it Riesling-Silvaner, and Luxembourg and Austria call it Rivaner."}},"service":{"temp":"45 to 50 degrees Fahrenheit, 7 to 10 degrees Celsius: well chilled, as a light aromatic white.","glass":"A white wine glass, kept small so the wine stays cold.","decant":"No: pour it cold and young, straight from the bottle.","age":"Enjoy it young, within a year or two of the vintage; freshness is its charm."},"food":{"with":[{"dish":"Green salads and spring vegetables","why":"a light, mild wine that does not overwhelm them"},{"dish":"Mild fresh cheeses","why":"soft acidity and gentle fruit suit a mild cheese"},{"dish":"Trout or other simply cooked fish","why":"light body and quiet fruit let the fish lead"},{"dish":"Cold cuts on a picnic table","why":"easy, soft and happy to be served cold"}]},"labels":[{"term":"Rivaner","means":"The name German growers often give a dry, fresh Müller-Thurgau; also used in Luxembourg and Austria."},{"term":"Riesling-Silvaner","means":"The Swiss name, from the parents it was long thought to have."},{"term":"Liebfraumilch","means":"A mild, lightly sweet German blend in which Müller-Thurgau is one of the permitted main grapes."}],"guest":[{"q":"Why the long name?","a":"It is the breeder's name and his home: Hermann Müller, a botanist from the Swiss canton of Thurgau, bred the grape at Geisenheim in Germany in 1882."},{"q":"Is it like Riesling?","a":"It is a child of Riesling, but gentler: lighter, softer and lower in acidity, with a scent of elderflower and a faint Muscat note, and meant for drinking young, not for keeping."},{"q":"Is it sweet?","a":"It can be either. Bottles labelled trocken are dry and Rivaner usually is; others carry a little sweetness, and it is one of the grapes of soft, sweetish Liebfraumilch."}],"compare":[{"slug":"riesling","tell":"Riesling, its parent, has piercing acidity, lime and long life; Müller-Thurgau is softer and lighter, with a scent of elderflower and nutmeg, and fades sooner."},{"slug":"silvaner","tell":"Silvaner is firmer and more savoury, with pear, hay and damp earth; Müller-Thurgau is lighter and more scented, with elderflower and nutmeg."},{"slug":"muscat-blanc","tell":"Muscat smells loudly of fresh grapes and orange blossom; Müller-Thurgau carries only a faint Muscat note over apple and peach."}],"profile":{"c":"w","st":"Pale straw · Body light to medium · Acid low to medium · Alcohol low to moderate · Dry to off dry; no oak","fruit":"Yellow apple, ripe pear and white peach; lemon and green apple from high, cool sites; a light Muscat grape note","other":"Elderflower, lilac and a faint nutmeg note; fresh hay from mountain vineyards; soft, mild and made to be enjoyed young","tell":"Light and soft, with elderflower, mild acidity and a faint nutmeg or Muscat note: Riesling's shape without its acidity or length","regions":"Throughout Germany, widely in Rheinhessen, Baden and Franken; Alto Adige and Trentino at altitude; Switzerland (as Riesling-Silvaner); Luxembourg and Austria (as Rivaner)","confuse":"Riesling has far higher acidity, lime and petrol with age; Silvaner is firmer and more savoury; Muscat is much more loudly grapey"},"targets":["germany","rheinhessen","baden","franken","pfalz","mosel","trentino-alto-adige","switzerland"],"cards":[{"id":"gt-tckepmjb","front":"Müller-Thurgau: who bred it, where and when?","back":"Hermann Müller, a botanist from the Swiss canton of Thurgau, at Geisenheim in Germany in 1882. He wanted Riesling's character in a vine that ripens early."},{"id":"gt-zzjxrji1","front":"Müller-Thurgau: its true parents, and the old mistake?","back":"Riesling and Madeleine Royale, a table grape, as DNA work showed. For a century it was taken for Riesling crossed with Silvaner, hence Rivaner and Riesling-Silvaner."},{"id":"gt-4f3ujp8n","front":"Müller-Thurgau in the glass: what should a taster expect?","back":"A pale, light, soft white with mild acidity: apple, white peach and elderflower with a faint nutmeg or Muscat note, for drinking young."}],"questions":[{"id":"gt-139xxbb5","q":"A guest sees Rivaner on a German list and asks which grape it is. What do you answer?","opts":["Riesling","Müller-Thurgau","Silvaner","Pinot Blanc"],"a":1,"exp":"Rivaner is a name for Müller-Thurgau, usually on a dry, fresh wine. It was coined from Riesling and Silvaner, once thought to be the parents; DNA showed Riesling and Madeleine Royale."},{"id":"gt-hvb8hbek","q":"A guest asks where the name Müller-Thurgau comes from. What do you tell her?","opts":["The German words for miller and valley","Two neighbouring villages on the river Main","The man who bred it and his home canton","A mill and a monastery in the Rheingau"],"a":2,"exp":"Hermann Müller, from the Swiss canton of Thurgau, bred the grape at Geisenheim in 1882, and it carries his name and his home. It is no place in Germany."},{"id":"gt-0pm62ucj","q":"A guest asks for a fresher, more scented Müller-Thurgau from high mountain vineyards. From which region do you choose it?","opts":["Baden","The Pfalz","Rheinhessen","Alto Adige"],"a":3,"exp":"Alto Adige grows Müller-Thurgau on airy slopes up to about 1,000 metres, which keeps it fresh and scented. Rheinhessen, the Pfalz and Baden are lower and warmer."}],"sources":[{"title":"Wines of Germany: Müller-Thurgau","url":"https://www.winesofgermany.com/our-wine/grape-varieties/grape-variety/104/m%C3%BCller-thurgau"},{"title":"Alto Adige Wines: Müller-Thurgau in Alto Adige","url":"https://www.altoadigewines.com/en/wine-varieties/mueller-thurgau"},{"title":"wein.plus Glossary: Rivaner","url":"https://glossary.wein.plus/rivaner"},{"title":"German Wines USA: five German bred wine crossings to try","url":"https://germanwineusa.com/5-to-try-german-bred-wine-crossings/"}]},
"pedro-ximenez": {"slug":"pedro-ximenez","name":"Pedro Ximénez","colour":"white","kind":"grape","status":"new","mode":"home","aliases":["PX"],"notIn":[],"say":"PEH-droh hee-MEH-neth","lines":{"s10":"Pedro Ximénez is the black, syrupy sweet Sherry of sun dried grapes: raisin, fig and coffee, poured small after dinner.","s20":"Pedro Ximénez is a white grape of Andalusia, dried in the sun until it is nearly a raisin. It makes a black, very sweet wine that tastes of raisin, fig and dark chocolate, for a small glass at the end of the meal.","s45":"Pedro Ximénez pours like syrup and tastes of raisins. It is a white grape of southern Spain, at home in the hot hills of Montilla near Córdoba, and it gives Sherry its black, sweet wine. Expect raisin, dried fig and date, then coffee, dark chocolate and liquorice. It is very sweet, thick and velvety. The grapes are laid in the sun on mats until they shrivel, and the juice is strengthened with grape spirit and aged for years in cask. Pour a small glass over vanilla ice cream, or beside blue cheese. Legend says a soldier brought it from the Rhine, but it is most likely Andalusian.","sale":"Pedro Ximénez is the dessert you drink: raisin, fig and dark chocolate, thick enough to coat the glass. A little goes a long way with the bananas Foster, or simply over a scoop of vanilla ice cream."},"glass":{"lead":"Raisin, dried fig and date with coffee, dark chocolate and liquorice, black, thick and very sweet.","notes":[{"family":"dried fruit","notes":["raisin","dried fig","date","prune"]},{"family":"honeyed","notes":["treacle","grape syrup","honey"]},{"family":"age","notes":["roast coffee","dark chocolate","liquorice"]}],"ladder":[{"climate":"moderate","where":"Jerez and Sanlúcar: hot too, but damper and cooled by the Atlantic","notes":["raisin","dried fig"]},{"climate":"warm","where":"Montilla-Moriles, inland, hotter and drier, where the grape ripens further","notes":["raisin","date","grape syrup"]}],"structure":"Very sweet. Acidity low to medium, alcohol high from fortification, body full, thick and syrupy."},"worlds":{"home":{"home":"Andalusia. The grape is most at home in Montilla-Moriles, in the hot hills south of Córdoba, where it also makes dry wines aged under flor, usually with no added spirit. For the sweet wine the bunches are dried in the sun on mats, pressed, fortified and aged in a solera; Jerez buys in much of its must.","abroad":"Málaga, along the coast, also makes sweet wines from it. Beyond Andalusia it is scarce, and Argentina's Pedro Giménez, for all its name, is a different grape."}},"service":{"temp":"54 to 57 degrees Fahrenheit, 12 to 14 degrees Celsius: lightly chilled, never ice cold.","glass":"A copita or a small white wine glass, for a pour of about 60 millilitres.","decant":"No. It is bottled ready to pour.","age":"Bottled ready to drink after years in cask; it keeps for years unopened and for weeks once open."},"food":{"with":[{"dish":"Vanilla ice cream","why":"poured over the top, the wine is the sauce"},{"dish":"Blue cheese","why":"salt and bite against deep sweetness"},{"dish":"Bananas Foster","why":"raisin and treacle meet the caramel and the rum"},{"dish":"Dark chocolate tart","why":"bitter cocoa steadies the sugar"}]},"labels":[{"term":"PX","means":"The usual short name for Pedro Ximénez, on lists and labels."},{"term":"Montilla-Moriles","means":"A denomination near Córdoba, apart from Sherry, where the grape is most at home."},{"term":"Vino dulce natural","means":"A naturally sweet wine: its sugar comes from the sun dried grapes, not from blending."},{"term":"Cream","means":"A sweetened Oloroso, often sweetened with Pedro Ximénez: softer and lighter than PX itself."},{"term":"VOS, VORS","means":"Certified average ages of twenty and thirty years in the solera."}],"guest":[{"q":"It looks black. Is it a red wine?","a":"It is a white grape. The colour comes from drying the grapes in the sun until they are nearly raisins, and then from years in cask, which turn the wine from amber to almost black."},{"q":"How sweet is it?","a":"Very: at least 212 grams of sugar a litre, and often far more. That is why I pour a small glass, as dessert itself or over vanilla ice cream."},{"q":"Is it a Sherry?","a":"It can be. The grape makes the sweet Sherry called PX, though much of the fruit is grown in Montilla-Moriles, a separate region near Córdoba that also bottles its own."}],"compare":[{"slug":"palomino","tell":"Palomino makes the dry Sherries, pale Fino to dark Oloroso, with almond or walnut; Pedro Ximénez is the sweet one, black and syrupy, with raisin and fig."},{"slug":"muscat-blanc","tell":"Rutherglen Muscat keeps a grapey lift of orange peel over its raisin and toffee, amber to brown; Pedro Ximénez is blacker and thicker, with fig, coffee and liquorice."}],"profile":{"c":"w","st":"Mahogany to opaque black · Body full and syrupy · Acid low to medium · Alcohol high, fortified · Very sweet, from sun dried grapes","fruit":"Raisin, dried fig, date and prune; grape syrup","other":"Treacle and honey; roast coffee, dark chocolate and liquorice with age; a thick, velvety texture","tell":"Black, syrupy and intensely sweet: raisin and fig from white grapes dried in the sun, with coffee and liquorice from long ageing in air","regions":"Montilla-Moriles in Córdoba, its home; Jerez, which ages wine from Montilla's must; Málaga","confuse":"Rutherglen Muscat keeps a grapey lift and is amber to brown; Cream Sherry is a sweetened Oloroso, lighter and less sweet; Palomino makes the dry Sherries"},"targets":["jerez","spain"],"cards":[{"id":"gt-28eoipem","front":"Pedro Ximénez: how are the grapes treated before pressing, and why?","back":"They are laid on mats in the sun for a week or more, a step called asoleo, until nearly raisins. The drying concentrates the sugar for a black, syrupy sweet wine."},{"id":"gt-duk4hag6","front":"Where is most Pedro Ximénez grown, and how does it reach Jerez?","back":"In Montilla-Moriles, a separate denomination near Córdoba, where it ripens more reliably. It is brought to Jerez as must, under a long standing permission."},{"id":"gt-7q12q4st","front":"How much sugar must a Pedro Ximénez Sherry hold, and how is it served?","back":"At least 212 grams a litre. Serve a small pour, about 60 millilitres, lightly chilled, at 54 to 57 degrees Fahrenheit, 12 to 14 degrees Celsius."}],"questions":[{"id":"gt-y0axzc6f","q":"A guest wants a sweet Sherry to pour over vanilla ice cream: black, thick and tasting of raisin and fig. Which do you bring?","opts":["Pale Cream","Moscatel","Pedro Ximénez","Dry Oloroso"],"a":2,"exp":"Pedro Ximénez, from sun dried grapes, is the black, syrupy one, with raisin and fig. Moscatel is sweet too, but grapey and scented with orange, and an Oloroso is dark but dry."},{"id":"gt-vlxxaldd","q":"A blind wine is opaque brown black and syrupy, intensely sweet, with raisin, dried fig, date, coffee and liquorice, the warmth of added spirit and no scent of fresh grape. Which is it most likely to be?","opts":["Pedro Ximénez","Vintage Port","Rutherglen Muscat","Tokaji Aszú"],"a":0,"exp":"Black colour, a syrupy body and raisin, fig and coffee mark Pedro Ximénez, from sun dried white grapes. Rutherglen Muscat is amber to brown and keeps the perfume of the Muscat grape."},{"id":"gt-dvz7ec5x","q":"A guest reads that the grapes for her sweet Sherry were grown inland, not at Jerez. Which region grows much of the Pedro Ximénez that Jerez ages?","opts":["Ribera del Duero","Montilla-Moriles","Jumilla and Yecla","Rías Baixas"],"a":1,"exp":"Much Pedro Ximénez is grown in Montilla-Moriles, in the province of Córdoba, where it ripens more reliably, and is brought to Jerez as must. The other three lie far from Andalusia."}],"sources":[{"title":"Sherry Wines, the regulatory council: Pedro Ximénez","url":"https://www.sherry.wine/sherry-wine/naturally-sweet-sherry-wine/pedro-ximenez"},{"title":"Consejo Regulador Montilla-Moriles: the Pedro Ximénez wine","url":"https://www.montillamoriles.es/en/wines/pximenez-montilla-moriles-wine/"},{"title":"Foods and Wines from Spain: Montilla-Moriles, the denomination","url":"https://www.foodswinesfromspain.com/en/wine/regions/andalusia/montilla-moriles-do"},{"title":"The Oxford Companion to Wine","url":""}]},
"trebbiano": {"slug":"trebbiano","name":"Trebbiano","colour":"white","kind":"family","status":"new","mode":"home","aliases":["Ugni Blanc","Trebbiano Toscano","Trebbiano Abruzzese","Trebbiano Romagnolo","Trebbiano Spoletino"],"notIn":["trebbiano di soave","trebbiano di lugana"],"say":"treb-BYAH-noh, in France oo-NYEE BLAHN","lines":{"s10":"Trebbiano is central Italy's everyday white: light, crisp and lemony, and in France, as Ugni Blanc, it is distilled into Cognac.","s20":"Trebbiano is a family of white grapes that share a name across central Italy, and the one that travelled is Trebbiano Toscano. It makes a light, dry white of lemon and green apple, crisp and quiet, made to be poured cold and young.","s45":"Trebbiano is a family name more than a single grape, shared by several different white vines of central Italy. The one that travelled is Trebbiano Toscano, which the French call Ugni Blanc. It tastes of lemon and green apple, with a faint note of almond. It is light, dry and crisp, with high acidity. It is made in steel and sold young, and in France the same sharp wine is distilled into Cognac. It is happy beside fried fish, antipasti or a simple pasta, as long as it is cold. Around Modena, the cooked juice of Trebbiano is aged for years in small casks to become traditional balsamic vinegar.","sale":"An Italian trattoria puts Trebbiano on the table without being asked: cold, crisp and at ease with every plate. It wants the fried starters, and it will run on happily to the pasta."},"glass":{"lead":"Lemon and green apple with a faint note of almond, light, dry and crisp, quiet in scent and short on the finish.","notes":[{"family":"citrus","notes":["lemon juice","lemon peel"]},{"family":"orchard fruit","notes":["green apple","ripe pear"]},{"family":"nutty","notes":["bitter almond","hazelnut"]},{"family":"mineral","notes":["sea salt","wet stone"]}],"ladder":[{"climate":"cool","where":"The Charente and Gascony, on France's Atlantic side","notes":["lemon juice","green apple"]},{"climate":"warm","where":"Central Italy, from Tuscany and Umbria to Abruzzo","notes":["lemon peel","ripe pear","bitter almond"]}],"structure":"Dry. Acidity high, alcohol low to moderate, body light, with little scent and a short, clean finish."},"worlds":{"home":{"home":"Central Italy. Trebbiano Toscano fills out Orvieto, where it is called Procanico, has a part in Frascati, and is dried with Malvasia for Vin Santo. Trebbiano d'Abruzzo may be Trebbiano Abruzzese, a separate vine that can age, or Toscano. Romagna and Spoleto have Trebbianos of their own. Most is made in steel and sold young.","abroad":"As Ugni Blanc it crossed into France, where it is distilled for Cognac and Armagnac and blended into the crisp whites of Gascony, with a part in the whites of Provence. Elsewhere it is little seen on a label."}},"service":{"temp":"45 to 50 degrees Fahrenheit, 7 to 10 degrees Celsius; an old vine Trebbiano d'Abruzzo a little warmer.","glass":"A white wine glass; a small glass for Vin Santo.","decant":"No. A light, fresh white is poured cold, straight from the bottle.","age":"Within a year or two as a rule; the rare old vine Trebbiano d'Abruzzo can keep twenty years."},"food":{"with":[{"dish":"Antipasti: olives, cured meats, fresh cheese","why":"a quiet wine suits a mixed plate"},{"dish":"Spaghetti with clams","why":"lemon and salt, with nothing in the way"},{"dish":"Fried fish and vegetables","why":"crisp acidity freshens each bite"},{"dish":"Roast chicken with lemon","why":"a light body and bright acidity"},{"dish":"Almond biscuits, with Vin Santo","why":"the Tuscan custom at the end of dinner"}]},"labels":[{"term":"Ugni Blanc","means":"The French name of Trebbiano Toscano: the grape of Cognac and Armagnac, and of Gascony's crisp whites."},{"term":"Trebbiano d'Abruzzo","means":"The white of Abruzzo, from Trebbiano Abruzzese or Trebbiano Toscano, at least 85 per cent together."},{"term":"Procanico","means":"The local name of Trebbiano Toscano in Orvieto and around Lake Bolsena."},{"term":"Trebbiano Spoletino","means":"A separate vine of Spoleto and Montefalco in Umbria: late ripening, high in acidity, with lemon peel and salt."},{"term":"Trebbiano di Soave, di Lugana","means":"Verdicchio in the Veneto, and on Lake Garda a vine DNA ties to it, now Turbiana; no Trebbiano at all."}],"guest":[{"q":"Is Trebbiano the same as Pinot Grigio?","a":"No, though it fills the same place at the table. Both are light, dry and easy. Trebbiano is sharper and more lemony, where Pinot Grigio is softer, with pear."},{"q":"Is it true that Cognac is made from this grape?","a":"Yes. As Ugni Blanc, Trebbiano Toscano makes a thin, sharp wine low in alcohol, which is poor to drink and ideal to distil. Cognac and Armagnac both depend on it."},{"q":"Why does one Trebbiano d'Abruzzo taste plain and another so rich?","a":"Two vines share the name. Much is light and lemony, made for seafood. From old vines and low crops, Trebbiano Abruzzese turns waxy and salty, with hazelnut, and can keep twenty years."}],"compare":[{"slug":"pinot-grigio","tell":"Pinot Grigio is as light but softer, with pear and lower acidity; Trebbiano is sharper and more lemony, with a shorter finish."},{"slug":"verdicchio","tell":"Verdicchio has more flavour and a firm bitter almond finish; Trebbiano Toscano is lighter and plainer. Trebbiano di Soave is Verdicchio; Trebbiano di Lugana its near twin."},{"slug":"garganega","tell":"Garganega, as Soave, has more to say, with white peach and a bitter almond finish; Trebbiano is plainer, with lemon and green apple alone."}],"profile":{"c":"w","st":"Pale lemon · Body light · Acid high · Alcohol low to moderate · Dry and neutral; amber and sweet as Vin Santo","fruit":"Lemon, green apple and pear, faint in scent; sharper and thinner from France's Atlantic side","other":"A faint bitter almond; sea salt and wet stone from the better sites; hazelnut and a waxy feel in old vine Trebbiano d'Abruzzo; almost never new oak","tell":"Pale, light and high in acidity with very little scent: lemon, green apple and a short, clean finish, the quiet white of central Italy","regions":"Tuscany, Umbria, Lazio, Abruzzo and Romagna in Italy; as Ugni Blanc, Cognac, Armagnac, Gascony and Provence in France","confuse":"Pinot Grigio is softer, with pear; Garganega adds white peach and bitter almond; Verdicchio has more flavour and a firmer finish; Trebbiano di Soave is Verdicchio, Lugana's Turbiana nearly so"},"targets":["tuscany","umbria","abruzzo","lazio","emilia-romagna","italy","south-west-france","provence","france"],"cards":[{"id":"gt-8j9dd30z","front":"Trebbiano Toscano: what do the French call it, and what for?","back":"Ugni Blanc. Its thin, sharp wine, low in alcohol, is distilled for Cognac and Armagnac, and it goes into the crisp whites of Gascony and of Provence."},{"id":"gt-b4fzfkdd","front":"Why is Trebbiano called a family and not a grape?","back":"Several different vines share the name: Trebbiano Toscano, Abruzzese, Romagnolo and Spoletino are separate varieties. Trebbiano di Soave and Trebbiano di Lugana are no Trebbiano at all: the first is Verdicchio, the second its near twin."},{"id":"gt-1gqs0ars","front":"Trebbiano d'Abruzzo: which Trebbiano is in the bottle?","back":"Trebbiano Abruzzese or Trebbiano Toscano, at least 85 per cent together. Abruzzese is a separate vine: from old vines and low crops it gives a waxy, salty white that can age."}],"questions":[{"id":"gt-e8jmszjz","q":"A guest reads the back label of an Orvieto and asks which grape Procanico is. What do you tell him?","opts":["Trebbiano Toscano","Malvasia Bianca","Grechetto di Orvieto","Verdicchio Bianco"],"a":0,"exp":"Procanico is Trebbiano Toscano as Orvieto and Lake Bolsena call it, and it gives the blend crisp volume. Grechetto is its partner there, a separate grape that brings the nut and the bitter twist."},{"id":"gt-x2gqyulo","q":"A blind white is pale, light and dry, with high acidity, faint lemon and green apple, almost no scent, no oak and a short, clean finish. Which grape is it most likely to be?","opts":["Verdicchio","Garganega","Fiano","Trebbiano"],"a":3,"exp":"Very little scent, high acidity and a short finish point to Trebbiano. Verdicchio would show more flavour and a firm bitter almond finish, Garganega white peach, and Fiano a waxy weight."},{"id":"gt-33zhju04","q":"A guest orders almond biscuits to finish and asks for the Tuscan wine made to go with them, from dried Trebbiano and Malvasia. Which do you bring?","opts":["Recioto di Soave","Vin Santo","Moscato d'Asti","Marsala"],"a":1,"exp":"Vin Santo is Tuscany's wine of dried Trebbiano and Malvasia, aged for years in small sealed casks and poured with almond biscuits. Recioto di Soave is dried Garganega, from the Veneto."}],"sources":[{"title":"Bureau National Interprofessionnel du Cognac: the grape varieties of Cognac","url":"https://www.cognac.fr/en/discover/le-vignoble/cognac-grapes-varieties/"},{"title":"Consorzio Tutela Aceto Balsamico Tradizionale di Modena: the product specifications","url":"https://www.balsamicotradizionale.it/en/the-product-specifications"},{"title":"Vitis: Genetic studies on Trebbiano and morphologically related varieties by SSR and AFLP markers","url":"https://ojs.openagrar.de/index.php/VITIS/article/view/4527"},{"title":"The Oxford Companion to Wine","url":""}]},
"verdicchio": {"slug":"verdicchio","name":"Verdicchio","colour":"white","kind":"grape","status":"new","mode":"home","aliases":["Trebbiano di Soave","Turbiana"],"notIn":[],"say":"vair-DEEK-kyoh","lines":{"s10":"Verdicchio is the lemony white of the Marche on Italy's Adriatic coast, sharp and fresh, with a firm finish of bitter almond.","s20":"Verdicchio grows in the hills of the Marche, behind the Adriatic port of Ancona. It is a dry white with high acidity, and it tastes of lemon, green apple and fennel, ending on a clean bitterness of almond.","s45":"Verdicchio comes from the Marche, from the hills behind the Adriatic port of Ancona, and it is that coast's white for fish. It tastes of lemon, green apple and fennel, and it ends on a clean bitterness of almond. It is dry and medium in body, with high, bright acidity. Most is bottled young from steel tanks. A Riserva rests longer, and over ten years it turns to honey and toasted almond. It belongs with fried seafood, raw fish or a fish stew. In the 1950s it was given a green bottle shaped like an amphora, and that bottle carried its name round the world.","sale":"When the plate is sharp, salty or fried, Verdicchio holds its ground: bright lemon, high acidity and a firm finish of bitter almond. Have it with the shrimp remoulade or the fried seafood."},"glass":{"lead":"Lemon, green apple and wild fennel on high, bright acidity, dry and medium in body, closing on bitter almond and a salty edge.","notes":[{"family":"citrus","notes":["lemon zest","lemon pith"]},{"family":"orchard fruit","notes":["green apple","yellow apple"]},{"family":"herbal","notes":["wild fennel","anise"]},{"family":"nutty","notes":["bitter almond","green almond"]},{"family":"mineral","notes":["struck flint","sea salt"]},{"family":"age","notes":["honey","beeswax","toasted almond"]}],"ladder":[{"climate":"cool","where":"Matelica, a high inland valley closed to the sea","notes":["lemon zest","green apple","struck flint"]},{"climate":"moderate","where":"The Castelli di Jesi, nearer the Adriatic, and Lugana","notes":["yellow apple","wild fennel","bitter almond"]}],"structure":"Dry. Acidity high, alcohol medium to medium plus, body medium, with a faintly oily feel and a firm, bitter almond finish."},"worlds":{"home":{"home":"The Marche, on Italy's Adriatic coast. Verdicchio dei Castelli di Jesi, in the hills west of Ancona, is the larger and broader zone; Verdicchio di Matelica, in a high inland valley, is leaner and slower to open. Both hold at least 85 per cent Verdicchio and are made mostly in steel, and the Riserva of each ages for years.","abroad":"It hardly leaves Italy. In the Veneto it is Trebbiano di Soave, a minor partner in Soave, and on the clay shore of Lake Garda its near twin, Turbiana, makes Lugana, typically a little rounder."}},"service":{"temp":"45 to 50 degrees Fahrenheit, 7 to 10 degrees Celsius; an aged Riserva a few degrees warmer.","glass":"A white wine glass; a wider bowl for an aged Riserva.","decant":"Usually unnecessary. A closed Riserva may gain from a few minutes of air in the glass.","age":"Most within three years; a Riserva from Jesi or Matelica keeps ten years or more, gaining honey and toasted almond."},"food":{"with":[{"dish":"Fried seafood, lightly battered","why":"high acidity lifts the batter and the salt"},{"dish":"Adriatic fish stew with tomato","why":"the Marche's own dish: acidity meets the tomato"},{"dish":"Raw fish and shellfish","why":"lemon and a salty edge season the fish"},{"dish":"Shrimp remoulade","why":"bright acidity stands up to mustard and lemon"},{"dish":"Roast rabbit or mushrooms, with a Riserva","why":"an aged wine brings honey and toasted almond"}]},"labels":[{"term":"Verdicchio dei Castelli di Jesi","means":"The larger zone, in the hills west of Ancona; Classico marks its historic core."},{"term":"Verdicchio di Matelica","means":"The smaller zone, a high inland valley: leaner, sharper and slower to open."},{"term":"Riserva","means":"Longer aged, at least eighteen months, and since 2010 a DOCG of its own in each zone."},{"term":"Lugana, Turbiana","means":"The Lake Garda white and its grape, once called Trebbiano di Lugana; DNA study ties it to Verdicchio."},{"term":"Trebbiano di Soave","means":"Verdicchio's name in the Veneto, a minor partner to Garganega in Soave; a different vine from Trebbiano Toscano."}],"guest":[{"q":"I like Sauvignon Blanc. Will I like Verdicchio?","a":"I think so. It has the same brisk acidity and lemon, but it is quieter in scent, with fennel and bitter almond where Sauvignon Blanc has gooseberry and cut grass."},{"q":"Can an Italian white like this really age?","a":"This one can. Its high acidity keeps it fresh, and a Riserva from a good year moves from lemon and green apple to honey, beeswax and toasted almond over ten years or more."},{"q":"The list has a Lugana. Is that the same grape?","a":"As near as makes no difference. Lugana, from the clay shore of Lake Garda, is made from Turbiana, which DNA study ties to Verdicchio. It tends to be a little rounder and gentler."}],"compare":[{"slug":"garganega","tell":"Garganega, as Soave, is softer and rounder, with white peach and gentler acidity; Verdicchio is leaner and sharper, with lemon, fennel and green almond."},{"slug":"trebbiano","tell":"Trebbiano Toscano is a different vine, lighter and plainer; Verdicchio has more flavour and a bitter almond finish. Trebbiano di Soave is Verdicchio; Trebbiano di Lugana its near twin."},{"slug":"fiano","tell":"Fiano is broader and waxier, with ripe pear, honeysuckle and hazelnut; Verdicchio is leaner and higher in acidity, with lemon and a bitter almond finish."}],"profile":{"c":"w","st":"Pale straw with green glints · Body medium · Acid high · Alcohol medium to medium plus · Dry, with a firm, faintly bitter finish","fruit":"Lemon zest, green apple; yellow apple from the riper sites of the Castelli di Jesi","other":"Wild fennel and anise, bitter and green almond, struck flint and a salty edge; honey, beeswax and toasted almond with age; mostly no oak","tell":"High acidity with lemon, fennel and a firm bitter almond finish on a medium body, a green glint in the colour: Verdicchio from the Marche","regions":"Verdicchio dei Castelli di Jesi and Verdicchio di Matelica in the Marche; Lugana on Lake Garda, as Turbiana; Soave, as Trebbiano di Soave","confuse":"Garganega is softer, with white peach; Fiano is broader and waxier, with hazelnut; Trebbiano Toscano is lighter and plainer; Vermentino is saltier and softer in acidity, with lime and thyme"},"targets":["marche","lombardy","veneto","italy"],"cards":[{"id":"gt-jmc0vfnz","front":"Verdicchio: name its two zones in the Marche and how they differ.","back":"Verdicchio dei Castelli di Jesi, in the hills west of Ancona, is larger and broader. Verdicchio di Matelica, in a high inland valley closed to the sea, is leaner, sharper and slower to open."},{"id":"gt-cczglthe","front":"What do Trebbiano di Soave and Turbiana have to do with Verdicchio?","back":"One is its other name, the other its near twin. DNA study ties Verdicchio to Trebbiano di Soave in the Veneto and to Turbiana, the grape of Lugana on Lake Garda. Neither is Trebbiano Toscano."},{"id":"gt-hu3sn5ot","front":"Verdicchio blind: what gives the glass away?","back":"A green glint, high acidity, lemon, green apple and fennel on a medium body, and a firm finish of bitter almond. With age it gains honey and toasted almond."}],"questions":[{"id":"gt-qunjiaos","q":"A blind Italian white is pale straw with a green glint, dry and medium in body, with high acidity, lemon, green apple and fennel, no oak and a firm finish of bitter almond. Which grape is it most likely to be?","opts":["Arneis","Garganega","Verdicchio","Fiano"],"a":2,"exp":"High acidity with lemon, fennel and a firm bitter almond finish points to Verdicchio. Garganega shares the almond but is softer, with white peach; Arneis is soft in acidity, and Fiano is broader and waxier."},{"id":"gt-qb23ptqa","q":"A guest enjoyed a Lugana beside Lake Garda and asks for a wine of the same grape from its home on the Adriatic side of Italy. Which do you bring?","opts":["Verdicchio di Matelica","Orvieto Classico","Gavi di Gavi","Trebbiano d'Abruzzo"],"a":0,"exp":"Lugana is made from Turbiana, which DNA study ties to Verdicchio, the grape of the Marche. Trebbiano d'Abruzzo tempts because Turbiana was once called Trebbiano di Lugana, but it is made from other vines."},{"id":"gt-70g6poo4","q":"A guest wants a Verdicchio with some years behind it, turned to honey and toasted almond, to pour with roast rabbit and mushrooms. Which word on the label do you look for?","opts":["Superiore","Classico","Passito","Riserva"],"a":3,"exp":"Riserva marks the longer aged wine, a DOCG in each zone since 2010. Classico names the historic core of the Jesi zone and says nothing of age, and Passito is a sweet wine from dried grapes."}],"sources":[{"title":"Associazione Italiana Sommelier: the production rules of Castelli di Jesi Verdicchio Riserva DOCG","url":"https://aisitalia.it/Disciplinari/Marche/DOCG/Castelli%20di%20Jesi%20Verdicchio%20Riserva.pdf"},{"title":"Associazione Italiana Sommelier: the production rules of Lugana DOC","url":"https://aisitalia.it/Disciplinari/Veneto/DOC/Lugana.pdf"},{"title":"Wine Grapes","url":""},{"title":"The Oxford Companion to Wine","url":""}]},
"agiorgitiko": {"slug":"agiorgitiko","name":"Agiorgitiko","colour":"red","kind":"grape","status":"new","mode":"home","aliases":[],"notIn":[],"say":"ah-yor-YEE-tee-koh","lines":{"s10":"Agiorgitiko, Saint George's grape, makes Nemea in the Peloponnese: deep purple, plum and black cherry, with a soft, velvety tannin.","s20":"Agiorgitiko makes the red wine of Nemea, in the hills south west of Corinth. It is deep in colour and soft in tannin, with plum, black cherry and a touch of cinnamon, easy young and richer after time in oak.","s45":"Agiorgitiko takes its name from Saint George, and its home is Nemea, in the north east Peloponnese. It is deep purple, with black plum, black cherry and a touch of cinnamon. The tannin is soft and the feel generous. Altitude shapes it: the warm valley floor gives plush wines, and the cooler hillside villages give firmer, fresher ones. Young Nemea sees little oak, while a Reserve from the slopes takes on vanilla, cedar and tobacco. Offer it with grilled lamb, moussaka or a tomato braise. In Nemea Hercules killed the lion, and the valley's old sweet red was known as the blood of Hercules.","sale":"Agiorgitiko is Saint George's grape, from the valley where, in legend, Hercules killed the lion: black plum, black cherry and a touch of cinnamon. I would pour it with the lamb or the moussaka, for a table curious about Greek wine."},"glass":{"lead":"Black plum, black cherry and a touch of cinnamon in a deep purple glass, generous in body, with a soft, velvety tannin.","notes":[{"family":"black fruit","notes":["black plum","black cherry"]},{"family":"red fruit","notes":["sour cherry","strawberry"]},{"family":"spice","notes":["cinnamon","clove"]},{"family":"oak","notes":["vanilla pod","cedar"]},{"family":"earth","notes":["tobacco leaf","leather"]}],"ladder":[{"climate":"cool","where":"The high plateau of Asprokambos","notes":["strawberry","sour cherry","red cherry"]},{"climate":"moderate","where":"The hillside villages such as Koutsi","notes":["black cherry","sour cherry","cinnamon"]},{"climate":"warm","where":"The valley floor of Nemea, on red clay","notes":["ripe black plum","black cherry","dried fig"]}],"structure":"Dry. Acidity medium, tannin medium and soft, alcohol medium plus, body medium to full, and deep in colour."},"worlds":{"home":{"home":"Nemea, in the north east Peloponnese, where a wine labelled Nemea is Agiorgitiko alone and altitude decides the style: plush from the red clay of the valley floor, firmer and fresher from hillside villages such as Koutsi, bright, late picked reds and much of the rosé from the high plateau.","abroad":"Outside Greece it is rare, planted by only a handful of growers. At home it also makes rosé and blends with Cabernet Sauvignon, sold as regional wine of the Peloponnese."}},"service":{"temp":"59 to 63 degrees Fahrenheit, 15 to 17 degrees Celsius, as for a medium red.","glass":"A Bordeaux glass, with room to swirl, for a Reserve; an ordinary red wine glass suits a young Nemea.","decant":"Usually unnecessary for a young Nemea; consider it for a Reserve or a Cabernet blend after a sample.","age":"Young Nemea within three to five years; a Reserve or Grande Reserve from the slopes ten and more."},"food":{"with":[{"dish":"Grilled lamb","why":"soft tannin and ripe plum beside the char"},{"dish":"Moussaka or pastitsio","why":"ripe black plum meets the cinnamon and the béchamel"},{"dish":"Beef braised in tomato","why":"the wine's cherry and cinnamon suit the sauce"},{"dish":"Pork souvlaki","why":"an easy red for grilled, herby pork"}]},"labels":[{"term":"Nemea","means":"The Peloponnese appellation for red from Agiorgitiko alone, dry above all, with rare sweet reds."},{"term":"Reserve, Grande Reserve","means":"The national ageing terms; Grande Reserve holds four years at least between oak and bottle."},{"term":"PGI Peloponnese","means":"Regional wine of the same vineyards: Agiorgitiko rosé and blends with Cabernet Sauvignon or Merlot."},{"term":"Koutsi, Asprokambos","means":"Village names that may stand beside Nemea: the slopes and the high plateau."}],"guest":[{"q":"How do I say it, and what does it mean?","a":"Ah yor YEE tee ko. It means Saint George's grape, and on a list you will often find it simply as Nemea, the place it comes from."},{"q":"Is Greek red wine heavy?","a":"Not this one. Agiorgitiko is deep in colour but soft in tannin, with black plum and black cherry, so a young Nemea drinks easily, even before the food arrives."},{"q":"Why does this blend with Cabernet not say Nemea?","a":"A wine labelled Nemea is Agiorgitiko alone. Cabernet Sauvignon stiffens its soft frame and adds blackcurrant, but the blend is sold as regional wine of the Peloponnese."}],"compare":[{"slug":"xinomavro","tell":"Xinomavro, from Naoussa in the north, is pale, sharper and far more tannic, with sun dried tomato and black olive; Agiorgitiko is deeper in colour, softer and plusher."},{"slug":"merlot","tell":"Merlot is as soft and plummy, but often shows cocoa and bay leaf; Agiorgitiko is deeper purple, with sour cherry and a touch of cinnamon."},{"slug":"sangiovese","tell":"Sangiovese is paler and tarter, with tomato leaf, dried oregano and a dusty grip; Agiorgitiko is deeper and softer, with black plum and cinnamon."}],"profile":{"c":"r","st":"Deep purple to ruby · Body medium to full · Acid medium · Tannin medium, soft and velvety · Alcohol medium plus · Dry","fruit":"Black plum, black cherry and sour cherry; strawberry in rosé","other":"Cinnamon and clove; vanilla, cedar and tobacco from oak in a Reserve; raisin and dried fig in the rare sweet reds","tell":"Deep colour with a soft, velvety tannin, plum and a touch of cinnamon: plush where Xinomavro is pale and sharp","regions":"Nemea in the Peloponnese, with regional wine of the Peloponnese and Corinth; rare outside Greece","confuse":"Xinomavro is pale, sharp and tannic, with sun dried tomato; Merlot is as soft but often shows cocoa and bay leaf"},"targets":["nemea","greece"],"cards":[{"id":"gt-vc81hvs6","front":"Agiorgitiko: which appellation does it make, and with what other grapes?","back":"Nemea, in the north east Peloponnese, and none: a red labelled Nemea is Agiorgitiko alone. Blends with Cabernet Sauvignon are regional wine."},{"id":"gt-24ymn1kj","front":"How does altitude change Agiorgitiko within Nemea?","back":"The warm valley floor on red clay gives plush wines and the old sweet reds; the hillside villages give firmer, fresher wines; the cool high plateau gives bright, late picked reds and much of the rosé."},{"id":"gt-60opuj4f","front":"Agiorgitiko: what does its name mean, and what legend belongs to Nemea?","back":"Saint George's grape. Nemea is where Hercules killed the lion, and the valley's old sweet red was called the blood of Hercules."}],"questions":[{"id":"gt-dqf33jeq","q":"A Greek red poured blind is deep purple, with black plum, black cherry and a touch of cinnamon, medium acidity and a soft, velvety tannin. Which grape is it?","opts":["Xinomavro","Mavrodaphne","Agiorgitiko","Limnio"],"a":2,"exp":"Deep colour, plum and cherry and a soft tannin mark Agiorgitiko of Nemea. Xinomavro, the nearest miss, is pale and high in acid with a firm grip, and smells of sun dried tomato and black olive."},{"id":"gt-dzk7k6qx","q":"A guest sees a Peloponnese red that blends Agiorgitiko with Cabernet Sauvignon and asks why it is not labelled Nemea. What do you tell her?","opts":["Nemea must come from the coast","Nemea must be Agiorgitiko alone","Nemea must age ten years","Nemea must be a sweet wine"],"a":1,"exp":"A red labelled Nemea is Agiorgitiko alone, so a blend with Cabernet Sauvignon is sold as regional wine. Nemea does allow rare sweet reds, but most of it is dry, so sweetness is the nearest wrong answer."},{"id":"gt-nhv50mn3","q":"A guest has heard of an old sweet red of Nemea, once called the blood of Hercules, and asks how it was made. What do you tell her?","opts":["From grapes frozen on the vine","By adding sugar to the finished dry wine","From grapes touched by noble rot","From sun dried or overripe Agiorgitiko"],"a":3,"exp":"Nemea's old sweet red came from sun dried or overripe Agiorgitiko, left naturally sweet or fortified. Noble rot, the nearest wrong answer, makes Sauternes and Tokaji, not the sweet reds of Nemea."}],"sources":[{"title":"Wine Enthusiast: what is Agiorgitiko","url":"https://www.wineenthusiast.com/varietals/agiorgitiko"},{"title":"Wein-Plus wine guide: PDO Nemea","url":"https://wineguide.wein.plus/wine-regions/p-d-o-nemea-o-p-a-p-nemea"},{"title":"Neos Kosmos: growing Greek grapes in Australia","url":"https://neoskosmos.com/en/2025/12/08/news/greece/growing-greek-grapes-in-australia/"},{"title":"The Oxford Companion to Wine","url":""}]},
"montepulciano": {"slug":"montepulciano","name":"Montepulciano","colour":"red","kind":"grape","status":"new","mode":"home","aliases":[],"notIn":["prugnolo gentile","rosso di montepulciano","vin santo"],"say":"mon-teh-pool-CHAH-noh","lines":{"s10":"Montepulciano is the dark, generous red of Abruzzo: black cherry and plum, soft acidity and plenty of round tannin.","s20":"Montepulciano is the red grape of Italy's Adriatic side, above all in Abruzzo, where it makes Montepulciano d'Abruzzo. It is deep purple and plummy, with black cherry, soft acidity and a round, generous grip.","s45":"Montepulciano grows down the Adriatic side of Italy, and Abruzzo is its heart. It ripens late, into October, and gives a deep purple wine of black cherry and plum. The acidity is soft and the tannin plentiful but round. Most is fermented quickly and bottled young, while a few hill estates age it for years in large casks. Pressed off its skins after a few hours, it also makes Cerasuolo, a cherry red rosé. It belongs with skewers of grilled lamb, sausages or pasta in a meat sauce. Do not confuse it with Vino Nobile di Montepulciano, the Tuscan town's wine, which is Sangiovese.","sale":"Montepulciano is a grape, not the Tuscan town, and in Abruzzo it makes a dark, round red full of black cherry. I would pour it with the lamb or the sausage pasta, or begin with a glass of its cherry red rosé."},"glass":{"lead":"Black cherry and black plum in a deep purple glass, with soft acidity and plentiful, ripe, round tannin.","notes":[{"family":"black fruit","notes":["black cherry","black plum","blackberry"]},{"family":"red fruit","notes":["sour cherry","wild strawberry"]},{"family":"spice","notes":["liquorice","cracked black pepper"]},{"family":"earth","notes":["leather","dried leaves"]},{"family":"oak","notes":["vanilla pod","cocoa"]}],"ladder":[{"climate":"moderate","where":"The Peligna valley and the Ofena plateau, high in the Apennines","notes":["sour cherry","black cherry"]},{"climate":"warm","where":"The Chieti hills near the Adriatic, and Australia's Barossa","notes":["ripe black plum","blackberry","plum jam"]}],"structure":"Dry. Acidity low to medium, tannin medium to medium plus, ripe and round, alcohol medium plus, body medium plus to full; deep purple."},"worlds":{"home":{"home":"Abruzzo is its heartland: Montepulciano d'Abruzzo, at least 85 per cent of the grape, the Colline Teramane DOCG in the Teramo hills, and Cerasuolo d'Abruzzo, its cherry red rosé. The Marche makes Conero from it and blends it into Rosso Piceno.","abroad":"Molise, Puglia and the Roma DOC grow it too. Beyond Italy it is a newcomer: Australia has planted it in warm regions such as the Barossa and the Riverland, where it ripens late and fully."}},"service":{"temp":"59 to 63 degrees Fahrenheit, 15 to 17 degrees Celsius; a Colline Teramane Riserva a little warmer.","glass":"A Bordeaux glass, with room for black cherry, black plum and ripe tannin to open.","decant":"Rarely for an everyday bottle; consider it for a young Colline Teramane Riserva after a taste.","age":"Most within three to five years; Colline Teramane and the slow, traditional estates ten or more."},"food":{"with":[{"dish":"Skewers of grilled lamb","why":"ripe fruit and round tannin meet char and fat"},{"dish":"Pasta alla chitarra in a meat ragù","why":"black plum and gentle acidity suit a rich sauce"},{"dish":"Aubergine baked with tomato and cheese","why":"round fruit beside tomato, cheese and fried aubergine"},{"dish":"Roast lamb, with Colline Teramane","why":"firmer tannin for a slow cooked roast"},{"dish":"Crawfish étouffée, with Cerasuolo","why":"a dry, cherry red rosé with grip meets the rich sauce"}]},"labels":[{"term":"Montepulciano d'Abruzzo","means":"At least 85 per cent Montepulciano from Abruzzo; Riserva ages at least two years."},{"term":"Colline Teramane","means":"The DOCG of the Teramo hills, at least 90 per cent Montepulciano; Riserva three years, one in wood."},{"term":"Cerasuolo d'Abruzzo","means":"The deep cherry red rosé of Montepulciano, its own DOC since 2010; cerasa means cherry."},{"term":"Conero, Rosso Piceno","means":"The Marche's Montepulciano reds: Conero from the headland above Ancona, Rosso Piceno blended with Sangiovese."},{"term":"Vino Nobile di Montepulciano","means":"Sangiovese from the Tuscan town of that name, not this grape."}],"guest":[{"q":"Is this the same as Vino Nobile di Montepulciano?","a":"No. Vino Nobile is Sangiovese from the Tuscan town of Montepulciano. This is the Montepulciano grape, grown down the Adriatic side and in Abruzzo above all."},{"q":"What is Cerasuolo?","a":"The rosé of Montepulciano in Abruzzo, named for the cherry. It is pressed off the skins after a few hours, so it is darker than most pinks, dry, with real grip."},{"q":"Will it be a heavy wine?","a":"Deep in colour and generous, but rarely hard: the acidity is soft and the tannin round, so it drinks easily young."}],"compare":[{"slug":"sangiovese","tell":"Sangiovese is paler and tarter, with tomato leaf and a dusty grip; Montepulciano is deeper in colour, with black cherry and plum and softer acidity."},{"slug":"aglianico","tell":"Aglianico is firmer and more acidic, with high, drying tannin and a smoky edge; Montepulciano is rounder, softer in acidity and readier young."}],"profile":{"c":"r","st":"Deep purple ruby · Body medium plus to full · Acid low to medium · Tannin medium to medium plus, ripe and round · Alcohol medium plus · Dry","fruit":"Black cherry, black plum, blackberry; sour cherry and wild strawberry in the Cerasuolo rosé","other":"Liquorice, black pepper and leather, with a touch of dried leaves; vanilla and cocoa where new barrels are used","tell":"Deep purple with ripe black cherry and plum, plentiful but round tannin and soft acidity: dark and generous, rarely sharp","regions":"Abruzzo (Montepulciano d'Abruzzo, Colline Teramane, Cerasuolo d'Abruzzo), the Marche (Conero, Rosso Piceno), Molise, Puglia, Lazio","confuse":"Sangiovese is paler and tarter, with tomato leaf; Aglianico is firmer and more acidic; Vino Nobile di Montepulciano is Sangiovese, not this grape"},"targets":["abruzzo","marche","lazio","puglia","italy"],"cards":[{"id":"gt-0s79t4x0","front":"Montepulciano d'Abruzzo and Vino Nobile di Montepulciano: which grape is in each?","back":"Montepulciano d'Abruzzo is the Montepulciano grape. Vino Nobile di Montepulciano is Sangiovese, named for a Tuscan town."},{"id":"gt-d4bn6y0d","front":"Montepulciano: what is Cerasuolo d'Abruzzo, and how is it made?","back":"Its deep cherry red rosé, a DOC since 2010. The grapes are pressed off their skins after a few hours, so it keeps colour and real tannin."},{"id":"gt-mnplf0sg","front":"Montepulciano: the Abruzzo DOCG of the Teramo hills, and its rules?","back":"Montepulciano d'Abruzzo Colline Teramane: at least 90 per cent Montepulciano, firmer than the plain DOC; Riserva three years, at least one in wood."}],"questions":[{"id":"gt-hb4kdcjd","q":"An Italian red poured blind is deep purple, with ripe black cherry and plum, soft acidity and plentiful but round tannin. Which grape is it?","opts":["Sangiovese","Aglianico","Montepulciano","Nebbiolo"],"a":2,"exp":"Deep colour, ripe black cherry and plum, soft acidity and round tannin are Montepulciano. Sangiovese, the nearest wrong option, would be paler and tarter, with tomato leaf and dusty tannin."},{"id":"gt-fbe60xp5","q":"A guest asks for a Montepulciano d'Abruzzo and the server brings a Vino Nobile di Montepulciano. What has gone wrong?","opts":["Vino Nobile is the rosé of the same grape","Vino Nobile is Sangiovese from a Tuscan town","Vino Nobile is Montepulciano from the Marche","Vino Nobile is the Riserva of the same wine"],"a":1,"exp":"Montepulciano d'Abruzzo is named for the grape; Vino Nobile is named for the Tuscan town and made mainly from Sangiovese. The rosé of the Montepulciano grape is Cerasuolo d'Abruzzo."},{"id":"gt-2lk2vxi1","q":"A guest wants an Italian rosé dark and firm enough for grilled meat. Of these four on the list, which do you pour?","opts":["Cerasuolo d'Abruzzo","Lambrusco di Sorbara","Cerasuolo di Vittoria","Chiaretto di Bardolino"],"a":0,"exp":"Cerasuolo d'Abruzzo is Montepulciano pressed after a few hours on its skins: cherry red, dry and gripping. Cerasuolo di Vittoria, the nearest miss, shares the word but is a Sicilian red."}],"sources":[{"title":"Regione Abruzzo: production rules of Montepulciano d'Abruzzo DOC","url":"https://www.regione.abruzzo.it/system/files/agricoltura/vitivinicolo/albi-disciplinari/montepulciano_d_abruzzo_doc_disciplinare.pdf"},{"title":"Consorzio Tutela Vini d'Abruzzo: Cerasuolo d'Abruzzo DOC","url":"https://www.vinidabruzzo.it/wp-content/uploads/2024/04/CERASUOLO-DABRUZZO-DOC.pdf"},{"title":"Australian Wine Research Institute: Montepulciano","url":"https://www.awri.com.au/files/attachment/s1830"},{"title":"Wine and Viticulture Journal: alternative varieties, Montepulciano","url":"https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/2629/3320/files/AV_Montepulciano_MayJune16WVJ.pdf"}]},
"touriga-franca": {"slug":"touriga-franca","name":"Touriga Franca","colour":"red","kind":"grape","status":"new","mode":"home","aliases":["Touriga Francesa"],"notIn":[],"say":"too-REE-gah FRAHN-kah, or too-REE-gah frahn-SAY-zah","lines":{"s10":"Touriga Franca is the Douro's dependable red, juicy and perfumed: blackberry, black plum and rose petal, softer than Touriga Nacional.","s20":"Touriga Franca grows across the Douro and gives Port and the dry reds their flesh and perfume. It is lighter and juicier than Touriga Nacional, with blackberry, rose petal and a firm but supple grip.","s45":"Touriga Franca seldom works alone: it is the other half of many Douro blends. It is at home on the Douro's hot schist, ripening late and cropping reliably. In the glass it is deep red, with blackberry, black plum and rose petal. It is lighter and juicier than Touriga Nacional, with fresh acidity and a firm but supple grip. The two are blended for Port and dry Douro reds: Nacional gives structure, and Franca flesh and perfume. It goes with grilled lamb, roast pork or cured ham. Touriga Nacional is one of its parents, so in many a Douro blend parent and child share the glass.","sale":"Touriga Franca shows the softer, more fragrant side of the Douro: blackberry and rose petal with a grip that stays gentle. It suits the roast pork, and any table that finds Touriga Nacional too much."},"glass":{"lead":"Blackberry, black plum and rose petal in a deep red glass, juicy and medium plus in body, with fresh acidity and a supple grip.","notes":[{"family":"black fruit","notes":["blackberry","black cherry","black plum"]},{"family":"floral","notes":["rose petal","rockrose"]},{"family":"red fruit","notes":["pomegranate","red cherry"]},{"family":"oak","notes":["vanilla pod","toast"]}],"ladder":[{"climate":"moderate","where":"The wetter Baixo Corgo and the Douro's higher, cooler slopes","notes":["black cherry","rose petal","pomegranate"]},{"climate":"warm","where":"The Cima Corgo and the Douro Superior","notes":["blackberry","black plum","rockrose"]}],"structure":"Dry. Acidity medium plus, tannin medium plus to high, firm but velvety, alcohol medium plus, body medium plus."},"worlds":{"home":{"home":"The Douro, where it ripens late and crops reliably on the hot schist slopes. It is blended with Touriga Nacional and the other favoured grapes for Port and for the dry Douro reds, bringing flesh and a rose petal perfume, and a few cellars bottle it alone.","abroad":"It seldom leaves northern Portugal. California grows a little, in Lodi above all, mostly for fortified wine or blended with Touriga Nacional."}},"service":{"temp":"61 to 64 degrees Fahrenheit, 16 to 18 degrees Celsius, as for a full red or a Vintage Port.","glass":"A tall Bordeaux glass for the dry wines; a small tulip for Port.","decant":"Consider it for a young dry red after tasting a sample; decant a Vintage Port off its crust.","age":"Most dry reds within five to ten years; within a Vintage Port blend, for decades."},"food":{"with":[{"dish":"Grilled lamb chops","why":"juicy black plum and a supple grip suit the char"},{"dish":"Roast pork","why":"fresh acidity and gentle tannin cut the fat"},{"dish":"Cured ham","why":"salt beside ripe, juicy blackberry"},{"dish":"Duck confit","why":"rose petal and black cherry beside rich, salty duck"}]},"labels":[{"term":"Touriga Francesa","means":"The older name of Touriga Franca, still seen on some labels; the grape has no French origin."},{"term":"Douro","means":"Dry reds of the Port valley, often blends of Touriga Franca and Touriga Nacional."},{"term":"Port","means":"Fortified wine of the Douro, most of it red; Touriga Franca is one of its five favoured grapes."}],"guest":[{"q":"Is Touriga Franca related to Touriga Nacional?","a":"Yes, it is its child: DNA shows a cross of Touriga Nacional and an old Douro grape called Marufo. It is lighter and juicier than its parent, with more rose petal in the perfume."},{"q":"Is it a French grape?","a":"No. Its old name, Touriga Francesa, sounds French, but it is a Portuguese grape of the Douro, and Portugal now calls it Touriga Franca."},{"q":"Why is it so often blended?","a":"On its own it is supple and perfumed but a little less deep than Touriga Nacional. Together they balance: Nacional gives the structure, Franca the flesh and fragrance."}],"compare":[{"slug":"touriga-nacional","tell":"Touriga Nacional, its parent, is darker and firmer, with violet and bergamot; Touriga Franca is lighter and juicier, with rose petal and a suppler grip."},{"slug":"tempranillo","tell":"Tinta Roriz, Tempranillo in the Douro, is rounder and softer in acidity, with strawberry and red plum; Touriga Franca is more perfumed, with rose petal and a firmer grip."}],"profile":{"c":"r","st":"Deep ruby to purple · Body medium plus · Acid medium plus · Tannin medium plus to high, firm but velvety · Alcohol medium plus · Dry","fruit":"Blackberry, black cherry and black plum, with a tang of pomegranate","other":"Rose petal and rockrose; vanilla and toast where French oak is used; a supple, juicy texture","tell":"Blackberry and rose petal with a supple grip: the softer, more fragrant partner beside Touriga Nacional in a Douro blend","regions":"The Douro, for Port and dry reds; a little elsewhere in northern Portugal and in California","confuse":"Touriga Nacional is darker and firmer, with violet and bergamot; Tinta Roriz is rounder and softer in acidity, with strawberry and red plum"},"targets":["douro","portugal"],"cards":[{"id":"gt-9r31u2zp","front":"Touriga Franca: who are its parents, and what was its old name?","back":"Touriga Nacional and Marufo, by DNA. Its old name was Touriga Francesa, though the grape has no French origin."},{"id":"gt-k8nn1rvf","front":"What does Touriga Franca bring to a Port or Douro blend?","back":"Flesh, juicy blackberry and a perfume of rose petal and rockrose, beside the depth and structure of Touriga Nacional."},{"id":"gt-patjvpne","front":"Why do Douro growers value Touriga Franca on hot sites?","back":"It ripens late and crops reliably in the heat, and its thick skins give colour and tannin."}],"questions":[{"id":"gt-u2lces4o","q":"A guest sees Touriga Francesa on an older Port list and asks whether it is a French grape. What do you tell him?","opts":["Yes: it is Portugal's name for Cabernet Franc","No: it is an old name for Tempranillo","Yes: it came from Bordeaux in the 1800s","No: it is Touriga Franca, a Portuguese grape"],"a":3,"exp":"Touriga Francesa is the older name of Touriga Franca, a Portuguese grape born of Touriga Nacional and Marufo. Cabernet Franc, the nearest miss in sound, is no relation."},{"id":"gt-ge23jymu","q":"A dry Douro red poured blind is deep red but supple, with blackberry, black cherry and rose petal, fresh acidity and a gentler grip than a Touriga Nacional. Which grape leads it?","opts":["Tinto Cão","Touriga Franca","Sousão","Tinta Barroca"],"a":1,"exp":"Blackberry with rose petal and a supple grip mark Touriga Franca. Tinta Barroca, the nearest miss, is soft and fleshy too, but it ripens early, is planted on cooler sites and is prized for sugar."},{"id":"gt-ygk1wv2t","q":"A guest asks why a Douro red blends Touriga Franca with Touriga Nacional instead of bottling either alone. What is the usual reason?","opts":["Nacional gives structure, Franca flesh and perfume","The law forbids a single grape Douro red","Nacional cannot ripen on the schist","Franca adds sweetness to the dry wine"],"a":0,"exp":"Touriga Nacional brings depth and firm tannin, Touriga Franca juicy blackberry and a rose petal perfume, so each fills out the other. Douro law allows a varietal red, so the law is the nearest wrong answer."}],"sources":[{"title":"Wines of Portugal: Touriga Franca, Portuguese grape varieties","url":"https://winesofportugal.com/en/portuguese-wines/grape-varieties/touriga-franca"},{"title":"Wein-Plus glossary: Touriga Franca","url":"https://glossary.wein.plus/touriga-franca"},{"title":"Wine Folly: Touriga Franca","url":"https://winefolly.com/grapes/touriga-franca/"},{"title":"Wine Grapes","url":""}]},
"lambrusco": {"slug":"lambrusco","name":"Lambrusco","colour":"red","kind":"family","status":"new","mode":"home","aliases":["Lambrusco di Sorbara","Lambrusco Grasparossa","Lambrusco Salamino"],"notIn":[],"say":"lahm-BROO-skoh","lines":{"s10":"Lambrusco is the frothy red of Modena: violet, sour cherry and wild strawberry, sharp and cool, made to cut through rich pork.","s20":"Lambrusco is a family of grapes from Emilia, around Modena, made into a red wine with bubbles. Pale Sorbara is sharp, with violet and sour cherry, and dark Grasparossa brings blackberry and a firmer grip.","s45":"Lambrusco foams in the glass, a red wine with bubbles from the plain and foothills around Modena and Reggio Emilia. It is a family of grapes, not one. Sorbara is pale and sharp, with violet and sour cherry, while Grasparossa is dark, with blackberry and a firmer grip. The acidity is high and the tannin light. The bubbles are usually made in a sealed tank, and it comes dry, off dry or sweet. Pour it cool with prosciutto, mortadella and Parmigiano, the food of its own country. Labrusca, the Romans' word for a wild vine at the edge of a field, most likely gave the wine its name.","sale":"Lambrusco was made for the cured pork of its own country, and its sour cherry and cool foam scrub the palate clean. A cool bottle of dry Sorbara and the charcuterie board are old friends."},"glass":{"lead":"Sour cherry, wild strawberry and violet in a frothy red, pale and sharp from Sorbara, dark with blackberry and a firmer grip from Grasparossa.","notes":[{"family":"red fruit","notes":["sour cherry","wild strawberry","raspberry"]},{"family":"black fruit","notes":["blackberry","black plum"]},{"family":"floral","notes":["violet","rose petal"]},{"family":"lees and cellar","notes":["bread dough","fresh yeast"]}],"ladder":[{"climate":"cool","where":"The clay foothills of Castelvetro, as Grasparossa","notes":["blackberry","black plum"]},{"climate":"moderate","where":"The sandy plain of Sorbara, and Carpi for Salamino","notes":["violet","sour cherry","wild strawberry","raspberry"]}],"structure":"Dry to sweet, usually gently sparkling. Acidity high, tannin low to medium, alcohol low to medium, body light to medium; pale pink to deep purple."},"worlds":{"home":{"home":"Emilia, around Modena and Reggio: the sandy plain of Sorbara, the clay foothills of Castelvetro and the plain round Carpi each give their own Lambrusco and their own DOC. Across the Po, Lambrusco Mantovano is Lombardy's. Most is made sparkling in a sealed tank.","abroad":"Lambrusco Maestri, one member of the family, has travelled as far as Argentina and Australia; elsewhere it is barely grown."}},"service":{"temp":"50 to 57 degrees Fahrenheit, 10 to 14 degrees Celsius: pale Sorbara cooler, dark Grasparossa warmer.","glass":"A white wine glass or a tulip, so the perfume and the violet foam show.","decant":"No. Open it cool and pour it at once; the sparkle fades fast once opened.","age":"Drink it young, within a year or two of the harvest."},"food":{"with":[{"dish":"Prosciutto, mortadella and gnocco fritto","why":"bubbles and acidity cut the fat and salt"},{"dish":"Zampone or cotechino with lentils","why":"Grasparossa's firmer grip for rich, fatty pork"},{"dish":"Parmigiano Reggiano","why":"acidity and foam against salt and crystal"},{"dish":"Gumbo with andouille sausage","why":"a cool, frothy red against smoke and chilli"}]},"labels":[{"term":"Secco, Amabile, Dolce","means":"Dry, off dry to medium sweet, and sweet: the words that tell you the sugar."},{"term":"Lambrusco di Sorbara","means":"The pale, sharp member of the family, at least 60 per cent Sorbara; its own DOC since 1970."},{"term":"Lambrusco Grasparossa di Castelvetro","means":"The dark, firmer Lambrusco of the foothills south of Modena, at least 85 per cent Grasparossa."},{"term":"Frizzante, Spumante","means":"Frizzante is gently sparkling, the usual Lambrusco; spumante is fully sparkling."},{"term":"Rifermentato in bottiglia","means":"Refermented in the bottle and left on its yeast: cloudy, dry and a little bready."}],"guest":[{"q":"Isn't Lambrusco a sweet wine?","a":"It can be, but at home in Modena it is mostly drunk dry. Look for secco on the label; amabile is off dry to medium sweet and dolce is sweet."},{"q":"A red with bubbles? Should it be cold?","a":"Cool rather than icy: a pale Sorbara about as cold as a white, a dark Grasparossa a touch warmer. The chill and the foam keep it fresh beside rich food."},{"q":"Is Lambrusco one grape?","a":"A family of several. Sorbara is pale and sharp, Grasparossa dark and firmer, Salamino deep and round, and each has its own DOC around Modena."}],"compare":[{"slug":"barbera","tell":"Barbera shares the high acidity and low tannin but is still, with black cherry and a balsamic note; Lambrusco foams, with violet and wild strawberry."},{"slug":"gamay","tell":"Gamay is still, with kirsch and sometimes banana from whole bunch fermentation; Lambrusco foams and bites harder, with wild strawberry and a sharp, frothy finish."},{"slug":"glera","tell":"Glera, as Prosecco, is white, with green apple, pear and acacia blossom; Lambrusco is red, with sour cherry and violet, though both get their bubbles in a tank."}],"profile":{"c":"r","st":"Pale pink to deep purple, frothy · Body light to medium · Acid high · Tannin low to medium · Alcohol low to moderate · Dry to sweet","fruit":"Sour cherry, wild strawberry and raspberry in Sorbara; blackberry and black plum in Grasparossa","other":"Violet and rose petal; bread dough and yeast where refermented in bottle; a frothy, foaming sparkle","tell":"A sparkling red with a violet foam, sharp acidity and little tannin: sour cherry and violet when pale, blackberry when dark","regions":"Emilia around Modena and Reggio: Sorbara, Castelvetro, and Santa Croce near Carpi; Mantua in Lombardy","confuse":"Sparkling Shiraz is fuller, riper and darker; Brachetto d'Acqui is sweet and rose scented; Barbera shares the acidity but not the foam"},"targets":["emilia-romagna","lombardy","italy"],"cards":[{"id":"gt-66b6d4in","front":"Lambrusco: name the three DOCs of 1970 around Modena and each style.","back":"Sorbara, pale and sharp; Grasparossa di Castelvetro, dark and firmer; Salamino di Santa Croce, deep and round. The broader Modena and Reggiano DOCs blend the family."},{"id":"gt-kbfp1wpd","front":"On a Lambrusco label, what do secco, amabile and dolce mean?","back":"Dry; off dry to medium sweet; sweet. At home in Modena, dry Lambrusco is the tradition."},{"id":"gt-gughy359","front":"Lambrusco: how are the bubbles in most bottles made?","back":"By a second fermentation in a sealed tank, the Charmat or Martinotti method, stopped by chilling when the fizz and the sweetness are right. Some is refermented in the bottle."}],"questions":[{"id":"gt-0op3wofj","q":"A frothy red poured cool is pale ruby, almost pink, with violet, sour cherry and wild strawberry, sharp acidity and almost no tannin. Which Lambrusco is it?","opts":["Lambrusco Mantovano","Lambrusco Grasparossa di Castelvetro","Lambrusco di Sorbara","Lambrusco Salamino di Santa Croce"],"a":2,"exp":"Pale colour, violet and sharp acidity mark Sorbara, the pale member of the family. Salamino, the nearest miss, is deeper in colour and rounder, and Grasparossa darker and firmer still."},{"id":"gt-wodm2lvk","q":"A guest orders Lambrusco with the charcuterie but says she dislikes sweet wine. Which word do you look for on the label?","opts":["Secco","Dolce","Frizzante","Amabile"],"a":0,"exp":"Secco means dry. Amabile, the nearest miss, is off dry to medium sweet, and dolce is sweet; frizzante speaks of the sparkle, not the sugar."},{"id":"gt-chros9hn","q":"A table orders zampone, a rich pork sausage served with lentils, and asks for a Lambrusco with enough grip for it. Which do you bring?","opts":["Lambrusco di Sorbara","Lambrusco Salamino di Santa Croce","Reggiano Lambrusco amabile","Lambrusco Grasparossa"],"a":3,"exp":"Grasparossa is the dark, firmer Lambrusco of the foothills, the local match for zampone and cotechino. Salamino, the nearest miss, is deep in colour but round and soft; Sorbara is pale and sharp."}],"sources":[{"title":"Regione Emilia-Romagna, Agricoltura: the DOP and IGP wines and their production rules","url":"https://agricoltura.regione.emilia-romagna.it/dop-igp/temi/vini-dop-e-igp-emilia-romagna"},{"title":"Enoteca Regionale Emilia-Romagna: Lambrusco and its DOC","url":"https://enotecaemiliaromagna.it/en/wines-and-wineries/regional-wines/lambrusco/"},{"title":"Gambero Rosso International: Lambrusco at the restaurant, guidelines for restaurateurs","url":"https://www.gamberorossointernational.com/?p=311743"},{"title":"Falstaff: what is Lambrusco","url":"https://falstaff.com/en/grape-varieties/lambrusco"}]}
};
